        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-gaussian-sequence-inverse-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $X$ and $Y$ be real Hilbert spaces and let $K:X\\to Y$ be a compact linear operator of infinite rank, viewed as an inverse model with exact datum $Kx$. Here compact means that the image of every bounded subset has compact closure. Write $N(K)=\\{x\\in X:Kx=0\\}$ and $H_0=\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}K}$. Fix a positive singular system $(s_j,v_j,u_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$ for $K$, where $\\mathbb N=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$, $s_1\\ge s_2\\ge\\cdots>0$, $s_j\\to0$, $Kv_j=s_ju_j$, $(v_j)$ is a complete orthonormal basis of $N(K)^\\perp$, and $(u_j)$ is a complete orthonormal basis of $H_0$. A complete orthonormal basis is an orthonormal family whose closed linear span is the whole indicated subspace. These are the compact SVD conventions; in particular, this model concerns minimum-norm representatives in $N(K)^\\perp$ and does not estimate a kernel component.\n\nFor a truth $x\\in N(K)^\\perp$, let $\\theta_j=\\langle x,v_j\\rangle_X$. The complete-basis coefficient map identifies $N(K)^\\perp$ bijectively and isometrically with $\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$, so every $\\theta\\in\\ell^2(\\mathbb N,\\mathbb R)$ is a model parameter. For $\\epsilon>0$, take the functional white-noise experiment on the separable Hilbert space $H_0$ with observations $Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_Y+\\epsilon W(g)$, where $W$ is centered, has covariance $\\mathbb E[W(g)W(h)]=\\langle g,h\\rangle_Y$, and has independent standard-normal values on every finite orthonormal tuple. The countable complete basis makes $H_0$ separable. Define $\\xi_j=W(u_j)$ and $Z_j=Y_x(u_j)/s_j$. The sequence sample space is $\\mathbb R^{\\mathbb N}$ with the product Borel sigma-algebra, meaning the sigma-algebra generated by its coordinate projections. The coordinate law is the pushforward of the functional experiment under $y\\mapsto(y(u_j)/s_j)_{j\\in\\mathbb N}$; this map is measurable because each coordinate is a cylinder evaluation followed by scalar multiplication. The compact SVD expansion gives $\\langle Kx,u_j\\rangle_Y=s_j\\theta_j$, and the positive-noise white-noise coordinate theorem gives the independent standard-normal coordinates $\\xi_j$. Hence the normalized coordinates satisfy $Z_j=\\theta_j+\\epsilon s_j^{-1}\\xi_j,\\qquad j\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nFor the zero-noise boundary $\\epsilon=0$, define the sequence experiment separately by the point mass at $\\theta$, so $Z_j=\\theta_j$. This boundary is not an instance of the positive-noise functional experiment.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-ip-white-noise-spectral-sequence-representation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space with a fixed complete orthonormal basis $(u_j)$, indexed by $J=\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ for $m\\in\\mathbb N_0$ or by $J=\\mathbb N_{>0}$; the empty basis covers $H=\\{0\\}$. Let $Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_H+\\epsilon Z(g)$ be the isonormal white-noise experiment with $\\epsilon>0$, and write $\\mu_j=\\langle Kx,u_j\\rangle_H$. For every parameter $x$ and every basis index $j$, the observations $Y_x(u_j)$ are independent and have laws $N(\\mu_j,\\epsilon^2)$, where $N$ is the scalar normal law defined in the experiment.\n\nFor $g\\in H$, set $P_Ng=\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_Hu_j$, with $P_N=I$ once all vectors of a finite basis have been included. Then\n\n$$Y_x(g)=L^2(\\mathbb P)\\text{-}\\lim_{N\\to\\infty}\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_HY_x(u_j).$$\n\nThus every finite collection of observation functionals is reconstructed from the coefficient sequence in mean square. This does not assert almost-sure convergence of the full sequence of partial sums, a simultaneous path identity for all test vectors, or an $H$-valued observation.\n\nMore precisely, let $\\mathcal C_H$ be the cylinder sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^H$, let $\\mathcal C_J$ be the sigma-algebra on $\\mathbb R^J$ generated by its coordinate evaluations, and let $P_x$ be the field sampling law. Define $S:\\mathbb R^H\\to\\mathbb R^J$ by $S(y)_j=y(u_j)$ and $Q_x=S_*P_x$, where a pushforward is $S_*P_x(B)=P_x(S^{-1}(B))$. There is a measurable map $T:(\\mathbb R^J,\\mathcal C_J)\\to(\\mathbb R^H,\\mathcal C_H)$, depending only on the indexed basis, such that for every parameter $x$,\n\n$$S_*P_x=Q_x,\\qquad T_*Q_x=P_x,\\qquad S\\circ T=\\operatorname{id}_{\\mathbb R^J}.$$\n\nThese parameter-independent maps give equivalence of the field cylinder experiment and its coefficient experiment. One such map is the following. For $k\\ge1$ and $g\\in H$, let $N_k(g)$ be the least integer $N\\ge k$ with $\\|g-P_Ng\\|_H\\le2^{-k}$ and put\n\n$$a_{g,k}(z)=\\sum_{j\\in J,\\ j\\le N_k(g)}\\langle g,u_j\\rangle_Hz_j,\\qquad T(z)(g)=\\begin{cases}\\lim_{k\\to\\infty}a_{g,k}(z),&\\text{if the limit exists in }\\mathbb R,\\\\0,&\\text{otherwise}.\\end{cases}$$\n\nFor each fixed $x$ and $g$, $T(S(Y_x))(g)=Y_x(g)$ almost surely, and the same holds jointly for each fixed finite list of test vectors. The exceptional event may depend on $x$ and on that list; no intersection over all $g\\in H$ is asserted. In dimension zero the coefficient space is a singleton and $T$ returns the field with value zero at $g=0$.\n\nIf $H=L^2([0,1])$ and the basis is one of the wavelet or trigonometric bases used to define the negative Sobolev carrier $H(\\delta)$, then under countable choice and for $\\delta>1/2$ the same coordinate sequence is a Borel Gaussian random element in $H(\\delta)$ with mean coefficients $Kx$ and noise level $\\epsilon$. In infinite dimension it is not an $L^2([0,1])$-valued sample.",
