        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-ip-isonormal-white-noise-experiment",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space, where separable means having a countable dense subset, and let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a probability space. A real isonormal Gaussian process on $H$ is a family $Z(g)$ of real random variables with finite second moments, indexed by $g\\in H$, such that $g\\mapsto[Z(g)]$ is linear into $L^2(\\mathbb P)$, $\\mathbb E Z(g)=0$, and $\\mathbb E[Z(g)Z(h)]=\\langle g,h\\rangle_H$. It is also required that for every finite orthonormal tuple $(e_1,\\ldots,e_m)$ the variables $Z(e_1),\\ldots,Z(e_m)$ are independent and each has density $\\varphi(t)=(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-t^2/2}$. For any finite list of vectors, expressing them in an orthonormal basis of their finite-dimensional span shows that their process values have the corresponding linear-image Gaussian law. Here $L^2(\\mathbb P)$ identifies square-integrable random variables that agree almost surely. When $H\\ne\\{0\\}$, all unit-vector marginals have the same law; write $N(a,\\sigma^2)$ for the law of $a+\\sigma Z(e)$ for any unit vector $e\\in H$ and $\\sigma\\ge0$.\n\nLet $X$ be a set, let $\\Theta\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty parameter set, let $K:X\\to H$ be a signal map, and let $\\epsilon>0$. For $x\\in\\Theta$ and $g\\in H$, define the observation functional\n\n$$Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_H+\\epsilon Z(g).$$\n\nThe associated statistical inverse experiment has sample space $\\mathbb R^H$ with the cylinder sigma-algebra generated by the evaluation maps $y\\mapsto y(g)$, and its sampling law $P_x$ is the law of the random function $g\\mapsto Y_x(g)$. Each sample is indexed by test functionals on $H$; it is not asserted to be a vector in $H$ or to have a continuous path. If $H=\\{0\\}$, linearity forces $Z(0)=0$ and the experiment is deterministic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
