          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, with the profile notation of step 1.3, for the matching upper bounds define the product-Borel score $$F(X)=\\sum_{i\\ge1}h_ie^{-z_i^2/2}\\cosh(z_iX_i)-\\sum_{i\\ge1}h_i.$$ The first series is nonnegative and may be infinite; then the test rejects. Under the null its expectation is $\\sum h_i<\\infty$, so it is finite almost surely by [F6], and its centered partial sums converge in mean square with variance $V$. The bounded-amplitude fourth-moment calculation in step 1.2 and [F3], followed by the null variance tail bound in [F2], give $F\\Rightarrow N(0,U_0^2)$. For $n$ large put $$Q_i^2=2\\log(i+1)+4\\log\\log(i+3)+4\\log\\log n,\\qquad \\mathcal Y=\\{\\exists i:|X_i|>Q_i\\}.$$ Step 1.2 and a countable union bound give $\\mathbb P_0(\\mathcal Y)\\le2(\\log n)^{-2}\\sum_{i\\ge1}[(i+1)(\\log(i+3))^2]^{-1}=o(1)$. For every fixed $K$, $\\max_{i\\le n(\\log n)^K}Q_i=\\sqrt{2\\log n}+o(\\sqrt{\\log n})$. Reject on $\\{F>H_\\ell\\sqrt V\\}\\cup\\mathcal Y$ for the significance problem, and on $\\{F>V/2\\}\\cup\\mathcal Y$ for total error. These are unions with a centered-cosh score, rather than an intersection or a positive-exponential uncentered score.",
