          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, with the profile notation of step 1.3, under the null put $A_i=g_i\\xi_{z_i}(G_i)$ for the finite prior in step 2.2. They are centered and independent, and $\\sum\\mathbb E A_i^2\\to U_0^2$ along any subsequence with $U\\to U_0\\in(0,\\infty)$. On $z_i\\le B$, step 1.2 and the vanishing maximum coordinate variance give $\\sum\\mathbb E A_i^4\\to0$. The omitted linear sum has variance tending uniformly to zero as $B\\to\\infty$; the omitted quadratic sum has expectation tending to zero. Apply [F3] to the finite bounded-amplitude arrays and then let $B\\to\\infty$, using Chebyshev for the linear tail. The same fourth-moment bound gives concentration of their quadratic sums. Thus $$\\sum A_i\\ \\Rightarrow\\ N(0,U_0^2),\\qquad \\sum A_i^2\\to U_0^2,\\qquad \\max_i|A_i|\\to0$$ in probability for the last two limits. Each factor $1+A_i=1-g_i+g_ie^{-z_i^2/2}\\cosh(z_iG_i)$ is positive. On $\\max|A_i|\\le\\delta<1/2$, [F10] gives $|\\sum\\log(1+A_i)-\\sum A_i+\\tfrac12\\sum A_i^2|\\le C\\delta\\sum A_i^2$. Let $\\delta\\downarrow0$. The likelihood from step 1.1 therefore has $\\log L\\Rightarrow N(-U_0^2/2,U_0^2)$. The addition of terms converging in probability and the successive tail limits preserve the displayed distribution limit by squeezing distribution functions between thresholds differing by an arbitrary positive tolerance.",
