        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, with the profile notation of step 1.3, if $M$ stays bounded, fix $B>0$. For $z_i\\le B$, $h_iz_i^2\\le C_Bh_0(i/n)^{2\\beta}$ by [F2]. Writing the hyperbolic addition formula and using $|\\sinh(z\\mu)|\\le z|\\mu|e^{B|\\mu|}$ gives $$\\mathbb E\\left|h_i\\bigl(\\xi_{z_i}(G_i+\\mu_i^*)-\\mathbb E\\xi_{z_i}(G_i+\\mu_i^*)\\bigr)\\right|^4\\le C_B(h_iz_i^2)^4(1+|\\mu_i^*|^4)e^{4B|\\mu_i^*|}.$$ Here the fourth moments of $\\cosh(zG)-\\mathbb E\\cosh(zG)$ and $\\sinh(zG)$ are bounded respectively by $C_Bz^8$ and $C_Bz^4$, by the same Gaussian exponential integration as in step 1.2. Since $z_i\\le B$ implies $i\\le C_B n$, the summed fourth moments are $O_B(nh_0^4)n^{o(1)}=n^{-1+o(1)}\\to0$. The variance of the omitted $z_i>B$ centered sum is at most its null variance tail plus $o(1)$, by step 4.2 and bounded $M$. Apply [F3] to the retained finite arrays and then let $B\\to\\infty$ to obtain $F-M\\Rightarrow N(0,U_0^2)$. The assertion is uniform in the sense that every sequence of residual alternatives with bounded $M$ obeys this limit. If $M\\to\\infty$, step 4.2 and Chebyshev instead show $\\mathbb P_{\\mu^*}(F\\le t)\\to0$ for every bounded threshold. These conclusions and $M\\ge U^2-o(1)$ prove the upper type-II limits $\\Phi(H_\\ell-U_0)$ and $\\Phi(-U_0/2)$. To justify passage to the supremum, any contrary sequence has either a bounded-mean subsequence (then a convergent-mean subsequence) or a subsequence with $M\\to\\infty$; both cases give the asserted bound. Let $d\\downarrow0$ for the alternatives excluded in step 3.2. Under the null step 2.3 gives the corresponding errors $\\ell+o(1)$ and $\\Phi(-U_0/2)+o(1)$. If the first test has size $p_\\varepsilon>\\ell$, multiply its indicator by $\\ell/p_\\varepsilon$; otherwise leave it unchanged. Its size is then at most $\\ell$ and every power changes by $o(1)$. This proves the exact-level Gaussian upper bound and the total-error upper bound.",
