          "claim": "In the Gaussian branch, write $\\rho=r/r_\\varepsilon^*$, $p=B/D>0$ and $c=c_{\\alpha,\\beta,q}$. For a comparison radius $R=t r_\\varepsilon^*$, let $T^\\ell_{\\varepsilon,t}$ be the score-and-maximum significance test in steps 2.3 and 5.1, with its exact-level scaling, and let $T^{\\rm tot}_{\\varepsilon,t}$ be their unscaled total-error test. The proved estimates are uniform over each fixed $t\\in[1/k,k]$: otherwise a countersequence has a convergent subsequence of $t$ in that compact interval, and all the compact-transition estimates already proved apply with a positive finite limiting $U=ct^p$, contradicting the countersequence. Consequently, for every integer $k\\ge2$ there is a sufficiently small noise threshold such that for all smaller $\\varepsilon$ and every $t\\in[1/k,k]$, the profile dimension is $n>e$, $|u_\\varepsilon(t r_\\varepsilon^*)-ct^p|\\le1/k$, the maximal type-II error of $T^\\ell_{\\varepsilon,t}$ at radius $t r_\\varepsilon^*$ is at most $\\Phi(H_\\ell-ct^p)+1/k$, and the total error of $T^{\\rm tot}_{\\varepsilon,t}$ there is at most $2\\Phi(-ct^p/2)+1/k$. Both tests are therefore defined on this whole compact range, and the significance test has size at most $\\ell$ for every such noise level. Fix $j_1=1$ and recursively let $j_k$ be the least integer $j>j_{k-1}$, $j\\ge k$, for which all these uniform properties hold for every $0<\\varepsilon\\le2^{-j}$. These integers exist by the uniform convergence just proved. For $0<\\varepsilon\\le2^{-j_2}$ define $k_\\varepsilon=\\max\\{k\\ge2:\\varepsilon\\le2^{-j_k}\\}$. This is a nonempty finite set because $j_k\\ge k$; $k_\\varepsilon\\to\\infty$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$. The least-integer rule and this finite maximum specify one deterministic diagonal, depending only on the fixed model, significance level and the given $\\delta_\\varepsilon$ family, not on the unknown truth or the observations.",
