          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "For this single diagonal, define the Gaussian-regime test family for every known radius $r>0$ as follows. When $\\rho<1/k_\\varepsilon$, use the constant randomized significance test $\\chi^\\ell_{\\varepsilon,r}\\equiv\\ell$ and the never-reject total-error test $\\chi^{\\rm tot}_{\\varepsilon,r}\\equiv0$. When $1/k_\\varepsilon\\le\\rho\\le k_\\varepsilon$, use $T^\\ell_{\\varepsilon,\\rho}$ and $T^{\\rm tot}_{\\varepsilon,\\rho}$. When $\\rho>k_\\varepsilon$, use $T^\\ell_{\\varepsilon,k_\\varepsilon}$ and $T^{\\rm tot}_{\\varepsilon,k_\\varepsilon}$, constructed at the smaller comparison radius $k_\\varepsilon r_\\varepsilon^*$, never at the possibly invalid profile dimension associated with $r$. For $\\varepsilon>2^{-j_2}$ set the family to the same two trivial tests; this finite-noise convention does not affect an asymptotic statement. Every test is product-Borel, each significance test has size at most $\\ell$, and every formula $\\log\\log n$ actually evaluated has $n>e$. Write $k=k_\\varepsilon$. In the middle case the upper risk bounds in step 6.1 hold with error $1/k$, and $|u_\\varepsilon(r)-c\\rho^p|\\le1/k$, so the attained errors are bounded by the stated Gaussian profiles plus a quantity tending to zero. In the small-radius case, monotonicity of the relaxed extremal problem gives $0\\le u_\\varepsilon(r)\\le ck^{-p}+1/k\\to0$; the two trivial tests have respective errors $1-\\ell$ and $1$ whenever the alternative is nonempty, and no larger errors if it is empty, matching the profile upper bounds up to a vanishing quantity. In the large-radius case, the actual alternative is a subset of the comparison-radius alternative, so both achieved errors are no larger than their comparison-radius errors, bounded by $\\Phi(H_\\ell-ck^p)+1/k$ and $2\\Phi(-ck^p/2)+1/k$, respectively, and these tend to zero. Also $u_\\varepsilon(r)\\ge ck^p-1/k\\to\\infty$, as required for the displayed profiles. These bounds apply to all three cases with the same deterministic $k_\\varepsilon$; hence they also apply to ratios oscillating between them, without selecting a test by a subsequence.",
