          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For fixed $z>0$ and $v>0$, put $f(v)=(\\cosh(zv)-1-a(z)v^2)/v^q$. Differentiation and the absolutely convergent series show that $$\\frac{f'(v)}{v^{1-q}}=\\sum_{k\\ge1}\\frac{(2k-q)z^{2k}v^{2k-2}}{(2k)!}-(2-q)a(z).$$ This expression is strictly increasing in $v$: its terms with $k\\ge2$ have strictly positive coefficients and positive powers. It is zero at $v=z$ by the definition of $a$. Consequently $f$ decreases to $z$ and increases after $z$. Direct substitution gives $f(z)=-b(z)$, so $\\cosh(zv)-1\\ge a(z)v^2-b(z)v^q$. Evenness proves the assertion for negative $v$, and direct substitution proves equality at $0,z,-z$.",
