          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $q$ be the unique point of $B\\cap\\operatorname{int}D_R$, and put the current attaching circle $G$ transverse to $D_R$. Its intersection set is finite: it is a compact zero-dimensional transverse submanifold, disjoint from $\\partial D_R=R$. If it is empty there is nothing to do. Otherwise choose $p\\in G\\cap\\operatorname{int}D_R$. A sufficiently close framed parallel $B'$ of $B$ is disjoint from $B\\cup G\\cup R$ and meets $D_R$ once, at a point $q'$ close to $q$. Indeed near $q$ the transverse arc $B$ has product coordinates in which $D_R$ is $z=0$ and $B$ is a vertical interval; choose the parallel's displacement there tangent to the disk. Away from this coordinate ball the compact sets $B$ and $D_R$ are disjoint, so a sufficiently small parallel creates no other intersection. The displacement can be interpolated in the framed normal disk bundle without changing the prescribed parallel's framing.",
