          "claim": "Let $K,J$ be any disjoint oriented knot components in the links below,\nand let $D$ be a regular oriented diagram of $K\\cup J$. The inclusion\n$E_K\\hookrightarrow S^3\\setminus K$ is a homotopy equivalence by radial\nretraction in the punctured normal tube. By [F5], write\n$[J]=m[\\mu_K]$. Let $\\ell(D)$ be the half-sum of mixed crossing signs;\n[F7] makes it an integer independent of the chosen diagram. At a mixed\ncrossing where $K$ passes over $J$ with sign $\\epsilon$, switch it. The\nhalf-sum changes by $-\\epsilon$. In the crossing ball, straighten $K$ to\nthe positive $x$-axis: for $\\epsilon=+1$ the old under-arc of $J$ runs in\nthe positive $y$ direction, and the new arc minus the old arc represents\nthe negative positive-meridian class; for $\\epsilon=-1$ the class reverses.\nThus the switch changes $[J]$ by $-\\epsilon[\\mu_K]$, so it changes $m$ by\nthe same integer. After all such switches, $J$ passes over $K$ at every\nprojected intersection. Raise $J$ above $K$ and move its projection into a\ndisjoint disk. This is an isotopy in the complement of $K$; the final pair\nhas a split diagram, so its half-sum is zero, and $[J]=0$ because it lies\nin a $3$-ball disjoint from $K$. Tracking the equal changes proves\n$\\ell(D)=m$. In particular, when $J=L_f$ is a framed degree-one parallel\nof an attaching component $L$, its framing integer is this meridian\ncoefficient. A computed framing value zero then gives the preferred\nlongitude condition by [F6].",
