        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-piccirillo-conway-pair",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $C$ denote the Conway knot represented by the Conway diagram in Figure 1 of\nPiccirillo's paper, and let $K_P$ denote the knot represented by the partner\ndiagram in Figure 2 of that paper. Choose an orientation on each diagram. The\nsymbols $C$ and $K_P$ refer to these specified diagram isotopy classes.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "alg-seifert-surface-from-diagram",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $D$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram with exactly one component, and let $c$ be its number of crossings. Perform the following construction in its projection sphere $S^2$.\n\n1. Choose a point $q\\in S^2$ outside the finite diagram. In pairwise disjoint small disks about the crossings, replace each crossing by the unique noncrossing smoothing compatible with the strand orientations. Let the resulting oriented circles be the Seifert circles and let $s$ be their number.\n2. For each Seifert circle $C$, take the closure of the component of $S^2\\setminus C$ that does not contain $q$. Under the stated choice axiom, each such region is an embedded compact disk. The finite family of disk regions is nested or disjoint. Orient each disk to induce the given orientation of its boundary circle, then place nested disks at distinct nearby heights ordered by inclusion; the resulting disk interiors are pairwise disjoint in $S^3$.\n3. For every original crossing, attach one rectangular band along short boundary intervals on the two smoothing arcs. A half-twist means a single $180$-degree turn of the band across the crossing neighborhood. Choose its direction, endpoint orientations, and over/under order to match the original crossing.\n4. Return the finite disk-and-band construction data together with $(s,c)$.\n\nThe returned data are a finite attached two-dimensional complex. This algorithm's definition alone does not assert that the resulting image is embedded, is a surface, is connected, or has the original knot as boundary; the separate correctness lemma establishes those properties.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-seifert-surface-from-diagram-procedure-properties",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For every finite regular oriented diagram with exactly one component, the Seifert diagram procedure terminates after one finite smoothing scan and one finite band-record scan. It returns exactly one disk record for each Seifert circle and exactly one band record for each original crossing. Termination alone asserts no embeddedness, boundary equality, or genus minimality.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-seifert-bands-embedded-orientable-boundary-correct",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $D$ be a finite regular oriented diagram of a single knot, and let the Seifert algorithm supply its finite disk-and-band construction data. These disks and bands can be realized in $S^3$ as a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface $F$ with exactly one boundary component, whose oriented boundary is the knot represented by $D$. The bands can be chosen mutually disjoint and disjoint from disk interiors except at their attaching intervals.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-knot-seifert-pairing-and-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere, and let $F\\subset S^3$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, oriented so that its boundary orientation is the given orientation of $K$. Let $g$ be the nonnegative integer determined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g$. Choose an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ of the first singular homology group $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$, represented by integral one-cycles on $F$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be positively oriented in $S^3$ whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent basis of $F$. The positive push-off $a_i^+$ is the cycle carried to a sufficiently small positive normal level in a bicollar of $F$.\n\nFor disjoint oriented closed curves $J,L\\subset S^3$, orient a meridian $\\mu_L$ of $L$ so that a positive tangent to $L$ followed by a positive basis of its normal disk gives the ambient orientation. The linking number $\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)$ is the integer determined by $[J]=\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)[\\mu_L]$ in $H_1(S^3\\setminus L;\\mathbb Z)$, using the Alexander-duality identification that sends $[\\mu_L]$ to $1$. Extend linking additively over integral one-cycles. The Seifert pairing is $\\theta_F(x,y)=\\operatorname{lk}(x^+,y)$, and its matrix in the chosen basis is $V=(V_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{2g}$, where $V_{ij}=\\theta_F(a_i,a_j)=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The surface and basis are part of the data; this definition asserts no knot-only matrix invariant.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-seifert-pairing-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented embedded circle in the oriented standard three-sphere, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ be the ordered integral basis and positive normal push-off convention used to define the Seifert matrix $V$ above. Every class of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$ has a representative that is a finite integral sum of pairwise disjoint smooth oriented embedded circles in $F$. For such representatives $x$ and $y$, the sum of the pairwise linking numbers between the positive push-off of $x$ and $y$ depends only on $[x]$ and $[y]$, is independent of the sufficiently small positive push-off, and equals the bilinear extension of $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The linking number of two disjoint oriented circle components is symmetric. In particular, the Seifert pairing is a well-defined bilinear map $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\times H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\to\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
