          "source": "cor-knot-alexander-normalization-symmetry-and-genus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice and let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot. For a nonzero Laurent polynomial $f(t)=\\sum_j c_jt^j$, define $\\deg f=\\max\\{j:c_j\\ne0\\}-\\min\\{j:c_j\\ne0\\}$; this is its exponent width and is unchanged by multiplication by a unit $\\pm t^k$. Let $g(K)$ be the minimum genus of a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface in $S^3$ with oriented boundary $K$, and let $\\Delta_K$ be any generator of the zeroth elementary ideal of its one-variable Alexander module. Then every such generator satisfies\n\n$$\\Delta_K(1)=\\pm1,\\qquad \\Delta_K(t^{-1})\\doteq\\Delta_K(t),\\qquad \\deg\\Delta_K\\le 2g(K),$$\n\nwhere $\\doteq$ means equality up to a unit $\\pm t^k$. Consequently, there is a unique associate $\\Delta_K^\\#(t)$ with $\\Delta_K^\\#(1)=1$ and $\\Delta_K^\\#(t^{-1})=\\Delta_K^\\#(t)$; its exponent support is centered in $[-g(K),g(K)]$. Under the existing convention the knot-normalized fraction $D_K(t)=\\Delta_K(t)/(1-t)$ is distinct from the polynomial $\\Delta_K$; because $\\Delta_K(1)=\\pm1$, it has a simple pole at $t=1$.",
