      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Fix a point $x_0$ in a complementary region $R_0$. First decompose the edges of $P$ into finitely many edge-disjoint simple cycles. In a nonempty remaining even-degree graph, follow unused edges until a vertex first repeats; the segment between its two occurrences is a simple cycle, including a loop edge as a one-edge cycle. Removing that cycle subtracts two incident half-edges at each of its vertices and leaves every degree even. Repeat until no edge remains. This terminates because each removal deletes an edge; a vertex-free circle is already one cycle. Now choose two piecewise-smooth paths $\\alpha,\\beta$ from $x_0$ to the same point in a region $R$, avoiding the finitely many vertices and meeting open edges transversely in finitely many points. Their concatenation $\\gamma=\\alpha*\\beta^{-1}$ is a closed test path; it need not be embedded or be an edge-path of $P$. Its intersections are counted with traversal multiplicity, so $I(\\gamma,P)=I(\\alpha,P)+I(\\beta,P)$ modulo two. For each simple graph cycle, choose small vertex disks disjoint from the compact image of $\\gamma$ and round that cycle inside them. The rounded cycle remains embedded and has exactly the same test-path intersections as its original edges. By [F2] it separates $S^2$ into two sides. Each transverse encounter switches the side occupied by $\\gamma$, and a complete traversal returns to its initial side, even when the test path self-intersects or retraces an arc. Thus $\\gamma$ meets each rounded cycle evenly. The graph cycles partition all open edges and $\\gamma$ misses their vertices, so summing their intersection counts proves $I(\\gamma,P)=0$ modulo two. Therefore $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ have the same crossing parity. Coloring $R$ by this parity gives a well-defined function, independent also of the chosen endpoint inside $R$, since two such endpoints are joined within $R$ without crossing $P$. All decompositions, rounding choices and perturbations are finite; no choice axiom is used.",
