            "F1",
            "F8",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "If $c>0$, the connected projection has finite disk faces. To see this, thicken a spanning tree to a disk, then add the remaining edge strips one at a time. The core of each added edge lies in one complementary disk and is a properly embedded arc there. Its strip cuts that disk along the arc; after rounding corners, the two new boundary curves are smooth Jordan curves, and [F7] makes their closed sides disks. At each stage the new edge lies in one old region and splits it into exactly the two regions adjacent to the new edge, so these disk complements correspond one-to-one to the projection regions. Thus each complementary region has a disk core disjoint from the graph. Now suppose $D$ is reduced and alternating. Color its regions by [F3]. At one crossing choose the color name so the $A$-smoothing follows the two black sectors. Moving along a projection arc to the next crossing, alternation switches which branch is over; the chart definition of the $A$-smoothing therefore continues to follow the same color. Connectedness propagates this choice to every crossing. Thus the all-$A$ state circles trace the black disk cores and the all-$B$ circles trace the white disk cores. Embed the black Tait graph by placing vertices in black cores and drawing each crossing edge through its crossing disk; its complementary faces are precisely the white disk cores. Hence it is connected by [F6], has $c$ edges, $s_A$ vertices, and $s_B$ faces by [F4]. Euler's formula [F5] gives $s_A-c+s_B=2$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $c>0$. Put the all-$A$ state on the lower level $S^2\\times\\{0\\}$ and the all-$B$ state on the upper level $S^2\\times\\{1\\}$. Outside the crossing disks, join corresponding state arcs by product rectangles; inside each crossing disk, join the two local smoothings by one saddle cobordism. The resulting compact surface $\\Sigma$ has boundary the $s_A+s_B$ state circles and exactly $c$ index-one saddles, hence $\\chi(\\Sigma)=-c$. Its incidence graph has one vertex for each state circle and one edge for each saddle; connectedness of the projection makes this graph connected, because each projection arc lies on the state circles incident to its endpoint saddles. Thus $\\Sigma$ is connected. Cap each boundary circle by a disk, obtaining a connected closed surface $\\widehat\\Sigma$ with $\\chi(\\widehat\\Sigma)=s_A+s_B-c$. Give it a finite cellulation and take cellular chains $C_i$ over $\\mathbb F_2$, with boundary maps $\\partial_i$. If $r_i=\\operatorname{rank}\\partial_i$ and $h_i=\\dim H_i$, then rank-nullity gives $\\dim C_i=h_i+r_i+r_{i+1}$; alternating the sum over $i=0,1,2$ cancels the $r_i$ terms and yields $\\chi(\\widehat\\Sigma)=h_0-h_1+h_2$. Connectedness gives $h_0=1$. A 2-cycle has equal coefficients on the two faces adjacent to every edge; the dual graph of a connected closed surface is connected, so all face coefficients agree and $h_2=1$. Therefore $\\chi(\\widehat\\Sigma)=2-h_1\\le2$, and $s_A+s_B-c\\le2$. For $c=0$, a connected projection is one circle and $s_A=s_B=1$, giving the same inequality. Combining this bound with step 1.1 proves $\\operatorname{span}_A\\langle D\\rangle\\le4c$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
