        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "At any crossing, reducedness in [F2] says the two black sectors belong to distinct black regions and the two white sectors belong to distinct white regions. The all-$A$ smoothing circles trace the black regions, so changing one crossing from all-$A$ joins two distinct state circles by a band and merges them; the all-$B$ change similarly merges two distinct white state circles. If a state changes $j\\ge1$ crossings from all-$A$, the first change lowers the circle count to $s_A-1$, and each later change can increase it by at most one; hence its circle count is at most $s_A+j-2$. Its term's degree is therefore at most $c-2j+2(s_A+j-3)=c+2(s_A-3)$, four below the all-$A$ term's top degree $c+2(s_A-1)$. The all-$A$ term has top coefficient $(-1)^{s_A-1}\\ne0$, so it cannot cancel. The same argument at all-$B$ proves a nonzero bottom coefficient. When $c=0$, the single state has bracket $1$, and the same conclusions hold vacuously.",
