          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-alternating-checkerboard-normal-pairing-is-definite",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $D$ be a connected alternating regular link diagram on an oriented projection sphere. Choose either checkerboard color. Put a disk in each region of that color and join the opposite colored sectors at every crossing by a half-twisted band, with the twist chosen so that the boundary follows the over-under diagram. After rounding corners these disks and bands form a compact connected embedded spanning surface $F$ for the represented link. If $D$ has crossings, let $G$ be the corresponding Tait graph and orient its edges arbitrarily. Under the retraction of $F$ onto $G$, write a rational homology class as the unique graph cycle $a=\\sum_{e\\in E(G)}a_e e$. There is a single sign $\\epsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$ such that\n$$q(a)=\\epsilon\\sum_{e\\in E(G)}a_e^2=\\operatorname{lk}(c,\\tau c)$$\nfor every oriented rational multicurve $c$ representing $a$, where $\\tau c$ is its normal transfer. Thus $q$ is positive definite or negative definite on $H_1(F;\\mathbb Q)$. For a crossingless connected diagram, use either spanning disk and the zero-dimensional definite form.",
