      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose first that there is at least one crossing. Build a small regular neighborhood of the projection graph from disjoint crossing disks and narrow edge strips. To see that its complementary faces are disks, choose a finite spanning tree of the graph; its neighborhood is a disk, by adding each tree strip between two distinct disk components. Add the remaining edge strips one at a time. Each edge core is a properly embedded arc in one complementary disk; its strip cuts that disk along the arc. After rounding corners the two new boundary curves are smooth Jordan curves, and [F4] makes both closed sides disks. At each step the added edge lies in one old face and splits it into the two new faces, so these complementary disks correspond one-to-one to the regions of the projection. Their closures are disjoint from the graph and serve as disk cores. Put a disk in each core of the chosen color. At every crossing join the two appropriate disk cores by a narrow band with one half-twist; choose its direction so that its boundary strands have the diagram's prescribed overpass and underpass. The neighborhoods are disjoint, so the resulting surface is embedded. Its boundary is the usual height realization of $D$. Collapsing each disk to a vertex and each band to its core retracts $F$ onto the Tait graph $G$. This graph is connected by [F2], so $F$ is connected and has a supplied finite disk-and-band presentation.",
