          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-finite-good-covers-of-compact-smooth-boundary-manifolds-compute-integral-cohomology",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. A compact smooth manifold $M$, possibly with boundary, has a finite relatively open good cover refining any specified open cover. Its nonempty finite intersections are contractible, with contractions depending smoothly on the endpoint and contraction parameter. The cover can be chosen with a smooth subordinate partition of unity whose supports lie in their assigned cover members. For its finite nerve $N$, there is a homotopy equivalence $M\\to |N|$, and simplicial nerve cohomology identifies with singular cohomology of $M$ with coefficients in any abelian group. These identifications commute with coefficient homomorphisms, in particular reduction $\\mathbb Z\\to\\mathbb F_2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-integral-two-classes-in-four-manifolds-have-surface-representatives-and-characteristic-parity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $B$ be a compact oriented smooth four-manifold, possibly with boundary. Every $x\\in H_2(B;\\mathbb Z)$ is represented by a closed oriented smooth embedded surface $i:F\\hookrightarrow\\operatorname{Int}B$, with disconnected or empty $F$ allowed. For its oriented normal plane bundle $\\nu_F$,\n$$\\langle w_2(TB),\\rho_2x\\rangle=\\langle e(\\nu_F),[F]\\rangle\\pmod2.$$\nIf $x$ is torsion, this integer normal Euler number is zero. In particular $w_2(TB)$ evaluates to zero on the reductions of all integral torsion degree-two classes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-cohomology-over-a-pid",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $R$ be a PID, $C$ a chain complex of free\n$R$-modules, and $G$ an $R$-module. Then naturally\n$$0\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^1_R(H_{n-1}C,G)\\to H^n\\operatorname{Hom}_R(C,G)\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{ev}_n}\\operatorname{Hom}_R(H_nC,G)\\to0$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-spin-c-lifts-exist-iff-w2-has-an-integral-lift",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $M$ be a compact oriented smooth four-manifold, possibly with boundary, with a Riemannian metric. Its oriented tangent frame bundle admits a Spin$^c(4)$ lift if and only if $w_2(TM)$ is the reduction of some class in $H^2(M;\\mathbb Z)$. The criterion includes torsion classes. It asserts existence, not a bijection between Spin$^c$ structures and integral lifts of $w_2$.",
          "uses": [
