          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-given-spin-and-spinc-structures-and-associated-spinor-bundles",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let V→X be an oriented Euclidean real vector bundle of rank n, with oriented frame bundle P_SO(V). Use Spin(n)=Spin_N=1(ℝⁿ,−‖·‖²), the norm-one even subgroup of the negative-square Clifford algebra, and its standard twisted-Clifford homomorphism λ_n:Spin(n)→SO(n). A Spin structure on V is a principal Spin(n)-bundle P with a Spin(n)-equivariant two-fold map $\\widetilde\\lambda:P\\to P_{SO}(V)$ covering the identity on X and satisfying $\\widetilde\\lambda(pg)=\\widetilde\\lambda(p)\\lambda_n(g)$. Put $\\operatorname{Spin}^c(n)=(\\operatorname{Spin}(n)\\times U(1))/\\langle(-1,-1)\\rangle$ and define $\\lambda_n^c:\\operatorname{Spin}^c(n)\\to SO(n)$ by $\\lambda_n^c([s,z])=\\lambda_n(s)$. A Spin$^c$ structure is a principal Spin$^c(n)$-bundle $P_c$ with a $\\operatorname{Spin}^c(n)$-equivariant map $\\widetilde\\lambda_c:P_c\\to P_{SO}(V)$ over the identity satisfying $\\widetilde\\lambda_c(p g)=\\widetilde\\lambda_c(p)\\lambda_n^c(g)$. A spinor bundle is the associated bundle for the specified real Clifford representation; a Spin$^c$ spinor bundle is $P_c\\times_{\\tau^c}S$, where the specified complex representation $\\tau$ of $\\operatorname{Spin}(n)$ and integer $U(1)$ weight $k$ satisfy $\\tau(-1)(-1)^k=I_S$, and $\\tau^c([s,z])=z^k\\tau(s)$. This condition is exactly that $(-1,-1)$ act trivially, so the representation descends to the quotient. For the complex Clifford spinor representation, $\\tau(-1)=-I_S$ and the standard weight is $k=1$. These are given structures; this definition asserts no existence criterion.",
          "uses": [
