          "claim": "Establish removal for the possibly nontrivial limiting bundle near a point of $S$. The needed small punctured curvature gauge has an intrinsic construction. On a dyadic coordinate annulus, choose a frame at one basepoint and transport it along the two spherical path families of [F3]'s annular proof. Transport is defined in finitely many bundle charts along each path, and its variation identity is intrinsic: the covariant derivative of the endpoint frame is the integral of transported curvature over the path variation. The two caps' transports differ by the holonomy of the same explicit spherical lune of area at most $2\\pi$. Thus their quaternionic average supplies a global annular frame with potential at most $C r\\|F\\|_\\infty$, without assuming that the punctured bundle already has a global trivialization. On a sufficiently small punctured ball, finite energy and [F2] give, for $r_j=2^{-j}$ and $E_j$ the energy on $\\{r_j/4<|x|<4r_j\\}$, the bounds $\\|F\\|_{L^\\infty(\\{r_j/2<|x|<2r_j\\})}\\le C r_j^{-2}\\sqrt{E_j}$ and $\\sum_jE_j\\le5E$. These are applications on ordinary balls away from the puncture; all rescaled metric bounds are uniform. Make the total energy small enough for the annular threshold. Align neighboring frames by their relative value at one fixed point of each overlap, as in [F3]. The relative frame then differs from identity by at most $C(\\sqrt{E_j}+\\sqrt{E_{j+1}})$ on that overlap, by integrating its gauge equation along bounded-length angular/radial paths. Average these aligned frames with a dyadic partition having only adjacent overlaps and normalize their quaternionic norm. This gives ONE smooth frame on the whole punctured smaller ball. Its potential $B$ obeys $|B|\\le C r_j^{-1}(\\sqrt{E_j}+\\sqrt{E_{j+1}})$ on the $j$th band, hence $\\int|B|^4\\le C\\sum_j(E_j^2+E_{j+1}^2)\\le CE^2$. This reconstructs the punctured $L^4$ argument for the curved metric; no Euclidean-ASD theorem is applied to $A_\\infty$.",
