ng the two background connections. Weak energy measures have unique limits against the continuous test functions, so their atomic weights and positions agree; the smooth background measure has no atoms. This gauge extends over every exceptional point: in their two smooth extension frames its derivative is bounded by the same equation, and the length distance on a punctured four-ball has the Euclidean completion, so it has a unique continuous limit at the puncture. The bounded derivative gives a weak Lipschitz extension, and the equation then bootstraps to smoothness. The ideal limits are therefore the same. With the countable neighborhood bases just constructed, failure of Hausdorff separation would select a point in every intersection of two shrinking neighborhood bases, giving a sequence with those two different limits. Thus the topology is Hausdorff. Finally second-countability gives a countable subcover of every open cover under AC. If no finite subcover existed, choose a point outside its first $n$ members; a convergent subsequence would contradict the member containing its limit. The sequential compactness in step10.1 therefore gives compactness of the ideal space and of the closed original-moduli closure. This is precisely the compactification by lower-charge smooth limits and integer point labels in the Statement.",
