    "lem-asd-regularity-perturbation-and-orientability": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "lemma",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-asd-moduli-fredholm-index-and-lattice-setup",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a closed oriented Riemannian four-manifold and let $P\\to X$ be a smooth principal $SU(2)$-bundle with $c_2(P)[X]=-1$. The Hodge star on two-forms is $*$, and $F_A^-=(F_A-*F_A)/2$ denotes the anti-self-dual projection of the curvature of a connection $A$. The equation used in this item is $F_A^-=0$, so the curvature is self-dual; this convention and the negative second Chern number are fixed together for the positive-definite orientation. The self-dual moduli set is the set of smooth connections satisfying $F_A^-=0$, modulo the action of smooth gauge transformations of $P$. At a solution, the deformation complex is $0\\to\\Omega^0(X;\\operatorname{ad}P)\\xrightarrow{d_A}\\Omega^1(X;\\operatorname{ad}P)\\xrightarrow{d_A^-}\\Omega^-(X;\\operatorname{ad}P)\\to0$, and its gauge-fixed operator is $D_A=d_A^*\\oplus d_A^-$. For index calculations, $D_A$ is completed from the first Sobolev space of one-forms to the direct sum of the zeroth Sobolev spaces of zero-forms and anti-self-dual two-forms.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-asd-deformation-complex-index-formula",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $X$ be a closed connected oriented smooth Riemannian four-manifold, let $b_1(X)=\\dim H^1(X;\\mathbb R)$, let $b_-(X)$ be the dimension of the negative subspace of its real intersection form, and let $P\\to X$ be a smooth principal $SU(2)$-bundle. Put $k=-\\langle c_2(P),[X]\\rangle$. For the real Fredholm operator $D_A=d_A^*\\oplus d_A^-$ from first Sobolev one-forms with values in $\\operatorname{ad}P$ to zeroth Sobolev zero-forms and anti-self-dual two-forms, its Fredholm index is $8k-3(1-b_1(X)+b_-(X))$. In particular, if $\\pi_1(X)=0$, the intersection form is positive definite, and $c_2(P)[X]=-1$, then $\\operatorname{ind}_{\\mathbb R}(D_A)=5$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-high-sobolev-coulomb-slices-for-irreducible-su-two-connections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $P$ be a smooth principal $SU(2)$-bundle over a connected closed Riemannian four-manifold $X$. Fix a smooth reference connection and use the corresponding finite-chart Sobolev norms. Let $\\mathcal A^3$ be its affine $H^3$ space of connections, and let $\\mathcal G^4$ be the group of $H^4$ sections of the bundle with fiber $SU(2)$ and conjugation transition maps. A connection is called irreducible when its stabilizer in this group is precisely the two constant central transformations. The irreducible locus is open. Its quotient $\\mathcal B^*=\\mathcal A^{3,*}/(\\mathcal G^4/\\{\\pm1\\})$ is a Hausdorff, second-countable smooth Hilbert manifold. At each irreducible $A$, a neighborhood is represented uniquely by a sufficiently small ball in $\\ker(d_A^*:H^3(\\Lambda^1\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)\\to H^2(\\operatorname{ad}P))$.\n\nThe corresponding quotient of $H^2(\\Lambda^-\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)$ is a smooth Hilbert bundle over $\\mathcal B^*$, with smooth curvature section $[A]\\mapsto F_A^-$. Its slice linearization is $a\\mapsto d_A^-a$. Fixing a point of $P$ above $x_0\\in X$, quotienting instead by transformations with value $1$ there gives a principal $SO(3)$-bundle over $\\mathcal B^*$. In local product coordinates the curvature section is its pullback. In particular its linearization has three additional kernel directions and the same cokernel; framing must not be identified with the unbased deformation problem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-bounded-smoothing-perturbations-give-open-dense-asd-transversality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Use the unbased $H^3/H^4$ irreducible connection quotient on a closed Riemannian four-manifold and its $H^2$ anti-self-dual curvature bundle. Let $U$ be an open subset covered by finitely many Coulomb slices centered at smooth irreducible connections $A_i$. In each of these charts use a bounded open ball of radius $R_i$ in $S_i=\\ker d_{A_i}^*\\subset H^3$. Let $V$ be open, with its closure contained in the union of the images of smaller closed balls of radii $r_i<R_i$, all these images lying in $U$.\n\nAllow smooth sections $\\sigma$ supported in $U$, whose representatives in the specified charts are smooth maps into $H^3$ and have finite uniform $H^3$ norm on these chart balls. Interpret them in the $H^2$ equation bundle by inclusion. Give this class the topology generated by the uniform $H^3$ section norm on the finitely many chart balls and the $C^1(H^3,H^2)$ seminorms on compact subsets of those balls. Then the perturbations for which $\\Phi+\\sigma$, $\\Phi([A])=F_A^-$, is transverse to zero at every zero in $\\overline V$ form an open dense subset. The zero set in $V$ is a smooth finite-dimensional manifold of dimension equal to the unbased ASD index. In particular that dimension is five in the simply connected positive-definite charge $c_2=-1$ case.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-su-two-asd-determinant-lines-are-gauge-equivariantly-orientable",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $X$ be a closed simply connected oriented Riemannian smooth four-manifold and $E$ a Hermitian rank-two bundle with fixed unit determinant. Over its affine $H^3$ space of determinant-one connections, consider the real Fredholm family\n$$D_A=d_A^*\\oplus d_A^-:H^3(\\Lambda^1\\otimes\\mathfrak{su}(E))\\longrightarrow H^2((\\Lambda^0\\oplus\\Lambda^-)\\otimes\\mathfrak{su}(E)).$$\nIts determinant line admits an orientation preserved by every $H^4$ determinant-one gauge transformation. It consequently descends to an orientable line bundle on the unbased irreducible quotient. The same assertion holds after any continuous gauge-equivariant family of compact perturbations of these operators. At the regular zero set of a smooth curvature perturbation with compact slice derivative, this orientation induces an orientation of the actual finite-dimensional moduli manifold. The orientation is not asserted to be canonical without additional choices.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F3], the quotient and equation bundle are smooth in the stated $H^3/H^4$ spaces. In a slice centered at $A$ the equation is $F_A^-+d_A^-a+(a\\wedge a)^-$. The derivative of each allowed perturbation factors through the compact inclusion $H^3\\hookrightarrow H^2$, and [F4] proves Fredholmness of the slice derivative with the same index as [F1]. Formula [F2] gives that index as five.",
