          "claim": "**Small error for the given curved metric.** The Hodge projection differs from its Euclidean value by $O(r^2)$ in the inner ball. Thus $E_\\lambda=F_{A_\\lambda}^-$ satisfies $|E_\\lambda|\\le C\\lambda^2r^2/(\\lambda^2+r^2)^2\\le C\\lambda^2/(\\lambda^2+r^2)$ there, and $|E_\\lambda|\\le C_\\rho\\lambda^2$ on the fixed cutoff annulus. Direct radial integration gives $\\|E_\\lambda\\|_2\\le C\\lambda^2\\sqrt{1+|\\log\\lambda|}$ and $\\|F_{A_\\lambda}\\|_2\\le\\Lambda$ uniformly. For the Green norm use [F2]'s kernel bound. If the pole has $|x|\\le2\\lambda$, split at radius $4\\lambda$; the inner contribution is $O(\\lambda^2)$ and the outer shells contribute $C\\lambda^2\\int_\\lambda^{2\\rho}dr/r$. If $s=|x|>2\\lambda$, split into $|x-y|<s/2$, $|y|<2s$ outside that set, and $|y|\\ge2s$. The first integral is at most $C(\\lambda^2/s^2)s^2=C\\lambda^2$, the second at most $Cs^{-2}\\lambda^2\\int_0^{2s}r^3/(\\lambda^2+r^2)dr\\le C\\lambda^2$, and the third is bounded by the same logarithmic shell integral. A pole outside the coordinate region only improves the bound, and the fixed annulus contributes $O(\\lambda^2)$. The non-singular kernel term is smaller; metric volumes are uniformly comparable. Consequently $\\|E_\\lambda\\|_\\sharp\\le C\\lambda^2(1+|\\log\\lambda|)$ and $\\|E_\\lambda\\|_B\\to0$ in the actual fixed metric.",
