          "source": "lem-high-sobolev-coulomb-slices-for-irreducible-su-two-connections",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $P$ be a smooth principal $SU(2)$-bundle over a connected closed Riemannian four-manifold $X$. Fix a smooth reference connection and use the corresponding finite-chart Sobolev norms. Let $\\mathcal A^3$ be its affine $H^3$ space of connections, and let $\\mathcal G^4$ be the group of $H^4$ sections of the bundle with fiber $SU(2)$ and conjugation transition maps. A connection is called irreducible when its stabilizer in this group is precisely the two constant central transformations. The irreducible locus is open. Its quotient $\\mathcal B^*=\\mathcal A^{3,*}/(\\mathcal G^4/\\{\\pm1\\})$ is a Hausdorff, second-countable smooth Hilbert manifold. At each irreducible $A$, a neighborhood is represented uniquely by a sufficiently small ball in $\\ker(d_A^*:H^3(\\Lambda^1\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)\\to H^2(\\operatorname{ad}P))$.\n\nThe corresponding quotient of $H^2(\\Lambda^-\\otimes\\operatorname{ad}P)$ is a smooth Hilbert bundle over $\\mathcal B^*$, with smooth curvature section $[A]\\mapsto F_A^-$. Its slice linearization is $a\\mapsto d_A^-a$. Fixing a point of $P$ above $x_0\\in X$, quotienting instead by transformations with value $1$ there gives a principal $SO(3)$-bundle over $\\mathcal B^*$. In local product coordinates the curvature section is its pullback. In particular its linearization has three additional kernel directions and the same cokernel; framing must not be identified with the unbased deformation problem.",
          "uses": [
