          "claim": "Suppose a rectangle $r$ is contained in the chosen fundamental square. Write its southwest and northeast corners as $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbf x$ and its northwest and southeast corners as $y_1,y_2\\in\\mathbf y$; all other state points are common. In $J(\\mathbf x,\\mathbf x)-J(\\mathbf y,\\mathbf y)$ the common-point terms cancel, the ordered corner pair contributes $1$, and each point of $\\mathbf x$ strictly inside $r$ contributes twice, once against each changed corner. Thus this difference is $1+2\\#(\\mathbf x\\cap\\operatorname{Int}r)$. In $2J(\\mathbf x,\\mathbb O)-2J(\\mathbf y,\\mathbb O)$ the same cancellation leaves twice the number of $O$-markings in $r$. Substituting these two counts into [F1] gives $M_{\\mathbb O}(\\mathbf x)-M_{\\mathbb O}(\\mathbf y)=1-2n_{\\mathbb O}(r)+2\\#(\\mathbf x\\cap\\operatorname{Int}r)$. Replacing every $O$ by $X$ gives the stated formula for $M_{\\mathbb X}$.",
