          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-grid-bridge-like-refinement",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a finite nonempty planar marked grid with vertical-over crossing convention. A horizontal segment is a cap if its two incident vertical segments both extend above it or both extend below it; otherwise it is monotone. A marked stabilization splits a marked cell into a two-by-two block with the chosen marking type twice at opposite corners, the other type once and one empty corner, preserving one of each type per row and column. A legal column commutation exchanges adjacent columns with disjoint or strictly nested closed vertical marking intervals. There is a finite sequence of marked stabilizations and legal commutations carrying $G$ to a grid in which every cap has no crossing and every monotone horizontal segment has exactly one crossing. Thus the resulting grid is bridge-like: slightly tilt its crossing-containing horizontal segments in the direction of height travel and round its empty caps to obtain a bridge diagram. Every operation respects the oriented $O,X$ convention of the grid-diagram definition, and the number of link components is unchanged. The decorated planar diagram before and after the entire refinement is planar isotopic after rounding corners; in particular no Reidemeister move is needed for this refinement. No global grid-move generation theorem is assumed.",
