          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "A cyclic permutation is a change of fundamental cut on the same grid torus. It identifies states, rectangles, and marking monomials bijectively. The finite pair-count gradings also agree under this change. To check this directly for a column cut, use the signed set $P=\\mathbf x-\\mathbb O$ in $M_O=I(P,P)+1$. The wrapping column contains one state point of sign $+1$ and one $O$ of sign $-1$. Only their comparisons with nonwrapping points change. At an integer state height the signed number of all points below is zero, and at the height of a marking in row $r$ it is one, since the states have heights $0,\\ldots,n-1$ and the markings have one height in each open row. Thus the signed difference of the below/above counts for either wrapping point, excluding itself, is one. Multiplying by the two signs and adding gives zero; the comparisons between the two wrapping points cancel from this sum and do not change. Hence $I(P,P)$ is unchanged. Apply the same count to $\\mathbf x-\\mathbb X$, and interchange coordinates for a row cut. Both Maslov functions and the Alexander formula are unchanged. The torus-identification map is therefore a filtered chain isomorphism, with inverse the opposite cut change and zero inverse homotopies.",
