        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-full-marked-index-two-compactification-gives-the-boundary-disk-curvature-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the actual balanced $S^3$ knot data with $k\\ge1$ and THE chosen surface complex structure and genuine constant-collar path constructed in the corresponding multipointed or singly pointed full-marked low-index count theorem, whose construction excludes every nonconstant strip of index at most zero as well as giving full-marked index-one and index-two regularity and finite counts. For $k=1$ this is the sphere-avoiding path from the singly pointed theorem. Label the individual cut components so $A_i$ contains $w_i,z_i$ and $B_i$ contains $w_i,z_{i+1}$, with cyclic indices.\n\nFor fixed generators $x,y$ and nonnegative integer vectors $W,Z$, let $\\widehat{\\mathcal M}_2(x,y;W,Z)$ consist of ALL actual index-two strips with these ends and marked-degree vectors, modulo longitudinal translation, across all realized Whitney classes. This is a Hausdorff second-countable topological one-manifold. It has a compactification as a compact one-manifold with finite boundary, whose boundary points are exactly:\n\n1. Ordered pairs of actual rigid index-one strip orbits with matched intermediate generator and marked vectors summing to $W,Z$.\n2. When $x=y$, the actual unit $A_i$ or $B_i$ boundary-disk orbits with boundary node value $x$ and the prescribed marked vector, attached to the constant generator strip.\n\nEach point has the actual exhaustive one-sided gluing collar of its respective type. No extra boundary points occur, and no differentiability at a prescribed compactifying parameter is asserted.\n\nLet $a_i(x),b_i(x)\\in\\mathbb F_2$ be the actual finite node-fixed affine-orbit counts of the $A_i,B_i$ disks at $x$. On the free module generated by the generators over $R=\\mathbb F_2[U_1,\\ldots,U_k,V_1,\\ldots,V_k]$, define $D$ by the actual finite rigid-strip counts with weight $U^{n_w}V^{n_z}$. Then\n$$D^2x=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^k a_i(x)U_iV_i+\\sum_{i=1}^k b_i(x)U_iV_{i+1}\\right)x.$$\nSetting every $V_i=1$ gives the actual full filtered minus count operator $d$ and\n$$d^2x=\\left(\\sum_{i=1}^k(a_i(x)+b_i(x))U_i\\right)x.$$\nFor $k=1$ the actual singly pointed unit-disk theorem supplies $a_1(x)=b_1(x)=0$, and hence $D^2=d^2=0$. For $k\\ge2$ the counts $a_i,b_i$ are not evaluated here, so square-zero in that case and unit disk count one are not concluded. Invariance and absolute normalization are not asserted. The full marked compactification and curvature identity retain the actual remaining multipointed boundary counts explicitly.",
