          "source": "thm-regular-boundary-disk-attachment-has-an-exhaustive-nodal-half-collar",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Use the actual regular boundary-disk attachment data and the family $g_\\epsilon$ with its fixed phases in the matching theorem. Consider the specified nodal configuration consisting of the constant strip $x$ and this one nonconstant disk $v$ at its lower boundary, with node value $x$ and no other bubble or strip component.\n\nAfter applying the longitudinal normalization used in the convergence, every bounded-energy sequence of actual $J_t$ strips $u_n:x\\to x$ converging to this configuration in the actual smooth component-core, complete energy and collapsed neck/tail sense of bubbled-strip compactness is eventually\n$$u_n(s,t)=g_{\\epsilon_n}(s-b_n,t),\\qquad \\epsilon_n\\downarrow0$$\nafter a subsequence, with $b_n$ tending to its attaching position in the chosen longitudinal normalization. If only convergence, rather than monotone scales, is desired, the conclusion is $\\epsilon_n\\to0$ for the whole sufficiently late sequence with its chosen component normalization.\n\nAfter decreasing $\\epsilon_0>0$, two members with $0<\\epsilon,\\epsilon'<\\epsilon_0$ are longitudinal translates only if $\\epsilon=\\epsilon'$ and that translate is the same center. The actual translation quotient therefore has a local nodal half-collar parametrized by $0\\le\\epsilon<\\epsilon_0$, with zero the specified nodal disk orbit. Here the local topology is generated by the countable actual component-core, energy and neck/tail image tests described in the proof, and the ordinary actual solution topology at positive parameters. Thus this is a collar of actual solutions, with exactly one interval germ at this regular disk stratum.\n\nThe theorem presupposes an actual regular node-fixed index-two disk, not that all degenerations have that form. It supplies no low-index classification of other sphere/disk strata, no count for internal-pair components, and no target connected-sum or polygon gluing theorem.",
