          "claim": "Use the standard genus-one splitting of the oriented three-sphere: the horizontal circles bound parallel meridian disks in one solid torus, and the vertical circles bound parallel meridian disks in the other; their primitive meridians meet once. Taking all n parallel disks cuts each side into n balls. The row and column marking condition puts one O and one X in every corresponding cut ball. Push the horizontal O-to-X segments into the lower side and the vertical X-to-O segments into the upper side, using their evident rectangular bridge disks. These simultaneous arcs give exactly the oriented knot of the grid, since every vertical segment passes over every horizontal segment. Thus this is an actual balanced 2n-pointed diagram of K, not only an abstract torus count. Its analytic symmetric rank is $1+n-1=n$, and w=O,z=X. Give it the actual flat integrable data of [F1]. The identity on states identifies its full polynomial count matrix with the algorithmic one.",
