          "claim": "Check the remaining good-endpoint condition before applying [F3]. Positivity gives a nonnegative domain D for every actual integrable strip. Its index $n_x(D)+n_y(D)$ is nonnegative. If it is zero, every cell touching either state has coefficient zero. The local equation $\\Delta D=1_x-1_y$ then forces x=y, since at any changed vertex the four coefficients would have nonzero alternating sum. Consequently $\\Delta D=0$, and telescoping its row and column differences gives $D_{ij}=a_i+b_j$. Because x is one point in every row and column, the n cells immediately northeast of its state points have total coefficient $\\sum_i a_i+\\sum_j b_j=0$. The sum of ALL cell coefficients is n times that sum, hence zero. Nonnegativity gives D=0. The actual ordered-root energy bound is then zero, so the strip is constant. Therefore no nonconstant index-at-most-zero strip exists. Finiteness, index-one regularity and square zero were already supplied by [F1,F5]; no index-two regularity of this special integrable path is assumed. The precise endpoint hypotheses of [F3] hold.",
