          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(\\Sigma,\\alpha,\\beta,z)$ be a complete globally weakly admissible pointed Heegaard diagram for any closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold, and let a specified beta handleslide band avoid z and the remaining beta curves. There is a representative gamma of this actual slide, followed by pointed attaching-curve isotopies, and there are genuine allowed chosen hat data on both diagrams, such that the actual triangle map\n$$F(x)=m_2(x,\\Theta_{\\beta\\gamma})$$\nis a chain homotopy equivalence. Its count is the finite actual zero-basepoint index-zero triangle count, with no triangle translation quotient. The proof gives an explicit finite inverse and inverse chain homotopies by actual quadrilateral counts. The diagram and the whole required finite multidiagram are globally weakly admissible; no rational-homology-sphere or torsion restriction is imposed.\n\nThe analogous alpha move follows by conjugating the source and surface complex structures and interchanging the two attaching families. Auxiliary chosen-data comparisons supplied by the named continuation theorem may be composed before and after these maps wherever its stated endpoint hypotheses apply. This theorem proves the globally weak hat handleslide operation. It does not replace an ordinary sector-admissibility hypothesis by global weakness, assert positive-basepoint minus invariance, or infer absolute grading or naturality.",
