          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-heegaard-polygon-spin-c-difference-is-the-capped-periodic-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $\\Sigma$ be an oriented genus-$h$ surface, let $k\\ge1$ and $m=h+k-1$, and give $q\\ge3$ balanced attaching systems $\\boldsymbol\\eta^0,\\ldots,\\boldsymbol\\eta^{q-1}$ of $m$ circles. Supply their disjoint compression disks in oriented handlebodies $U_i$, their compatible Morse functions with $k$ index-zero and $m$ index-one critical points, and $k$ distinct points $\\mathbf w$ with one in each cut component of every system. Distinct systems meet transversely and finitely. Use the usual product tori in $\\operatorname{Sym}^m\\Sigma$. Let $W$ be the oriented polygon cobordism obtained from the polygon times $\\Sigma$ by attaching $e_i\\times U_i$ along $e_i\\times\\Sigma$, with rounded corners. A realized Whitney polygon is a continuous disk map with these successive torus edges and specified corner generators; no holomorphic representative is assumed.\n\nThe finite Morse construction below assigns such a class $u$ a Spin-c structure $\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbf w}(u)$ on $W$, restricting to the specified Morse labels of its corners. If $u,v$ have the same corners, their domain difference $P=D(u)-D(v)$ has boundary an integral sum of full attaching circles. Capping those circles with the supplied disks along polygon legs gives a class $H(P)\\in H_2(W;\\mathbb Z)$. With the common source/surface orientations, their Spin-c difference is\n$$\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbf w}(u)-\\mathfrak s_{\\mathbf w}(v)=j\\operatorname{PD}_W H(P),$$\nwhere $j:H^2(W,\\partial W;\\mathbb Z)\\to H^2(W;\\mathbb Z)$. The sign is fixed by the positive index-one/index-two gradient modification having determinant degree $+2$ on a positive transverse disk.\n\nIn particular, if the two Spin-c structures and every $w$-multiplicity agree, then $P$ is a sum of actual doubly-periodic domains for the adjacent Heegaard pairs, each with all $w$-multiplicities zero. For a triangle these are the $\\alpha\\beta$, $\\beta\\gamma$ and $\\alpha\\gamma$ pairs. Their capped classes need not be zero individually. Fixed all $w,z$ multiplicities imply this hypothesis without any extra normalization convention.\n\nThe construction is compatible with gluing polygons along a common Heegaard boundary. Compatible Spin-c structures on the pieces have the usual extension orbit under the image of $H^1$ of that common boundary in $H^2$ of the glued cobordism; no unique extension is asserted. Along an actual continuous class-preserving family and its actual neck-smoothing, the assigned total Spin-c structure is constant, and the glued pieces recover that total extension, not an arbitrarily chosen member of the orbit. Point-divisor multiplicity vectors at every supplied $w,z$ mark add under these actual gluings. These assertions do not supply an analytic compactification or a gluing existence theorem. For supplied small attaching-curve isotopies away from the marks, transport the compatible Morse data in the actual handlebody collars and supply a continuous transport of the polygon maps. On the unchanged cobordism this carries their Spin-c labels and capped periodic classes, including torsion information. If one witness point in every old region misses the swept curves, all coefficients at those witnesses are unchanged. Hence strict strong admissibility, and the corresponding sector or global weak witness conditions, pass to the transported lattice. No existence of the continuous polygon-map transport is inferred from the mere isotopy of its boundary curves.\n\nThe statement does not identify all literal Whitney classes with their domains, divide genus-two classes by a sphere action, assert finiteness of classes with one domain, or supply any analytic count or Maslov-index formula. The point target $m=0$ has only the constant map and the domain conclusion is zero.",
          "uses": [
