        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-globally-weak-multidiagrams-admit-coherent-all-index-regular-polygon-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Fix a finite ordered list of distinct attaching systems with pairwise transverse product tori in $X=\\operatorname{Sym}^m\\Sigma$, $m\\ge1$, and no triple intersection. Suppose every nonzero full-curve multi-periodic domain vanishing at the nonempty finite basepoint set $Z$ has both signs. Suppose also that each component of the complement of any one attaching system contains a basepoint. Fix a finite maximum arity $N$. Common compatible protected geometry with already supplied exact product-angle generator charts may be retained; their planes are $\\mathbb R^m$ and $\\prod_j e^{i\\theta_j}\\mathbb R$ for arbitrary fixed $0<\\theta_j<\\pi$. Then there is a choice of common nearly symmetric target geometry and coherent source-dependent polygon structures for every ordered sub-list with at most $N$ inputs, having the following properties.\n\nAll structures fix whole common neighborhoods of the diagonal, every chosen region divisor and the basepoint divisors, and whole neighborhoods of the boundary tori and generator charts. They have genuine time-constant endpoint collars on each autonomous strip end. Every nonconstant zero-$Z$ end strip is regular, at every actual index. Every nonconstant zero-$Z$ polygon, with its conformal parameters included, is regular at every actual index. There are no constant stable polygons. On each finite-tree conformal face the limiting structures are precisely the already chosen structures on its vertices; their comparisons on retained cores and after every fixed number of neck derivatives are exponentially small. The uniform neck bounds and autonomous translated-neck limits required for the actual polygon compactness and gluing theorems hold.\n\nWhen a compatible common protected tensor and an admissible coherent reference system have been supplied, the choices can be made arbitrarily close to that system in any prescribed finite collection of smooth coefficient norms on its compactified-family charts; the prescribed protected tensor and its actual corner angles remain unchanged. In particular a supplied integrable circular model is a legitimate reference. No count-preservation claim follows merely from this closeness.\n\nThe same relative construction gives generic smooth one-parameter coherent families between two such regular coherent choices with the same end paths and protected geometry. The parameter is included in the universal dimension, fixed endpoints are retained, and no regularity of every intermediate slice is asserted. This is a construction of analytic choices. It does not yet assert finite counts or an independence theorem for the resulting operations, and it does not replace sector admissibility by the stated stronger global hypothesis.",
