          "claim": "Choose pairwise disjoint crossing neighborhoods small enough to avoid all other Seifert-circle boundaries. Put the two projected tangent lines on the coordinate axes. Up to reversing both orientations and exchanging axes, the strand directions are east-and-north or east-and-south. In the first case the orientation-compatible noncrossing pairings are west-to-north and south-to-east; in the second they are north-to-east and west-to-south. Thus in each case the two smoothing arcs are locally parallel and equally directed. Any disk region for a third circle whose interior meets this crossing neighborhood contains the neighborhood and both affected circles, so it is an ancestor of each; its assigned height is lower than their disk heights. Raise narrow collars of both attaching intervals into a local slab above every disk sheet meeting the neighborhood. Attach a rectangle along those intervals with one half-twist. Of the two twist directions, choose the one whose long sides project to the original overpassing and underpassing branches. The equal arrow directions on the smoothing arcs permit opposite boundary parameterizations at the two attaching edges of the rectangle, so its orientation extends the orientations of both disks across the gluing. The crossing neighborhoods are disjoint; finiteness lets all collars and bands be thin enough to avoid every other disk sheet and one another.",
