          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "We prove that a rounded polygonal region is a smooth disk. Take the finite triangulation in [F3]. The dual adjacency graph of its triangles, with an exterior root adjacent across every boundary edge, is connected. Indeed the triangles at any one vertex form a consecutive cyclic fan or a consecutive interval fan, so all their sectors are joined through shared edges, and connectedness of the triangulated region joins all fans. Choose a finite spanning tree of this dual graph rooted at the exterior. Process triangles in increasing distance from that root. Remove each triangle and its edge toward its parent: this edge is free, since its only other incident triangle has already been removed or is exterior. Removing a triangle with its free side leaves its two other sides and vertices, preserves connectedness, and preserves $V-E+F$. Plane Euler in [F3], with the exterior counted as one extra face, gives initial value $V-E+F=1$. At the end the remaining connected graph $K$ therefore has $V-E=1$, so it is a tree: a spanning tree already has $V-1$ edges, leaving no edge outside that spanning tree.",
