          "claim": "Every term in $\\partial_t^2x$ corresponding to a two-step path $x\\to y\\to z$ has exponent $(\\operatorname{gr}_t(y)-\\operatorname{gr}_t(x)+1)+(\\operatorname{gr}_t(z)-\\operatorname{gr}_t(y)+1)=\\operatorname{gr}_t(z)-\\operatorname{gr}_t(x)+2$, which is independent of the intermediate generator $y$. Its coefficient is therefore the coefficient of $z$ in $\\partial^2x$, multiplied by that common power of $v$. Since $\\partial^2=0$, $\\partial_t^2=0$. For every differential term the exponent is nonnegative: writing its $U$-power as $k$, the Maslov relation gives $M(y)-M(x)+1=2k$, and the Alexander filtration gives $A(y)-A(x)\\le k$, hence $2k-t(A(y)-A(x))\\ge0$ for $0\\le t\\le2$. Thus $D_G^t$ is a chain complex over $\\mathcal R$.",
