        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-distributions-conormal-kernels-and-cotangent-relation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth $n$-manifold. A distribution on $M$ is a continuous complex-linear functional on compactly supported smooth densities on $M$. Its topology is generated locally by the fixed-support seminorms $\\max_{|\\alpha|\\le m}\\sup_K|\\partial^\\alpha\\varphi|$, followed by the locally convex test-function topology. In a chart $x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_n)$ a test density is $\\varphi(x)|dx|$; under a coordinate change it transforms by the absolute Jacobian. A distribution is compactly supported when its support, the complement of its largest vanishing open set, is compact.\n\nFor a smooth submersion $p:Y\\to M$, define the pullback $p^*u$ by transposing fiber integration: in submersion coordinates $p(x,v)=x$,\n$$\n\\langle p^*u,\\psi(x,v)|dx\\,dv|\\rangle =\\left\\langle u,\\left(\\int\\psi(x,v)|dv|\\right)|dx|\\right\\rangle.\n$$\nThe absolute density quotient gives the fiber density in other submersion coordinates. For a distributional density $V$ on $Y$, meaning a continuous functional on compactly supported smooth scalar functions, define its support by the same largest-vanishing-open-set rule. If $q:Y\\to M$ is proper on $\\operatorname{supp}V$, then for a test function $\\varphi$ on $M$ the set $q^{-1}(\\operatorname{supp}\\varphi)\\cap\\operatorname{supp}V$ is compact. Choose a smooth compact cutoff $\\beta$ equal to one near this set and define the pushforward by\n$$\n\\langle q_*V,\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle V,\\beta(\\varphi\\circ q)\\rangle.\n$$\nThe support property makes this independent of $\\beta$; the cutoff is needed because $\\varphi\\circ q$ need not be compactly supported on all of $Y$.\nA smooth density $\\mu$ times a distribution $u$ in the test-density convention is the distributional density $\\mu u$ defined by $\\langle\\mu u,\\varphi\\rangle=\\langle u,\\varphi\\mu\\rangle$.\n\nLet $Z\\subseteq G\\times X$ be an embedded submanifold whose two projections are submersions. For a smooth density $\\omega_Z$ on $Z$, its incidence delta kernel is the distributional density\n$$\n\\langle K_Z,\\Phi\\rangle=\\int_Z\\Phi|_Z\\,\\omega_Z.\n$$\nIn coordinates $(g,x)=(u,t)$ with $Z=\\{t=0\\}$, this is the delta distribution in the $t$ variables paired with the chosen density on $Z$. The conormal bundle and associated cotangent relation are\n$$\nN^*Z=\\{(z,\\zeta)\\in T^*(G\\times X):z\\in Z,\\ \\zeta|_{T_zZ}=0\\}, \\qquad C_Z=\\{(g,\\eta;x,\\xi):(g,x;\\eta,-\\xi)\\in N^*Z\\setminus0\\}.\n$$\nHere $N^*Z\\setminus0$ removes the zero covector, and the minus sign is the distribution-kernel convention for an operator from $X$ to $G$. Since both projections are submersions, every nonzero conormal in this relation has both $\\eta\\ne0$ and $\\xi\\ne0$. An analytic incidence kernel means that $Z$, its local defining maps, and the coefficients of $\\omega_Z$ are real analytic. This definition covers the incidence kernels used below; it does not assert a general Fourier-integral-operator calculus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-smooth-manifold",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **smooth $n$-manifold** is a pair $(M,\\mathcal S)$ in which $M$ is a\ntopological $n$-manifold\n([[def-topological-manifold-without-boundary]]) and $\\mathcal S$ is a **smooth\nstructure** on $M$: a maximal smooth atlas\n([[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]]).\nBecause\n[[thm-each-smooth-atlas-is-contained-in-a-unique-maximal-smooth-atlas]] sends\nevery smooth atlas to the unique maximal atlas containing it, a smooth manifold\nis equivalently specified by a topological manifold $M$ together with any one\nsmooth atlas $\\mathcal A$, the structure being the generated $[\\mathcal A]$. A\nchart $(U,\\varphi)\\in\\mathcal S$ is called a **smooth chart** (or a **chart of\nthe smooth structure**); its domain is a **coordinate domain** and its\ncoordinate functions are **smooth coordinates** on $U$. When the structure is\nclear from context, the manifold itself is written $M$ in place of\n$(M,\\mathcal S)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-c-r-and-smooth-maps-between-smooth-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ and $N$ be smooth manifolds, let $F:M\\to N$ be a map, let $p\\in M$ and\nlet $r\\in\\mathbb N_0\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$, where $\\mathbb N_0=\\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}$.\nFor $m,q\\in\\mathbb N_0$ and an open set $E\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$, a map\n$g:E\\to\\mathbb R^q$ is **of class $C^r$** as follows. When $m=0$, every such\nmap is declared $C^r$. When $m\\ge1$, each component $g_l$ with $l<q$ must be\n$C^r$ in the sense of\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]]; when $q=0$ this\ncomponent condition is vacuous.\nSuppose $F$ is continuous at $p$. Then $F$ is **of class $C^r$ at $p$** when\nthere are smooth charts $(U,\\varphi)$ of $M$ at $p$ and $(V,\\psi)$ of $N$ at\n$F(p)$ with $F(U)\\subseteq V$ such that the coordinate representative\n\n$$\\psi\\circ F\\circ\\varphi^{-1}:\\varphi(U)\\longrightarrow\\psi(V)$$\n\n([[def-coordinate-representation-of-a-map]]) is of class $C^r$ on a\nneighbourhood of $\\varphi(p)$ in this componentwise Euclidean sense. By\n[[lem-chart-independence-of-c-r-smoothness]], this condition is independent of\nthe chosen charts: if one such representative is $C^r$, then every one is, so\n\"some charts\" may be read as \"any charts\". A map is **$C^r$ on an open set**\n$W\\subseteq M$ when it is continuous on $W$ and $C^r$ at every point of $W$. A\nmap that is $C^r$ for every finite $r$ — equivalently $C^\\infty$ — is called\n**smooth**; the term **$C^r$ map between smooth manifolds** is reserved for the\ncase where $F$ is continuous and the representative condition holds at every\npoint of $M$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-density-bundle-and-smooth-density",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a smooth manifold $M^n$, with boundary allowed, the **density bundle** is $DM=\\coprod_{p\\in M}D(T_pM)$. In coordinates $x$, let $|dx|=|dx^1\\cdots dx^n|$ be the density taking value one on the coordinate frame. On overlaps,\n$$|dy|=|\\det D_x y|\\,|dx|.$$\nA smooth density is a section with smooth real coefficient in these frames. Its support is the closure of its nonzero locus. The absolute determinants are positive smooth transition functions and satisfy the cocycle identities by the chain rule. A countable atlas and [[thm-vector-bundle-construction-from-a-smooth-cocycle]] therefore give a smooth line bundle. For boundary charts the same gluing proof uses half-space product charts; smoothness of transitions follows from their local extensions, and Hausdorffness and second countability follow as for the supplied cocycle construction. The fibers are lines by [[prop-one-densities-form-a-one-dimensional-vector-space]]. When $n=0$ the empty frame trivializes $DM=M\\times\\mathbb R$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-real-analytic-germ-in-several-variables",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $n\\ge1$ and $a\\in\\mathbb R^n$. A real analytic germ at $a$ is an equivalence class of real functions agreeing on a neighborhood of $a$, represented on some polydisc $|x_i-a_i|<r_i$, $r_i>0$, by an absolutely convergent series $f(x)=\\sum_{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N^n}c_\\alpha(x-a)^\\alpha$ with real coefficients. Here $0\\in\\mathbb N$, $\\alpha!=\\prod_i\\alpha_i!$, and $c_\\alpha=D^\\alpha f(a)/\\alpha!$. A vector germ has a finite positive number of such components, with a common polydisc obtained by taking coordinatewise minima of their radii.\n\nThe multi-index convention and absolute convergence are those of [[def-multivariable-power-series]]; for $n=1$ this agrees with [[def-real-analytic-function]]. The same coefficients define the complexification. Indeed choose a positive real polyradius $s<r$; absolute convergence at $a+s$ bounds $|c_\\alpha|s^\\alpha$ uniformly. [[thm-power-series-define-holomorphic-functions-in-several-variables]] therefore gives a holomorphic sum on the complex $s$-polydisc and the displayed derivative formula. Restriction to the real slice recovers $f$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-multivariable-power-series",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix $m\\ge1$ and read $\\mathbb C^m$ through\n[[rem-complex-euclidean-space-dictionary]]. A **multi-index** is\n$\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N^m$, with $|\\alpha|=\\sum_{k<m}\\alpha_k$ and\n$\\alpha!=\\prod_{k<m}\\alpha_k!$ as in\n[[def-ck-and-multi-index-notation-in-several-variables]], every index running\nover $k<m$ from $0$. For $w\\in\\mathbb C^m$ the **complex monomial** is\n\n$$w^\\alpha:=\\prod_{k<m}w_k^{\\alpha_k},$$\n\na finite product in the multiplicative commutative monoid of $\\mathbb C$\n([[def-monoid-finite-product]], [[thm-complex-numbers-form-a-field]]) of natural powers\nin $\\mathbb C$ ([[def-complex-integer-powers]]); for the zero multi-index\n$w^0=1$.\n\n**Enumerating the index set.** $\\mathbb N$ is countable and\n[[lem-finite-powers-of-countable-sets-are-countable]] makes $\\mathbb N^m$ at\nmost countable; it is infinite, so there is a bijection\n$\\sigma:\\mathbb N\\to\\mathbb N^m$ ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]],\n[[def-countable]]).\n\nLet $c:\\mathbb N^m\\to\\mathbb C$ and $a,z\\in\\mathbb C^m$. The **multi-indexed\npower series** $\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha$ **converges absolutely at $z$**\nwhen the complex series $\\sum_n c_{\\sigma(n)}(z-a)^{\\sigma(n)}$ converges\nabsolutely ([[def-complex-series-power-series-and-absolute-convergence]]) for one\nbijection $\\sigma$, equivalently for every one. The two conditions agree, and the\nsums agree, because for bijections $\\sigma,\\tau$ the series along $\\tau$ is a\nrearrangement of the series along $\\sigma$: applying\n[[thm-absolute-convergence-of-complex-series]] to the nonnegative series of\nmoduli transfers convergence, and applying it again to the series itself\ntransfers the sum. That common value is written $\\sum_\\alpha c_\\alpha(z-a)^\\alpha$\nand no other notion of unordered sum is introduced.\n\n**Box partial sums.** For $N\\in\\mathbb N$ put\n$B_N:=\\{\\alpha\\in\\mathbb N^m:\\alpha_k\\le N\\ \\text{for every }k<m\\}$, a finite\nset, and let $S_N(z)