          "claim": "Let $\\kappa$ be an analytic chart change. After shrinking to nested compact neighborhoods, $|\\kappa(q)-\\kappa(y)|\\ge c|q-y|$ and $D\\kappa(q)$ is invertible uniformly. The pulled-back packet has phase $ \\phi(y,q,\\omega)=\\omega\\cdot(\\kappa(q)-\\kappa(y))+\\frac{i}{2}|\\kappa(q)-\\kappa(y)|^2. $ It is holomorphic after complexification, vanishes at $y=q$, and has derivative $-D\\kappa(q)^T\\omega$ there. First [F6] allows $\\omega$ to range over a bounded open ball about its nonzero reference direction: its reference phase $(q-y)\\cdot\\omega+i|\\omega||q-y|^2/2$ is exactly the unit-direction packet with parameter $\\lambda|\\omega|$, and [F6] equates this phase to the Gaussian of width $\\lambda$ on that open ball. Apply the same reference criterion in the new chart before pulling its packets back. We may therefore reparameterize the open frequency ball by $p=D\\kappa(q)^T\\omega$; this is an analytic local diffeomorphism of open frequency parameters and is precisely the cotangent rule. The phase is now normalized and positive in the sense of [F6]. The Jacobian amplitude for scalar distributions is analytic and nonzero: the sign of the real determinant is constant on the smaller connected chart, so its absolute value has an analytic continuation. For distributional densities the amplitude is $1$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F6",
