          "claim": "Work in an analytic ambient chart centered at $x_0$. The hypersurface remains $C^2$ in that chart, since changing from its given smooth chart is a smooth diffeomorphism. A real linear coordinate change makes its tangent plane nonvertical. The $C^2$ implicit graph theorem then writes it as $x_n=\\psi(x')$, with $\\psi$ of class $C^2$: the inverse of a $C^2$ map with invertible derivative is $C^2$, by differentiating the identity for its inverse twice. Translate to have $\\psi(0)=0$. Shrink to a convex $x'$ ball on which $\\|D^2\\psi\\|\\le 2C$. Taylor's integral formula gives $ q_-(x'):=D\\psi(0)x'-C|x'|^2\\le\\psi(x')\\le D\\psi(0)x'+C|x'|^2=:q_+(x'). $ Both supporting quadratics are real analytic and have the same differential as $\\psi$ at $0$.",
