        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "We make the exact packet resolution explicit, without importing a Fourier inversion theorem with an additional choice assumption. In $\\mathbb R^n$, set $ m^\\lambda_{a,b}(y)=2^{-n/2}\\pi^{-3n/4}\\lambda^{3n/4}e^{i\\lambda y\\cdot b-\\lambda|y-a|^2/2}. $ Then, with the frequency integral interpreted by the indicated Gaussian regularization, $ u(y)=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\int e^{-\\varepsilon|b|^2}\\langle u,\\overline{m^\\lambda_{a,b}}\\rangle m^\\lambda_{a,b}(y)\\,da\\,db $ in distributions. Indeed the $b$ integral is $(\\pi/\\varepsilon)^{n/2}e^{-\\lambda^2|y-y'|^2/(4\\varepsilon)}$, by completing the square and the Gaussian contour formula [F4]. The $a$ integral is $(\\pi/\\lambda)^{n/2}e^{-\\lambda|y-y'|^2/4}$. Including the squared normalization, their product is a Gaussian kernel in $y-y'$ with mass tending to $1$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ and width tending to $0$. Pairing against a compact test, each fixed derivative converges uniformly on the support of $u$: split into a small translation neighborhood controlled by uniform continuity and its Gaussian tail. Thus [F1] justifies the distributional limit. The limiting normalization is exactly one, as also follows by multiplying $(2\\pi/\\lambda)^n$ and $(\\pi/\\lambda)^{n/2}$ by the squared packet constant. Applying this identity on data space, or directly transposing the incidence integral, gives the same object kernel as direct transposition of the incidence integral.",
