        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The paired map $\\chi:V\\to T^*X$ is a submersion. Here is a local linear-algebra proof. On $C_Z$, the one-form $p\\,dz-\\xi\\,dx$ vanishes by the conormal equations; hence its differential also vanishes. The tangent space $L$ of $C_Z$ therefore is isotropic for the nondegenerate bilinear alternating form $dp\\wedge dz-d\\xi\\wedge dx$. Its dimension is $\\dim G+\\dim X$, half the ambient dimension, so $L$ equals its alternating orthogonal complement. If $d\\pi_R(L)$ were a proper subspace, the nondegenerate form $d\\xi\\wedge dx$ would give a nonzero vector $w$ orthogonal to it. Then $(0,w)$ would be orthogonal to $L$, hence in $L$, and would lie in the kernel of $d\\pi_L$. This contradicts injectivity. Thus $d\\pi_R$ is onto. The argument uses only the matrix criterion for an alternating form to be nondegenerate and the dimension formula for its orthogonal complement. By [F3], $\\chi$ has a local analytic section $v=v(Q,P)$ onto an open neighborhood of $(x_0,\\xi_0)$.",
