          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "For $v\\in V$ real there is a unique nearby real $t_0(v)$ with $z(t_0(v),X(v))=q$. At this point $\\Psi_t=0$ by step 1.1. Put $D=D_xz$, $T=D_tz$, evaluated there. Its complex $t$ Hessian is $ H=B+iT^TT,\\qquad B=-\\partial_t^2(z\\cdot p), $ where $B$ is real symmetric and $T$ is injective. This matrix is invertible: if $Hw=0$, the imaginary part of $w^*Hw$ is $|Tw|^2$, forcing $w=0$. Apply [F7] to the map retaining $(x,v)$ and replacing $t$ by $\\Psi_t$; it gives a unique nearby complex critical point $t_c(x,v)$ for all nearby complex $x,v$. Shrink the original fiber neighborhood so it contains no other critical point: outside a small ball about $t_0$, continuity and the injectivity of the local coordinate chart preserve a positive lower bound for the phase gradient or for $|z-q|$. Define $\\psi(x,v)=\\Psi(t_c(x,v),x,v)$.",
