          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "We prove its strict object positivity directly, rather than assume a positivity property of a formal stationary phase. At $(t_0,X(v),v)$ the full Hessian in $(t,x)$ is a complex symmetric matrix $ \\mathcal H=\\mathcal R+i\\mathcal S,\\qquad \\mathcal S=[T\\ D]^T[T\\ D],\\qquad \\mathcal R=-\\partial^2_{t,x}(z\\cdot p). $ For a real object variation $h$, let $\\tau$ be the complex solution of the stationary linearized equation $\\mathcal H_{tt}\\tau+\\mathcal H_{tx}h=0$. Write $U=(\\operatorname{Re}\\tau,h)$ and $W=(\\operatorname{Im}\\tau,0)$. The real part of the stationary equation gives $(\\mathcal R U)_t=(\\mathcal S W)_t$. Therefore $ \\operatorname{Im}\\bigl((\\tau,h)^T\\mathcal H(\\tau,h)\\bigr) =U^T\\mathcal S U-W^T\\mathcal S W+2W^T\\mathcal R U =|T\\operatorname{Re}\\tau+Dh|^2+|T\\operatorname{Im}\\tau|^2. $ This is precisely $\\operatorname{Im}(h^T\\psi_{xx}h)$ by differentiating the critical-point identity. If it is zero, injectivity of $T$ gives $\\operatorname{Im}\\tau=0$, and $T\\tau+Dh=0$. The remaining stationary equation is $\\mathcal R_{tt}\\tau+\\mathcal R_{tx}h=0$. These two equations say that $(\\tau,h,\\dot p=0)$ is a tangent variation of the conormal equations $(D_tz)^Tp=0$ and has $\\dot z=\\dot p=0$. By injectivity of $d\\pi_L$ it is zero, so $h=0$. Compactness of the real unit sphere now gives a strictly positive lower bound for $\\operatorname{Im}\\psi_{xx}$, uniform for $v$ in a smaller compact neighborhood in $V$.",
