          "claim": "Apply analytic stationary phase at the complex critical point in step 2.3. Its applicability here includes uniform contour and remainder estimates as follows. At the base real parameters the real $t$ contour has positive quadratic imaginary part $T^TT$; its outer boundary has a fixed positive imaginary margin by step 2.2. For nearby parameters move the interior contour through $t_c(x,v)$ using the holomorphic quadratic coordinates in [F4], keeping the boundary fixed. In those coordinates the contour remains a graph whose Gaussian real part is bounded below by a fixed positive quadratic form, by openness of positive definiteness. Interpolate to its linear tangent contour as in the Gaussian-contour proof; the cubic error is dominated after shrinking the fixed ball. Stokes deformation consequently has a fixed exponential boundary margin. Cauchy bounds for the amplitude and the inverse coordinate map are uniform on smaller complex parameter neighborhoods. Applying the normalized remainder in [F4] with $N=\\lfloor\\epsilon\\lambda\\rfloor$ gives $ K_\\lambda=\\lambda^{-d/2}e^{i\\lambda\\psi}A+E_\\lambda. $ The leading coefficient of $A$ is $(2\\pi)^{d/2}b(t_c(x,v),x)(\\det(-i\\Psi_{tt}(t_c(x,v),x,v)))^{-1/2}$ on the fixed analytic branch. At $x=X(v)$ the critical point is the real $t_0(v)$, where the density coefficient and Hessian determinant are nonzero; continuity and holomorphy therefore make this coefficient elliptic on a smaller neighborhood. Its coefficients have uniform factorial bounds. For real parameters $\\operatorname{Im}\\psi\\ge0$, so normalized exponential errors also give absolute exponential errors. For fixed object derivatives take a smaller complex neighborhood where $|\\operatorname{Im}\\psi|$ is less than half the boundary/remainder margin and apply Cauchy; the cutoff transition pieces already have the corresponding finite-order real derivative bounds. Hence $E_\\lambda=O(e^{-c_m\\lambda})$ in every fixed object derivative as claimed.",
