        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-isotopy-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$.\n\n1. **Compact main case.** Let $M$ be a compact smooth manifold, possibly with boundary, let $N$ be a smooth manifold without boundary, let $F:M\\times I\\to N$ be a smooth isotopy of embeddings that is constant near the ends of $I$, and let $W$ be an open neighbourhood of $F(M\\times I)$ in $N$. Then there is an ambient isotopy $H:N\\times I\\to N$ with $H_0=\\mathrm{id}_N$, every $H_t$ a diffeomorphism, $H_t\\circ F_0=F_t$ for all $t\\in I$, $H_t=\\mathrm{id}_N$ outside $W$ for every $t$, and $H_t$ stationary near the ends; if $F$ is constant near the ends with parameter $\\varepsilon$, then $H_t=\\mathrm{id}_N$ for $t\\le\\varepsilon/2$ and $H_t=H_1$ for $t\\ge1-\\varepsilon/2$.\n2. **Relative form.** Let $N$ be boundaryless, $U\\subseteq N$ open, $A\\subseteq U$ compact, and let $F:U\\times I\\to N$ be a smooth isotopy of embeddings whose track image is open in $N\\times I$. Then there is a compactly supported ambient isotopy $H$ of $N$ with $H_t\\circ F_0=F_t$ on a neighbourhood of $A$ for every $t$.\n3. **Boundary stratum.** If $N$ has boundary and $F(M\\times I)\\subseteq\\partial N$, then the ambient isotopy of clause 1 can be chosen with every $H_t$ carrying $\\partial N$ onto itself; if $F(M\\times I)\\subseteq N\\setminus\\partial N$, it can be chosen compactly supported in $N\\setminus\\partial N$.\n4. **General isotopies.** For the same compact source (possibly with boundary) and boundaryless target as in clause 1, every smooth isotopy extends with support in a compact subset of any prescribed neighbourhood $W$ of $F(M\\times I)$. The endpoint-constancy hypothesis and the stationary-end conclusion are both omitted; all other conclusions of clause 1 hold.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-reverse-mirror-of-oriented-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix the orientation-reversing reflection $r:S^3\\to S^3$ chosen for the mirror convention in the oriented connected-sum definition. If $K$ is a smooth oriented knot, its mirror $r(K)$ carries the orientation induced from $K$ by $r$. The reverse mirror of $K$, denoted $K^\\dagger$, is the image $r(K)$ with its knot orientation reversed. Thus $K^\\dagger$ changes both the ambient orientation convention and the orientation of the knot.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-locally-flat-proper-surface-and-concordance-collars",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a four-dimensional manifold with boundary and let $W$ be a compact surface with boundary. An embedding $e:(W,\\partial W)\\to(X,\\partial X)$ is proper when $e^{-1}(\\partial X)=\\partial W$. It is locally flat at an interior point of $W$ when there are neighborhoods of the point and its image and a homeomorphism of pairs from those neighborhoods to $(\\mathbb R^4,\\mathbb R^2)$, with the standard linear plane $\\mathbb R^2\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. It is locally flat at a boundary point when there are such neighborhoods and a homeomorphism of pairs to $(\\mathbb R^4_+,\\mathbb R^2_+)$, where $\\mathbb R^4_+=\\{x_4\\geq0\\}$ and $\\mathbb R^2_+=\\{x_2=x_3=0,\\ x_4\\geq0\\}$; the line $\\partial\\mathbb R^2_+$ lies in $\\partial\\mathbb R^4_+$. A collar of a boundary circle of $W$ is an embedding of that circle times $[0,\\varepsilon)$ into $W$ that is the identity on the circle at parameter $0$; a concordance collar is required to agree with the corresponding product collar of the ambient end. A smooth proper surface has the analogous smooth charts and collars, which are also topological locally flat charts.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-morse-function-and-excellent-morse-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a smooth manifold and let $f:M\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth.\n\n- The function $f$ is a **Morse function** when every critical point of $f$ is\n  nondegenerate\n  ([[def-critical-point-and-critical-value-of-a-smooth-function]],\n  [[def-nondegenerate-critical-point-nullity-index-and-coindex]]).\n- The function $f$ is an **excellent Morse function** when it is Morse and any\n  two distinct critical points have distinct critical values.\n\nThus excellence is stronger than the Morse condition: it excludes repeated\ncritical values, not repeated indices.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-nondegenerate-critical-point-nullity-index-and-coindex",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $f:M\\to\\mathbb R$ be smooth and let $p$ be a critical point of $f$. The\nHessian $\\operatorname{Hess}_p(f)$ is a symmetric bilinear form on the\nfinite-dimensional real vector space $T_pM$\n([[def-hessian-of-a-function-at-a-critical-point]]).\n\n- The **nullity** of $p$ for $f$ is\n  $$\\operatorname{null}_p(f):=\\dim\\ker\\bigl(\\operatorname{Hess}_p(f)\\bigr).$$\n- The critical point $p$ is **nondegenerate** when\n  $\\operatorname{null}_p(f)=0$.\n- The **index** of $p$ for $f$ is the largest dimension of a subspace of $T_pM$\n  on which $\\operatorname{Hess}_p(f)$ is negative definite.\n- The **coindex** of $p$ for $f$ is the largest dimension of a subspace of\n  $T_pM$ on which $\\operatorname{Hess}_p(f)$ is positive definite.\n\nThe positivity and negativity conventions are those of\n[[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]].",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
