      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose first that a smooth proper disk $D\\subset B^4$ has boundary $K$. Choose an interior point $p\\in D$ and a sufficiently small round four-ball $E$ about $p$ contained in $\\operatorname{int}B^4$. In orthonormal coordinates at $p$, the local graph theorem writes $D$ as $z=g(u)$ over a disk in its tangent plane, with $g(0)=0$ and $Dg(0)=0$. For small radius $R$, the function $r\\mapsto r^2+g(r\\theta)^2$ has derivative $2r+O(r^3)>0$ for every unit direction $\\theta$ and $0<r\\le R$. Hence $D\\cap\\partial E$ is one radial graph over the tangent circle. Replacing $g$ by $\\lambda g$, $0\\le\\lambda\\le1$, and solving the same strictly increasing radial equation gives an isotopy through smooth embeddings of $S^1$ in $\\partial E\\cong S^3$ to the equatorial circle. By [F4], with target $\\partial E\\cong S^3$, this isotopy extends to an ambient isotopy of $S^3$; hence $D\\cap\\partial E$ is ambient isotopic to the standard unknot. The family of round balls moving the center of $E$ to $0$ along a path in $\\operatorname{int}B^4$ is an isotopy of compact embeddings supported in $\\operatorname{int}B^4$; apply [F4] with target $\\mathbb R^4$ to extend it. Hence $B^4\\setminus\\operatorname{int}E$ is diffeomorphic, with boundary collars, to the standard shell $S^3\\times I$. Removing $\\operatorname{int}(D\\cap E)$ from $D$ gives an annulus in that shell with boundary $K$ and a standard unknot.",
