    "cex-knot-kt-21-boundary-case": {
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "counterexample",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-abstract-oriented-knot-cobordism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An oriented circle is a smooth one-dimensional manifold diffeomorphic to the standard oriented circle. An abstract oriented cobordism from an oriented circle $C_0$ to an oriented circle $C_1$ is a compact oriented smooth surface $F$ together with orientation-preserving identifications of its incoming and outgoing boundary components such that $\\partial F=-C_0\\sqcup C_1$. Here $-C_0$ denotes the same circle with its orientation reversed. The cobordism is abstract: no embedding of $F$, $C_0$, or $C_1$ into a common three- or four-manifold is included in the data.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-knot-seifert-pairing-and-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere, and let $F\\subset S^3$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, oriented so that its boundary orientation is the given orientation of $K$. Let $g$ be the nonnegative integer determined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g$. Choose an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ of the first singular homology group $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$, represented by integral one-cycles on $F$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be positively oriented in $S^3$ whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent basis of $F$. The positive push-off $a_i^+$ is the cycle carried to a sufficiently small positive normal level in a bicollar of $F$.\n\nFor disjoint oriented closed curves $J,L\\subset S^3$, orient a meridian $\\mu_L$ of $L$ so that a positive tangent to $L$ followed by a positive basis of its normal disk gives the ambient orientation. The linking number $\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)$ is the integer determined by $[J]=\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)[\\mu_L]$ in $H_1(S^3\\setminus L;\\mathbb Z)$, using the Alexander-duality identification that sends $[\\mu_L]$ to $1$. Extend linking additively over integral one-cycles. The Seifert pairing is $\\theta_F(x,y)=\\operatorname{lk}(x^+,y)$, and its matrix in the chosen basis is $V=(V_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{2g}$, where $V_{ij}=\\theta_F(a_i,a_j)=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The surface and basis are part of the data; this definition asserts no knot-only matrix invariant.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-knot-connected-sum-and-prime-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a coordinate orientation-reversing reflection $r:S^3\\to S^3$ for the mirror convention. Let $K$ and $J$ be smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented three-sphere. A connected-sum datum consists of one smoothly embedded closed three-ball around a short subarc of $K$ and one around a short subarc of $J$, each meeting its knot in a single trivial properly embedded arc, together with the fixed standard orientation-reversing boundary identification of the two standard oriented ball boundaries that joins the oriented arcs into one circle. Choose this standard map once in the standard two-ball model so that it commutes with $r$ and an orientation-preserving half-turn interchanging the two balls preserves the joining convention. Here a trivial arc is an embedded interval in a ball that is carried by an orientation-preserving diffeomorphism of ball-arc pairs to a straight diameter. Remove the interiors of the two balls and glue the remaining boundary spheres using this fixed identification; the resulting oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere is a connected sum of $K$ and $J$, denoted $K\\#J$ for the specified datum.\n\nThe **standard unknot** is the oriented unit circle in a fixed coordinate two-plane of $S^3\\subset\\mathbb R^4$. A knot is **nontrivial** if it is not ambient isotopic through orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of $S^3$ to the standard unknot. A nontrivial knot is **prime** if it has no connected-sum presentation whose two summand knots are both nontrivial.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-metabolic-seifert-form-and-algebraic-knot-concordance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when a knot's Seifert pairing is formed. An integral Seifert lattice is a pair $(H,V)$ in which $H$ is a free abelian group of rank $2g$ for some $g\\geq0$, and $V:H\\times H\\to\\mathbb Z$ is bilinear with $V-V^T$ unimodular. The rank-zero lattice is allowed, with the determinant of its empty skew matrix equal to $1$. A metabolizer is a primitive direct summand $M\\subset H$ of rank $g$ such that $V(x,y)=0$ for every $x,y\\in M$; the lattice is metabolic when it has a metabolizer. A chosen Seifert surface of an oriented knot gives such a lattice through the positive-push pairing. Its form is algebraically slice when that lattice is metabolic.\n\nAn isomorphism of integral Seifert lattices $(H,V)$ and $(H',V')$ is an integral group isomorphism $P:H\\to H'$ satisfying $V'(Px,Py)=V(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in H$. Let $\\mathcal S$ be the commutative monoid of these isomorphism classes under orthogonal direct sum, where $(H,V)\\oplus(H',V')$ has underlying group $H\\oplus H'$ and pairing $V\\oplus V'$, with the rank-zero lattice as identity. The integral algebraic concordance group $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ is the abelian group generated by symbols $[V]$ for $[V]\\in\\mathcal S$, subject to $[V\\oplus W]=[V]+[W]$ and $[M]=0$ for every metabolic lattice $M$. Two selected Seifert forms are algebraically concordant when they represent the same element of $G_{\\mathbb Z}$. This form relation does not assert the existence of a smooth or locally flat topological concordance annulus between the knots.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-smooth-slice-iff-concordant-to-unknot",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. A smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere bounds a smooth properly embedded disk in the unit four-ball $B^4=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\lVert x\\rVert^2\\le1\\}$ if and only if it is smoothly concordant to the standard unknot.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
