          "id": "step-1-2",
          "step": "1.2",
          "claim": "If $n\\ne0$, $x\\in H$, and $nx\\in M$, then $nx\\in L$ and therefore $x=n^{-1}(nx)\\in L$ because $L$ is a $\\mathbb Q$-subspace. Thus $x\\in M$, so $M$ is saturated in $H$.",
