          "claim": "We prove by induction on $r$ that every saturated subgroup $P\\subset\\mathbb Z^r$ is a direct summand. The cases $P=0$ and $P=\\mathbb Z^r$ are immediate. Otherwise choose a nonzero $p\\in P$, divide its coordinates by their greatest common divisor, and use saturation to see that the resulting primitive vector $e_1$ still lies in $P$. The Euclidean algorithm gives a unimodular change of basis carrying $e_1$ to the first standard basis vector, so write $\\mathbb Z^r=\\mathbb Ze_1\\oplus H'$. Every $p'\\in P$ has a unique form $ae_1+h'$ with $h'\\in H'$, and $h'=p'-ae_1\\in P\\cap H'$. Therefore $P=\\mathbb Ze_1\\oplus(P\\cap H')$. The subgroup $P\\cap H'$ is saturated in $H'$; its rank is $\\operatorname{rank}P-1$, since the projection of $P$ to $\\mathbb Ze_1$ is onto. Induction makes $P\\cap H'$ a direct summand of $H'$, hence $P$ a direct summand of $\\mathbb Z^r$.",
