          "claim": "First consider disjoint oriented knots $\\alpha,\\beta$ and two admissible choices $P,P'$ bounding $\\alpha$ with the same product collar, while $Q$ is fixed. Their difference $C=P-P'$ is a closed rational $2$-cycle supported in the interior of $B^4$, because the common boundary collars cancel. By [F3], $C=\\partial Z$ for a finite rational $3$-chain $Z$ supported in the interior. Put $Z$ and $Q$ in general position relative to their boundary collars. The transverse intersection $Z\\cap Q$ is a compact oriented $1$-chain. The product-orientation convention in [F4] makes its boundary count equal to the intersection count $(P-P')\\cdot Q$ plus the term from $Z\\cap\\partial Q$; the latter is empty because $Z$ lies in the interior and $\\partial Q=\\beta\\subset S^3$. By [F2], its signed boundary count is zero, so $P\\cdot Q=P'\\cdot Q$. The same argument with $Q-Q'$ shows independence of the second bounding chain. Bilinearity gives this independence for finite rational combinations of knots.",
