    "lem-slice-disk-induces-seifert-metabolizer": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-knot-seifert-pairing-and-matrix",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot in the standard oriented three-sphere, and let $F\\subset S^3$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, oriented so that its boundary orientation is the given orientation of $K$. Let $g$ be the nonnegative integer determined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g$. Choose an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ of the first singular homology group $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$, represented by integral one-cycles on $F$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be positively oriented in $S^3$ whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent basis of $F$. The positive push-off $a_i^+$ is the cycle carried to a sufficiently small positive normal level in a bicollar of $F$.\n\nFor disjoint oriented closed curves $J,L\\subset S^3$, orient a meridian $\\mu_L$ of $L$ so that a positive tangent to $L$ followed by a positive basis of its normal disk gives the ambient orientation. The linking number $\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)$ is the integer determined by $[J]=\\operatorname{lk}(J,L)[\\mu_L]$ in $H_1(S^3\\setminus L;\\mathbb Z)$, using the Alexander-duality identification that sends $[\\mu_L]$ to $1$. Extend linking additively over integral one-cycles. The Seifert pairing is $\\theta_F(x,y)=\\operatorname{lk}(x^+,y)$, and its matrix in the chosen basis is $V=(V_{ij})_{i,j=1}^{2g}$, where $V_{ij}=\\theta_F(a_i,a_j)=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The surface and basis are part of the data; this definition asserts no knot-only matrix invariant.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-seifert-pairing-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented embedded circle in the oriented standard three-sphere, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ be the ordered integral basis and positive normal push-off convention used to define the Seifert matrix $V$ above. Every class of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$ has a representative that is a finite integral sum of pairwise disjoint smooth oriented embedded circles in $F$. For such representatives $x$ and $y$, the sum of the pairwise linking numbers between the positive push-off of $x$ and $y$ depends only on $[x]$ and $[y]$, is independent of the sufficiently small positive push-off, and equals the bilinear extension of $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The linking number of two disjoint oriented circle components is symmetric. In particular, the Seifert pairing is a well-defined bilinear map $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\times H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\to\\mathbb Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-poincare-lefschetz-duality",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $M$ be a compact $R$-oriented $n$-manifold with boundary $A$, for a commutative unital ring $R$. Cap with its relative fundamental class gives isomorphisms, for every integer $p$,\n$$S_p:H^p(M;R)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_{n-p}(M,A;R),\\qquad T_p:H^p(M,A;R)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_{n-p}(M;R).$$\nHere both maps send a class $a$ to $a\\cap[M,A]$ in the displayed target. Empty boundary recovers Poincaré duality. Disconnected and empty manifolds are included. AC is inherited only from the exhaustion and local universal-coefficient arguments in Poincaré duality.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-linking-number-equals-four-dimensional-intersection-number",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be disjoint finite rational $1$-cycles in the boundary $S^3$ of the standard oriented $4$-ball $B^4$, each represented as a finite rational linear combination of oriented knots. Let $P$ and $Q$ be finite rational $2$-chains in $B^4$ with $\\partial P=\\alpha$ and $\\partial Q=\\beta$, with common standard product collars at their respective boundaries and with their interiors in general position. Then the signed interior intersection number $P\\cdot Q$ equals the bilinear extension of the linking number $\\operatorname{lk}(\\alpha,\\beta)$ defined by positive meridians in $S^3$.",
          "uses": [
