          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-regular-borel-measure-on-an-lch-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A **regular Borel measure** here is a Radon measure $\\mu$ for which every\nBorel $E$ satisfies $\\mu(E)=\\sup_{K\\subseteq E,\\ K\\text{ compact}}\\mu(K)$,\nwith equality allowed at $+\\infty$. Thus regularity strengthens the preceding\nopen-set formula; it is not being used as a synonym for Radon.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For every Borel $A$ and every countable Borel partition $(A_j)$ of $A$, the Euclidean norm satisfies $|\\mu(A_j)|\\le\\sum_{i=1}^m|\\mu_i(A_j)|$. Summing over $j$ gives $V(A)\\le\\lambda(A)$ for $\\lambda:=\\sum_{i=1}^m|\\mu_i|$, so $V$ is finite and $V(\\Omega)<\\infty$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "If $A=\\bigsqcup_{k\\ge1}E_k$ is a disjoint Borel union, then intersecting any partition of $A$ with each $E_k$ and using countable additivity of each coordinate shows $V(A)\\le\\sum_kV(E_k)$. Conversely, for fixed $N$ and $\\varepsilon>0$, choose for each of the finitely many $k\\le N$ a partition of $E_k$ whose sum exceeds $V(E_k)-\\varepsilon/N$. Their union, together with $A\\setminus\\bigcup_{k\\le N}E_k$, is a partition of $A$, so $V(A)\\ge\\sum_{k\\le N}V(E_k)-\\varepsilon$. Letting $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ and then $N\\to\\infty$ proves countable additivity. Thus $V$ is a finite positive Borel measure.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "For every $f\\in C_c(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^m)$ with $|f|\\le1$, approximate $f$ uniformly by finite-valued Borel simple functions whose values have norm at most one. The partition definition bounds each simple integral by $V(\\Omega)$, and finiteness of the coordinate measures lets the uniform errors tend to zero. Hence the displayed supremum is at most $V(\\Omega)$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Since $V\\le\\lambda$ and $\\lambda=\\sum_i|\\mu_i|$ is a finite regular Borel measure by [F1], for every Borel $A$ and $\\varepsilon>0$ there is compact $K\\subseteq A$ with $\\lambda(A\\setminus K)<\\varepsilon$, whence $V(A\\setminus K)<\\varepsilon$. There is also an open $G\\supseteq A$ with $\\lambda(G\\setminus A)<\\varepsilon$, whence $V(G\\setminus A)<\\varepsilon$. Thus $V$ is a regular Borel measure in the sense of [[def-regular-borel-measure-on-an-lch-space]], and in particular a Radon measure.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "given"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "If $V(\\Omega)=0$, the zero field proves the reverse inequality. Otherwise fix $0<\\varepsilon<V(\\Omega)/4$ and choose a countable Borel partition $(A_j)$ with sum greater than $V(\\Omega)-\\varepsilon$; truncate after $N\\ge1$ so the tail is below $\\varepsilon$. Omit indices with $\\mu(A_j)=0$ or $V(A_j)\\le\\varepsilon/(4N)$; the sum of the omitted partition values is at most $\\varepsilon/4$. For every retained index, compact inner regularity [F2] gives a nonempty compact $K_j\\subset A_j$ with $V(A_j\\setminus K_j)<\\varepsilon/(8N)$. Since there are finitely many pairwise disjoint compact sets $K_j$ in the metric space $\\Omega$, choose pairwise disjoint open neighborhoods $N_j$ with compact closures in $\\Omega$. Outer regularity [F2] gives open $G_j\\supset K_j$ with $V(G_j\\setminus K_j)<\\varepsilon/(8N)$. Put $U_j=G_j\\cap N_j$. Then $K_j\\subset U_j$, $V(U_j\\setminus K_j)<\\varepsilon/(8N)$, and the closures of the $U_j$ remain pairwise disjoint compact subsets of $\\Omega$. Define $$\\psi_j(x):=\\frac{\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Omega\\setminus U_j)}{\\operatorname{dist}(x,\\Omega\\setminus U_j)+\\operatorname{dist}(x,K_j)}.$$ This is continuous and compactly supported, equals one on $K_j$, and vanishes outside $U_j$. Set $e_j=\\mu(A_j)/|\\mu(A_j)|$ and $f=\\sum_j e_j\\psi_j$. The disjoint closures of the $U_j$ imply disjoint supports and $|f|\\le1$. For bounded Borel vector fields, the partition definition and uniform simple-function approximation give $|\\int g\\cdot d\\mu|\\le\\int|g|\\,dV$. Thus replacing $e_j\\mathbf1_{A_j}$ by $e_j\\psi_j$ loses at most $V(A_j\\setminus K_j)+V(U_j\\setminus K_j)$ for each retained $j$. Summing these errors, the omitted terms, and the tail gives $\\int f\\cdot d\\mu>V(\\Omega)-3\\varepsilon$. Letting $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$ proves the reverse bound.",
