          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-bv-one-sided-trace-on-lipschitz-graph",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. In a Lipschitz graph chart let $G$ be the side $z>h(y)$, and let $u\\in BV(G)$ locally, with finite $L^1$ norm and variation on each smaller bounded graph collar under consideration. On every smaller base box $B$ there is a unique local $L^1(B)$ trace $\\tau u$ such that\n$$u(y,h(y)+t)\\longrightarrow\\tau u(y)\\quad\\text{in }L^1(B)\\text{ as }t\\downarrow0\\text{ through almost every }t.$$\nThe trace is linear and satisfies a bound $\\|\\tau u\\|_{L^1(B)}\\le C(a^{-1}\\|u\\|_{L^1(C_{2a})}+|Du|(C_{2a}))$, where $C_{2a}$ is a fixed larger graph collar with positive margins at the chart sides and $a>0$ is sufficiently small. For every smooth vector field $\\phi$ compactly supported away from those sides,\n$$\\int_Gu\\operatorname{div}\\phi=-\\int_G\\phi\\cdot dDu+\\int \\tau u(y)\\phi(y,h(y))\\cdot(Dh(y),-1)\\,dy.$$\nThe corresponding assertions hold for the subgraph with the normal reversed. For an interval endpoint in dimension one, the same assertions mean the one-sided scalar limit, the usual endpoint sign, and counting measure on the boundary.",
