          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-lipschitz-graph-area-and-calculus-under-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $m\\ge1$, let $Q\\subseteq\\mathbb R^m$ be open, and let $h:Q\\to\\mathbb R$ be locally Lipschitz. Then $h$ is differentiable almost everywhere, its partial derivatives are measurable and are its weak derivatives. On every open region where $h$ is $L$-Lipschitz, $|Dh|\\le L$ almost everywhere. Let $H^m$ denote the repository's unnormalised Hausdorff measure, put $c_m=H^m((0,1]^m)$, and write $\\sigma_m=c_m^{-1}H^m$ also on Euclidean spaces containing $m$-dimensional subsets. The graph map $F(y)=(y,h(y))$ satisfies, for every nonnegative Borel $a$,\n$$\\int_{F(Q)}a(x)\\,d\\sigma_m(x)=\\int_Qa(F(y))\\sqrt{1+|Dh(y)|^2}\\,dy.$$\nFor the epigraph $G=\\{(y,z):y\\in Q,\\ z>h(y)\\}$, a smooth vector field compactly supported away from the chart sides satisfies\n$$\\int_G\\operatorname{div}\\phi\\,dx=\\int_Q\\phi(y,h(y))\\cdot(Dh(y),-1)\\,dy.$$\nThus its outward normal is $(Dh,-1)/\\sqrt{1+|Dh|^2}$ almost everywhere. The sign reverses for a subgraph. The graph surface measure is locally finite and Radon on $F(Q)$ with its relative topology, and its null sets coincide with graph images of Lebesgue-null subsets of the base.",
