          "claim": "The link complement is path-connected by [F6], so its deformation retract $E_L$ is path-connected. The tube construction makes $E_L$ a smooth manifold with boundary, hence locally path-connected and semilocally simply connected. By [F8] and [F9], inclusion induces an isomorphism $i_*:\\pi_1(E_L,b)\\to\\pi_1(X_L,b)$. By [F8] and [F10], it also induces an isomorphism $i_*:H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)$. The positive meridians lie in $E_L$ and map to the same classes, so the meridian basis is preserved. An $E_L$-loop closes in the restricted cover exactly when its image under $i_*$ lies in $\\ker\\phi$, so the component containing $\\widetilde b$ induces the subgroup\n$$\ni_*^{-1}(\\ker\\phi)=\\ker(\\phi\\circ i_*).\n$$\nThis subgroup is exactly the kernel of the abelianization of $\\pi_1(E_L,b)$ because $i_*$ is an isomorphism. To check connectedness, take $y\\in E_L$, a path $\\delta$ in $E_L$ from $b$ to $y$, and a lift $\\widetilde\\delta$ from a chosen point $\\widetilde b$ over $b$. Any other point over $y$ is $h\\widetilde\\delta(1)$ for some deck transformation $h$, by [F7]. Surjectivity of $i_*$ gives a loop $\\alpha$ in $E_L$ whose lift from $\\widetilde b$ ends at $h\\widetilde b$. The reverse of $\\widetilde\\delta$, this lifted loop, and $h\\widetilde\\delta$ concatenate to a path in $p^{-1}(E_L)$ from $\\widetilde\\delta(1)$ to $h\\widetilde\\delta(1)$. Thus the restriction is connected. By [F4] it is the connected maximal abelian cover of $E_L$, with the same deck group $H_1(X_L;\\mathbb Z)\\cong H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)$.",
