            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every path-connected topological space $X$ and $x_0\\in X$, the Hurewicz map\n$$h:\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\longrightarrow H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$$\nis surjective and has kernel $[\\pi_1(X,x_0),\\pi_1(X,x_0)]$. Hence it induces a natural isomorphism\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0)^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\cong H_1(X;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe proof is choice-free and requires no CW, separation, local path-connectivity or local simple-connectivity assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-string-link-exterior-homology-and-meridian-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Use integral homology. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ be a nonempty labeled oriented smooth link in $S^3$ with chosen pairwise disjoint product tubes and exterior $E_L$. Then $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$, with the positive meridians as its ordered basis. Each boundary torus $T_i$ has a product basis consisting of its positive meridian and a degree-one section. There is a unique degree-one class on $T_i$ whose image has zero $i$th meridian coordinate; this class is primitive and has a simple closed representative.\n\nLet $\\tau$ be an $r$-component string link with chosen disjoint product tubes in $D^2\\times[0,1]$, exterior $X_\\tau$, and bottom and top punctured disks $P_-$ and $P_+$. Then each $\\pi_1(P_\\pm)$ is free of rank $r$. Positive endpoint meridians with the compatible accesses transported from a point-punctured-disk spine form an ordered free basis. For arbitrary independently chosen access paths, the corresponding meridian is $c_i m_i c_i^{-1}$, where $m_i$ is the compatible-stem meridian and $c_i$ is the recorded access-path difference; these conjugates need not be a free basis. In particular $H_1(P_\\pm;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$, and $H_2(P_\\pm;\\mathbb Z)=0$. The exterior satisfies $H_1(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^r$ and $H_2(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)=0$. Each end inclusion sends its ordered positive meridian basis to the ordered positive meridian basis of $H_1(X_\\tau;\\mathbb Z)$. Consequently both induced homomorphisms on $H_1$ are isomorphisms and both induced homomorphisms on $H_2$ are surjective.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-based-string-link-and-labeled-link-peripheral-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $D^2$ be the oriented closed disk and give $C=D^2\\times[0,1]$ the product orientation. An $r$-component string link is an ordered family of pairwise disjoint smooth proper embeddings $\\tau_i:[0,1]\\to C$, for $1\\le i\\le r$, with fixed distinct endpoints $\\tau_i(0)=(p_i,0)$ and $\\tau_i(1)=(p_i,1)$ and fixed product collars at the endpoints. Orient each interval from bottom to top. The exterior $X_\\tau$ is obtained by removing the relative interiors of chosen pairwise disjoint product tubes around the intervals. The bottom and top punctured disks $P_-$ and $P_+$ are the copies of $D^2$ with the interiors of the endpoint tube disks removed.\n\nChoose a basepoint on the outer boundary of $P_-$, a path in the side boundary of $C$ to the corresponding top basepoint, and basing paths from these points to the component meridians. Each end inclusion, with the chosen connecting path for the top end, induces a based homomorphism $\\iota_\\pm:\\pi_1(P_\\pm)\\to\\pi_1(X_\\tau)$. For each string component choose a parallel path on its tube boundary from the bottom endpoint to the top endpoint, and choose access paths that close it to a based loop using the fixed side connector. The resulting element of $\\pi_1(X_\\tau)$ is its based string-link longitude; the parallel and access paths are part of the peripheral datum. The positively oriented boundaries of the endpoint tube disks are the designated meridian loops. Stacking two string links means rescaling them into the lower and upper halves of $C$ and identifying their matching endpoint collars; closure joins each top endpoint to the matching bottom endpoint outside $C$ and retains the component labels.\n\nFor a nonempty labeled oriented link $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ in the oriented three-sphere, choose pairwise disjoint closed product tubes $N(K_i)$, put $E_L=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}\\bigl(\\bigcup_iN(K_i)\\bigr)$, and write $T_i=\\partial N(K_i)$. A positive meridian $\\mu_i$ is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, with the disk orientation fixed by the rule that the oriented tangent of $K_i$ followed by the disk orientation gives the ambient orientation. A degree-one parallel is a simple closed curve on $T_i$ whose projection to $K_i$ has degree $+1$. A based longitude is the class of such a parallel transported to the common basepoint by a specified path. When the positive meridians form a basis of $H_1(E_L;\\mathbb Z)$, the self-meridian coordinate of a longitude is its coefficient on $[\\mu_i]$. A preferred zero-self-meridian longitude is a chosen degree-one parallel whose self-meridian coordinate is zero. The choice, when it exists, is part of the peripheral datum; this definition asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. Changing a common basing path conjugates the peripheral group elements simultaneously.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-oriented-crossing-sign-writhe-and-blackboard-framing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ for the smooth normal-bundle structure used in the\nframing clause. For a knot component $K$, its normal bundle is the fibrewise\nquotient $\\nu K=TS^3|_K/TK$, and a normal section is a smooth section of this\nbundle.\n\nLet $D$ be a regular oriented link diagram in a projection plane $P$ of an\noriented $3$-sphere, after moving the link off the point at infinity. Fix the\nunit viewing normal $n$ pointing toward the viewer. Orient $P$ by the rule that\nan ordered basis $(u,v)$ of $P$ is positive exactly when $(n,u,v)$ is a positive\nbasis of the ambient $3$-space. At a crossing $c$, let $v_{\\rm over}$ and\n$v_{\\rm under}$ be the nonzero projected tangent vectors following the\norientations of the overpassing and underpassing strands. Define\n$$\\operatorname{sgn}(c):=\\operatorname{sgn}\\det_P(v_{\\rm over},v_{\\rm under})\\in\\{+1,-1\\}.$$\nReversing the orientation of $P$ reverses every crossing sign.\n\nThis convention makes the positive geometric Artin half twist positive. In\nthe braid chart $(x,y,t)$ with ambient orientation $(x,y,t)$, view along $+y$;\nthe induced positive orientation of the $(x,t)$ projection plane is the ordered\nbasis $(\\partial_t,\\partial_x)$. For the positive half twist, label $i$ moves\nfrom $x=-h$ to $x=h$ below the midpoint, while label $i+1$ moves from $x=h$ to\n$x=-h$ above it, with $h>0$. Thus the over and under projected tangent vectors\nin coordinates $(t,x)$ are $(1,-2h)$ and $(1,2h)$, whose determinant is\n$4h>0$.\n\nThe **writhe** of $D$ is\n$$\\operatorname{wr}(D):=\\sum_{c\\text{ a crossing of }D}\\operatorname{sgn}(c).$$\nFor a link component $K_i$, its **self-writhe**\n$\\operatorname{wr}_i(D)$ is the sum over crossings whose two branches belong to\n$K_i$; the total writhe also includes all mixed crossings between distinct\ncomponents.\n\nThe **blackboard framing** of a component of $D$ is the homotopy class of the\nnormal section represented by the positive quarter-turn in $P$ of the projected\noriented tangent. The projection is an immersion, so this vector never\nvanishes; it is perpendicular to the projected tangent, hence also to the\nspatial tangent, and therefore represents a nonzero normal section of the\ncomponent in the ambient $3$-sphere.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.8,>=stealth]\n  \\draw[->] (-1,0) -- (1,0) node[right] {$v_{\\rm over}$};\n  \\draw[->] (0,-1) -- (0,1) node[above] {$v_{\\rm under}$};\n  \\node[above left] at (0,0) {$+1$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```\n\nIn the displayed local chart, the over tangent is the positive horizontal\nvector and the under tangent is the positive vertical vector, so the determinant\nis $+1$. This figure illustrates the determinant rule only; no proof below\ndepends on a picture.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
