          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "First apply [F9] to the given surface, fixed pointwise on its boundary. The resulting surface is neat. Incompressibility passes to it, and a boundary-compressing witness passes in either direction; absence of boundary compressions therefore passes as well. Work with this neat representative for the following smooth collar constructions. The boundary-fixing topological ambient extension pulls any resulting boundary-parallel product back to the actual original surface. Thus this preparation does not add a neatness hypothesis to the Statement. If $S$ is boundary-incompressible, the first conclusion holds. Otherwise choose a boundary-compressing disk $D$, with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$ and $\\alpha$ an essential proper arc of $S$. A boundary disk on a torus whose interior misses $\\partial S$ can be pushed inward to compress $S$, unless $S$ is itself a disk: a boundary circle bounding a disk on the connected surface forces the surface to be that disk. An innermost choice among the finitely many inessential boundary circles provides such a boundary disk. But a disk has no essential proper arc. Therefore every boundary circle of $S$ on the torus $T$ containing $\\beta$ is essential. By [F2] its complementary regions are annulus strips. The arc $\\beta$ lies in one of them. If both endpoints lie on the same boundary copy of that strip, it cuts off a disk there. Gluing this disk to $D$ gives a disk with boundary $\\alpha$ and an interval of $\\partial S$; incompressibility of $S$ forces that circle to bound a disk on $S$, so $\\alpha$ would cut off a disk on $S$, contrary to essentiality. Thus $\\beta$ spans its annulus strip $A_0$.",
