          "claim": "First apply [F9] to the given surface, fixed pointwise on its boundary. The resulting surface is neat. Incompressibility passes to it, and a boundary-compressing witness passes in either direction; absence of boundary compressions therefore passes as well. Work with this neat representative for the following smooth collar constructions. The boundary-fixing topological ambient extension pulls any resulting boundary-parallel product back to the actual original surface. Thus this preparation does not add a neatness hypothesis to the Statement. Take a small product neighborhood of $A$ and collars of its incident boundary tori. They exist by a finite coordinate construction: choose the normal side using orientations of $M$ and $A$, combine finitely many adapted chart fields by normalized finite bumps, and use their short flow and the local inverse and compact separation arguments in [F7]. Normalize the two boundary curves on each incident torus by [F3]. Their parallel strip parameters and the product parameter of $A$ supply circle coordinates on the neighborhood, agreeing at the attaching rims. Circle parameter discrepancies have degree $\\pm1$ and are adjusted by interpolation of their increasing, or decreasing, angular lifts; this is finite. Round the quadrant corners in the base coordinate, keeping the circle coordinate. The resulting neighborhood $N_0$ is an ordinary circle bundle with $A$ vertical. If the annulus meets two different original tori, its base is a pair of pants with two original boundary circles and one internal boundary. If it meets one original torus, its band attaches to the same base boundary circle: the two possible band identifications give a pair of pants with two internal boundaries, or a once-punctured Möbius band with one. The total space is orientable, since it is a submanifold of $M$; in the Möbius case the fiber orientation reverses with the base orientation. These are actual finite band-and-collar quotient parameters. 