          "claim": "There are initially at most two frontiers. The only filling that can leave another frontier is the pair-of-pants case with just one original boundary torus; its new base is an annulus with at most one cone. Its remaining boundary groups still inject, by this explicit finite calculation. Before filling the orientable circle bundle over a pair of pants has group $F(a,c)\\times\\langle h\\rangle$: lift the two free base generators, and the oriented fiber $h$ is central and injective by [F2]. Filling adds the primitive relation $c^q h^p=1$, with $q\\ge1$, by [F2]'s gluing pushout. The quotient by central $h$ is $\\mathbb Z*C_q$. The two boundary base loops have images $a$ and $(ac)^{-1}$, both of infinite order by reduced words (for $q=1$, both are $a^{\\pm1}$). Also $h$ has infinite order: the presentation maps to the additive reals by $h\\mapsto1$, $c\\mapsto-p/q$, $a\\mapsto0$. Thus a boundary element projecting trivially must first have zero base exponent, then zero fiber exponent. This proves boundary-torus injection after the intermediate filling. Step 2.1 is therefore applicable again. After finitely many such steps, either no frontier remains or every remaining frontier is incompressible in $M$.",
