        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-choice-implies-dependent-implies-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In ZF,\n\n$$\\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{DC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,\\mathrm{fin}}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_{\\omega,2},\\qquad \\mathrm{AC}\\Longrightarrow\\mathrm{AC}_2.$$\n\nDC here includes a prescribed initial point.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-knot-exterior",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice, as required for the smooth normal-bundle structure in the tubular-neighbourhood interface. Let $K:S^1\\to S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot and let $\\nu(K)=TS^3|_K/TK$ be its quotient normal bundle. Equip $\\nu(K)$ with a chosen smooth fibre norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$. Let $\\Phi:\\Omega\\to S^3$ be a tubular-neighbourhood map from an open neighbourhood $\\Omega$ of the zero section in $\\nu(K)$ that restricts to the inclusion of $K$. For $\\epsilon>0$, define the closed disk subbundle\n$$\nD_\\epsilon\\nu(K):=\\{v\\in\\nu(K):\\|v\\|\\le\\epsilon\\}.\n$$\nChoose $\\epsilon$ so that $D_\\epsilon\\nu(K)\\subseteq\\Omega$, and set $N(K)=\\Phi(D_\\epsilon\\nu(K))$. The fibre norm, tubular map, and radius are part of the chosen tubular data. The **exterior** of this chosen tube is\n$$\nE_K:=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}_{S^3}N(K).\n$$\nRetain its boundary $T_K:=\\partial E_K=\\partial N(K)$, orient $E_K$ by restriction of the fixed orientation of $S^3$, and orient $T_K$ as the boundary of $E_K$. The choice of tubular map and radius is part of the input to this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-knot-normal-circle-bundle-trivialization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every smooth oriented embedding $K:S^1\\to S^3$, let $\\nu_K=K^*TS^3/dK(TS^1)$ be the quotient normal bundle. Orient its two-dimensional fibres so that a positive tangent to $K$ followed by a positive basis of the normal fibre gives the fixed orientation of $S^3$. Then $\\nu_K$ admits a smooth orientation-preserving bundle isomorphism $\\nu_K\\cong K\\times\\mathbb R^2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-knot-exterior-is-irreducible",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth knot with a chosen closed tubular neighborhood $N(K)$, and let $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. Every smoothly embedded two-sphere in the interior of $E_K$ bounds a smoothly embedded closed three-ball contained in $E_K$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-compact-smooth-three-manifold-pairs-admit-compatible-whole-star-triangulations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth three-manifold with boundary allowed. Let $S_1,\\ldots,S_l$ be finitely many pairwise disjoint compact neatly properly embedded smooth surfaces with supplied smooth two-sided product neighborhoods. Let $C$ be a finite disjoint union of smooth circles in $\\partial M$ containing every component of $\\bigcup_i\\partial S_i$. Then $M$ has a finite whole-star smooth triangulation in which $\\partial M$, all $S_i$ and $C$ are subpolyhedra, full after subdivision. Every compact boundary subsurface whose boundary consists of components of $C$ is a subpolyhedron as well.\n\nHere neat means that the surfaces meet the ambient boundary transversely, with the usual product charts there. The conclusion supplies the PL structure compatible with these smooth data. It does not identify an arbitrary preexisting topological or PL marking with that structure.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-haken-torus-bound-for-a-supplied-compatible-smooth-triangulation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable irreducible smooth three-manifold with nonempty boundary, and supply a finite compatible whole-star smooth manifold triangulation with $t$ triangular faces. Let $\\mathcal S$ be a finite disjoint family of two-sided incompressible smooth interior tori. If no component of $M$ cut open along $\\mathcal S$ is homeomorphic to $T^2\\times I$, then\n$$\n|\\mathcal S|\\le 4t+\\dim_{\\mathbb F_2}H_2(M;\\mathbb F_2)-1.\n$$\nThus any process accumulating cuts whose every finite stage has this no-product property can make only finitely many cuts. The family may be empty.\n\nThe supplied triangulation is compatible with the smooth structure in the stated whole-star sense. This result asserts no compatibility of an arbitrary topological marking and does not alter the scope of any other finiteness item.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-smooth-incompressible-torus-cuts-inject-groups-and-preserve-irreducibility",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth three-manifold, and let $\\mathcal T$ be a finite disjoint family of two-sided incompressible smooth tori in its interior, with smooth product neighborhoods. Then every torus group injects into $M$, every boundary copy of a cutting torus injects into its incident cut component, and every component of the manifold cut open along $\\mathcal T$ injects into $M$. If $M$ is irreducible, every such cut component is irreducible in the smooth category. Empty families are allowed. No choice axiom is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-smooth-loop-theorem-and-incompressibility-criterion-in-orientable-three-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable smooth three-manifold, let $F$ be a compact connected smooth subsurface of $\\partial M$, and let $N\\lhd\\pi_1(F)$ be normal. If a continuous disk map into $M$ has its boundary in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ with class outside $N$, there is a smoothly properly embedded disk in $M$ whose boundary lies in $\\operatorname{Int}F$ and has class outside $N$. No pointwise preservation of the original boundary loop is asserted.\n\nConsequently a compact neatly properly embedded two-sided smooth surface $S$ in such a compact orientable smooth three-manifold is incompressible if and only if its inclusion induces an injection on fundamental groups, component by component. Here incompressibility means absence of a smooth embedded compressing disk, with the conventions of the indicated definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-choice-free-relative-transversality-for-a-compact-smooth-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $P$ be a compact smooth manifold with boundary, let $N$ be a boundaryless Hausdorff smooth manifold, and let $i:P\\to N$ be a smooth embedding. Let $Z\\subset N$ be a closed embedded smooth submanifold without boundary. If $i$ is transverse to $Z$ along $\\partial P$, then an arbitrarily small jointly smooth ambient isotopy, supported in any prescribed neighborhood of $i(P)$ and fixed on a neighborhood of $i(\\partial P)$, makes $i(P)$ transverse to $Z$. The isotopy may be stationary near its time endpoints. No choice axiom is assumed. The same assertion holds for any finite family of such $Z$, provided the initial boundary restriction is transverse to every member. Empty source and empty intersection are allowed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "lem-smooth-minimal-torus-splittings-have-unique-isotopy-class",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $M$ be a compact connected orientable irreducible smooth three-manifold with nonempty boundary consisting of tori. If $\\mathcal T$ and $\\mathcal U$ are finite inclusion-minimal systems of disjoint two-sided incompressible smooth interior tori such that every cut piece is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered, then a jointly smooth ambient isotopy of $M$, fixed near its boundary and stationary near the time endpoints, carries $\\mathcal T$ to $\\mathcal U$.\n\nThis is a uniqueness assertion conditional on the two finite systems. It makes no existence or Haken finiteness assertion.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "def-incompressibility-seifert-fibration-and-jsj-system",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a compact smooth $3$-manifold. A properly embedded surface is **two-sided** if it has a product neighbourhood $F\\times[-1,1]$ in $M$. A **compressing disk** for a two-sided surface $F$ is an embedded disk $D\\subset M$ with $\\operatorname{int}(D)\\cap F=\\varnothing$ and $\\partial D$ an essential simple closed curve in $F$; $F$ is **incompressible** if it has no compressing disk. A **boundary-compressing disk** is an embedded disk $D$ with $\\partial D=\\alpha\\cup\\beta$, where $\\alpha=D\\cap F$ is an arc not cutting off a disk in $F$, $\\beta=D\\cap\\partial M$, and $\\operatorname{int}(D)$ misses $F\\cup\\partial M$; $F$ is **boundary-incompressible** if it has no such disk. A surface is **boundary-parallel** if it cobounds a product with a subsurface of $\\partial M$, and it is **essential** if it is incompressible, boundary-incompressible, and not boundary-parallel. $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. An incompressible torus is boundary-parallel if it cobounds a product $T^2\\times[0,1]$ with a component of $\\partial M$; $M$ is **atoroidal** if every incompressible torus is boundary-parallel. A **Seifert fibration** is a decomposition into circles with local models given by fibered solid tori; in the model $(D^2\\times S^1)/\\langle(z,w)\\mapsto(e^{2\\pi i/q}z,e^{2\\pi ip/q}w)\\rangle$, where $\\gcd(p,q)=1$, the core fiber has multiplicity $q$. Fibers of multiplicity $1$ are **regular** and those of multiplicity greater than $1$ are **exceptional**. A finite family of pairwise disjoint incompressible tori is a **JSJ system** if every component obtained by cutting $M$ along the family is atoroidal or Seifert-fibered. It is **inclusion-minimal** if no proper subfamily is a JSJ system. Atoroidal does not mean hyperbolic.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "4.1",
            "6.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The exterior is a compact oriented smooth manifold with torus boundary. To verify the last assertion, orthonormalize the global normal frame of [F2] in the metric used for the tube's disk subbundle. Its chosen tube is then smoothly parametrized by $S^1\\times D^2$, so its boundary is $T^2$. The exterior is connected as well: any two distinct points in it are joined by an embedded great-circle arc in $S^3$ (choose a half great circle in the antipodal case). Apply [F6]'s compact relative transversality to this arc and the knot, fixing its endpoint neighborhoods. A transverse one-dimensional arc cannot meet the one-dimensional knot in a three-manifold, so it avoids the knot. There is a continuous retraction $S^3\\setminus K\\to E_K$, equal to the identity outside the tube and sending a nonzero normal vector of length $r\\le\\epsilon$ radially to the vector of length $\\epsilon$ in the same direction. It is continuous at the tube boundary and fixes both endpoints. The retracted arc is a path in the exterior. The coincident-endpoint case is constant. Thus $E_K$ is connected, and it is irreducible by [F1]–[F2].",
