      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Here is the boundary-relative arrangement required in [F2]. Frame the normal disks of each link component $K$ by the inward tangent of $F_0$ and its oriented normal. The inward ray of $F_1$ has winding zero relative to this framing. Indeed a parallel copy $K_i$ lying just inside either $F_i$ satisfies $\\operatorname{lk}(K,K_i)=-\\sum_{J\\ne K}\\operatorname{lk}(K,J)$: the surface obtained by removing the collar at $K$ is disjoint from $K$ and has boundary $K_i$ together with the other link components, so linking with that boundary is zero. The difference of these two self-linkings is the ray winding: in $S^1\\times(D^2\\setminus0)$ the difference of the two sections is their angular degree times the positive meridian, which has linking one. Thus the angular difference has a smooth real lift $\\theta(k)$. In the tubular coordinates the maps $(k,z)\\mapsto(k,\\exp(it\\chi(|z|)(\\delta-\\theta(k)))z)$, where $\\chi=1$ near zero and is zero near the tube boundary, give an ambient isotopy fixing $L$; their inverses use the negative angle. Apply it to $F_1$, with a fixed $0<\\delta<\\pi$. Its inward ray is now everywhere at angle $\\delta$ from that of $F_0$. Compactness and the smooth collar expansions make the two surfaces disjoint on a common sufficiently small punctured collar of $L$. On the remaining compact part, choose a generic sufficiently $C^1$-small perturbation of $F_1$ transverse to $F_0$, fixed on these collars, by [F2]. The straight small perturbation path consists of embeddings: locally this follows by projecting to the tangent planes and the inverse function theorem, and away from a fixed diagonal neighborhood compactness gives a positive separation between distinct source points. Extend this surface isotopy ambiently by [F2]; its velocity is zero on $L$, so the ambient flow fixes $L$. The remaining intersection is a compact transverse one-manifold, hence a finite union of circles. Trim a small tubular neighborhood of $L$, calling the exterior $X$ and the trimmed surfaces $G_0,G_1$; their boundary curves are disjoint.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
