        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Cut $X$ along $G_0$ and stack the resulting cobordism $Y$ over the integers, gluing the positive face of one copy to the negative face of the next. A lifted loop changes its index by its algebraic intersection with $G_0$, which is its total linking number with $L$. The same construction using $G_1$ therefore gives the same cover $p:\\widetilde X\\to X$ by [F1] and the classification of covering spaces. Write $P_n=t^nP_0$ for the $G_0$ lifts and $Q_r=t^rQ_0$ for the $G_1$ lifts. The closed slabs $Y_n$ between $P_n$ and $P_{n+1}$ have disjoint interiors, and $p$ is injective on $Y_n\\setminus P_{n+1}$. The second stacking description shows that $Q_0$ separates $\\widetilde X$ into a negative and a positive component $C_-,C_+$, with $Q_r\\subset C_-$ precisely for $r<0$. Indeed the negative slabs in the $G_1$ stacking form $C_-$ and the positive slabs form $C_+$. Each cut exterior is connected: the positive and negative sides of a connected spanning surface are joined around its nonempty boundary in the normal-disk half-plane model, and any complementary component must abut one of those sides. Removing the small link tube retains that joining passage on its boundary tori. Thus the slabs and both half-stacks are connected. Compactness of $Q_0$ and local finiteness of the slabs imply that $Q_0$ meets only finitely many $P_n$.",
