        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Suppose there is an interior intersection and choose the largest $n$ with $Q_0\\cap P_n\\ne\\varnothing$. The positive end is the same in the two stackings, since a positive meridian increases both indices. Thus every higher face $P_j$, $j>n$, lies on the positive side $C_+$ of $Q_0$. Choose a component of $C_-\\cap\\operatorname{Int}Y_n$ incident to a curve of $Q_0\\cap P_n$ and let $\\widetilde M$ be its closure. It misses $P_{n+1}$, so lies in the half-open slab on which $p$ is injective. No other $Q_r$ cuts it. For $r>0$, $Q_r\\subset C_+$. For $r<0$, maximality implies $Q_r\\cap P_n=\\varnothing$, since such an intersection would translate to $Q_0\\cap P_{n-r}$. But translating a point of $Q_0\\cap P_n$ shows that $Q_r$ meets $P_{n+r}$, strictly below $P_n$. The connected $Q_r$, disjoint from the separating cut face $P_n$, therefore stays entirely below that face and misses $Y_n$. Consequently $M=p(\\widetilde M)$ is the closure of a component of $X\\setminus(G_0\\cup G_1)$, and restoring the link collars gives a compact three-manifold with corners abutting each surface from only one side. At least one interior intersection curve belongs to its boundary.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
