        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Triangulate the finitely many seam circles and collar edges, extend these triangulations over the compact surface faces by the two-dimensional case of [F3], and then triangulate this $M$ relative to its faces $\\partial_iM=M\\cap F_i$ by [F3]. In the second barycentric subdivision let $A$ be the simplicial neighborhood of $\\partial_0M$ together with the primal one-skeleton, and put $B=\\overline{M\\setminus A}$. In each tetrahedron the part left outside that neighborhood is the cone from its barycenter towards the barycenters of its triangular faces not on $\\partial_0M$; gluing these pieces exhibits $B$ as a neighborhood of the dual graph, rooted on its boundary disks $B\\cap F_1$. Replace the face $\\partial_0M$ of $F_0$ by $T=\\overline{\\partial A\\setminus\\partial_0M}$. For $F_1$ remove only $B\\cap F_1$, not its entire $M$-face, and insert $\\overline{\\partial B\\setminus(B\\cap F_1)}$. These are allowed finite moves: push $F_0$ through the initial face collar and then through a neighborhood of the primal graph; push $F_1$ through a neighborhood of the dual graph rooted on $B\\cap F_1$. For each graph take a spanning forest rooted on its attaching face. Crossing a forest edge together with its new vertex is a ball isotopy. Every remaining edge attaches a product solid cylinder to two disks of the current surface, exactly [F4]. The graphs have finitely many edges and each root lies on the current surface; all feet approach from the selected side in $M$. Thus orientation and connectedness of each spanning surface are preserved. All moves are relative to $L$: in its product collar taper every displacement to zero at the core $L$, using the normal disk radial coordinate, and round the new collar arcs to join $L$ with the shifted frontier. The graphs and their surgery feet are away from $L$. This changes only a collar and is an isotopy fixing its core.",
