        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "In this last case their union, with the orientation on the second surface reversed and their common link collars rounded, is a connected embedded closed oriented surface $\\Sigma$ in $S^3$. Its second integral cohomology is $\\mathbb Z$, so [F5] says its complement has exactly two components. The local two-sided surface charts show that the closure of either component has the whole $\\Sigma$ as boundary: each boundary subset is open and closed in the connected surface, and each side occurs in every chart. Choose one such closure $M$. Its faces are the whole of the two original surfaces, with the small common link collar restored. Apply the construction of $A$ and $B$ once more. Both modified surfaces are now the common replacement surface $T=\\overline{\\partial A\\setminus\\partial_0M}$ (up to the opposite small parallel pushes), so they are ambient isotopic through that product neighborhood, fixed on $L$. This final step does not assert that the original disjoint surfaces were parallel. Concatenate the finite moves from $F_0$ to its last modified surface, this last isotopy, and the reversed finite moves from $F_1$ to its last modified surface. All are ambient isotopies fixing $L$, embedded arc surgeries, or their reverses, and all spanning surfaces are connected and oriented. This is the required finite sequence.",
