      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The two surgery disks and a narrow strip in $F$ joining them have a disk neighbourhood $D$. Replacing the disks by the lateral annulus of the cylinder changes this neighbourhood into a once-punctured torus $P$ with the same boundary $\\partial D$. Write $F_0=\\overline{F\\setminus D}$. Any old homology class can be represented in $F_0$: replace each of its finitely many arcs through $D$ by an arc along $\\partial D$, which changes the cycle by a boundary in the disk. Take open collar neighbourhoods in $F$ and $F'$ so that $F_0$ and respectively $D$ or $P$ form open covers whose intersection retracts onto $\\partial D$. In the Mayer–Vietoris sequence [F3], the map from $H_0(\\partial D;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z$ to the direct sum of the two $H_0$ groups is $n\\mapsto(n,-n)$ and is injective. For $F=F_0\\cup D$, the $H_1$ map from the intersection circle has image generated by $([\\partial D],0)$, since $H_1(D;\\mathbb Z)=0$. For $F'=F_0\\cup P$, the same image is generated by $([\\partial D],0)$ because the oriented boundary of $P$ is zero in $H_1(P;\\mathbb Z)$, while $H_1(P;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^2$ on its longitudinal and meridional curves. Thus $H_1(F')\\cong H_1(F)\\oplus\\mathbb Z^2$, with the old classes as a direct summand. Choose the added basis classes $b,c$ from those two curves; together with the old basis they are an integral basis.",
