          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "The surgery retains the old oriented surface outside the two attaching disks, so its positive normal there is unchanged and the old matrix block remains $V$. Use product coordinates $(t,w)$ on the cylinder, with $w\\in D^2$, and take $c=\\{t_0\\}\\times\\partial D^2$. Let $b$ follow one longitudinal side of the tube and return through $F_0$; it crosses each cross-section disk once. The disks $\\{t_0\\}\\times D^2$ and their sufficiently close parallel disks miss every old basis curve and its push-off, so [F4] gives zero for the two Seifert pairings between $c$ and every old basis curve. Along the lateral annulus, the positive normal points either out of or into the solid cylinder. If it points out, the radius-one cross-section disk misses $b^+$, giving $V(b,c)=0$, while the disk of slightly larger radius bounds $c^+$ and meets $b$ once; it gives $\\operatorname{lk}(b,c^+)=+1$ after orienting $c$, hence $V(c,b)=+1$ by the symmetry in [F4]. If it points in, the radius-one disk meets $b^+$ once, giving $V(b,c)=+1$ after orienting $c$, while the disk of slightly smaller radius bounds $c^+$ and misses $b$, giving $\\operatorname{lk}(b,c^+)=0$ and therefore $V(c,b)=0$. These intersection statements follow from the explicit single transverse point in the product coordinates and [F4]. In either case the concentric parallel meridians have $V(c,c)=0$. The remaining entries involving $b$ are integers $x$, $y$, and $z=\\operatorname{lk}(b^+,b)$. Thus in the ordered basis $(a_1,\\ldots,a_m,b,c)$ the matrix has exactly one of the two displayed forms.",
