          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$ be a nonempty smooth oriented link, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface whose oriented boundary is $L$, and let $g$ be its genus. The compact-surface normal form gives $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\mathbb Z^{2g+r-1}$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be a positive ambient frame whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent frame of $F$. For an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\dots,a_m$, with $m=2g+r-1$, choose circle-cycle representatives on $F$ and let $a_i^+$ be a sufficiently small copy in the positive normal direction. For disjoint oriented embedded circles $C,D\\subset S^3$, define $\\operatorname{lk}(C,D)$ by $[C]=\\operatorname{lk}(C,D)[\\mu_D]$ in $H_1(S^3\\setminus D;\\mathbb Z)$, where $\\mu_D$ is the positive meridian whose oriented normal disk follows the positive tangent to $D$ in the ambient orientation; extend linking bilinearly to finite sums of such circles. Define the positive-push Seifert matrix by $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. The link-surface pairing lemma proves this matrix is independent of the chosen cycle representatives and push-off level. The surface and basis remain part of the data; this definition asserts no link-only matrix invariance.",
