        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-seifert-matrix-s-equivalence",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $V$ be an even-order square matrix with integer entries, of size $n=2g$. A unimodular congruence replaces $V$ by $P^TVP$ for an integer matrix $P$ with determinant $+1$ or $-1$. For $x,y\\in\\mathbb Z^n$ as column vectors and $z\\in\\mathbb Z$, the two elementary enlargements of $V$ are\n$$E_0(V;x,y,z)=\\begin{pmatrix}V&x&0\\\\y^T&z&0\\\\0^T&1&0\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad E_1(V;x,y,z)=\\begin{pmatrix}V&x&0\\\\y^T&z&1\\\\0^T&0&0\\end{pmatrix}.$$\nTwo even-order integral matrices are **S-equivalent** if one can be obtained from the other by a finite sequence of unimodular congruences, elementary enlargements, and reverse elementary enlargements.",
