          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-seifert-matrix-skew-part-is-surface-intersection",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented embedded circle in the oriented standard three-sphere, let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface with boundary $K$, and let $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ be any ordered integral basis of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$ with Seifert matrix $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. Let $J_F$ be the matrix of the algebraic intersection form on $F$, with a transverse intersection of oriented curves $u,v$ counted positively exactly when their ordered tangent vectors form a positive basis of $TF$. Then $V-V^T=J_F$. The matrix $J_F$ is skew-symmetric and unimodular; when $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ is a standard handle basis, it is a direct sum of blocks $\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-integral-witt-and-seifert-isometric-structures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when an integral Seifert lattice comes from a knot's positive-push pairing. For an integral Seifert lattice $(H,V)$, its integral Witt class is its class in $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ from the definition of metabolic Seifert form and algebraic knot concordance. An isomorphism $(W,B,T)\\to(W',B',T')$ of rational isometric structures is a rational-linear bijection $P:W\\to W'$ such that $B'(Px,Py)=B(x,y)$ and $PT=T'P$. A rational isometric structure is a triple $(W,B,T)$ in which $W$ is a finite-dimensional even-dimensional $\\mathbb Q$-vector space, $B:W\\times W\\to\\mathbb Q$ is a nonsingular symmetric bilinear form, $T:W\\to W$ is an invertible linear map satisfying $B(Tx,Ty)=B(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in W$, and $I-T$ is invertible. Such a structure is metabolic if it has a $T$-invariant subspace $L\\subset W$ with $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}L=\\frac12\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}W$ and $B|_{L\\times L}=0$. Define the rational Witt group $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ by direct sum of isomorphism classes, with metabolic structures set equal to zero. For a nonsingular integral Seifert matrix $V$, the associated matrix pair is $(B,T)=(V+V^T,V^{-1}V^T)$; whether this pair is a rational isometric structure is established separately.\n\nThe definition concerns finite-rank bilinear forms and isometry data. It does not identify the unrelated Witt Lie algebra with either $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ or $G_{\\mathbb Q}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "The matrix $B=V+V^T$ is symmetric. Modulo $2$, $V+V^T\\equiv V-V^T$; by [F1] the determinant of $V-V^T$ is odd, so $\\det B$ is odd and in particular nonzero. Thus $B$ is nonsingular over $\\mathbb Q$.",
