    "thm-levine-algebraic-concordance-classification": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-metabolic-seifert-form-and-algebraic-knot-concordance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when a knot's Seifert pairing is formed. An integral Seifert lattice is a pair $(H,V)$ in which $H$ is a free abelian group of rank $2g$ for some $g\\geq0$, and $V:H\\times H\\to\\mathbb Z$ is bilinear with $V-V^T$ unimodular. The rank-zero lattice is allowed, with the determinant of its empty skew matrix equal to $1$. A metabolizer is a primitive direct summand $M\\subset H$ of rank $g$ such that $V(x,y)=0$ for every $x,y\\in M$; the lattice is metabolic when it has a metabolizer. A chosen Seifert surface of an oriented knot gives such a lattice through the positive-push pairing. Its form is algebraically slice when that lattice is metabolic.\n\nAn isomorphism of integral Seifert lattices $(H,V)$ and $(H',V')$ is an integral group isomorphism $P:H\\to H'$ satisfying $V'(Px,Py)=V(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in H$. Let $\\mathcal S$ be the commutative monoid of these isomorphism classes under orthogonal direct sum, where $(H,V)\\oplus(H',V')$ has underlying group $H\\oplus H'$ and pairing $V\\oplus V'$, with the rank-zero lattice as identity. The integral algebraic concordance group $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ is the abelian group generated by symbols $[V]$ for $[V]\\in\\mathcal S$, subject to $[V\\oplus W]=[V]+[W]$ and $[M]=0$ for every metabolic lattice $M$. Two selected Seifert forms are algebraically concordant when they represent the same element of $G_{\\mathbb Z}$. This form relation does not assert the existence of a smooth or locally flat topological concordance annulus between the knots.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-integral-witt-and-seifert-isometric-structures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when an integral Seifert lattice comes from a knot's positive-push pairing. For an integral Seifert lattice $(H,V)$, its integral Witt class is its class in $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ from the definition of metabolic Seifert form and algebraic knot concordance. An isomorphism $(W,B,T)\\to(W',B',T')$ of rational isometric structures is a rational-linear bijection $P:W\\to W'$ such that $B'(Px,Py)=B(x,y)$ and $PT=T'P$. A rational isometric structure is a triple $(W,B,T)$ in which $W$ is a finite-dimensional even-dimensional $\\mathbb Q$-vector space, $B:W\\times W\\to\\mathbb Q$ is a nonsingular symmetric bilinear form, $T:W\\to W$ is an invertible linear map satisfying $B(Tx,Ty)=B(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in W$, and $I-T$ is invertible. Such a structure is metabolic if it has a $T$-invariant subspace $L\\subset W$ with $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}L=\\frac12\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}W$ and $B|_{L\\times L}=0$. Define the rational Witt group $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ by direct sum of isomorphism classes, with metabolic structures set equal to zero. For a nonsingular integral Seifert matrix $V$, the associated matrix pair is $(B,T)=(V+V^T,V^{-1}V^T)$; whether this pair is a rational isometric structure is established separately.\n\nThe definition concerns finite-rank bilinear forms and isometry data. It does not identify the unrelated Witt Lie algebra with either $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ or $G_{\\mathbb Q}$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-integral-seifert-witt-group-injects-into-rational-isometric-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every element of the integral algebraic concordance group has an invertible integral Seifert-matrix representative, allowing the empty matrix for the zero-rank representative. If $V$ is any such representative, the rule\n$$\n[V]\\longmapsto[(\\mathbb Q^{\\operatorname{rank}V},V+V^T,V^{-1}V^T)]\n$$\ndefines an injective homomorphism into the rational isometric Witt group. An integral Seifert form represents zero in the integral group if and only if it is itself metabolic. These are finite algebraic assertions and require no choice principle; knot-derived forms retain their separately stated choice assumptions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rational-isometric-witt-group-has-reciprocal-primary-decomposition",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a monic irreducible polynomial $p\\in\\mathbb Q[t]$ with $p(0)\\ne0$, put $p^*(t)=p(0)^{-1}t^{\\deg p}p(t^{-1})$. For each such self-reciprocal polynomial $p=p^*$ other than $t-1$, let $G_p$ be the subgroup of the rational isometric Witt group generated by structures whose characteristic polynomial is a power of $p$. Then restriction to the generalized primary subspaces induces an isomorphism\n$$\nG_{\\mathbb Q}\\longrightarrow\\bigoplus_{p=p^*,\\ p\\ne t-1}G_p.\n$$\nA pair of distinct reciprocal primary factors contributes a metabolic structure and therefore contributes zero. Only finitely many components of any given class are nonzero. No choice principle is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-rational-primary-isometric-classes-are-hermitian-witt-classes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p\\in\\mathbb Q[t]$ be monic, irreducible, and self-reciprocal, with $p(0)\\ne0$ and $p\\ne t-1$. Put $E=\\mathbb Q[t]/(p)$, write $\\alpha=t+(p)$, and give $E$ the involution $\\iota(\\alpha)=\\alpha^{-1}$. If $p\\ne t+1$, the primary component $G_p$ of the rational isometric Witt group is isomorphic to the hermitian Witt group of $E$ with this involution. Here the latter group is generated by finite-dimensional nonsingular hermitian forms, with orthogonal direct sum as addition and forms having an isotropic half-dimensional $E$-subspace set to zero. If $p=t+1$, the same conclusion holds with the group generated by even-dimensional nonsingular symmetric forms over $\\mathbb Q$, modulo metabolic forms. For a semisimple primary isometry, the comparison sends a hermitian form $h$ to $(\\operatorname{Tr}_{E/\\mathbb Q}h,\\alpha)$ on its underlying rational vector space. These comparisons require no choice principle.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-hermitian-number-field-signature-kernel-is-killed-by-four",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $E/F$ be a quadratic extension of number fields with its nontrivial involution. In the hermitian Witt group $W(E/F)$, the kernel of all signatures is killed by $4$. The joint signature image is a free abelian subgroup of $\\mathbb Z^s$ of rank at most $s$, where $s$ is the number of real places of $F$ at which $E$ becomes complex. No choice principle is required.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-rational-symmetric-signature-kernel-is-killed-by-four",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $W(\\mathbb Q)$ denote the Witt group of nonsingular symmetric bilinear forms over $\\mathbb Q$, with orthogonal sum as addition and metabolic forms set to zero. For every such form $q$, four times its Witt class equals its signature times the class of the four-dimensional identity form. In particular, the kernel of the signature is killed by $4$, including its restriction to the subgroup of even-dimensional classes. The signature image is $\\mathbb Z$ on the full group and $2\\mathbb Z$ on the even-dimensional subgroup.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-integral-algebraic-concordance-has-independent-free-two-and-four-torsion-families",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "In the integral algebraic concordance group put\n$$\nF_b=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&b\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad P_n=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&-n^2\\end{pmatrix},\\qquad Q_q=\\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\\\0&-(3q-1)/4\\end{pmatrix},\n$$\nwhere $b,n$ range over positive integers and $q$ ranges over primes congruent to $3$ modulo $4$ other than $3$. The classes $[F_b]$ are independent of infinite order, each $[P_n]$ has exact order two, and each $[Q_q]$ has exact order four. The three families together generate their direct sum, isomorphic to\n$$\n\\mathbb Z^{(\\mathbb N)}\\oplus(\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z)^{(\\mathbb N)}\\oplus(\\mathbb Z/4\\mathbb Z)^{(\\mathbb N)}.\n$$\nMoreover the classes $[P_n]$ have linearly independent images in $G_{\\mathbb Z}/2G_{\\mathbb Z}$. These are pure algebraic assertions about the displayed forms; no choice principle is required.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-countable-signature-groups-with-exponent-four-kernel-split",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume full AC. Let $G$ be a countable abelian group and $s:G\\to\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}\\mathbb Z$ a homomorphism whose kernel $K$ satisfies $4K=0$. Then $K$ is precisely the torsion subgroup of $G$, and for cardinals $r,u,v\\le\\aleph_0$,\n$$G\\cong\\mathbb Z^{(r)}\\oplus(\\mathbb Z/2\\mathbb Z)^{(u)}\\oplus(\\mathbb Z/4\\mathbb Z)^{(v)}.$$\nIf $G$ contains elements $f_i,p_i,q_i$, indexed by positive integers, such that the $f_i$ are integer-linearly independent, the order-two elements $p_i$ have linearly independent images in $G/2G$, and the $2q_i$ are linearly independent order-two elements with $4q_i=0$, then $r=u=v=\\aleph_0$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Both groups in [F1] are countable. A rational structure is specified, after one finite basis choice, by its dimension and two finite rational matrices; the isometry and nonsingularity conditions select a subset of this countable set. An integral Seifert lattice is likewise specified by a finite integer matrix. The corresponding presented groups are quotients of the countable sets of finite signed lists of these matrices: finite lists of rationals or integers have explicit natural-number codes, so a countable union over their lengths is countable. Quotients are countable by the surjection from these codes. This argument does not identify the two types of object. Use the injection [F2] to regard $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ as a subgroup of $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ for the rest of the proof.",
