        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each primary group of [F3] other than $t+1$, the involution on $E$ is nontrivial. Indeed if its distinguished generator equalled its inverse, its irreducible polynomial would divide $t^2-1$; $t-1$ is excluded by invertibility of $I-T$ and $t+1$ was separated. If $F=E^{\\sigma}$, choose $0\\ne\\delta$ with $\\sigma\\delta=-\\delta$. Every $z\\in E$ has the unique decomposition $(z+\\sigma z)/2+\\delta(z-\\sigma z)/(2\\delta)$ with coefficients in $F$, so $E/F$ is quadratic. Take all hermitian signatures at real places of $F$ where $E$ becomes complex, and take the ordinary rational signature on the $t+1$ component. These form a homomorphism $s:G_{\\mathbb Q}\\to\\bigoplus_{j\\in J}\\mathbb Z$. The index set $J$ is at most countable: there are countably many rational irreducible polynomials and finitely many real places for each number field. Each individual class has only finitely many primary components, so its total signature has finite support. With [A1] fix an enumeration of this index set, adding zero coordinates if needed, to put the target inside $\\bigoplus_{n\\ge1}\\mathbb Z$.",
          "step": "2.1",
