          "claim": "In either group, the elements $F_b,P_n,Q_q$ of [F5] meet the three infinitude tests of [F6]. A finite nonzero integer combination of the $F_b$ cannot be torsion: if some nonzero multiple vanished, projection to each distinct primary factor would contradict the infinite order of its $F_b$. The $2Q_q$ are independent order-two elements, by distinct primary factors and exact order four. For the rational quotient by doubles, project to the quadratic primary factor of $P_n$, identified with a hermitian Witt group by [F3], and then take hermitian rank modulo two. This is a homomorphism because metabolic hermitian forms have even rank. The two-dimensional rational structure of $P_n$ has rank one over its quadratic field, so the homomorphism takes value one there; it vanishes on the other $P_m$ by their distinct primary factors, and on every double by its target $\\mathbb Z/2$. Hence the $P_n$ are independent modulo doubles in $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ as well as in $G_{\\mathbb Z}$, the latter also following directly from [F5]. Thus the $P_n$ are independent in the quotient by doubles in each group. This last condition forces independent order-two summands after splitting; independent order-two elements alone would not suffice, since doubles of order-four elements also have order two. There are infinitely many indicated primes $q$ by the finite Euclidean argument in [F5].",
