          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-metabolic-seifert-form-and-algebraic-knot-concordance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when a knot's Seifert pairing is formed. An integral Seifert lattice is a pair $(H,V)$ in which $H$ is a free abelian group of rank $2g$ for some $g\\geq0$, and $V:H\\times H\\to\\mathbb Z$ is bilinear with $V-V^T$ unimodular. The rank-zero lattice is allowed, with the determinant of its empty skew matrix equal to $1$. A metabolizer is a primitive direct summand $M\\subset H$ of rank $g$ such that $V(x,y)=0$ for every $x,y\\in M$; the lattice is metabolic when it has a metabolizer. A chosen Seifert surface of an oriented knot gives such a lattice through the positive-push pairing. Its form is algebraically slice when that lattice is metabolic.\n\nAn isomorphism of integral Seifert lattices $(H,V)$ and $(H',V')$ is an integral group isomorphism $P:H\\to H'$ satisfying $V'(Px,Py)=V(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in H$. Let $\\mathcal S$ be the commutative monoid of these isomorphism classes under orthogonal direct sum, where $(H,V)\\oplus(H',V')$ has underlying group $H\\oplus H'$ and pairing $V\\oplus V'$, with the rank-zero lattice as identity. The integral algebraic concordance group $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ is the abelian group generated by symbols $[V]$ for $[V]\\in\\mathcal S$, subject to $[V\\oplus W]=[V]+[W]$ and $[M]=0$ for every metabolic lattice $M$. Two selected Seifert forms are algebraically concordant when they represent the same element of $G_{\\mathbb Z}$. This form relation does not assert the existence of a smooth or locally flat topological concordance annulus between the knots.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-positive-hopf-plumbing-has-trefoil-seifert-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. A positive Hopf band is an oriented annulus whose two boundary components, with their induced orientations, have linking number $+1$. Let $F$ be the genus-one Seifert surface obtained by plumbing two positive Hopf bands in a square, with oriented core curves $a,b$ forming a positive handle basis, and choose the plumbing side so that $a^+$ bounds a disk in $S^3$ disjoint from $b$. The oriented boundary of this standard positive two-band plumbing is the right-handed trefoil. In the positive-push convention $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$ with ordered basis $(a,b)$, the Seifert matrix is\n$$\nV=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\\\-1&-1\\end{pmatrix}.\n$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-integral-witt-and-seifert-isometric-structures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice when an integral Seifert lattice comes from a knot's positive-push pairing. For an integral Seifert lattice $(H,V)$, its integral Witt class is its class in $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ from the definition of metabolic Seifert form and algebraic knot concordance. An isomorphism $(W,B,T)\\to(W',B',T')$ of rational isometric structures is a rational-linear bijection $P:W\\to W'$ such that $B'(Px,Py)=B(x,y)$ and $PT=T'P$. A rational isometric structure is a triple $(W,B,T)$ in which $W$ is a finite-dimensional even-dimensional $\\mathbb Q$-vector space, $B:W\\times W\\to\\mathbb Q$ is a nonsingular symmetric bilinear form, $T:W\\to W$ is an invertible linear map satisfying $B(Tx,Ty)=B(x,y)$ for all $x,y\\in W$, and $I-T$ is invertible. Such a structure is metabolic if it has a $T$-invariant subspace $L\\subset W$ with $\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}L=\\frac12\\dim_{\\mathbb Q}W$ and $B|_{L\\times L}=0$. Define the rational Witt group $G_{\\mathbb Q}$ by direct sum of isomorphism classes, with metabolic structures set equal to zero. For a nonsingular integral Seifert matrix $V$, the associated matrix pair is $(B,T)=(V+V^T,V^{-1}V^T)$; whether this pair is a rational isometric structure is established separately.\n\nThe definition concerns finite-rank bilinear forms and isometry data. It does not identify the unrelated Witt Lie algebra with either $G_{\\mathbb Z}$ or $G_{\\mathbb Q}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
