          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-knot-planar-code",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Work with smooth oriented knots in the standard oriented three-sphere; a knot is a one-component oriented link. A **finite planar code for an oriented knot diagram** consists of finite vertex and edge sets, incidence data, a cyclic order of the four half-edges at every crossing vertex, a pairing of opposite half-edges into the two local branches, an orientation of each edge, and a choice of which branch is over at each crossing. Every other vertex is a bend vertex of valence two. The code includes a finite plane-drawing certificate: rational coordinates for the vertices and bends, and a finite polygonal path for every edge. The certificate must realize the incidences and cyclic orders, have no edge self-intersection, and have pairwise disjoint edge interiors except at the specified bend and crossing vertices; at a crossing the two branches must be transverse. These conditions are checked by finitely many exact segment-intersection tests. Edge orientations must join coherently across each paired branch and each bend vertex, and following the edges through the crossing pairings and the degree-two bend vertices must give one oriented circle. Thus an admissible code represents a connected regular oriented diagram of a single knot. If there are no crossing vertices, the code is one oriented polygonal circle and has one arc. Smooth each bend in a small disk disjoint from all crossings and other edges; the resulting smooth diagram has the same crossing data.\n\nAn **under-arc** is a maximal portion of the oriented circle between consecutive points where the knot passes under at a crossing; the over branch is not cut. With no crossings the entire circle is the unique under-arc. A **positive meridian** of an oriented branch is the oriented boundary of a small normal disk, with disk orientation chosen so that the oriented tangent to the branch followed by the disk orientation agrees with the ambient orientation of $S^3$. Planarity, incidence, orientation consistency, one-component tracing and the over/under data are input validity conditions; a procedure receiving an invalid finite record must report invalid input rather than repair it. The code stores no algebraic crossing sign: the oriented plane and the direction of the over branch determine which incident under branch is on its left and which is on its right.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-compact-knot-has-choice-free-tube",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K\\subset\\mathbb R^3$ be the image of a compact smooth embedded circle, and let $E:N^\\perp K\\to\\mathbb R^3$ be the Euclidean normal-addition map $E(p,v)=p+v$. There is a real $\\varepsilon>0$ such that $E$ restricts to a diffeomorphism from $\\{(p,v):\\|v\\|<\\varepsilon\\}$ onto an open neighborhood $N_\\varepsilon$ of $K$. Moreover, $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus K$ strongly deformation retracts onto $\\mathbb R^3\\setminus N_\\varepsilon$ by pushing each nonzero normal vector radially to length $\\varepsilon$. The construction uses only a finite cover of the compact circle and no choice axiom.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-cw-homotopy-equivalence-inclusions-are-strong-deformation-retracts",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $A\\subset X$ is a CW subcomplex and its inclusion is a homotopy equivalence, then $X$ strongly deformation retracts onto $A$. Moreover, if $(Y,B)$ is a CW pair and $u_0,u_1:B\\to Z$ are homotopic, then the adjunction spaces $Z\\cup_{u_0}Y$ and $Z\\cup_{u_1}Y$ are homotopy equivalent relative to their common subspace $Z$.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-cw-quotients-and-collapse-of-a-contractible-subcomplex",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,A)$ be a CW pair with $A\\ne\\varnothing$ and supplied characteristic maps. The ordinary quotient $X/A$ is a CW complex, with one vertex $*$ replacing $A$ and one cell of the same dimension for every cell of $X\\setminus A$.\n\nIf $A$ admits a contraction $H:A\\times I\\to A$, $H(-,0)=\\mathrm{id}_A$, $H(-,1)=a\\in A$, then the quotient map $q:X\\to X/A$ is a homotopy equivalence and a weak homotopy equivalence. If $H(a,t)=a$ for every $t$, the constructed inverse and inverse homotopies are based at $a$ and $*$. No choice principle is required; a contraction is one witness, not a family of selected contractions.",
          "uses": [
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-polygonal-jordan-curve",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "If $P\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ is a polygon, then $\\mathbb R^2\\setminus P$ has exactly two regions, one bounded and one unbounded, and\n\n$$\\operatorname{Fr}(U)=P$$\n\nfor each of them. Regions and frontiers are from [[def-plane-region-and-frontier]]. The proof uses the finite simple edge cycle of [[def-polygonal-arc-and-polygon]] and polygonal crossing parity from [[lem-polygonal-crossing-parity-is-locally-constant]].",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-fundamental-group-of-finite-wedge-of-circles",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $Q=\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$ be pointed at $[0]$, and for $r\\in\\mathbb N$ put\n\n$$W_r:=\\bigvee_{j<r}(Q,[0]).$$\n\nThen $\\pi_1(W_r,w)$ is the free group on the $r$ standard loops, one traversing each circle summand once. In particular it has rank $r$ ([[def-rank-of-a-finite-rank-free-group]]). For $r=0$, $W_0$ is a point and the basis is empty.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-higher-dimensional-spheres-are-simply-connected",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every natural number $n\\ge2$, the unit sphere $S^n\\subseteq\\mathbb R^{n+1}$ is simply connected ([[def-simply-connected]]).",
          "uses": [
            "5.1",
            "7.1"
