mon point, the quotient is a sphere with its two poles identified.\nTo identify its homotopy type explicitly, let $Z$ be $S^2$ with an interval attached at its two distinct poles. Give $Z$ the finite CW structure in which the interval is a 1-cell and its endpoints are 0-cells, so the interval is a contractible subcomplex. Collapsing it identifies the poles, so [F4] makes $Z$ homotopy equivalent to the sphere-with-poles-identified quotient. The two endpoint maps of the interval into $S^2$ are homotopic along a fixed path; choose this path to be the $e^{it}$ meridian cut at $t=0$. By the attaching-map clause of [F3], $Z$ is homotopy equivalent to the adjunction space with both endpoints attached at one pole, namely $S^2\\vee S^1$, and the new circle is the retained positive meridian.\nFor this wedge with wedge point $w$, choose $q\\in S^2\\setminus\\{w\\}$ and $r\\in S^1\\setminus\\{w\\}$, and set\n$$A=S^1\\cup(S^2\\setminus\\{q\\}),\\qquad B'=(S^1\\setminus\\{r\\})\\cup S^2.$$\nThese are open in the wedge topology and cover $S^1\\vee S^2$. The first deformation retracts to $S^1$, the second to $S^2$, so both are path-connected; their overlap $(S^1\\setminus\\{r\\})\\cup(S^2\\setminus\\{q\\})$ is nonempty and contracts to $w$ by contracting each punctured summand to $w$. Thus the sets and overlap satisfy the path-connected open-cover hypotheses of van Kampen [F5], which gives $\\pi_1(S^1\\vee S^2,w)\\cong\\pi_1(S^1,w)*\\pi_1(S^2,w)\\cong\\mathbb Z*1\\cong\\mathbb Z$, generated by the positive meridian circle. Finally the normal radial homotopy [F2] proves the asserted retraction onto $E_K$ in every case.",
