          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-quotient-group-universal-property",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "A homomorphism that kills a normal subgroup factors uniquely through the quotient group.\n\nIf $N\\mathrel{\\trianglelefteq}G$, $f:G\\to H$ is a homomorphism, and\n$N\\subseteq\\ker f$, then there is a unique homomorphism $\\bar f:G/N\\to H$\nsuch that $\\bar f(gN)=f(g)$ and $f=\\bar f\\circ\\pi$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-commutator-and-commutator-subgroup",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $G$ be a group. For $g,h\\in G$, their **commutator** is\n\n$$[g,h]:=ghg^{-1}h^{-1}.$$\n\nThis convention is fixed throughout; some sources use its inverse. By the\ninverse laws of [[lem-group-inverse-laws]], one has\n$[g,h]^{-1}=hgh^{-1}g^{-1}=[h,g]$.\n\nThe **commutator subgroup**, or **derived subgroup**, is the subgroup generated\nby all commutators:\n\n$$[G,G]:=\\langle\\{[g,h]:g,h\\in G\\}\\rangle.$$\n\nThe generated subgroup notation is that of [[def-generated-subgroup]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-derived-subgroup-is-characteristic-and-abelianization-is-universal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every group $G$, the derived subgroup $G'=[G,G]$ is characteristic, hence normal. The quotient $G^{\\mathrm{ab}}:=G/G'$ is abelian and has the following universal property: for every homomorphism $f:G\\to A$ into an abelian group, there is a unique homomorphism $\\bar f:G^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\to A$ with $f=\\bar f\\circ q$, where $q:G\\to G/G'$ is the quotient map.",
