          "source": "lem-integral-khovanov-frobenius-algebra-defines-a-cobordism-functor",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, with $\\deg1=1$, $\\deg X=-1$, and structure maps $m,\\eta,\\Delta,\\epsilon$ given by $\\eta(1)=1$, $\\epsilon(1)=0$, $\\epsilon(X)=1$, $\\Delta(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1$, and $\\Delta(X)=X\\otimes X$. There is an additive symmetric monoidal functor $F$ from the category of closed Bar-Natan smoothing cobordisms before local relations to finite free graded abelian groups. Here closed means that objects consist of circles, including the empty collection, and there are no vertical side intervals. The target has all integral linear maps, graded by their homogeneous degrees, and its symmetry is the ordinary interchange of tensor factors, without a Koszul sign from quantum degrees.\n\nExplicitly, $F(C)$ is the free group on the labelings of the finite circle set of $C$ by $1,X$, graded by the sum of the label degrees. For a connected surface $\\Sigma$ of genus $g$, with $p$ incoming and $q$ outgoing circles, its map in any temporary ordering of those circles is $F(\\Sigma)=\\Delta_q H^g m_p$, where $H=m\\Delta$, $m_0=\\eta$, $m_1=1_{\\mathcal A}$, $m_p$ is iterated multiplication for $p\\ge2$, $\\Delta_0=\\epsilon$, $\\Delta_1=1_{\\mathcal A}$ and $\\Delta_q$ is iterated comultiplication for $q\\ge2$. For a disconnected surface, take the tensor product over its connected components and route each factor to its actual boundary circle. The empty surface is sent to $1_{\\mathbb Z}$. This construction is independent of all temporary orderings, respects embedded isotopy, stacking and disjoint union, sends the elementary birth, death, merge and split to $\\eta,\\epsilon,m,\\Delta$, and sends a surface to a homogeneous map of degree $\\chi(\\Sigma)$. It respects S, T and four-tube and therefore descends to the closed Bar-Natan quotient category.",
