          "claim": "**A local integral comparison category.** Use oriented blue facets of label $1$ and red facets of label $2$; two blue facets meet a red facet along each seam, with the usual merge orientation. Decorations on blue facets are powers of a dot. One can define their closed evaluation by cutting at the circular seams and applying the two Frobenius algebras $B=\\mathbb Z[X]/(X^2)$, $\\epsilon_B(1)=0$, $\\epsilon_B(X)=1$, $\\Delta_B(1)=1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1$, $\\Delta_B(X)=X\\otimes X$, and $R=\\mathbb Z$ with counit $-1$ and comultiplication $-1\\otimes1$. Contract a seam by $\\epsilon_B(x\\bar y)$, where the positive blue side is selected by right-hand cyclic order and $\\bar X=-X$. Cut-independence is the unit, counit, associativity and Frobenius identities of these displayed algebras. Equivalently, this is the following direct specialization of the two-pigment evaluation. With roots $x_1,x_2$, put $d=x_1-x_2$, $h=x_1+x_2$, $t=-x_1x_2$, and use $B_h=\\mathbb Z[h,t][X]/(X^2-hX-t)$. Its primitive idempotents have counits $\\beta_1=1/d$, $\\beta_2=-1/d$, and conjugation interchanges them. A connected blue facet of genus $g$ with $b$ seam circles contributes $\\beta_i^{1-g-b}$; a red facet contributes $(-1)^{1-g-b}$; the seam contributes $\\beta_i$ for its positive blue pigment. Sum over the two colors, which must be different at each seam. This is a finite tensor contraction in $B_h$ and $R$, hence is integral before any localization. Define states of a closed web as foams from the empty web modulo the common radical of every closing evaluation; define all other morphisms by bending to a closed web and taking that radical. Gluing preserves the radical because every closing after gluing is a closing before gluing. These definitions provide a local category, with no assertion of full faithfulness for a previously defined homology-valued functor.",
