      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Write $B_+,B_-$ for the fixed bridge balls, $D_i^\\pm$ for their spanning disks, and $T_\\pm=P\\cap B_\\pm$. Arrange finite transverse intersections, with $P\\cap S\\cap K=\\varnothing$, and take the knot tube sufficiently small and adapted to these intersections. A product neighborhood of each $D_i^\\pm$ identifies a neighborhood of its bridge with the usual boundary-parallel ball-arc. Cutting $B_\\pm\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ along the disks $D_i^\\pm\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ gives a ball: successively slide each bridge along its displayed disk into a boundary collar, and remove its displayed half-tube; each cut opens that collar tunnel. These moves use disjoint same-side disk neighborhoods. In particular this is a *complete* meridian-disk system, not merely a collection of compressing disks. Throughout the proof the knot and $S$ themselves stay fixed; a puncture of $P$ is allowed to travel along the fixed knot.",
