          "claim": "The remaining move changes a spanning disk, not $P$. Suppose a type-II arc $\\gamma$ cuts off a disk $E_0$ from $T\\setminus N(K)$ with no other intersection arcs in its interior. On its spanning disk, retain the component of the cut along $\\gamma$ containing the bridge, and replace the other component by a nearby copy of $E_0$. The union along $\\gamma$ is a disk: two disks glued along one boundary interval. Its new foot is the old retained foot together with $\\partial E_0\\setminus\\gamma$, pushed a little on $S$. Push its interior off $T$ on the retained disk's side and round the seam. Since $E_0$ has no punctures or other intersection arcs, the replacement misses $K$ except on its original bridge, is disjoint from all other same-side spanning disks, and deletes $\\gamma$ and any intersections on the discarded part without adding any. This move III is valid even when $\\gamma$ is not innermost on its spanning disk. In particular it is a disk replacement, not an unproved assertion that two disks in a knotted region are isotopic.",
