        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-sobolev-spaces-on-compact-riemannian-domain",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary, with no orientability assumption. Fix a finite smooth atlas of interior and boundary charts and a smooth partition of unity subordinate to it. For an integer $k\\geq0$, define $H^k(M)$ as the completion of $C^\\infty(M)$ in the sum of the Euclidean $W^{k,2}$ norms of the localized coordinate representatives; derivatives in a boundary chart are taken on the corresponding half-space. Define $H_0^1(M)$ as the closure of $C_c^\\infty(M^\\circ)$ in $H^1(M)$. Define $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ by the fixed finite boundary atlas and partition of unity, using the Euclidean fractional Slobodeckij space $W^{1/2,2}(\\mathbb R^{n-1})$ for each localized representative. Define $H^{-1/2}(\\partial M)$ as the continuous dual of $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$ with the Riemannian boundary density pairing. These are the fixed-atlas norms used on this page; no atlas-independence assertion is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-rellich-compactness-from-w-one-p-zero-to-lp",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be a\nbounded open set and let $1\\le p<\\infty$. Then $W^{1,p}_0(\\Omega)$ is\ncompactly embedded in $L^p(\\Omega)$: the inclusion is bounded, and every\nsequence bounded in $W^{1,p}_0(\\Omega)$ has a subsequence converging in\n$L^p(\\Omega)$. No regularity of $\\partial\\Omega$ is needed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-lax-milgram",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space, let $a$ be a bounded coercive sesquilinear form on $H$ with constants $M,\\alpha$ ([[def-bounded-coercive-and-symmetric-sesquilinear-forms]]), and let $F:H\\to\\mathbb K$ be a bounded conjugate-linear functional with norm $\\|F\\|=\\sup_{\\|v\\|\\le1}|F(v)|$. Then there is a unique $u\\in H$ with $$a(u,v)=F(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H,$$ and it satisfies $\\alpha\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|$, that is $\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|/\\alpha$. The real bilinear case is the same statement with $a$ symmetric or not, $F$ a bounded linear functional, and the conjugation read as the identity.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$, be a\nbounded $C^1$ domain in the graph sense of\n[[def-bounded-c-k-domain-and-boundary-charts]], let $\\partial\\Omega$ carry the\nchart-independent surface measure of\n[[def-surface-integral-on-a-compact-c-one-hypersurface]] and\n[[lem-surface-integral-is-independent-of-c-one-boundary-charts]], let\n$1\\le p<\\infty$ and $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$. Then there is a\nunique bounded linear operator\n$$T:W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)\\longrightarrow L^p(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$$\nwith $Tu=u|_{\\partial\\Omega}$ for every\n$u\\in C(\\overline\\Omega)\\cap W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$, and it satisfies\n$\\|Tu\\|_{L^p(\\partial\\Omega)}\\le C(\\Omega,p)\\|u\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}$. On each\nboundary chart the operator is the flat half-space trace of\n[[thm-trace-estimate-on-the-half-space]] transported by the flattening\ndiffeomorphism, and the chartwise definitions agree on overlaps; uniqueness\nholds because two bounded operators agreeing on the dense subspace\n$C(\\overline\\Omega)\\cap W^{1,p}(\\Omega)$ are equal.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-bounded-right-inverse-for-the-sobolev-trace",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$, $n\\ge2$, be a\nbounded $C^1$ domain, $1<p<\\infty$, $\\theta=1-1/p$, and let $T$ be the trace\noperator of [[thm-lp-trace-operator-on-a-bounded-c-one-domain]]. Then there is\na bounded linear operator\n$$R:W^{\\theta,p}(\\partial\\Omega;\\mathbb K)\\longrightarrow W^{1,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$$\nwith $T\\circ R=\\mathrm{id}_{W^{\\theta,p}(\\partial\\Omega)}$ and\n$\\|Rg\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}\\le C(\\Omega,p)\\|g\\|_{W^{\\theta,p}(\\partial\\Omega)}$.\nMoreover, for every open neighbourhood $U$ of $\\partial\\Omega$ in\n$\\mathbb R^n$ there is such an operator $R_U$ whose image is contained in the\nclasses vanishing a.e. outside $U$, with\n$\\|R_Ug\\|_{W^{1,p}(\\Omega)}\\le C(\\Omega,p,U)\\|g\\|_{W^{\\theta,p}(\\partial\\Omega)}$.\nThe right inverse is not unique and no canonical choice is claimed.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-higher-order-boundary-regularity-for-dirichlet-problems",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded\n$C^{k+2}$ domain, $n\\ge2$, $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, let\n$k\\ge0$, let $L,a$ be as in\n[[def-uniformly-elliptic-divergence-form-operator]] with\n$a^{ij}\\in W^{k+1,\\infty}(\\Omega)$, $b^i,c\\in W^{k,\\infty}(\\Omega)$ and all\ncoefficient derivatives bounded, and let $f\\in H^k(\\Omega)$. If\n$u\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$ is a weak solution of $Lu=f$ with zero boundary values\n([[def-weak-dirichlet-solution-for-a-divergence-form-operator]]), then\n$u\\in H^{k+2}(\\Omega)$ and\n$$\\|u\\|_{H^{k+2}(\\Omega)}\\le C\\big(\\|f\\|_{H^k(\\Omega)}+\\|u\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\big),$$\nwith $C$ depending only on $n,k,\\Omega$ and the coefficient bounds. The\nderivative gain is exactly two orders; the boundary regularity required at\norder $k$ is $C^{k+2}$, the coefficient regularity one order above the data\norder, and the companion page records the sharp two-derivative example. For\n$k=0$ the theorem is\n[[thm-global-h-two-dirichlet-regularity]].",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "thm-higher-order-sobolev-embedding",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $n\\ge2$, let $\\Omega$ be a bounded $W^{k,p}$-extension domain, $k\\ge1$ and $1\\le p<\\infty$. Let $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ and $u\\in W^{k,p}(\\Omega;\\mathbb K)$. Then:\n\n1. if $kp<n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every $q$ with $\\frac1q\\ge\\frac1p-\\frac kn$ (equivalently $1\\le q\\le\\frac{np}{n-kp}$);\n2. if $kp=n$, $\\|u\\|_{L^q(\\Omega)}\\le C(q)\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$ for every finite $q$;\n3. if $kp>n$, then for every integer $m\\ge0$ and every $0<\\alpha<1$ with $m+\\alpha<k-\\frac np$ there is a representative in $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ with $\\|u\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}\\le C\\|u\\|_{W^{k,p}(\\Omega)}$; when $k-\\frac np\\notin\\mathbb Z$ one may take $m=\\lfloor k-\\frac np\\rfloor$ and $\\alpha=k-\\frac np-m$.\n\nHere $C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)$ uses the continuous derivatives of the constructed representative on an ambient neighbourhood of $\\overline\\Omega$, with norm\n$$\\|g\\|_{C^{m,\\alpha}(\\overline\\Omega)}:=\\sum_{|\\beta|\\le m}\\sup_{\\overline\\Omega}|D^\\beta g|+\\sum_{|\\beta|=m}\\sup_{\\substack{x,y\\in\\overline\\Omega\\\\x\\ne y}}\\frac{|D^\\beta g(x)-D^\\beta g(y)|}{|x-y|^\\alpha}.$$\nFor $\\Omega=\\varnothing$ set these norm values to $0$. The proof supplies such an ambient representative, so no boundary differentiability or regularity of $\\partial\\Omega$ is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-collar-neighborhood-theorem",
