          "claim": "Write $a_{\\gamma_j}(v,w)=\\int_M\\gamma_jg(\\nabla v,\\nabla\\bar w)\\,dV_g$. By [F1], $\\langle\\Lambda_{\\gamma_1}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_{\\gamma_1}(u_1,u_2)$, since $u_2$ is an extension of $f_2$. Let $\\widetilde u_1$ solve the $\\gamma_2$ equation with boundary value $f_1$, which exists by [F2]. Then $\\langle\\Lambda_{\\gamma_2}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_{\\gamma_2}(\\widetilde u_1,u_2)$. Since $u_1-\\widetilde u_1\\in H_0^1(M)$ and $u_2$ solves the $\\gamma_2$ weak equation, Hermitian symmetry gives $a_{\\gamma_2}(u_1-\\widetilde u_1,u_2)=\\overline{a_{\\gamma_2}(u_2,u_1-\\widetilde u_1)}=0$. Hence $a_{\\gamma_2}(\\widetilde u_1,u_2)=a_{\\gamma_2}(u_1,u_2)$. Subtracting the two equalities gives the conductivity identity.",
