    "thm-ip-euclidean-schrodinger-calderon-uniqueness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A2",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-ip-alessandrini-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a compact smooth Riemannian domain with smooth boundary. If $\\gamma_1,\\gamma_2\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ are strictly positive and $u_j$ solves $\\operatorname{div}_g(\\gamma_j\\nabla_gu_j)=0$ with smooth boundary value $f_j$, then\n$$\\langle(\\Lambda_{\\gamma_1}-\\Lambda_{\\gamma_2})f_1,f_2\\rangle_{\\partial M} =\\int_M(\\gamma_1-\\gamma_2)g(\\nabla_gu_1,\\nabla_g\\overline{u_2})\\,dV_g.$$\nIf instead $q_1,q_2\\in C^\\infty(M,\\mathbb R)$ have trivial Dirichlet kernels for $-\\Delta_g+q_j$ and $u_j$ solves $(-\\Delta_g+q_j)u_j=0$ with smooth boundary value $f_j$, then\n$$\\langle(\\Lambda_{q_1}-\\Lambda_{q_2})f_1,f_2\\rangle_{\\partial M} =\\int_M(q_1-q_2)u_1\\overline{u_2}\\,dV_g.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-conductivity-dirichlet-to-neumann-map",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice.\n\nLet $(M,g)$ be a nonempty compact connected smooth Riemannian domain with nonempty smooth boundary. For a real-valued $\\gamma\\in C^\\infty(M)$ with $\\gamma(x)>0$ at every $x\\in M$, let $v_f\\in H^1(M)$ be the unique weak solution of $\\operatorname{div}_g(\\gamma\\nabla_gv_f)=0$ with trace $f\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$, whose existence is supplied by the positive-conductivity solvability lemma. Define the full-data physical conductivity Dirichlet-to-Neumann map $\\Lambda_\\gamma:H^{1/2}(\\partial M)\\to H^{-1/2}(\\partial M)$ by\n$$\\langle\\Lambda_\\gamma f,h\\rangle=\\int_M\\gamma\\,g(\\nabla_gv_f,\\nabla_g\\overline{V_h})\\,dV_g,$$\nwhere $V_h\\in H^1(M)$ is any extension of $h\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$. The weak equation makes this value independent of the extension. For smooth data, $\\Lambda_\\gamma f=\\gamma\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}v_f|_{\\partial M}$; this is the full physical flux convention. When $M=\\overline\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ with the Euclidean metric, this is the scalar Euclidean conductivity DN map.\n\nFor a real $q\\in C^\\infty(M)$ such that the only $w\\in H_0^1(M)$ satisfying $(-\\Delta_g+q)w=0$ weakly is $w=0$, let $u_f\\in H^1(M)$ be the unique weak solution of $(-\\Delta_g+q)u_f=0$ with trace $f$, whose existence follows from the nonresonant regularity lemma. Define the Schrödinger DN map $\\Lambda_q:H^{1/2}(\\partial M)\\to H^{-1/2}(\\partial M)$ by\n$$\\langle\\Lambda_qf,h\\rangle=\\int_M\\big(g(\\nabla_gu_f,\\nabla_g\\overline{V_h})+qu_f\\overline{V_h}\\big)\\,dV_g.$$\nFor smooth data this is $\\partial_{\\nu_{\\rm out}}u_f|_{\\partial M}$. Equality of these operators means equality on all of $H^{1/2}(\\partial M)$, not equality for a restricted set of boundary inputs.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ip-euclidean-cgo-solvability",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $n\\geq3$, let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded smooth domain contained in a ball $B(0,R)$, and let $q\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega,\\mathbb C)$. There are constants $C>0$ and $\\tau_0\\geq1$, depending only on $n$, $R$, and $\\|q\\|_{L^\\infty}$, such that for every $\\zeta\\in\\mathbb C^n$ satisfying $\\zeta\\cdot\\zeta=0$ and $|\\zeta|\\geq\\tau_0$, there is $r_\\zeta\\in H^1(\\Omega,\\mathbb C)$ for which\n$$u_\\zeta(x)=e^{i\\zeta\\cdot x}(1+r_\\zeta(x))$$\nsolves $(-\\Delta+q)u_\\zeta=0$ distributionally in $\\Omega$, and\n$$\\|r_\\zeta\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\leq C|\\zeta|^{-1}\\|q\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)},\\qquad \\|\\nabla r_\\zeta\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\leq C\\|q\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}.$$\nThe dot product in the null-phase condition is complex bilinear, and $|\\zeta|=(\\sum_{j=1}^n|\\zeta_j|^2)^{1/2}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-uniqueness-of-finite-borel-measures-from-their-fourier-transforms",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Finite complex Borel measures $\\mu,\\nu$ on $\\mathbb R^n$ with $\\widehat\\mu=\\widehat\\nu$ are equal. Here finite means finite total variation, and $n\\ge1$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
