      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Set $Q=q_1-q_2$. For exact $H^1$ solutions $u_j$ let $f_j=\\operatorname{tr}u_j\\in H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ and define the Hermitian weak forms $a_j(v,w)=\\int_\\Omega(\\nabla v\\cdot\\nabla\\overline w+q_jv\\overline w)\\,dx$. By the weak DN definition, $\\langle\\Lambda_{q_1}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_1(u_1,u_2)$. Let $\\widetilde u_1\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ be the $q_2$ solution with trace $f_1$, which exists by nonresonant weak solvability. Then $\\langle\\Lambda_{q_2}f_1,f_2\\rangle=a_2(\\widetilde u_1,u_2)$. Since $u_1-\\widetilde u_1\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)$ and $u_2$ solves the $q_2$ weak equation, $a_2(u_1-\\widetilde u_1,u_2)=\\overline{a_2(u_2,u_1-\\widetilde u_1)}=0$, where the conjugation uses real-valuedness of $q_2$. Subtracting the two weak pairings gives $\\langle(\\Lambda_{q_1}-\\Lambda_{q_2})f_1,f_2\\rangle=\\int_\\Omega Q u_1\\overline{u_2}\\,dx$. The maps agree on all of $H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$, so this integral vanishes. This establishes the weak-trace extension of [F1] used here.",
