          "id": "step-2-1",
          "step": "2.1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n$. Since $n\\geq3$, choose orthonormal $\\omega_1,\\omega_2\\in\\mathbb R^n$ perpendicular to $\\xi$; when $\\xi=0$, choose any orthonormal pair. For $\\tau\\geq1$, set $\\sigma_\\tau=(\\tau^2+|\\xi|^2/4)^{1/2}$ and\n$$\\zeta_1=\\tau\\omega_1+i\\sigma_\\tau\\omega_2+\\xi/2,\\qquad \\zeta_2=-\\tau\\omega_1-i\\sigma_\\tau\\omega_2+\\xi/2.$$\nThe orthogonality gives $\\zeta_j\\cdot\\zeta_j=\\tau^2-\\sigma_\\tau^2+|\\xi|^2/4=0$, $\\zeta_1+\\zeta_2=\\xi$, and $|\\zeta_j|\\to\\infty$. Apply [F2] to $q_1$ with phase $\\zeta_1$ and to $q_2$ with phase $-\\overline{\\zeta_2}$; the latter is null, and reality of $q_2$ lets us conjugate the resulting solution. Thus\n$$u_1=e^{i\\zeta_1\\cdot x}(1+r_1),\\qquad u_2=e^{-i\\overline{\\zeta_2}\\cdot x}(1+r_2),\\qquad \\|r_j\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}=O(\\tau^{-1}).$$\nSubstitute in step 1.1. Since $Q\\in L^2(\\Omega)\\cap L^\\infty(\\Omega)$,\n$$\\left|\\int_\\Omega Qe^{i\\xi\\cdot x}\\big(r_1+\\overline{r_2}+r_1\\overline{r_2}\\big)\\,dx\\right| \\leq \\|Q\\|_2(\\|r_1\\|_2+\\|r_2\\|_2)+\\|Q\\|_\\infty\\|r_1\\|_2\\|r_2\\|_2=O(\\tau^{-1}).$$\nTaking $\\tau\\to\\infty$ proves $\\int_\\Omega Qe^{i\\xi\\cdot x}\\,dx=0$ for every $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n$.",
