          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-gaussian-series-norm-tail-and-fourth-moment",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $(\\xi_j)_{j\\ge1}$ be given real random variables on a probability space such that every finite subfamily is independent and each variable has density $(2\\pi)^{-1/2}e^{-z^2/2}$. Let $a_j\\ge0$ be deterministic with $v:=\\sum_j a_j<\\infty$, and put $u:=\\sup_j a_j$ and $w:=\\sum_j a_j^2$. The nonnegative series $Q:=\\sum_j a_j\\xi_j^2$ is finite almost surely. Define $N:=\\sqrt Q$ on that event and set $N=0$ on its null complement. Then\n$$\\mathbb E N^2=v,\\qquad \\mathbb E N^4=v^2+2w\\le3v^2.$$\nFor every $t>0$,\n$$\\mathbb P\\{N^2>v+2\\sqrt{wt}+2ut\\}\\le e^{-t},\\qquad \\mathbb P\\{N>\\sqrt v+\\sqrt{2ut}\\}\\le e^{-t}.$$\nThe upper quantile $q(t):=\\inf\\{z\\ge0:\\mathbb P(N>z)\\le e^{-t}\\}$ is finite and satisfies\n$$\\mathbb P(N>q(t))\\le e^{-t},\\qquad q(t)\\le\\sqrt v+\\sqrt{2ut}.$$\nThese conclusions include $v=0$, in which case $N=q(t)=0$ almost surely. For a Gaussian series in a fixed orthonormal coordinate system with coordinate variances $a_j$, $N$ is its norm.",
