          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X$ and $Y$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces over the same scalar field and let $K:X\\to Y$ be compact. Write $\\kappa:=\\|K\\|$, $S:=K^*K$, and $|K|:=S^{1/2}$, and let $\\Lambda_K$ be zero together with the positive eigenvalues of $|K|$. An admissible spectral index is a bounded function $\\psi:\\Lambda_K\\to[0,\\infty)$ with $\\psi(0)=0$. Define $\\psi(|K|)$ by the compact spectral multiplier of $S$ with scalar function $t\\mapsto\\psi(\\sqrt t)$. For $\\rho>0$ define $X_{\\psi,\\rho}(K):=\\{\\psi(|K|)w:w\\in X,\\ \\|w\\|_X\\le\\rho\\}$. For $\\nu>0$, $\\psi_\\nu(t)=t^\\nu$ defines the power source ball $X_{\\nu,\\rho}(K)$. If $\\kappa>0$ and $q>0$, define the logarithmic spectral-index candidate by $\\psi_{\\log,q}(0)=0$ and $\\psi_{\\log,q}(t)=(1+\\log(\\kappa/t))^{-q}$ for positive $t\\in\\Lambda_K$; when it is bounded, it defines $X_{\\log,q,\\rho}(K)$.",
