          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $T:X\\to Y$ be an injective compact Hilbert–Schmidt operator of infinite rank between separable real Hilbert spaces, let $S=T^*T$, and let $(s_j,v_j,u_j)$ be a fixed positive singular system with $\\lambda_j=s_j^2$. Let $Z$ be a real isonormal Gaussian process on $Y$, meaning that $g\\mapsto Z(g)$ is linear into $L^2$, is centered, has covariance $\\mathbb E[Z(g)Z(h)]=\\langle g,h\\rangle_Y$, and has independent standard-normal values on every finite orthonormal tuple. In the weak observation experiment $Y_x(g)=\\langle Tx,g\\rangle_Y+\\sigma Z(g)$ with known noise standard deviation $\\sigma>0$, use the ordered filter $q_\\alpha$, the geometric-variance candidates $\\alpha_0>\\cdots>\\alpha_{m_{\\max}}$, and variances $v_m=\\sigma^2\\sum_j\\lambda_jq_{\\alpha_m}(\\lambda_j)^2$ from the preceding definition. For each candidate index $m$, the regularized estimator $\\widehat x_m$ is the $L^2$ limit of its finite singular-coordinate sums; its $j$th coordinate is $\\lambda_jq_{\\alpha_m}(\\lambda_j)\\theta_j+\\sigma s_jq_{\\alpha_m}(\\lambda_j)\\xi_j$, where $\\theta_j=\\langle x,v_j\\rangle_X$ and $\\xi_j=Z(u_j)$.\n\nTake as input the observed coordinates $y_j:=Y_x(u_j)$. For each candidate $m$ and truncation index $N$, compute\n$$\n\\widehat x_{m,N}^{\\mathrm{obs}}:=\\sum_{j=1}^N s_jq_{\\alpha_m}(\\lambda_j)y_jv_j.\n$$\nSince $y_j=s_j\\theta_j+\\sigma\\xi_j$, these partial sums have the displayed latent-coordinate coefficients and converge in $L^2(\\Omega;X)$ to $\\widehat x_m$ by the preceding risk-decomposition result. The quantities $v_{m_1,m_2}$ and $z_{m_1,m_2}(x)$ are determined by the known operator, filter, noise level and Gaussian coordinate law.\n\nFor $0\\le m_1<m_2\\le m_{\\max}$, set\n$$\nv_{m_1,m_2}:=\\sigma^2\\sum_{j\\ge1}\\lambda_j\\bigl(q_{\\alpha_{m_2}}(\\lambda_j)-q_{\\alpha_{m_1}}(\\lambda_j)\\bigr)^2\n$$\nand let $N_{m_1,m_2}$ be the norm of the centered estimator difference divided by $\\sigma$. For $x>0$, define its upper quantile by\n$$\nz_{m_1,m_2}(x):=\\inf\\{z\\ge0:\\mathbb P(N_{m_1,m_2}>z)\\le e^{-x}\\}.\n$$\nGiven fixed $\\beta>0$ and $\\gamma>0$, put $x_m:=2(1+\\gamma)\\log(v_{m+1}/v_0)$ and $\\eta_{m_1,m_2}:=\\sigma z_{m_1,m_2}(x_{m_1})+\\beta\\sqrt{v_{m_1,m_2}}$. Accept an index $m_1$ exactly when $\\|\\widehat x_{m_1}-\\widehat x_{m_2}\\|_X\\le\\eta_{m_1,m_2}$ for every $m_2$ with $m_1<m_2\\le m_{\\max}$, and output the smallest accepted index $\\widehat m$ and parameter $\\widehat\\alpha=\\alpha_{\\widehat m}$. The condition for $m_1=m_{\\max}$ is vacuous, so the finite search always returns an index. This rule uses only the observed sequence, the known operator and filter, the noise level, the fixed candidate grid, and $\\beta,\\gamma$; it does not use $x$ or its source index $\\varphi$.",
