      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Put $Q_n=\\sum_{j\\le n}a_j\\xi_j^2$, $v_n=\\sum_{j\\le n}a_j$, and $w_n=\\sum_{j\\le n}a_j^2$. On finite intervals integration by parts with $\\phi'(x)=-x\\phi(x)$ gives $\\int x^{2k}\\phi(x)\\,dx=[-x^{2k-1}\\phi(x)]+(2k-1)\\int x^{2k-2}\\phi(x)\\,dx$. The boundary terms vanish at infinity; the integrals converge since a polynomial times $e^{-x^2/2}$ is bounded by a constant times $e^{-x^2/4}$, whose integral is finite by [F3] and the given unit Gaussian integral. Starting from $\\int\\phi=1$ therefore gives $\\mathbb E\\xi_j^2=1$ and $\\mathbb E\\xi_j^4=3$. Thus $\\mathbb E Q_n=v_n$ and finite independence gives $\\mathbb E Q_n^2=v_n^2+2w_n$. Also $u\\le v$ and $w\\le uv\\le v^2$.",
