          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For every smooth oriented knot $K\\subset S^3$, let $B^4=\\{x\\in\\mathbb R^4:\\lVert x\\rVert^2\\le1\\}$ with boundary $S^3$. The oriented connected sum $K\\#K^\\dagger$ bounds a smooth properly embedded disk $D\\subset B^4$ for which the squared radial function $\\rho(x)=\\lVert x\\rVert^2$ restricts to a Morse function on the interior of $D$ with all critical points of index $0$ or $1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-oriented-knot-connected-sum-is-a-commutative-monoid",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. On oriented ambient-isotopy classes of smooth knots in $S^3$, the connected-sum operation is associative and commutative, and the standard unknot is a two-sided identity. Here “commutative monoid” means a set with an associative binary operation, a two-sided identity, and equality after interchanging the two inputs.",
          "uses": [
