          "source": "lem-finite-von-neumann-module-dimension-and-dense-quotients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $G$ be a discrete group, let $A=\\mathcal N G\\subseteq\\mathcal B(\\ell^2(G))$ have its canonical faithful normal trace $\\tau$, and give matrices the unnormalized trace $\\tau_r((a_{ij}))=\\sum_{i=1}^r\\tau(a_{ii})$. For a projection $p\\in M_r(A)$ put $\\dim_A(pA^r)=\\tau_r(p)$; for any right $A$-module take the supremum of dimensions of its finitely generated projective submodules. These dimensions are independent of the projective representation. If $L\\subseteq pA^r$ is any submodule and $q$ is the projection onto its Hilbert closure in $p\\ell^2(G)^r$, then $q\\in M_r(A)$, $q\\le p$, and\n$$\\dim_A L=\\tau_r(q),\\qquad \\dim_A(pA^r/L)=\\tau_r(p)-\\tau_r(q).$$\nIn particular a Hilbert-dense submodule has dimension-zero quotient. Modules of dimension zero are closed under submodules, quotients, extensions and filtered colimits. For any matrix $B\\in M_{r,s}(A)$, its kernel and Hilbert-closed image are projection modules, its algebraic image is isomorphic to its support projection module, and\n$$\\tau_s(P_{\\ker B})+\\tau_r(P_{\\overline{\\operatorname{im}B}})=s.$$",
          "uses": [
