    "lem-higher-order-blanchfield-pairings-are-nonsingular": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-blanchfield-pairing-and-higher-order-rho-invariant",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $M$ be a closed oriented three-manifold with a specified coefficient homomorphism $\\phi:\\pi_1(M_j)\\to\\Gamma$ on each component, where $\\Gamma$ is PTFA. The integral coefficient ring is $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[\\Gamma]$ with its fraction division ring $K\\Gamma$. More generally let $k$ be $\\mathbb Z$ or a commutative field, let $K$ be the fraction division ring of $k[\\Gamma]$, and let $k[\\Gamma]\\subseteq R\\subseteq K$ be an involution-stable unital intermediate ring. The involution fixes $k$ and sends $g$ to $g^{-1}$; on fractions it is the involutive anti-automorphism extending this formula. Taking $k=\\mathbb Z$ and $R=\\Lambda$ recovers the integral convention.\n\nIf $H_1(M;K)=0$, the coefficient Bockstein, oriented Poincaré duality and evaluation define the Blanchfield linking pairing\n$$B_{M,\\phi,R}:TH_1(M;R)\\times TH_1(M;R)\\longrightarrow K/R.$$\nHere $T$ denotes the submodule of elements with a nonzero right annihilator, the first variable is conjugate-linear, and the second is right-linear. Nonsingularity means that the adjoint\n$$\\overline{TH_1(M;R)}\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Hom}_R(TH_1(M;R),K/R)$$\nis an isomorphism of left $R$-modules, where $r\\cdot\\bar x=\\overline{x\\bar r}$ on the conjugate module. This is equivalent to the conjugate-dual formulation in the right-module convention. For $\\Gamma=\\mathbb Z$ with generator $t$, $R=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$ and $K=\\mathbb Q(t)$ give the classical coefficient convention. Nonsingularity is a separate property of the defined pairing, with its coefficient hypotheses supplied by the nonsingularity theorem.\n\nFor a homomorphism φ:π₁(M)→G and an extension ψ:π₁(W)→G over a compact oriented 4-manifold W with ∂W=M, define the signature defect by ρ(M,φ;W,ψ)=sign_G^(2)(W,ψ)−sign(W). It is an invariant of (M,φ) only when a separate bordism-independence theorem applies.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ptfa-integral-and-field-group-rings-have-compatible-ore-involutions",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Gamma$ be PTFA and let $k$ be either $\\mathbb Z$ or a commutative field. The group ring $A=k[\\Gamma]$ has no zero divisors, satisfies both Ore conditions, and has a fraction division ring $K$ in which every element is both a right and a left fraction of elements of $A$. The involution $\\overline{\\sum c_g g}=\\sum c_g g^{-1}$ extends uniquely to an involutive anti-automorphism of $K$.\n\nEvery intermediate unital ring $A\\subseteq R\\subseteq K$ has no zero divisors, satisfies both Ore conditions, and has this same $K$ as its fraction division ring. If $R$ is stable under the extended involution, that involution restricts to $R$ and descends to its additive quotient $K/R$. None of these assertions requires a choice principle.",
          "uses": [
