          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-integral-half-solutions-strebel-and-l2-signatures",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume full AC, matching the chosen finite twisted-chain and maximal-abelian-cover conventions. Let K be a smooth oriented knot, M(K) its preferred-longitude zero-framed surgery, W a compact connected oriented smooth 4-manifold with boundary M(K), and π=π₁(W). Put π^(0)=π and π^(j+1)=[π^(j),π^(j)]. A right Zπ-chain complex with coefficients in a left Z[π/π^(j)]-module is formed by tensoring over Zπ with that module.\n\nFor n≥0, W is an integral n-solution for K when H₁(M(K);Z)→H₁(W;Z) is an isomorphism, H₂(W;Z) is free of rank 2r for some finite r, and there are classes x₁,…,x_r,y₁,…,y_r in H₂(W;Z[π/π^(n)]) whose equivariant intersection pairings satisfy λ_n(x_i,x_j)=0 and λ_n(x_i,y_j)=δ_ij. It is an integral (n+1/2)-solution when, in addition, each x_i is the image of a class x̃_i in H₂(W;Z[π/π^(n+1)]) and λ_{n+1}(x̃_i,x̃_j)=0 for all i,j. Here λ_j is the equivariant intersection pairing induced by the orientation of W.\n\nFor a commutative ring R with 1≠0, a group G belongs to Strebel's class D(R) if every homomorphism between projective RG-modules is injective whenever its tensor with the trivial G-module R is injective. In this page R is Q or F_p for a prime p. For a countable discrete group G, its group von Neumann algebra N G is the weak-operator closure of CG in the left regular representation on ℓ²(G), with trace τ_G(T)=⟨Tδ_e,δ_e⟩. The von Neumann dimension of a finitely generated projective N G-module represented by a projection E is τ_G(E); for an arbitrary module it is the supremum of the dimensions of its finitely generated projective submodules.\n\nFor a compact oriented 4-manifold W with boundary, sign(W) is the inertia difference of the symmetric intersection form on H₂(W;R) modulo its radical (the subspace pairing to zero with every class), equivalently on the image of H₂(W;R)→H₂(W,∂W;R). Given a closed oriented 3-manifold M, a homomorphism φ:π₁(M)→G, a compact oriented 4-manifold W with ∂W=M, and an extension ψ:π₁(W)→G of φ, the L²-signature sign_G^(2)(W,ψ) is the von Neumann signature of the nonsingular part of the N G-coefficient intersection form. When such a W and extension exist, the signature defect is sign_G^(2)(W,ψ)−sign(W). It is called the rho invariant of (M,φ) only after its independence of the bounding data is established by a separate bordism argument.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-zero-surgery-identifies-exterior-classical-alexander-and-blanchfield-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Supply a finite polygonal oriented disk-band Seifert-surface model and local ambient smooth roundings for a smooth oriented knot $K\\subset S^3$, with compatible closed product tube, positive meridian $\\mu$, preferred longitude $\\lambda$, collars and peripheral paths. Put $X=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$, $T=\\partial X$, and let $M=X\\cup_T V$ be its actual preferred zero-longitude surgery, oriented to extend $X$. Let $k=\\mathbb Z$ or a commutative field, $R=k[t,t^{-1}]$, and let $F$ be its fraction field, so $F=\\mathbb Q(t)$ when $k=\\mathbb Z$. Use the associated based right coefficient systems sending $\\mu$ to $t$ with transport by the inverse unit, and their standard parallel Hermitian form.\n\nThe actual maps induce isomorphisms\n$$i_*:H_1(X;R)\\cong H_1(M;R),\\qquad j_*:H_1(X;R)\\cong H_1(X,T;R).$$\nThe closed Bockstein–duality pairing $B_M$ is defined on the whole first homology and is nonsingular. On $X$ use the following explicit classical cocycle rule: take an integral $R^\\vee$-valued two-cocycle $a$ whose cap class is $\\overline{j_*x}$, take one whole $F^\\vee$-valued one-cochain $b$ with $\\partial^*b=a$, and set $B_X(x,y)=[b(y)]\\in F/R$. Such primitives exist, this rule is independent of all representatives and choices, and\n$$B_X(x,y)=B_M(i_*x,i_*y).$$\nThus it is the nonsingular exterior classical Blanchfield form with the same positive-meridian, positive-push and first-variable conjugate-linear conventions. Based axis classes in the exterior are carried to their actual surgery classes. The constructions commute with the rational and prime coefficient maps wherever the displayed forms are compared; no choice principle, arbitrary knot projection theorem or four-dimensional normal-neighborhood claim is assumed.",
          "uses": [
