    "cex-ptfa-ore-localization-alone-does-not-give-blanchfield-nonsingularity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-cellular-chains-compute-homology-with-local-coefficients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(X,A)$ be a CW pair and $\\mathcal L$ a left $R$-module local system on $X$. The cellular local chain complex computes singular local homology:\n$$H_n\\bigl(C_*^{\\mathrm{cell}}(X,A;\\mathcal L)\\bigr)\\cong H_n(X,A;\\mathcal L).$$\nThe comparison is natural for cellular maps with correctly directed coefficient morphisms. Intrinsically,\n$$C_n^{\\mathrm{cell}}(X,A;\\mathcal L)\\cong H_n(X^n\\cup A,X^{n-1}\\cup A;\\mathcal L).$$\nIf a basepoint and one oriented lift of each cell outside $A$ are supplied on every component, the $n$th group is the direct sum of the corresponding coefficient fibers, and its boundary is the signed $R[\\pi]$ incidence matrix acting through monodromy.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The group $\\Gamma$ is torsion-free abelian, hence PTFA with its one-step series. The ring $\\Lambda$ is a commutative domain: after multiplying nonzero Laurent polynomials by monomials, lexicographically leading nonzero terms of ordinary polynomials have nonzero product over $\\mathbb Z$. Its fraction field is $K$. Give each circle its one-vertex, one-edge cell structure, use their eight product cells on $T^3$, and lift them to the oriented coordinate cells of $\\mathbb R^3$. Opposite faces differ by $x,y,1$, respectively. Thus [F1] identifies the coefficient complex with the exterior algebra on $e_1,e_2,e_3$, with differential the graded derivation $d(e_1)=a$, $d(e_2)=b$, $d(e_3)=0$. In particular $d_1(u,v,w)=au+bv$ and $d_2(p,q,r)=(-bp,ap,aq+br)$ in the ordered bases $(e_1,e_2,e_3)$ and $(e_1\\wedge e_2,e_1\\wedge e_3,e_2\\wedge e_3)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Evaluation at $x=1$ identifies $\\Lambda/(a)$ with the domain $\\mathbb Z[y^{\\pm1}]$; its kernel is exactly $(a)$ by multiplying by a power of $x$ and applying the ordinary identity $x^j-1=(x-1)(1+\\cdots+x^{j-1})$. If $au+bv=0$, reduction modulo $a$ gives $b\\bar v=0$, whence $v=at$ and $u=-bt$. Therefore every degree-one cycle has a unique expression $(-bt,at,w)$. The image of $d_2$ consists precisely of those expressions with arbitrary $t=p$ and $w\\in I$. Consequently $H_1(M;\\Lambda)\\cong\\Lambda/I\\cong\\mathbb Z$, generated by the third circle. It is nonzero, and all of it is $\\Lambda$-torsion because $a\\ne0$ annihilates it.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Over $K$, multiplication by $a$ is invertible. The operator $h(\\omega)=a^{-1}e_1\\wedge\\omega$ satisfies $dh+hd=1$ by the displayed graded-derivation differential. Hence the entire coefficient chain complex over $K$ is contractible and in particular $H_1(M;K)=0$. This verifies the vanishing assumption of the refuted statement without any finiteness or rank inference.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Any $\\Lambda$-linear map $\\Lambda/I\\to K/\\Lambda$ is determined by the image $q+\\Lambda$ of $1+I$, where $aq,bq\\in\\Lambda$. Set $r=aq$ and $s=bq$. Then $br=as$. Reducing this identity modulo $a$ and using the domain $\\Lambda/(a)$ from step 2.1 gives $r=at$ for some $t\\in\\Lambda$. Since $a\\ne0$ in the field $K$, the identity $aq=at$ forces $q=t\\in\\Lambda$. Thus the image of $1+I$ is zero, and $\\operatorname{Hom}_{\\Lambda}(\\Lambda/I,K/\\Lambda)=0$. The conjugate dual is also zero; the involution $x\\mapsto x^{-1},y\\mapsto y^{-1}$ preserves $I$ because $x^{-1}-1=-x^{-1}a$ and $y^{-1}-1=-y^{-1}b$.",
