        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For the fraction clause, send $as^{-1}\\in F$, with $a,s\\in A$ and $s\\ne0$, to the same expression in $K$. If also $as^{-1}=bt^{-1}$ in $F$, choose a nonzero common right multiple $su=tv=w$ in $A$. Multiplying on the right by $w$ gives $au=bv$ in $A$, and the same identity in $K$ proves equality there. Thus the map is well-defined. At a common denominator the addition formula is $(au+bv)w^{-1}$ in both division rings. For a product $as^{-1}bt^{-1}$ with $b\\ne0$, choose $bv=su$ nonzero in $A$. Then $s^{-1}b=uv^{-1}$ and the product is $au(tv)^{-1}$ in both rings; the case $b=0$ is immediate. Hence the map preserves multiplication, addition and unit. Its kernel is zero, since $as^{-1}=0$ implies $a=0$ in $K$ and hence in $A$. Uniqueness follows because a ring map extending $A$ must send the inverse of each nonzero $s\\in A$ to its inverse in $K$.",
