          "claim": "Put $A=k[G]$ and let $f:P\\to Q$ be as in the Statement. The canonical free module $F_P=A^{(P)}$ on the underlying set of $P$ has a surjection $\\pi_P:F_P\\to P$ sending $e_p$ to $p$. Projectivity gives one section $s_P$, and similarly gives $\\pi_Q:F_Q\\to Q$ and a section $s_Q$. Set $e=s_P\\pi_P$, so $e^2=e$. Define a map between actual free modules $$g:F_P\\longrightarrow F_Q\\oplus F_P,\\qquad g(x)=(s_Qf\\pi_Px,(1-e)x).$$ If an augmented vector $\\xi$ lies in $\\ker(g\\otimes k)$, its second coordinate says $\\xi=(s_P\\otimes k)(\\pi_P\\otimes k)\\xi$. Since $s_Q\\otimes k$ has left inverse $\\pi_Q\\otimes k$, its first coordinate implies $(f\\otimes k)(\\pi_P\\otimes k)\\xi=0$. The assumed injectivity yields $(\\pi_P\\otimes k)\\xi=0$, and hence $\\xi=0$. Thus $g\\otimes k$ is injective, so $g$ is injective by step 3.1. For $p\\in\\ker f$, $g(s_Pp)=0$; therefore $s_Pp=0$ and $p=0$. The two sections are supplied by the two individual projectivity assertions, not by a simultaneous lift selection. This proves the full right-projective assertion.",
