        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For either pair $(A,F)$ or $(B,K)$, the coefficient Bockstein $\\beta:H^1(\\operatorname{Hom}(C,F/A))\\to H^2(\\operatorname{Hom}(C,A))$ is an isomorphism, with the analogous notation for $(B,K)$. Indeed degree-two cohomology with values in the division ring is zero by closed duality and the proved first-homology vanishing. Its degree-one cohomology is zero by the one-vertex model: over the division ring $d_1$ has image zero or the whole degree-zero term. In the first case every degree-one cycle is a boundary; in the second split $d_1$ using one preimage of $1$, and a dual cocycle is a coboundary because it vanishes on $\\ker d_1=\\operatorname{im}d_2$. The exact coefficient segment in [F3] then proves $\\beta$ is an isomorphism. Define $B_A$ by inverse duality, then $\\beta_A^{-1}$, then evaluation, and define $B_B$ by the same procedure. This requires no inverse evaluation map, and hence asserts no unjustified general nonsingularity.",
