          "claim": "For a boundary component write $\\beta_i^j,b_i^j$ similarly. If its restricted homomorphism is trivial, step 1.1 gives $b_i^j=\\beta_i^j$. Otherwise step 2.1 and [F2], applied to that component's projective model, give $b_0^j=0$ and $b_1^j\\le\\beta_1^j-1=0$. Thus $b_1^j=0$, while $\\beta_0^j=\\beta_1^j=1$. In both cases $$\\beta_1^j-b_1^j=\\beta_0^j-b_0^j.$$ Summing over the finitely many components gives the identical equality for the total boundary Betti numbers $\\beta_i(M),b_i(M)$. It also holds for empty boundary, with all four numbers zero.",
