          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "On a selected connected component $N_j$, closed duality gives $H^2(N_j;K^\\vee)=0$ from $H_1(N_j;K)=0$. Also $H^1(N_j;K^\\vee)=0$. To see this without a universal-coefficient theorem, use the one-vertex model over $K$ from [F3], so $C_0=K$. Its $d_1$ image is either zero or $K$. In the first case $H_1=0$ says $C_1=\\operatorname{im}d_2$, so every one-cocycle vanishes. In the second case one vector $v$ with $d_1v=1$ splits $C_1=\\ker d_1\\oplus vK$; a one-cocycle vanishes on $\\ker d_1=\\operatorname{im}d_2$, so equals a zero-cochain composed with $d_1$. The dual identification in [F2] preserves these assertions. Exactness [F3] therefore makes the degree-one coefficient Bockstein an isomorphism. Consequently the restriction of $[\\nu]$ in step 2.1 is exactly the inverse-Bockstein class defining the linking form of $x|_{N_j}$. This applies to the finite component list separately.",
