        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The finite inertia calculations used below require no spectral theorem for an infinite-dimensional space. A nonzero Hermitian form on $\\mathbb C^n$ has a vector with nonzero real value: if its diagonal values on the coordinate vectors all vanish, a nonzero off-diagonal entry is detected by a sum $e_i+e_j$ or $e_i+i e_j$. Split off the line of such a vector by subtracting its orthogonal projection, and repeat in the orthogonal complement. Finite induction gives a diagonal form with positive, negative and zero entries. If there are $p$ positive entries, the positive coordinate subspace has dimension $p$, and any subspace of dimension greater than $p$ meets the span of the nonpositive coordinates, so cannot be positive definite. Thus $p$ is the maximal positive dimension; the same argument gives the maximal negative dimension. Congruence preserves these maxima by an invertible change of coordinates, and the diagonal description proves additivity under block sum and sign reversal under negation. For a Hermitian $2\\times2$ matrix, its real eigenvalues are $(\\operatorname{tr}C\\pm\\sqrt{(C_{11}-C_{22})^2+4|C_{12}|^2})/2$; their product is its determinant. Hence the trace/determinant tests used below follow explicitly. Every splitting is within a fixed finite-dimensional space.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For integers $b>0$ and $a$, put $w=a-b$, $A=\\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, $B=\\begin{pmatrix}1&w\\\\w&w^2+b\\end{pmatrix}$ and $V(a,b)=\\begin{pmatrix}A&I_2\\\\0&B\\end{pmatrix}$. Both diagonal blocks are integral and symmetric, so $V(a,b)-V(a,b)^T=\\begin{pmatrix}0&I_2\\\\-I_2&0\\end{pmatrix}$ has determinant one. Also $B$ is positive definite: its real quadratic form is $(x+wy)^2+b y^2$, and the same expression with absolute squares works over $\\mathbb C$. Its determinant is $b$ and its trace is positive. All these assertions follow directly from the displayed matrices.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $0<\\theta\\le\\pi$ write $c=1-e^{i\\theta}$ and $h=2-2\\cos\\theta>0$, so $|c|^2=h$. The Hermitian matrix of $V(a,b)$ is $\\begin{pmatrix}hA&cI_2\\\\\\bar cI_2&hB\\end{pmatrix}$. Subtracting the appropriate multiple of the lower block from the upper block, and the conjugate multiple of its column, is an invertible block congruence to $(hA-B^{-1})\\oplus hB$. Its second block contributes signature two. Put $C=hA-B^{-1}$. Since $\\operatorname{tr}A=0$ and $B^{-1}$ is positive definite, $\\operatorname{tr}C=-\\operatorname{tr}B^{-1}<0$. Direct determinant expansion, using $\\operatorname{tr}(BA)=2w$ and $\\det(BA)=-b$, gives $$\\det C=b^{-1}\\det(hBA-I_2)=b^{-1}(1-2wh-bh^2).$$ The polynomial has exactly one positive root $$s(a,b)=\\frac{-w+\\sqrt{w^2+b}}b.$$ For $0<h<s(a,b)$ its determinant is positive and its trace negative, so its two real eigenvalues are negative. At $h=s(a,b)$ it has one negative and one zero eigenvalue. For $h>s(a,b)$ its determinant is negative, so it has one positive and one negative eigenvalue. The signature of $H_\\theta(V(a,b))$ is consequently $0,1,2$ in those three cases, respectively. At $\\theta=0$ the Hermitian matrix is zero and its signature is zero.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The positive thresholds $s(a,b)$ are dense in $(0,4)$. Indeed fix $r\\in(-1,1)$ and set $a_b=\\lfloor br\\rfloor$ for positive integers $b$. For all sufficiently large $b$, $a_b/b<1$; put $d_b=1-a_b/b>0$. Then $$s(a_b,b)=d_b+\\sqrt{d_b^2+1/b},\\qquad |s(a_b,b)-2(1-r)|\\le2/b+1/\\sqrt b.$$ Here $|a_b/b-r|\\le1/b$ and $0\\le\\sqrt{d_b^2+1/b}-d_b\\le1/\\sqrt b$, which prove the bound. For any desired positive tolerance a sufficiently large integer makes its right side smaller than that tolerance. Given any target in $(0,4)$, take $r$ to be one minus half the target; the explicit floor formula then supplies thresholds arbitrarily close to it. This is a sequence defined by a formula, without selecting any infinite family of witnesses.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
