          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The function $h(\\theta)=2-2\\cos\\theta$ is continuous and strictly increasing from $0$ to $4$ on $[0,\\pi]$: its derivative is $2\\sin\\theta>0$ in the interior. Choose two real angles $\\alpha<\\theta_0<\\beta$ in $I$ and small disjoint open intervals around them whose closures are contained respectively in $I\\cap(0,\\theta_0)$ and $I\\cap(\\theta_0,\\pi)$. Their $h$ images are nonempty intervals in $(0,4)$. Step 3.1 supplies a pair $(a_-,b_-)$ with threshold in the first image and a pair $(a_+,b_+)$ with threshold in the second. If desired these finite witnesses can be specified by enumerating integer pairs by increasing $|a|+b$ and then increasing $a$ and taking the first pair whose explicit threshold is in the relevant interval. Thus the corresponding unique crossing angles satisfy $\\theta_-<\\theta_0<\\theta_+$ and lie in $I$.",
          "step": "4.1",
