          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "We first describe the finite linking calculation used in the construction. For disjoint oriented polygonal circles with a specified regular plane projection, the linking number is half the signed mixed-crossing sum. This can be proved with finite chains as follows. For the second curve $D$, extend each projected edge vertically downward from its actual height to a plane lying below both curves, and fill its resulting planar projected cycle by the finitely many oriented cone triangles from a plane point. The resulting finite two-chain has boundary $D$. Its intersections with the first curve $C$ occur exactly at the mixed crossings where $C$ is below $D$, with the usual oriented crossing signs. It therefore computes linking as the sum of that subset of signs. The full mixed sum is twice this sum: the difference between the sums where $C$ is below and above $D$ is the algebraic plane intersection of the two projected cycles, which is zero. To check the latter directly, fill one projected cycle by its finite planar cone chain and intersect the other with that chain; the signed endpoints of the resulting finite one-chain cancel. Independence of a finite spanning chain is equally finite: the difference of two such chains is a two-cycle in $\\mathbb R^3$, bounded by its cone three-chain. Intersecting that three-chain with the closed curve gives a finite one-chain whose signed boundary sum is zero, so the two intersection numbers agree. Finite subdivisions and perturbations needed to avoid vertices or nontransverse affine intersections avoid only finitely many bad affine conditions; a rational point outside their union exists, for example by trying rational points in a fixed enumeration. Thus no transversality selection on an infinite family is involved. The same computation applies after small local smoothing or to parallel pushed curves, because these changes take place in disjoint boxes without introducing intersections between the two curves.",
