          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "We prove $P^iH=q(P^iG)$ through index $r$. It holds at zero. Suppose it holds at $i<r$ and put $A=P^iG$. The restriction $q:A\\to P^iH$ is onto with kernel $N$. Moreover $$N\\subseteq G^{(r)}\\subseteq G^{(i+1)}=[G^{(i)},G^{(i)}]\\subseteq[A,A].$$ The abelianization of $A/N$ is $A/[A,A]$: expanding commutators shows $[A/N,A/N]=[A,A]/N$, since every finite commutator word in the quotient lifts by finitely many individual lifts. Thus the induced abelianization map is an isomorphism. Tensoring this isomorphism with $R_i$ is an isomorphism too, because tensoring its inverse gives an inverse on every generator $a\\otimes u$; exactness of tensor is not being assumed. The two maps to these isomorphic tensor groups commute with $q$. Their kernels consequently satisfy $P^{i+1}H=q(P^{i+1}G)$: a fixed element of the former has a lift in $A$, and its tensor value is zero by the isomorphism, hence that lift is in $P^{i+1}G$. This proves the finite induction.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The kernel of each restricted $q:P^iG\\to P^iH$ is $N$, already contained in $P^iG$ by step 1.1, proving the first displayed isomorphism. The preimage of $P^iH=q(P^iG)$ is $P^iG$, since it already contains $N$; this proves the second. Similarly the preimage in $P^iG$ of $P^{i+1}H$ is $P^{i+1}G$, giving the successive-quotient isomorphisms. For ordinary derived subgroups, expanding commutators and lifting each finite commutator word proves inductively $q(G^{(i)})=H^{(i)}$. Since $N\\subseteq G^{(r)}$, the same preimage argument gives $G/G^{(r)}\\cong H/H^{(r)}$. None of these conclusions follows merely from characteristicness: their actual kernels and preimages have been identified.",
