          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Signed intersection with the properly trimmed Seifert surface defines a homomorphism $H_1(X_K;\\mathbb Z)\\to\\mathbb Z$ taking $\\mu$ to one. In this finite model its chain-level justification is explicit. Undo the supplied finite local roundings, work with its finite polygonal faces, and move each fixed finite loop or chain into general position with these faces by finite subdivision and small piecewise-linear perturbations. Each perturbation excludes only finitely many improper affine incidences, so finite rational choices suffice. These operations preserve the homology class by the straight simplex prisms in sufficiently small convex coordinate boxes. For a two-chain in the interior of $X_K$, its transverse intersection with the oriented surface is a finite oriented one-chain. Its boundary is the intersection with the two-chain boundary: no boundary term comes from the surface boundary, which lies on $T$ outside that interior. The signed sum of this one-chain's endpoints is zero, so intersection counts agree on homologous one-cycles. Boundary collars move any fixed finite chain into the interior before this calculation. The meridian meets the surface once with positive sign by the supplied peripheral orientation. Thus step 1.1 makes this intersection homomorphism an isomorphism. All loops in a small positive parallel copy $F^+$ of the trimmed surface miss the original surface, so their images in $H_1(X_K)$ are zero. The parallel copy and its boundary longitude are supplied by the finite oriented ribbon charts and tube.",
