          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Supply a finite polygonal disk-band oriented Seifert surface, its local ambient smooth roundings, and the resulting smooth oriented knot $K=\\partial F\\subset S^3$. Supply a compatible closed product tube $N(K)$, a positive meridian $\\mu$, a preferred zero longitude $\\lambda$ obtained by trimming and paralleling $F$, and peripheral base paths. Put $X_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and let $M(K)$ be its zero-longitude filling. Then\n$$H_1(X_K;\\mathbb Z)=\\mathbb Z[\\mu],\\qquad \\lambda\\in\\pi_1(X_K)^{(2)}.$$\nBoth $\\pi_1(X_K)$ and $\\pi_1(M(K))$ are normally generated by their marked meridian images. These conclusions use no choice principle. The finite model and tubes are part of the input; no existence theorem for a neighborhood of an arbitrary locally flat surface is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-killing-deep-derived-normal-subgroups-preserves-mixed-derived-quotients",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $r\\ge0$, let $G$ be a group, and let $N\\triangleleft G$ satisfy $N\\subseteq G^{(r)}$, where $G^{(0)}=G$ and $G^{(i+1)}=[G^{(i)},G^{(i)}]$. Put $H=G/N$ and let $q:G\\to H$ be the quotient. Supply a finite list of commutative unital rings $R_0,\\ldots,R_{r-1}$. For a group $L$ define, through index $r$,\n$$P^0L=L,\\qquad P^{i+1}L=\\ker\\!\\left(P^iL\\longrightarrow (P^iL/[P^iL,P^iL])\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}R_i\\right).$$\nThe tensor map sends an abelianized class $a$ to $a\\otimes1$. Then for $0\\le i\\le r$, $N\\subseteq P^iG$ and the restriction of $q$ induces\n$$P^iG/N\\cong P^iH,\\qquad G/P^iG\\cong H/P^iH.$$\nFor $0\\le i<r$ it also induces\n$$P^iG/P^{i+1}G\\cong P^iH/P^{i+1}H.$$\nThese are the actual natural quotient maps. Every $P^iL$ is characteristic in $L$ and contains $L^{(i)}$. The same $q$ induces $G/G^{(r)}\\cong H/H^{(r)}$. If all $R_i=\\mathbb Q$, then $G/P^rG$ is PTFA. A final choice $R_i=\\mathbb F_p$ is allowed in the quotient conclusions, but no PTFA assertion is made for such a series. No choice axiom is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
