          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The supplied contractible basis consists of path-connected open sets, so connectedness implies path-connectedness: path components are open and their complements are open. Form the set of paths $\\gamma:o\\to x$, identifying two with the same endpoint when the loop $\\gamma*(\\gamma')^{-1}$ belongs to $N$. The subgroup laws make this an equivalence relation. For a member $U$ of the supplied contractible basis and a path $\\gamma$ ending in $U$, let $U_{[\\gamma]}$ consist of $[\\gamma*\\eta]$ for paths $\\eta$ lying in $U$ and starting at that endpoint. The projection to $U$ is bijective because any two such paths with the same endpoint are homotopic rel endpoints in $U$. If two such sets over $U$ meet they coincide; for two different basis neighborhoods, a smaller basis neighborhood of a common endpoint shows their intersections contain a basic set of this same form. They therefore define a topology for which $p^{-1}(U)$ is a disjoint union of open sets each homeomorphic to $U$. Surjectivity uses a path to each individual endpoint, rather than selecting all such paths. Thus this is an actual covering space constructed without a representative section.",
