          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Another finite path family changes each loop term by $d_{e(0)}-d_{e(1)}$ in the abelianization. After tensoring, its total coefficient at a vertex is that of the cycle boundary, hence zero. Thus $T_\\gamma(z)$ has one uniquely defined value $T(z)$, independent of paths or enlargement of the finite endpoint list. On a singular triangle the loops for edges $01,12,02$ satisfy $L_{01}L_{12}=L_{02}$ because the triangle fills their concatenation, so $T$ kills its signed boundary. For any finite two-chain use the finite union of its vertex images to get the same conclusion termwise, with its $k$ coefficients. Thus $T$ descends to homology. The relations in step 1.2 say $\\gamma_{e(0)}*e*\\gamma_{e(1)}^{-1}$ is homologous to $\\gamma_{e(0)}+e-\\gamma_{e(1)}$; in the weighted sum the extra path terms cancel by the cycle condition. Hence $ST=\\mathrm{id}$. On a based loop use the constant connector at its endpoint, giving $TS=\\mathrm{id}$ on every generator $[u]_{\\mathrm{ab}}\\otimes c$. This proves the natural isomorphism $H_1(Y;k)\\cong\\pi_1(Y)_{\\mathrm{ab}}\\otimes k$. Naturality follows by mapping a finite connector family forward; independence then removes that family from the map. No paths to all points of $Y$ and no basis of an infinite vector space were chosen.",
