    "thm-compact-manifolds-have-finite-presentation-complexes-without-choice": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-compact-manifolds-are-retracts-of-finite-polyhedra-without-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Every compact Hausdorff topological manifold $M$, with boundary allowed, admits an embedding $e:M\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb R^N$, an open neighborhood $U$ of $e(M)$ and a retraction $r:U\\to e(M)$. Moreover there is a finite Euclidean cubical complex $L$ with $e(M)\\subseteq L\\subseteq U$, so that $r|_L$ makes $M$ an actual retract of a finite CW complex. No choice principle, smooth or PL structure, triangulation of $M$, or general locally contractible-space retraction theorem is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=U\\cup V$, where $U$ and $V$ are open path-connected subsets of $X$, let $U\\cap V$ be path-connected, and fix $x_0\\in U\\cap V$. For the inclusion-induced maps in the diagram\n\n$$\\pi_1(U,x_0)\\longleftarrow\\pi_1(U\\cap V,x_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(V,x_0),$$\n\nthe group $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$, together with the two inclusion-induced homomorphisms, is a pushout ([[def-pushout-of-group-homomorphisms]]). Equivalently, the canonical homomorphism from any pushout $P$ of the displayed diagram to $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ is an isomorphism. No injectivity of either map from the overlap group is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-normal-cover-local-homology-detects-mixed-derived-kernels-without-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $W$ be a connected Hausdorff space with a supplied basis of contractible open neighborhoods, $o\\in W$, $\\pi=\\pi_1(W,o)$, and $N\\triangleleft\\pi$. Put $G=\\pi/N$ and let $k$ be any commutative unital ring. Use first-loop-first concatenation in $\\pi$. There is a canonical based normal path-class cover $p:W_N\\to W$ with $\\pi_1(W_N)=N$, whose right deck action is\n$$[\\gamma]\\cdot g=[a^{-1}*\\gamma]\\quad\\text{when }a\\in\\pi\\text{ represents }g.$$\nLet $\\mathcal R_x$ be the free $k$-module on $p^{-1}(x)$, with its induced right $k[G]$ action and transport along lifted paths. Each fiber is free of rank one as a right $k[G]$-module. Its base fiber is identified with $k[G]$ by the constant-path lift. Thus transport around a based loop $a$ is left multiplication by its quotient image $g^{-1}$.\n\nFor this specified right local system, the actual singular chain complex is naturally isomorphic to $C_*(W_N;k)$ as a complex of right $k[G]$-modules, and there is a natural isomorphism\n$$H_1(W;\\mathcal R)\\cong (N/[N,N])\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}k.$$\nThe right action on the last group is $[u]\\otimes c\\mapsto[a^{-1}ua]\\otimes c$ for $g=[a]\\in G$. A based loop $u\\in N$ with coefficient $1$ corresponds to $[u]\\otimes1$. In particular it represents zero in $H_1(W;\\mathcal R)$ exactly when it lies in the kernel of $N\\to(N/[N,N])\\otimes_{\\mathbb Z}k$. Taking $N=P^s\\pi$ gives exactly the next mixed-series kernel $P^{s+1}\\pi$ with coefficient ring $k$. For a based continuous map carrying $N$ into a normal subgroup $N\\prime$ of the target fundamental group, these identifications commute with its induced homology maps and quotient-group coefficient maps. This includes every connected Hausdorff manifold, with boundary allowed, and every connected finite polyhedron. No finite CW structure, compactness, finite generation of $\\pi$, or choice principle is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Use [F1] and retain the component of $L$ containing connected $i(M)$; it is still finite and still retracts onto $M$. Triangulate the finite polyhedron by its given finite geometric structure, choose a spanning tree of its finite one-skeleton, and take one generator for each oriented edge outside the tree. Each two-simplex gives its finite boundary-word relation. These give a finite presentation of $\\pi_1(L,i(o))$. Here is the finite van Kampen verification: a tree contracts by successively deleting leaf edges, so the one-skeleton has the free group on the edges outside it. Attaching a two-cell adds the normal relation given by its boundary, by [F2] applied to the cell with a thin based connector strip; the overlap retracts to its boundary circle, and the cell side contracts. Attaching a cell of dimension at least three adds no relation or generator because the same overlap is its simply connected boundary sphere. A sphere of dimension at least two is simply connected by [F2] on its two punctured-coordinate charts: they are Euclidean spaces and contract, and their overlap is path-connected. This verifies the presentation through the finite list of cells. A path from $i(o)$ to a chosen tree vertex changes basepoint and only conjugates these finitely many loops.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
