        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Put $P=\\pi_1(L,i(o))=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_a\\mid r_1,\\ldots,r_b\\rangle$. The based endomorphism $e=i_*r_*$ is idempotent and has image $i_*\\pi_1(M,o)$, with $i_*$ injective because $r_*i_*=\\mathrm{id}$. Choose a finite word $w_j$ representing $e(x_j)$ for each of the finitely many generators. Consider $$Q=\\langle x_1,\\ldots,x_a\\mid r_1,\\ldots,r_b,\\ x_1w_1^{-1},\\ldots,x_aw_a^{-1}\\rangle.$$ The map $e:P\\to\\operatorname{im}e$ kills these additional relations because $e^2=e$, so descends to $Q$. Conversely include $\\operatorname{im}e$ in $P$ and pass to $Q$. This gives inverse maps: on $e(x_j)$ the first composite is $e^2(x_j)=e(x_j)$; on the generating $x_j$ of $Q$ the second is $w_j=x_j$ by its new relation. Hence $Q\\cong\\operatorname{im}e\\cong\\pi_1(M,o)$, proving finite presentability by an explicit finite quotient, rather than assuming retracts preserve finite presentation.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Realize the finite presentation $Q$ by a bouquet of its generator circles and one two-cell per relator; the same [F2] calculation gives fundamental group $Q$. Map each generator circle to its based loop in $M$ under the isomorphism in step 2.1. Each relator has trivial based class, so has a based nullhomotopy; choose one for each of the finitely many relators and extend over its two-cell. This gives a based map inducing exactly that isomorphism. For each specified $\\gamma_h$, choose one finite word $v_h$ in these generators representing its based class, add a new generator $y_h$ and the relation $y_hv_h^{-1}$, and map $y_h$ exactly by $\\gamma_h$. A nullhomotopy of its relation extends this map. The added generator can be eliminated from the presentation, so the induced fundamental-group map is still the same isomorphism. This particular presentation complex has a finite polyhedral model. Triangulate every generator circle by three edges and read each nonempty relator on a subdivided boundary circle, so each boundary edge maps homeomorphically to one graph edge. Insert its mapping cylinder before attaching the disk. Each cylinder rectangle has distinct bottom endpoints in the graph and distinct private top endpoints; triangulate it by a fresh center and its four boundary edges. The private center distinguishes different rectangles even when their bottom edges coincide, and neighboring vertical sides agree, so these are finitely many honest simplices. Cone each top circle to a fresh vertex to attach its disk. The cylinder plus disk is just a disk with an extra annular collar, so this changes no underlying presentation space or graph marking. Empty relators may be omitted without affecting the group; with no generators the point suffices until marked generators are added. Thus $K$ has a finite simplicial triangulation of its underlying space, and its contractible open-star basis, while retaining its original one-vertex CW description. These operations produce the asserted finite two-complex with its actual marked loops.",
