          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F1], and pull the given coefficient system to $K$. The induced fundamental-group map is an isomorphism and matches $\\mu$, so [F2] identifies the actual $H_1$ of $K$ with that of $M$ for $\\Lambda$ and every $k[t,t^{-1}]$. Use [F3] on this actual finite two-complex. Its chain groups are finite free, with $C_0=\\Lambda$, a basis of one-cells including $e_\\mu$, and a finite basis of two-cells. Cell lifts and orientations require only finitely many choices. Their incidence coefficients reduce under every constant-ring map, so coefficient change on these finite cellular chains is literally coefficientwise change in their Laurent matrices. In particular augmentation $t=1$ gives exactly the ordinary integral cellular complex of $K$.",
