          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Suppose $x\\in T_\\Delta$ maps to zero in the rational quotient. It has a representative $r\\in\\Lambda_{\\mathbb Q}$, and $\\Delta r\\in\\Lambda$. Let $m\\ge1$ be the least positive integer clearing the finitely many denominators of $r$ and put $f=mr\\in\\Lambda$. If $m>1$, take a prime divisor $p$ of $m$; the least divisor greater than one is prime, so this requires no choice principle. Not every coefficient of $f$ is divisible by $p$, since otherwise $m/p$ would also clear the denominators of $r$, contrary to minimality. Primitivity likewise says $\\bar\\Delta\\ne0$ modulo $p$. Thus $\\bar\\Delta\\bar f\\ne0$ in the field Laurent polynomial ring. But $\\Delta f=m(\\Delta r)$ has all coefficients divisible by $p$, a contradiction. Hence $m=1$, so $r\\in\\Lambda$ and $x=0$. This proves the asserted injection.",
