            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-primitive-laurent-annihilators-preserve-nonzero-values-under-rational-and-prime-reduction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Put $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[t,t^{-1}]$ and $\\Lambda_{\\mathbb Q}=\\mathbb Q[t,t^{-1}]$. Let $\\Delta\\in\\Lambda$ be nonzero and primitive, meaning that the greatest common divisor of its finitely many integer coefficients is one. Define\n$$T_\\Delta=\\{x\\in\\mathbb Q(t)/\\Lambda:\\Delta x=0\\}.$$\nThe natural map $\\mathbb Q(t)/\\Lambda\\to\\mathbb Q(t)/\\Lambda_{\\mathbb Q}$ is injective on $T_\\Delta$.\n\nFor a prime $p$ such that $\\bar\\Delta\\ne0$ in $\\Lambda_p=\\mathbb F_p[t,t^{-1}]$, there is a well-defined additive reduction map\n$$\\rho_{p,\\Delta}:T_\\Delta\\longrightarrow\\mathbb F_p(t)/\\Lambda_p,\\qquad [a/\\Delta]\\longmapsto[\\bar a/\\bar\\Delta],\\quad a\\in\\Lambda.$$\nIt satisfies $\\rho_{p,\\Delta}(b x)=\\bar b\\,\\rho_{p,\\Delta}(x)$ for $b\\in\\Lambda$. For every nonzero $x\\in T_\\Delta$, one can compute a nonzero integer $D$ from $\\Delta$ and a chosen representative $a/\\Delta$ such that $\\rho_{p,\\Delta}(x)\\ne0$ for every prime $p\\nmid D$. No choice principle or factorization theorem for arbitrary rings is assumed. This is a map on the specified annihilated quotient module; no global reduction homomorphism from $\\mathbb Q(t)$ to $\\mathbb F_p(t)$ is asserted.",
