          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-tv-functional-on-l2-distributional-variation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be open and let $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. Every $u\\in X$ is integrable on compact subsets by Cauchy–Schwarz. Define its extended distributional total variation by\n$$TV_2(u;\\Omega):=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\ \\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2),\\ \\sup_{x\\in\\Omega}|\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}\\in[0,+\\infty],$$\nwhere $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)$ denotes smooth compactly supported vector fields, $|\\cdot|$ is the Euclidean norm, and $\\operatorname{div}\\varphi=\\partial_1\\varphi_1+\\partial_2\\varphi_2$. This definition treats $L^2$ functions as almost-everywhere classes and does not require $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ip-spatial-bv-and-distributional-tv",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $u\\in L^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$, where elements of $L^1$ are almost-everywhere equivalence classes. For each $i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,n\\}$, its distributional derivative $D_i u$ acts on $\\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ by\n$$\\langle D_i u,\\varphi\\rangle:=-\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\partial_i\\varphi\\,dx.$$\nThe function $u$ is in $BV(\\Omega)$ when each $D_i u$ is represented by a finite signed Radon measure and the resulting vector-valued measure $Du=(D_1u,\\ldots,D_nu)$ has finite total variation. Here a finite vector-valued Radon measure means that its partition variation is a finite Radon measure. For a Borel set $A\\subseteq\\Omega$, define\n$$|Du|(A):=\\sup\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty |Du(A_j)|:\\ (A_j)_{j\\ge1}\\text{ is a Borel partition of }A\\right\\},$$\nusing the Euclidean norm on each vector value. Define the spatial total variation by $TV(u;\\Omega):=|Du|(\\Omega)$. A Lebesgue-measurable set $E\\subseteq\\Omega$ of finite measure has finite perimeter relative to $\\Omega$ when $\\chi_E\\in BV(\\Omega)$; its relative perimeter is $P(E;\\Omega):=TV(\\chi_E;\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ip-smooth-compact-support-dense-in-c0",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every open set $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$, the space $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega)$ is dense in $C_0(\\Omega)$ in the uniform norm.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-ip-euclidean-radon-representation-under-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For every open $U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^d$, every bounded real linear functional on $C_0(U;\\mathbb R)$ is integration against a unique finite signed Radon measure. No Dependent Choice assumption is required.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-ip-vector-radon-variation-is-radon",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_m)$ have finite signed Radon coordinate measures. For Borel $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ define $$V(A):=\\sup\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty |\\mu(A_j)|:(A_j)_{j\\ge1}\\text{ is a Borel partition of }A\\right\\},$$ where the norm is Euclidean. Then $V$ is a finite positive Radon measure and $$V(\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega f\\cdot d\\mu:f\\in C_c(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^m),\\ |f(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "Every $L^2$ function is integrable on compact subsets by Cauchy–Schwarz, so its distributional derivatives are defined. For $i=1,2$, set $L_i(\\psi):=-\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\partial_i\\psi\\,dx$ on $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. If $\\psi\\ne0$, the vector field $\\varphi=\\psi e_i/\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$ and its negative are admissible in [F1]; hence $|L_i(\\psi)|\\le M\\|\\psi\\|_\\infty$. The same bound is immediate for $\\psi=0$. By [F3], $L_i$ extends uniquely to a bounded real linear functional on $C_0(\\Omega)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "derive-2-1",
          "claim": "Countable Choice and [F4] represent each extension by a finite signed Radon measure $\\mu_i$. Formula [F2] identifies $\\mu_i$ with $D_i u$, so $\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\mu_2)$ has finite vector Radon variation by [F5]. For every continuous compactly supported $g$ with $\\|g\\|_\\infty\\le1$, approximate uniformly by a smooth compactly supported $h$ with $\\|h-g\\|_\\infty<\\varepsilon$, and replace $h$ by $h/\\max\\{1,\\|h\\|_\\infty\\}$. The replacement has norm at most one and differs from $g$ by at most $2\\varepsilon$. Since $\\sum_i|\\mu_i|(\\Omega)<\\infty$, its integral against $\\mu$ approaches that of $g$ as $\\varepsilon\\downarrow0$. Thus the supremum over smooth unit fields equals the continuous-field supremum in [F5]. By [F2], $\\int u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi=-\\int\\varphi\\cdot d\\mu$; symmetry of the unit test class removes this minus sign. Consequently $|Du|(\\Omega)=M=TV_2(u;\\Omega)$, proving the assertion.",
