        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "cor-greens-theorem-from-general-stokes",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. For a compact smooth planar region $D$ oriented by $dx\\wedge dy$ and smooth $P,Q$ on a neighborhood, general Stokes gives\n$$\\int_{\\partial D}(P\\,dx+Q\\,dy)=\\int_D(Q_x-P_y)\\,dx\\,dy,$$\n$$\\int_{\\partial D}(P\\,dy-Q\\,dx)=\\int_D(P_x+Q_y)\\,dx\\,dy.$$\nWhen $D$ also has the supplied finite elementary Green decomposition required by the classical results, these are exactly their circulation and outward-flux formulas. Outer boundary curves run counterclockwise and holes clockwise.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-ip-vector-radon-variation-is-radon",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ be open and let $\\mu=(\\mu_1,\\ldots,\\mu_m)$ have finite signed Radon coordinate measures. For Borel $A\\subseteq\\Omega$ define $$V(A):=\\sup\\left\\{\\sum_{j=1}^\\infty |\\mu(A_j)|:(A_j)_{j\\ge1}\\text{ is a Borel partition of }A\\right\\},$$ where the norm is Euclidean. Then $V$ is a finite positive Radon measure and $$V(\\Omega)=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega f\\cdot d\\mu:f\\in C_c(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^m),\\ |f(x)|\\le1\\right\\}.$$",
          "uses": [
