          "id": "derive-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], choose $\\chi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2)$ with $0\\le\\chi\\le1$, $\\chi=1$ on $B(0,1)$, $\\operatorname{supp}\\chi\\subset B(0,2)$, and let $\\chi_R(x)=\\chi(x/R)$ for integers $R\\ge1$. This proves existence of a family with $\\sup_R R\\|\\nabla\\chi_R\\|_\\infty<\\infty$. Now fix any family satisfying all four conditions in the statement, write $C=\\sup_R R\\|\\nabla\\chi_R\\|_\\infty$, and put $v_R=\\chi_Ru$. Then $v_R\\in L^2$ has compact support, hence is in $L^1$, and $v_R\\to u$ in $L^2$ because $\\chi_R=1$ on $B(0,R)$ and $u\\in L^2$.",
