          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real Banach space and let $X^*$ be its continuous real dual. The dual pairing is evaluation, written $\\langle \\xi,x\\rangle_{X^*,X}:=\\xi(x)$ for $\\xi\\in X^*$ and $x\\in X$. Let $R:X\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper convex functional: $R$ never takes the value $-\\infty$, its effective domain $\\operatorname{dom}R:=\\{x\\in X:R(x)<+\\infty\\}$ is nonempty, and $R((1-t)x+tz)\\le (1-t)R(x)+tR(z)$ for $x,z\\in X$ and $t\\in[0,1]$, with $0\\cdot(+\\infty):=0$ for this convex-combination formula. For $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}R$, its subdifferential is\n\n$$\\partial R(x):=\\{\\xi\\in X^*:R(z)\\ge R(x)+\\langle\\xi,z-x\\rangle_{X^*,X}\\text{ for every }z\\in X\\}.$$\n\nAn element of $\\partial R(x)$ is a subgradient of $R$ at $x$. Set $\\partial R(x):=\\varnothing$ when $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}R$. The subdifferential may be empty at a point of $\\operatorname{dom}R$; no nonemptiness assertion is part of this definition.",
