          "source": "lem-ip-bv-lipschitz-trace-and-zero-extension",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Here $\\mathcal H^m$ on a boundary denotes normalized Hausdorff measure: the repository's unnormalised $m$-dimensional Hausdorff measure divided by $c_m=H^m((0,1]^m)$ for $m\\ge1$, and counting measure for $m=0$. Let $n\\ge2$ and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain, meaning a bounded open set whose boundary has a finite cover by coordinate neighborhoods in which it is the graph of a Lipschitz function and $\\Omega$ lies on one side of the graph. There is a unique bounded linear trace operator $T:BV(\\Omega)\\to L^1(\\partial\\Omega,\\mathcal H^{n-1})$ such that for every $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ and $\\phi\\in C_c^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb R^n)$, $$\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\phi\\,dx=-\\int_\\Omega\\phi\\cdot dDu+\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}Tu\\,\\phi\\cdot\\nu\\,d\\mathcal H^{n-1},$$ where $\\nu$ is the outward unit normal defined almost everywhere on $\\partial\\Omega$. The zero extension $\\widetilde u$ belongs to $BV(\\mathbb R^n)$ and satisfies $$TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^n)=TV(u;\\Omega)+\\|Tu\\|_{L^1(\\partial\\Omega)}\\le C_\\Omega\\bigl(\\|u\\|_{L^1(\\Omega)}+TV(u;\\Omega)\\bigr)$$ for a finite constant $C_\\Omega$ depending only on the domain. The same conclusions hold in dimension one for a bounded open set with a finite one-sided graph cover, with the two one-sided endpoint traces and counting measure on its finite boundary.",
