    "ex-ip-tv-bessel-deblurring-source-certificate": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-plancherel",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and let $n\\ge1$. Fourier transformation on Schwartz space extends uniquely to a surjective complex-linear isometry $\\mathcal F_2:L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)\\to L^2(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$. It preserves the first-variable-linear inner product, and hence is unitary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $n\\ge1$ and $f\\in L^1(\\mathbb R^n;\\mathbb C)$, with the componentwise integral and quotient conventions of [[def-complex-lp-and-euclidean-test-function-conventions]], [[def-l-p-space-as-a-quotient-by-null-functions]] and [[def-l-one-of-a-measure]]. Define\n$$\\widehat f(\\xi)=\\mathcal Ff(\\xi):=\\int_{\\mathbb R^n}f(x)\\exp(-2\\pi i x\\cdot\\xi)\\,dx,\\qquad \\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n.$$\nHere $x\\cdot\\xi=\\sum_{j=1}^n x_j\\xi_j$, Lebesgue measure is used, and the exponential is [[def-complex-exponential]]. Its unit modulus follows from [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]. The integral is evaluated using any measurable representative. Absolute convergence and representative independence are the obligations discharged by [[lem-l-one-fourier-transform-is-well-defined]]. The output is a function defined at every frequency, not merely an almost-everywhere class. At frequency zero the formula reads $\\widehat f(0)=\\int f$. No choice selection of representatives for a family of classes is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-schwartz-space-is-dense-in-l-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice and let $n\\ge1$. Every Schwartz function belongs to complex $L^2(\\mathbb R^n)$, and the classes represented by $\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R^n)$ are dense there.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-ip-tv-functional-on-l2-distributional-variation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be open and let $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. Every $u\\in X$ is integrable on compact subsets by Cauchy–Schwarz. Define its extended distributional total variation by\n$$TV_2(u;\\Omega):=\\sup\\left\\{\\int_\\Omega u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi\\,dx:\\ \\varphi\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2),\\ \\sup_{x\\in\\Omega}|\\varphi(x)|\\le1\\right\\}\\in[0,+\\infty],$$\nwhere $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R^2)$ denotes smooth compactly supported vector fields, $|\\cdot|$ is the Euclidean norm, and $\\operatorname{div}\\varphi=\\partial_1\\varphi_1+\\partial_2\\varphi_2$. This definition treats $L^2$ functions as almost-everywhere classes and does not require $u\\in L^1(\\Omega)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-ip-banach-convex-subgradient-and-dual-pairing",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ be a real Banach space and let $X^*$ be its continuous real dual. The dual pairing is evaluation, written $\\langle \\xi,x\\rangle_{X^*,X}:=\\xi(x)$ for $\\xi\\in X^*$ and $x\\in X$. Let $R:X\\to(-\\infty,+\\infty]$ be a proper convex functional: $R$ never takes the value $-\\infty$, its effective domain $\\operatorname{dom}R:=\\{x\\in X:R(x)<+\\infty\\}$ is nonempty, and $R((1-t)x+tz)\\le (1-t)R(x)+tR(z)$ for $x,z\\in X$ and $t\\in[0,1]$, with $0\\cdot(+\\infty):=0$ for this convex-combination formula. For $x\\in\\operatorname{dom}R$, its subdifferential is\n\n$$\\partial R(x):=\\{\\xi\\in X^*:R(z)\\ge R(x)+\\langle\\xi,z-x\\rangle_{X^*,X}\\text{ for every }z\\in X\\}.$$\n\nAn element of $\\partial R(x)$ is a subgradient of $R$ at $x$. Set $\\partial R(x):=\\varnothing$ when $x\\notin\\operatorname{dom}R$. The subdifferential may be empty at a point of $\\operatorname{dom}R$; no nonemptiness assertion is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-ip-tv-dual-source-certificate",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$, or let $\\Omega\\subseteq\\mathbb R^2$ be a bounded Lipschitz domain. Set $X=L^2(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and let $Y=L^2(\\Sigma,\\mu;\\mathbb R)$ for a measure space $(\\Sigma,\\mu)$, using the real integral inner products; under Countable Choice these are real Hilbert spaces. Let $A:X\\to Y$ be bounded and real-linear, and let $A^*:Y\\to X$ be its Hilbert adjoint, characterized by $\\langle Au,p\\rangle_Y=\\langle u,A^*p\\rangle_X$. Define $R:X\\to[0,+\\infty]$ as follows. If $\\Omega=\\mathbb R^2$, take $R(u)=TV_2(u;\\mathbb R^2)$, the supremum of $\\int u\\,\\operatorname{div}\\varphi$ over smooth compactly supported vector fields of Euclidean norm at most one. For bounded $\\Omega$, either take Neumann TV, $R(u)=TV(u;\\Omega)$ when $u\\in BV(\\Omega)$ and $+\\infty$ otherwise, or Dirichlet TV, $R(u)=TV(\\widetilde u;\\mathbb R^2)$ when the zero extension $\\widetilde u$ belongs to $BV(\\mathbb R^2)$ and $+\\infty$ otherwise. Thus the bounded-domain choices count, respectively, only the interior derivative or also the jump of the zero extension across the boundary. In the full-space case finite $R$ does not require global $L^1$ integrability.\n\nIdentify each $q\\in X$ with the continuous linear functional $h\\mapsto\\langle q,h\\rangle_X$ by the real Hilbert-space Riesz representation. For exact data $f=Au^\\dagger$, a **minimum-TV exact solution** is a point $u^\\dagger\\in X$ satisfying $Au^\\dagger=f$ and $R(u^\\dagger)\\le R(u)$ for every $u\\in X$ with $Au=f$. A vector $p^\\dagger\\in Y$ is a **dual source certificate** for $u^\\dagger$ if $A^*p^\\dagger\\in\\partial R(u^\\dagger)$, where $\\partial R$ is the convex subdifferential on the real Banach space $X$. The **TV source condition** at $u^\\dagger$ is that such a certificate exists; it does not assert uniqueness of the truth or of the certificate. For a chosen certificate, the associated Bregman distance is\n\n$$D_R^{A^*p^\\dagger}(u,u^\\dagger):=R(u)-R(u^\\dagger)-\\langle A^*p^\\dagger,u-u^\\dagger\\rangle_X,$$\n\nfor $u\\in\\operatorname{dom}R$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
