          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The multiplier $m$ is real, even, and satisfies $0<m\\le1$, so [F1] makes $A$ a contraction on complex $L^2$. For real Schwartz $g$, the integral formula [F2] gives $\\widehat g(-\\xi)=\\overline{\\widehat g(\\xi)}$, and multiplication by $m$ preserves this symmetry. Approximate $m\\widehat g$ in $L^2$ by Schwartz functions using [F3], then replace each approximant $s$ by $\\tfrac12(s(\\xi)+\\overline{s(-\\xi)})$. These symmetrized approximants remain Schwartz, converge to $m\\widehat g$, and have real inverse Fourier transforms by [F2]; [F1] and the closedness observation above pass this property to $Ag$. Density of real Schwartz functions and boundedness of $A$ extend real-valuedness to real $L^2$. By [F7], the complex Plancherel pairing restricts to the real Hilbert pairing. Multiplication by the real function $m$ is self-adjoint, so $\\langle Au,v\\rangle=\\langle u,Av\\rangle$ for real $u,v$, and $A^*=A$. If $Au=0$, then $m\\widehat u=0$ almost everywhere; strict positivity of $m$ and injectivity of the unitary Fourier transform give $u=0$. Thus $A$ is bounded, self-adjoint, and injective.",
