          "claim": "Because $\\phi$ is supported away from zero and from $b$, $u^\\dagger$ is constant near the origin, smooth across radii $a,b$, and zero for $|x|\\ge b$; hence it is a compactly supported smooth real function. Its gradient is $\\nabla u^\\dagger(x)=-\\phi(|x|)x/|x|$ wherever $\\phi(|x|)\\ne0$ and is zero elsewhere. The cutoff in the Statement exists by [F6] and gives $z\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb R^2)$ with $\\|z\\|_\\infty\\le1$. Integration by parts gives\n$$\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}u^\\dagger\\operatorname{div}z\\,dx=-\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}z\\cdot\\nabla u^\\dagger\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}\\phi(|x|)\\,dx=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}|\\nabla u^\\dagger|\\,dx.$$\nFor every admissible test field $y$, the same integration by parts gives $\\int u^\\dagger\\operatorname{div}y\\le\\int|\\nabla u^\\dagger|$, while the field $z$ attains equality. Thus $TV_2(u^\\dagger)=\\int u^\\dagger\\operatorname{div}z$. Setting $q=\\operatorname{div}z$, [F4] gives $TV_2(v)\\ge\\int vq=TV_2(u^\\dagger)+\\langle q,v-u^\\dagger\\rangle$ for every $v$, so $q\\in\\partial TV_2(u^\\dagger)$.",
