          "claim": "First handle the endpoints, rather than taking an inverse chart on a closed parameter interval. At each endpoint choose one of the given tube charts containing it and a short adjacent segment, and express the whole formal jet there. In that chart extend the coordinate curve through the endpoint as follows. For $t<0$ at $a$ use the Borel series $\\sum_{j\\ge0}v_jt^j\\chi(t/r_j)/j!$, where $v_j=\\partial_s^j\\beta(a)$ and a fixed smooth $\\chi$ equals one near zero and has compact support. Choose $r_j$ to be the largest dyadic number at most $2^{-j}$ for which every derivative of order $m<j$ of the $j$th summand is bounded by $2^{-j}$; such a radius exists since these bounds are constants times $r_j^{j-m}$. The tails converge with all fixed derivatives, and their derivatives at zero are the prescribed $v_j$. Join this negative-side series to the original curve for $t\\ge0$; matching all one-sided derivatives makes the join smooth. Repeat with the reversed parameter at $b$. The nonzero endpoint derivatives persist after shrinking the two extensions. They remain embedded: within each endpoint chart a coordinate with nonzero tangent derivative is strictly monotone; the endpoint image is separated from the compact remainder of the original curve, and the two distinct endpoint neighborhoods can be made disjoint. Thus $\\beta$ extends to an embedded open interval containing $J$.\nFor each transverse jet coefficient $T_{i,\\alpha}(s)$ in the chosen endpoint chart, extend that scalar coefficient by the same endpoint Borel construction, using its derivatives $\\partial_s^jT_{i,\\alpha}$ and a dyadic radius $r_{j,i,\\alpha}$. For fixed $j,i,\\alpha$, the finitely many derivative bounds of order $m<j$ guarantee a radius because the relevant terms scale as $r^{j-m}$; choose the largest admissible dyadic radius. For a parameter family, choose each such radius using the uniform bounds for that fixed coefficient and the finitely many derivative orders, so it is common across the family. Keep the given jet on $J$. On the newly added endpoint segments, define its components in every other chart by the ordinary coordinate chain rule from the single extended endpoint-chart jet. On overlaps with $J$ these transformed components agree with the given ones by the original formal compatibility. This preserves compatibility across the extended curve without requiring independently chosen extensions to satisfy new overlap identities. The construction is deterministic for the countable coefficient family and uses no choice axiom.",
