        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For each integer $k\\ge1$, take the actual extension $H_k$ produced by [F1]'s fixed cube formula for $P_a^k$. It is $C^k$ everywhere and smooth off $A$, since there it is a locally finite sum of smooth partition functions times polynomials. All these extensions are thereby specified without selecting elements of unspecified nonempty sets. Put $\\Delta_k=H_{k+1}-H_k$. It is $C^k$ and all its derivatives through order $k$ vanish on $A$. Taylor's formula at a nearest point of $A$, on a fixed compact neighborhood of $A$, gives for every $|\\beta|\\le k$ $$|D^\\beta\\Delta_k(z)|=o(t^{k-|\\beta|})\\qquad(t=\\operatorname{dist}(z,A)\\downarrow0),$$ uniformly near $A$. Indeed the top derivative is uniformly continuous and zero on $A$, and the lower estimates follow by Taylor's integral remainder along the segment from the nearest point. In the parameter-family case these remainders are uniform: the finite-order cube proof bounds $D^\\alpha H_k-D^\\alpha P_{a_z}^k$ by a constant times the specified Whitney remainder modulus; away from $A$ on a compact set its locally finite formula bounds every derivative by the coefficient bounds. Hence the $C^k$ moduli at $A$ used here are common to the family.",
