    "lem-ip-compact-manifold-dirichlet-spectrum-and-regularity": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-lax-milgram",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a real or complex Hilbert space, let $a$ be a bounded coercive sesquilinear form on $H$ with constants $M,\\alpha$ ([[def-bounded-coercive-and-symmetric-sesquilinear-forms]]), and let $F:H\\to\\mathbb K$ be a bounded conjugate-linear functional with norm $\\|F\\|=\\sup_{\\|v\\|\\le1}|F(v)|$. Then there is a unique $u\\in H$ with $$a(u,v)=F(v)\\qquad\\text{for every }v\\in H,$$ and it satisfies $\\alpha\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|$, that is $\\|u\\|\\le\\|F\\|/\\alpha$. The real bilinear case is the same statement with $a$ symmetric or not, $F$ a bounded linear functional, and the conjugation read as the identity.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-spectral-theorem-for-compact-self-adjoint-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ be a\nreal or complex Hilbert space ([[def-hilbert-space]]) and let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H)$ be a compact self-adjoint operator\n([[def-compact-linear-operator]],\n[[def-self-adjoint-positive-unitary-and-normal-operator]],\n[[def-bounded-linear-operator]]). Let\n\n$$\\Sigma:=\\{\\lambda:\\lambda\\ \\text{is an eigenvalue of}\\ T,\\ \\lambda\\ne0\\}$$\n\nbe its set of nonzero eigenvalues\n([[def-eigenvalue-eigenvector-eigenspace-and-spectrum]]), with eigenspaces\n$E_\\lambda=\\ker(T-\\lambda I)$ for $\\lambda\\in\\Sigma$. Then:\n\n1. $\\Sigma$ is a finite or countably infinite set of **real** numbers, each\n   eigenvalue has finite multiplicity in the sense $\\dim E_\\lambda<+\\infty$, and\n   for every real $\\varepsilon>0$ there are only finitely many\n   $\\lambda\\in\\Sigma$ with $|\\lambda|\\ge\\varepsilon$; in particular every point\n   of $\\mathbb F\\setminus\\{0\\}$ has a neighbourhood containing only finitely\n   many elements of $\\Sigma$, so the only possible accumulation point of\n   $\\Sigma$ is $0$;\n2. the closed linear span $M$ of $\\bigcup_{\\lambda\\in\\Sigma}E_\\lambda$ satisfies\n   $$M=(\\ker T)^\\perp=\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}T}$$\n   ([[def-orthogonality-and-orthogonal-complement]]), and $H=M\\oplus M^\\perp$\n   with $M^\\perp\\subseteq\\ker T$;\n3. for every $x\\in H$ the finite-subset net of $\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\Sigma}\\lambda P_\\lambda x$\n   over the orthogonal projections $P_\\lambda$ onto $E_\\lambda$ converges in\n   norm and\n   $$Tx=\\sum_{\\lambda\\in\\Sigma}\\lambda P_\\lambda x ;$$\n4. if in addition $H$ is a complex Hilbert space, then the nonzero spectrum\n   agrees with the nonzero eigenvalues,\n   $$\\sigma(T)\\cap\\{\\mu\\in\\mathbb C:\\mu\\ne0\\}=\\Sigma .$$\n\nNo Hilbert basis of $\\ker T$ is selected anywhere: only the orthonormal bases of\nthe finite-dimensional eigenspaces $E_\\lambda$, $\\lambda\\ne0$, are used.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-global-h-two-dirichlet-regularity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded $C^2$\ndomain, $n\\ge2$, $\\mathbb K\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$, let $L,a$ be as in\n[[def-uniformly-elliptic-divergence-form-operator]] with ellipticity\nconstant $\\theta$, bounds $M_a,M_b,M_c$ and $a^{ij}\\in W^{1,\\infty}(\\Omega)$,\n$b^i,c\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega)$, and let $f\\in L^2(\\Omega)$. If\n$u\\in H^1_0(\\Omega)$ is a weak solution of $Lu=f$ with zero boundary values\n([[def-weak-dirichlet-solution-for-a-divergence-form-operator]]), then\n$u\\in H^2(\\Omega)$ and there is\n$C=C(n,\\Omega,\\theta,M_a,M_b,M_c,\\|Da^{ij}\\|_\\infty)$ with\n$$\\|u\\|_{H^2(\\Omega)}\\le C\\big(\\|f\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}+\\|u\\|_{L^2(\\Omega)}\\big).$$\nThe $L^2$ norm of $u$ on the right cannot be deleted without a hypothesis\nexcluding the homogeneous kernel, as the companion counterexample shows; the\ntheorem is stated for zero Dirichlet data, and nonzero compatible boundary\ndata are handled by an $H^2$ lifting, with an $L^2$ residual forcing,\nbefore the theorem is applied.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-ip-riemannian-green-boundary-traces-and-wave-lifting",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(M,g)$ be a compact, nonempty smooth Riemannian manifold with smooth boundary, let $T>0$, set $Q=(0,T)\\times M$ and $\\Sigma=(0,T)\\times\\partial M$, and let $\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}$ be the outward unit normal. Write $\\nabla_g u$ for the metric gradient, $\\Delta_g u=\\operatorname{div}_g\\nabla_g u$, $dV_g$ for Riemannian volume, and $dS_g$ for the induced boundary measure. The $C^1$ norms below are computed in any fixed finite smooth atlas of the indicated compact sets. For $u,v\\in C^2([0,T]\\times M)$,\n$$\\int_Q\\big((\\partial_t^2-\\Delta_g)u\\,v-u(\\partial_t^2-\\Delta_g)v\\big)\\,dV_gdt =\\int_\\Sigma\\big(u\\,\\partial_{\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}}v-v\\,\\partial_{\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}}u\\big)\\,dS_gdt +\\int_M[\\partial_tu\\,v-u\\,\\partial_tv]_{t=0}^{t=T}\\,dV_g.$$\nFor every $h\\in C_c^\\infty(\\Sigma)$ there is a smooth lift $H$ on $[0,T]\\times M$ with $H|_\\Sigma=h$, supported in a fixed boundary collar and zero near $t=0,T$, such that $\\|H\\|_{C^1([0,T]\\times M)}\\le C\\|h\\|_{C^1(\\Sigma)}$. For every $u\\in C^1([0,T]\\times M)$ the classical normal trace obeys\n$$\\|\\partial_{\\nu_{\\mathrm{out}}}u\\|_{L^2(\\Sigma)}\\le \\big(T\\,\\operatorname{area}_g(\\partial M)\\big)^{1/2}\\|\\nabla_g u\\|_{C^0([0,T]\\times M)}.$$",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On each of finitely many relatively compact coordinate charts, metric eigenvalues and the volume density have positive lower and finite upper bounds. Thus the intrinsic $H^1$ norm is equivalent to the sum of coordinate $H^1$ norms of a fixed smooth partition of unity. A Cauchy sequence has $L^2$ limits for the localized functions and their first derivatives; testing against a smooth function and passing to the limit identifies the derivative limits as the weak derivatives of the function limit. This proves completeness; $V$, being a closure, is closed. Moreover $1\\le q+c\\le1+2\\|q\\|_\\infty$, so $b$ and the $H^1$ inner product have equivalent norms. Local mollification of compactly supported functions and a finite partition prove density of $V$ in $H$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "The inclusion $V\\hookrightarrow H$ is compact without an arbitrary-index choice argument. Indeed, a localized $v\\in V$ extends by zero across a flattened boundary: approximate in $H^1$ by localized interior smooth functions, extend those, and pass to the weak-derivative identities. All extensions have bounded support in a fixed coordinate box. On a cube $C$ of side $\\delta$, successively average in its $n$ coordinate variables. The one-variable identity $f(s)-f(t)=\\int_t^s f'(r)dr$ and Cauchy–Schwarz give $\\|f-\\operatorname{avg}_C f\\|_{L^2(C)}\\le n\\delta\\|Df\\|_{L^2(C)}$ by telescoping those averages; density extends this to $H^1$. Replacement by the mean on each cube of a fixed finite grid is therefore a finite-rank approximation to each coordinate inclusion, with error $O(\\delta)$ uniformly on the $H^1$ unit ball. Sum the finitely many coordinate reconstructions to approximate $V\\hookrightarrow H$ in operator norm by finite-rank maps. A bounded finite-dimensional set has finite nets; choosing $\\delta=2^{-k}$ makes the inclusion's image totally bounded, and completeness of $H$ makes its closure compact. Only countably many finite nets are involved.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.1",
            "given",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "In a coordinate chart $Av=f$ is $$-\\partial_i(\\rho g^{ij}\\partial_jv)+\\rho qv=\\rho f,\\qquad \\rho=\\sqrt{\\det g}.$$ The principal matrix is smooth uniformly positive on each compact chart. Localize by a smooth cutoff; its commutator with $A$ has order one. In a boundary chart flatten to $x_n\\ge0$ and localize away from the chart sides. The localization can be regarded as a zero-Dirichlet function on a bounded smooth domain agreeing with the half-space near its support and rounded elsewhere. Applying [F3] and the same interior estimate, then summing the finite cover, gives $\\|v\\|_{H^2}\\le C(\\|f\\|_2+\\|v\\|_{H^1})$. Testing the equation with $v$ gives $\\|v\\|_{H^1}\\le C(\\|f\\|_2+\\|v\\|_2)$ by absorbing the gradient term, so this is the stated $k=0$ estimate.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $f\\in H$, use [F1] to solve $b(Kf,v)=(f,v)_H$. The map $K:H\\to V$ is bounded, and step 2.1 makes $K:H\\to H$ compact. Symmetry gives $(Kf,h)_H=b(Kf,Kh)=(f,Kh)_H$, positivity gives $(Kf,f)_H=b(Kf,Kf)>0$ for $f\\ne0$, and density of $V$ makes $K$ injective. Its range is dense since a vector orthogonal to the range lies in the kernel by self-adjointness. Apply [F2] to obtain positive eigenvalues $\\kappa_j\\to0$ and a complete $H$-orthonormal family; put $\\mu_j=\\kappa_j^{-1}$. Repeated finite multiplicities are included. The vectors $e_j/\\sqrt{\\mu_j}$ are $b$-orthonormal and complete in $V$: if $b(v,e_j)=0$ for every $j$, then $(v,e_j)_H=0$ for every $j$, hence $v=0$. Orthogonal projection and completeness therefore give the asserted $b$-Parseval identity and convergence of the spectral projections in $V$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Here is the boundary induction for higher orders. Suppose $f\\in H^k$ and $v\\in H^{k+1}$ is already known. In a flattened boundary chart apply a tangential derivative $D_{x'}^\\alpha$, $|\\alpha|=k$, to the localized equation. Its commutator with the second-order operator contains derivatives of $v$ of order at most $k+1$ with bounded smooth coefficients. The localized $D_{x'}^\\alpha v$ has zero trace and lies in $H^1$; this follows first for tangential difference quotients, which preserve zero trace, and then by their $H^1$ limit. The $k=0$ local estimate of step 2.2 gives its $H^2$ bound in terms of $\\|f\\|_{H^k}+\\|v\\|_{H^{k+1}}$. If a derivative has total order $k+2$ and at least three normal factors, differentiate the equation $k$ times, isolating the term $\\rho g^{nn}D_n^2v$. Divide by the positive coefficient $\\rho g^{nn}$. Each other highest-order derivative has fewer normal factors because its two principal derivatives include a tangential derivative; all commutators have order at most $k+1$. Induction on the number of normal factors therefore bounds every derivative of order $k+2$. Interior patches allow derivatives in all directions. Difference quotients justify each differentiation before regularity is established; the bounds are uniform in their increments, and testing their weak limits identifies the claimed derivatives. Nested cutoffs and the finite cover give $\\|v\\|_{H^{k+2}}\\le C_k(\\|f\\|_{H^k}+\\|v\\|_{H^{k+1}})$, and the previous induction level absorbs the last term into $C_k(\\|f\\|_{H^k}+\\|v\\|_2)$. This proves all asserted elliptic estimates.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.2",
            "given",
