          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Set $c=1+\\|q\\|_\\infty$, $a(u,v)=b(u,v)-c(u,v)_{L^2}$, and let $P_J$ be projection on $e_1,\\ldots,e_J$. Solve the scalar equations $d_j''+(\\mu_j-c)d_j=(F,e_j)$ with the projected initial data, and put $u_J=\\sum_{j\\le J}d_je_j$. The scalar solutions exist for $L^1$ forcing by integration against the sine/hyperbolic-sine fundamental solution (at zero frequency use $t$); they have continuous first derivatives and absolutely continuous first derivatives on compact intervals. For $E_J=(\\|u_J'\\|_2^2+b(u_J,u_J))/2$, multiply each equation by $\\bar d_j'$ and take real parts to obtain $$E_J'=\\operatorname{Re}(P_JF+cu_J,u_J')\\le C E_J+\\sqrt{2E_J}\\|F\\|_2.$$ Apply this to $\\sqrt{E_J+\\eta}$ and let $\\eta\\downarrow0$ after integrating: multiplication by $e^{-Ct}$ gives $\\sqrt{E_J(t)}\\le C_T(\\sqrt{E_J(0)}+\\int_0^T\\|F\\|_2dt)$. This is exactly the asserted estimate, uniformly in $J$.",
