        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $J>K$, the same estimate for $u_J-u_K$ has data $(P_J-P_K)u_0,(P_J-P_K)u_1$ and forcing $(P_J-P_K)F$. These data tend to zero in $V\\times H$ by [F1]. The forcing tends to zero in $L^1_tH$ because the projections converge pointwise in $H$ and are contractive, so dominated convergence applies. Hence $u_J$ is Cauchy in $C([0,T];V)$ and $u_J'$ in $C([0,T];H)$. Their limits $u,w$ satisfy $u(t)-u(s)=\\int_s^t w(r)dr$, so $u\\in C^1([0,T];H)$ with derivative $w$ and the exact strong initial traces. Pass to the limit in the integrated finite-dimensional equation first for a finite spectral test and then for every $\\phi\\in V$, using boundedness of $a$ and the form-density in [F1]. This gives precisely the displayed weak equation and energy bound. No weak-in-time limiting trace is substituted for a strong trace.",
