          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For transverse degree $k\\ge3$, differentiating the reduced eikonal equation $k$ times gives a finite linear ODE for the degree-$k$ phase jet: its highest jet enters linearly, with coefficients determined by the Hessian and the first derivatives of the reduced Hamiltonian; the inhomogeneous terms involve only lower jets and derivatives of the symbol. This follows by the product rule: terms containing a highest derivative of $\\phi$ can have only one such factor, and the degree-$k+1$ derivative cancels against differentiation along the central Hamilton curve. Thus solve successively for every degree using [F2], matching all jets on overlaps. Apply [F1], or its explicit shrinking-cutoff series, to realize them. This yields a smooth phase with eikonal defect flat on each branch and $\\operatorname{Im}\\phi\\ge c|y|^2$ after shrinking the transverse tube.",
