        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Define $T_\\phi$ to be the coefficient of $h^{-1}$ in the exact conjugation identity $$P(e^{i\\phi/h}a)=e^{i\\phi/h}(h^{-2}p(x,d\\phi)a+h^{-1}T_\\phi a+Pa).$$ It is a first-order operator with derivative part $p_\\xi(x,d\\phi)\\cdot D$, so its coefficient of $\\partial_s$ on the ray is nonzero. Solve $T_\\phi a_0=0$ and $T_\\phi a_j=-Pa_{j-1}$ for $j\\ge1$ to infinite transverse order. The central $a_0$ equation is a scalar homogeneous ODE with nonzero initial value, hence has nonvanishing exponential solution. For each successive transverse degree and amplitude index the differentiated equations are linear ODEs with already known right sides, by the same cancellation of derivatives along the curve as in step 3.1. Realize these jets by [F1]. The separate asymptotic series $a(x;h)\\sim\\sum_{j\\ge0}h^ja_j(x)$ is realized by $\\sum_jh^ja_j(x)\\eta(h/\\delta_j)$, with $\\eta=1$ near zero and $\\delta_j\\downarrow0$ chosen so its $C^k$ tails are summable for every fixed $k$ and have the required $h$ asymptotic bounds. Explicitly, for each $j$ make $\\delta_j<2^{-j}$ and $\\delta_j^{j/2}(1+\\|a_j\\|_{C^j})<2^{-j}$; for fixed $k,J$, terms $j\\ge\\max(k,2J+2)$ are then $O(h^J)$ in $C^k$. Only finitely many earlier terms remain. This proves the full transport and Borel realization.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
