        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "On an embedded branch take $u_h=h^{-d/4}e^{i\\phi/h}a(x;h)$ and a fixed transverse cutoff. For a flat defect $r$, Taylor's formula gives $|D^\\alpha r|\\le C_{k,\\alpha}|y|^k$ for every $k$. Scaling $y=\\sqrt h z$ and using $\\operatorname{Im}\\phi\\ge c|y|^2$ shows $$\\|h^{-d/4}|y|^ke^{-c|y|^2/h}\\|_{L^2}\\le C_kh^{k/2}.$$ Every application of $P$ loses at most two powers of $h$. Choose $k$ and the amplitude order large enough to dominate that loss and any requested $N$. Derivatives of the transverse cutoff are supported at fixed positive distance from the ray and are $O(h^\\infty)$ by Gaussian decay. Thus the residual is $O(h^N)$ away from longitudinal endpoint cutoffs; at endpoints the commutator with a longitudinal cutoff remains, and no vanishing assertion is made there. This also proves uniform $L^2$ boundedness on each branch.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
