        {
          "id": "step-6-1",
          "claim": "To patch, use the compatible phase and amplitude jets constructed above and a finite partition in the curve parameter. On the overlap of adjacent descriptions, their phases and amplitudes differ by flat terms, so after multiplication by $h^{-d/4}e^{i\\phi/h}$ their difference and any fixed number of derivatives are $O(h^N)$ for every $N$, by step 5.1. Derivatives of the partition cancel because its sum is one on each branch; their remaining flat differences have the same bound. Nonadjacent branches meeting at a projected crossing have distinct covectors: otherwise the phase-space curve would repeat a point. Compactness separates those covectors uniformly off a small neighborhood of the parameter diagonal. Mixed products therefore tend to zero: integrate by parts against the nonzero difference of the two real phase gradients, shrinking crossing neighborhoods so it remains bounded below. Each integration gains $h$, while each transverse Gaussian derivative loses at most $h^{-1/2}$ in its integral bounds; arbitrarily many integrations give arbitrary decay. Away from crossings ordinary Gaussian decay applies. Thus patching preserves the residual, positivity, and the sum of the branch mass limits, including at projected self-intersections.",
