          "id": "step-7-1",
          "claim": "Write the density in a branch as $\\rho(s,y)|dsdy|$. Gaussian scaling and dominated convergence give $$|u_h|^2dV_X\\ \\longrightarrow\\ w(s)ds,\\qquad w(s)=\\frac{\\pi^{d/2}|a_0(s,0)|^2\\rho(s,0)}{\\sqrt{\\det\\operatorname{Im}H(s)}}.$$ This positive smooth function is fixed by the leading transport equation; choose the initial scalar amplitude to make $w(0)=1$. Coordinate changes preserve this expression as a density, and step 6.1 removes mixed terms. For a formally self-adjoint operator the leading transport equation and the Riccati determinant identity give $(p_{\\xi_s}w)'=0$: this follows by multiplying $T_\\phi a_0=0$ by $\\bar a_0$, subtracting its conjugate, and using $\\rho^{-1}\\partial_j(\\rho p_{\\xi_j})$ for the real divergence term. For the real wave operator in physical-time coordinates the normalized central covector has $|\\xi_t|=1$, hence $|p_{\\xi_t}|=2$ is constant; its mass density is therefore constant and the chosen normalization gives $w=1$. Arbitrary complex lower-order terms instead enter the scalar amplitude ODE, giving precisely the transported weight above.",
