          "claim": "Choose a smooth boundary defining function $\\rho$ positive inside. At the boundary, $\\nabla\\rho$ is a negative multiple of $\\nu_{out}$; hence for a tangent unit vector $\\omega$, strict convexity gives $(\\rho\\circ\\gamma_\\omega)''(0)=\\operatorname{Hess}\\rho(\\omega,\\omega)<0$. Compactness of the tangent sphere from [F7] makes this bound uniform near tangency. For a nearby inward direction, put $a=d\\rho_{x_0}(\\omega)>0$. Taylor's formula factors the boundary equation as $\\rho(\\gamma_\\omega(s))=s(a+sB(\\omega,s))$, with $B<0$ uniformly near tangency. The implicit function theorem gives the first positive exit $\\tau(\\omega)$ there, with $\\tau/a$ extending continuously and positively to tangent directions. Every other exit is transverse: a first exit with zero normal derivative would have $\\rho=0$, $\\rho'=0$, $\\rho''<0$, forcing $\\rho<0$ immediately before the exit. Thus all inward exits vary smoothly, and $\\tau\\to0$ at the equator.",
