          "claim": "Extend the central geodesic a distance $\\delta>0$ through both boundary endpoints in the no-return extension used by [F1], and take the polar centre $p=\\gamma(-\\delta)$ outside $M$. Choose $\\delta<t_0/4$ and $\\delta<(T-t_0-L)/4$. The polar differential based at $p$ is nonsingular along this extended ray for sufficiently small $\\delta$: on a short initial segment this follows from the identity differential at the origin, and on the remaining compact segment it follows by continuous dependence of the Jacobi matrix and the nonsingularity in [F1] as $\\delta\\downarrow0$. It is injective on a sufficiently narrow ray tube as well. Otherwise a sequence of collisions in shrinking tubes has limits on the central ray; that ray is embedded by [F1], so its two limiting parameters agree and one local inverse chart excludes the collision. Thus there is a nonsingular polar tube containing the whole ray, with its two radial ends outside $M$. In the tube write $x=(r,\\theta)$, $r=\\delta+s$ along the ray, and $dV_g=m(r,\\theta)drd\\theta$. The Gauss orthogonality proved in [F1] gives $g=dr^2+g_{\\alpha\\beta}d\\theta^\\alpha d\\theta^\\beta$ and $\\Delta_gr=m^{-1}\\partial_rm$.",
