        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix a chamber and let $k_r$ be the number of its ordered levels in the $r$th interval between critical or boundary levels. The chamber is a product of open simplices, one for each interval, with $k_r$ ordered coordinates. Its closure is the product of the corresponding closed simplices, hence compact. The facets of a closed simplex are exactly adjacent-coordinate equalities or a coordinate reaching one endpoint of its interval; across the finite family of chambers those endpoints are the knot extrema, tangle boundary levels, or critical values. Thus before resolving endpoint collisions the only faces are level collisions, outer endpoints, and critical-level events.",
