          "quote": "For an oriented link type $L$ in $S^3$, the Conway polynomial is the polynomial $\\nabla_L(z)\\in\\mathbb Z[z]$ characterized by the normalization $\\nabla_U(z)=1$ for the unknot $U$ and the skein relation\n$$\\nabla_{L_+}(z)-\\nabla_{L_-}(z)=z\\,\\nabla_{L_0}(z).$$\nHere $L_+$ and $L_-$ are identical oriented links outside a ball and have respectively a positive and negative crossing inside it, while $L_0$ is obtained in that ball by the orientation-preserving smoothing. For each integer $k\\ge0$, the $k$th Conway coefficient is $c_k(L):=[z^k]\\nabla_L(z)$. This carrier records the conventional skein interface; existence and uniqueness of the invariant are a separate well-definedness obligation. The skein characterization is on nonempty oriented links. If the link domain also includes the empty link, extend the invariant by the separate conventional value $\\nabla_{\\varnothing}(z)=0$; this empty-link value is not determined by the nonempty skein axioms.",
