          "claim": "For a long chord with one endpoint on a needle and one outside it, compactness of the family gives a uniform positive lower bound on its denominator. The two choices that put this endpoint on opposite needle branches have opposite factors $(-1)^{\\downarrow(P)}$. Their endpoint positions differ by at most $\\varepsilon$, so the mean-value estimate for $\\log(z-z')$ away from $z=z'$ bounds the difference of their integrands by $C\\varepsilon$ times bounded remaining factors. Integration over the fixed simplex preserves the $O(\\varepsilon)$ bound.",
