          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For a surviving nested chain of $k$ short chords, write their branch separations as $0<\\delta_k<\\cdots<\\delta_1<\\varepsilon$. Each short factor is bounded by $C\\,d\\delta_j/\\delta_j$ by step 1.1. The long chord interrupting the short chord nearest the critical point has denominator bounded away from zero; integrating its level variable produces a factor $O(\\delta_k)$ because its logarithmic primitive is Lipschitz there and its endpoint limits differ by $O(\\delta_k)$. The whole nested contribution is therefore bounded by a constant times $\\int_0^\\varepsilon \\frac{d\\delta_1}{\\delta_1}\\cdots\\int_0^{\\delta_{k-1}}\\frac{d\\delta_k}{\\delta_k}\\,\\delta_k=\\varepsilon$, by repeated use of $\\int_0^a(d\\delta/\\delta)\\delta=a$. This also covers $k=1$. At any noncritical level-collision face, each chord still joins two distinct points of the embedded knot or tangle, so every within-chord denominator remains nonzero and has a positive lower bound locally. If a within-chord denominator tends to zero, the endpoints lie on the two branches meeting at a critical point and are covered by steps 1.1–2.1. There are finitely many faces and pairing types at fixed $m$, so the degree-$m$ chamber integrals converge absolutely.",
