          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use coordinates $(z,t)\\in\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb R$ on oriented $\\mathbb R^3$, with $t$ increasing upward. A knot is a smooth embedding $K:S^1\\hookrightarrow\\mathbb C\\times\\mathbb R$ with the orientation of $S^1$. A strict Morse presentation is one for which $t|_K$ has nondegenerate critical points, meaning its second derivative in a local coordinate is nonzero there, and pairwise distinct critical values. Write $t_{\\min}:=\\min_{x\\in K}t(x)$, $t_{\\max}:=\\max_{x\\in K}t(x)$, and $r(K)$ for the number of local maxima. A parenthesized $q$-braid is a smooth braid with $q$ strands, each strictly increasing in $t$, together with an ordered binary parenthesization of its lower endpoints and an ordered binary parenthesization of its upper endpoints.\n\nFor $n\\ge0$, a degree-$n$ chord diagram is an oriented circle with $n$ disjoint unordered pairs of distinct points, called chords. Let $\\mathcal A_n^{\\mathrm{fr}}$ be the rational vector space on these diagrams modulo 4T, and let $\\mathcal A_n^{\\mathrm{un}}$ be its quotient by 1T as well. A 4T relation fixes one chord $pq$ and another chord endpoint $r$, moves the other endpoint of the second chord to just before or after $p$ and just before or after $q$, and takes the alternating sum of those four diagrams. A chord is isolated when it intersects no other chord, where intersection means that chord endpoints alternate around the circle; 1T sets every such diagram to zero. Put $\\widehat{\\mathcal A}^{\\mathrm{fr/un}}_{\\mathbb C}:=\\prod_{n\\ge0}(\\mathcal A_n^{\\mathrm{fr/un}}\\otimes_{\\mathbb Q}\\mathbb C)$, with multiplication induced by connected sum of diagrams and unit the empty diagram. For $q$ ordered upward strands, let $\\widehat{\\mathcal A}_{\\mathbb C}(q)$ be the completion of the associative algebra generated by $t_{ij}=t_{ji}$ for $1\\le i<j\\le q$, with relations $[t_{ij},t_{kl}]=0$ when $i,j,k,l$ are distinct and $[t_{ij},t_{ik}+t_{jk}]=0$ when $i,j,k$ are distinct. The g