          "claim": "Both $G_0$ and $G_1$ solve the same left differential equation. Differentiating $G_1^{-1}G_0$ therefore gives zero, so their ratio is constant. For $G_\\varepsilon(z)=G_0(z)G_0(\\varepsilon)^{-1}$, the endpoint expansions and $G_0(\\varepsilon)^{-1}=\\varepsilon^{-a}f(\\varepsilon)^{-1}$ give\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\varepsilon^{-b}G_\\varepsilon(1-\\varepsilon)\\varepsilon^a &=\\varepsilon^{-b}f(1-\\varepsilon)(1-\\varepsilon)^a\\varepsilon^{-a}f(\\varepsilon)^{-1}\\varepsilon^a\\\\ &=\\varepsilon^{-b}g(\\varepsilon)\\varepsilon^b\\,\\Phi_{\\mathrm{KZ}}\\,\\varepsilon^{-a}f(\\varepsilon)^{-1}\\varepsilon^a, \\end{aligned}$$\nwhere the second equality follows from $G_1(1-\\varepsilon)\\Phi_{\\mathrm{KZ}}=G_0(1-\\varepsilon)$. In each degree, $f(\\varepsilon)^{-1}-1$ and $g(\\varepsilon)-1$ are $O(\\varepsilon)$. Since $a$ and $b$ have positive degree, conjugation by $\\varepsilon^{\\pm a}$ or $\\varepsilon^{\\pm b}$ is a finite polynomial in $\\log\\varepsilon$ in the degree-$N$ quotient. Thus both outside factors in the last expression differ from $1$ by $O(\\varepsilon(1+|\\log\\varepsilon|)^N)$ in each degree, which tends to zero; the limit is $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{KZ}}$.",
