          "source": "lem-kontsevich-critical-point-normalization-and-associator",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For each finite degree cutoff $N$, every parenthesized endpoint cluster of the KZ connection has a unique coefficientwise tangentially regularized transport, obtained by removing its local residue factor. For three strands, fix the normalized positions $(0,z,1)$ and put $a=t_{12}/(2\\pi i)$ and $b=t_{23}/(2\\pi i)$. The equation $G'(z)=\\left(\\frac{a}{z}+\\frac{b}{z-1}\\right)G(z)$ has unique solutions $G_0,G_1$ with $G_0(z)=f(z)z^a$, $G_1(z)=g(1-z)(1-z)^b$, where $f$ and $g$ are analytic near $0$ and $f(0)=g(0)=1$. Their ratio $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{KZ}}:=G_1(z)^{-1}G_0(z)$ is independent of $z$ and equals the regularized limit $\\Phi_{\\mathrm{KZ}}=\\lim_{\\varepsilon\\downarrow0}\\varepsilon^{-b}G_\\varepsilon(1-\\varepsilon)\\varepsilon^a,\\qquad G_\\varepsilon(\\varepsilon)=1$.\nIf $K^+$ is obtained from a strict Morse knot presentation $K$ by inserting one copy of the fixed standard hump $H$, model the local birth by cutting the knot to a one-in one-out tangle $T_K$, composing with the standard one-in one-out hump tangle whose closure is $H$, and closing the composite. Then $Z(K^+)=Z(H)Z(K)$, $r(K^+)=r(K)+1$, and hence $I(K^+)=I(K)$. The inverse in $I(K)$ exists degreewise because the degree-zero coefficient of $Z(H)$ is $1$. The associator identities and independence under arbitrary changes of parenthesization are separate claims.",
