          "source": "thm-conway-second-coefficient-is-type-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\nabla_L(z)$ be a Conway skein invariant of oriented links, and for a knot $K$ put $a_2(K):=[z^2]\\nabla_K(z)$. Then $a_2$ has type at most two: its third finite difference vanishes on every three-singular knot. If $D$ is the degree-two chord diagram of a two-singular knot, then $D_2a_2(K)=1$ when the two chords intersect and $D_2a_2(K)=0$ when they do not. Thus its degree-two symbol is a nonzero weight system on the unframed 4T+1T quotient. Moreover, $a_2(U)=0$ for the unknot, and $a_2(T)=1$ for the positive trefoil $T$ represented by the closure of a two-strand braid with three successive positive Artin half twists; closure joins each upper endpoint to the corresponding lower endpoint outside the braid cylinder.\n\nThe two possible degree-two chord patterns are shown below. The first has alternating endpoints and the second has an isolated chord. The diagrams illustrate the symbol values; the proof is algebraic.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9,thick]\n  \\begin{scope}\n    \\draw (0,0) circle (1);\n    \\draw[->] (0.1,0.99) .. controls (0.35,0.95) and (0.52,0.84) .. (0.65,0.76);\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(60)},{sin(60)}) -- ({cos(240)},{sin(240)});\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(150)},{sin(150)}) -- ({cos(330)},{sin(330)});\n    \\node at (0,-1.35) {$D_{\\times}$, $w(D_{\\times})=1$};\n  \\end{scope}\n  \\begin{scope}[xshift=3.4cm]\n    \\draw (0,0) circle (1);\n    \\draw[->] (0.1,0.99) .. controls (0.35,0.95) and (0.52,0.84) .. (0.65,0.76);\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(40)},{sin(40)}) -- ({cos(130)},{sin(130)});\n    \\draw[very thick] ({cos(220)},{sin(220)}) -- ({cos(310)},{sin(310)});\n    \\node at (0,-1.35) {$D_{\\parallel}$, $w(D_{\\parallel})=0$};\n  \\end{scope}\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```",
