          "quote": "Let $S\\in\\{\\mathbb R,\\mathbb C\\}$ with Borel sigma-algebra $\\mathcal E$, let $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,\\mathbb P)$ be a\nprobability space, and let $Y=(Y_n)_{n\\ge0}$ be a stochastic process with each\n$Y_n:\\Omega\\to S$ a random element\n([[def-stochastic-process-and-finite-dimensional-distributions]]). The process\n$Y$ is **strictly stationary** when every finite-dimensional law is unchanged\nby a common nonnegative time shift: for every $r\\ge1$, every\n$0\\le n_1<\\cdots<n_r$ and every $m\\ge0$,\n\n$$\\mathcal L(Y_{m+n_1},\\ldots,Y_{m+n_r})=\\mathcal L(Y_{n_1},\\ldots,Y_{n_r}).$$\n\nThe **canonical path law** of $Y$ is the pushforward\n$\\mathbb P_Y=\\mathcal L(Y)$ of $\\mathbb P$ under the coordinate map\n$\\omega\\mapsto(Y_n(\\omega))_{n\\ge0}$, a probability measure on the product\nspace $S^{\\mathbb N_0}$ with its product sigma-algebra. The **left shift** is\n$\\theta(z)_n=z_{n+1}$ for $z=(z_n)_{n\\ge0}\\in S^{\\mathbb N_0}$.\n\nA strictly stationary process is called **ergodic** when the canonical shift $\\theta$ is ergodic\nfor $\\mathbb P_Y$ in the sense of\n[[def-ergodic-measure-preserving-system]]. The shift-invariance needed for that\ndefinition, namely that $\\theta$ is measure preserving for $\\mathbb P_Y$, is\nproved below for strictly stationary $Y$, so in that case\n$(S^{\\mathbb N_0},\\mathcal E^{\\otimes\\mathbb N_0},\\mathbb P_Y,\\theta)$ is a\nprobability measure-preserving system in the sense of\n[[def-measure-preserving-transformation-and-system]].",
