          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-ip-feature-estimability-through-adjoint-range",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X$ be a real Hilbert space and let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space. Let $K:X\\to H$ be bounded and linear, let $\\epsilon>0$, and let $Z$ be a jointly Gaussian centered family indexed by $H$, linear into $L^2$ with covariance $\\mathbb E[Z(g)Z(k)]=\\langle g,k\\rangle_H$. For each $x\\in X$ define $Y_x(g)=\\langle Kx,g\\rangle_H+\\epsilon Z(g)$. A continuous linear feature is a map $\\phi\\in X^*$, where $X^*$ is the continuous dual of $X$. By the Riesz representation theorem there is a unique $h_\\phi\\in X$ with $\\phi(x)=\\langle x,h_\\phi\\rangle_X$ for every $x\\in X$. The feature is **exactly linearly estimable by one probe** if some $\\Phi\\in H$ makes the statistic $Y_x(\\Phi)$ unbiased for $\\phi(x)$ for every $x\\in X$. Writing $R(K^*)=\\{K^*\\Phi:\\Phi\\in H\\}$ for the adjoint range, this holds exactly when $h_\\phi\\in R(K^*)$. Every such probe has variance $\\epsilon^2\\|\\Phi\\|_H^2$. This definition concerns one-probe unbiased linear estimators; it does not identify membership in $\\overline{R(K^*)}$ with exact estimability.",
