          "claim": "Suppose $p=\\varepsilon\\in\\{1,-1\\}$. Write the original oriented curve as $K(s)=(r e^{2\\pi i\\varepsilon s},\\sin\\theta_0 e^{2\\pi i q s})$, where $r=\\cos\\theta_0\\in(0,1)$. Put $\\theta_t=(1-t)\\theta_0$ and define $H_t(s)=(\\cos\\theta_t e^{2\\pi i\\varepsilon s},\\sin\\theta_t e^{2\\pi i q s})$. Each $H_t$ lies in $S^3$; its first coordinate is an injective circle parameterization because $|\\varepsilon|=1$ and $\\cos\\theta_t>0$, and the family is smooth including $t=1$. It begins with the given orientation and ends at the core circle with orientation sign $\\varepsilon$.",
