          "claim": "Choose integers $c,d$ with $pd-qc=1$ and let $L=c\\alpha+d\\beta$ be the corresponding simple curve on the Heegaard torus; its algebraic intersection with $K$ is one. At the intersection, the boundary $m$ of a small normal disk splits into arcs $\\gamma_x,\\gamma_y$ on the two solid-torus sides, while $L$ splits into its crossing arc and complementary arc $L_1$. Inserting $L_1L_1^{-1}$ into $m=\\gamma_x\\gamma_y$ gives $m=(\\gamma_xL_1)(L_1^{-1}\\gamma_y)$. The first factor winds $c$ times around the $x$ core and the second winds $-d$ times around the $y$ core, hence $m=x^c y^{-d}$. Its abelianization under $x\\mapsto q$, $y\\mapsto p$ is $cq-dp=-1$, so it is opposite the positive normal-disk meridian, whose class is the positive generator by [F3]. Thus $\\mu=m^{-1}=y^d x^{-c}$. Setting $\\alpha=d$ and $\\beta=-c$ gives $p\\alpha+q\\beta=1$ and the stated word; another Bezout solution changes the exponents by $(qk,-pk)$, so the word is unchanged because $y^{qk}=h^k=x^{pk}$ and $h$ is central.",
