          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $p,q$ be coprime nonzero integers, let $K=T(p,q)$ be the oriented primitive slope curve in the standard Heegaard torus, and let $\\rho_t(z_1,z_2)=(e^{2\\pi i p t}z_1,e^{2\\pi i q t}z_2)$ be the weighted circle action on the unit $S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2$. Choose $N(K)$ to be a sufficiently small closed metric tube around the orbit $K$ for the round metric; this tube is invariant because $\\rho_t$ is isometric and preserves $K$. Set $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. Let $x$ and $y$ be based generators of the fundamental groups of the two solid-torus pieces obtained by cutting $E_K$ along the annulus left on the Heegaard torus, oriented so that the annulus core maps to $x^p$ and $y^q$. Then\n\n$$\\pi_1(E_K)=\\langle x,y\\mid x^p=y^q\\rangle.$$\n\nThe common element $h=x^p=y^q$ is central and represents a regular fiber of a Seifert fibration of $E_K$ over a disk. The fibration has two distinguished core fibers of multiplicities $|p|$ and $|q|$; a fiber of multiplicity one is regular, and a fiber of multiplicity greater than one is exceptional. Thus there are exactly two exceptional fibers when $|p|,|q|>1$, and one is regular when one absolute parameter is $1$.\n\nFor any integers $\\alpha,\\beta$ with $p\\alpha+q\\beta=1$, compatible based paths identify the positive normal-disk meridian and preferred longitude in the peripheral system as\n\n$$\\mu=y^\\alpha x^\\beta,\\qquad \\lambda=h\\mu^{-pq}.$$\n\nHere the positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, and the preferred longitude has degree one around $K$ and maps to zero in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$. Their homology classes form a basis of $H_1(\\partial E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, so both classes are primitive.",
