          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring ([[def-commutative-ring]]) and let $M$ be a\nfinitely presented $R$-module ([[def-finitely-presented-module-and-algebra]]),\nso that there are $m,n\\in\\mathbb N$ and a presentation\n\n$$R^m\\xrightarrow{\\ A\\ }R^n\\xrightarrow{\\ \\beta\\ }M\\longrightarrow0 .$$\n\nFix the standard bases $e_1,\\dots,e_m$ of $R^m$ and $f_1,\\dots,f_n$ of $R^n$\n([[def-finitely-presented-module-and-algebra]]) and let $A=(a_{ij})$ be the\n$n\\times m$ matrix of the $R$-linear map $\\alpha$ with respect to them: the\n$j$-th column of $A$ is the coordinate vector of $\\alpha(e_j)$, so\n$\\alpha(e_j)=\\sum_{i=1}^n a_{ij}f_i$. Such a matrix is called a **presentation\nmatrix** of $M$ (for the chosen presentation). A **minor** of $A$ of size\n$r\\times r$ is the determinant $\\det(a_{i_sj_t})_{s,t=1}^r$\n([[def-determinant-of-a-square-matrix]]) of the square submatrix obtained by\nchoosing rows $i_1<\\dots<i_r$ and columns $j_1<\\dots<j_r$; determinants of\n$1\\times1$ and $0\\times0$ submatrices are allowed, with the empty determinant\nequal to $1$.\n\nFor $k\\ge0$ the **$k$-th elementary ideal** $E_k(M)$ of the presented module $M$\nis the ideal of $R$ generated by the determinants of **all**\n$(n-k)\\times(n-k)$ minors of $A$ ([[def-generated-and-principal-ideals]],\n[[def-left-right-and-two-sided-ideal]]): if $n-k>n$ or $n-k>m$ there is no such\nminor, and the ideal generated by the empty set is $0$. In particular no minor\nis nonzero-by-convention: the conventions are\n$$E_k(M)=R\\quad\\text{for }k\\ge n,\\qquad E_k(M)=0\\quad\\text{for }k<n-m .$$\nFor $0\\le k\\le\\min(n,m)$ the ideal $E_k(M)$ is the ideal generated by the\n$(n-k)\\times(n-k)$ minors; e.g. $E_1(M)$ is generated by the\n$(n-1)\\times(n-1)$ minors of $A$, and $E_n(M)=R$ because the $0\\times0$ minor\nis $1$. This is the $k$-th **Fitting ideal** $\\operatorname{Fit}_k(M)$ of the\nStacks Project (Tag 07Z6, Lemma 15.8.2 and Definition 15.8.3), which is\nindexed by the size $n-k$ of the complementary minors; the two conventions\n$0=E_{-1}\\subset E_0\\subset\\cdots$ agree on the range $0\\le k\\le\\min(n,m)$.\n\n**Caveats.** (i) The notation $E_k(M)$ suppresses the chosen presentation; the\nideal defined here is a priori attached to the presentation and it is the\ncontent of [[lem-elementary-ideals-are-independent-of-the-presentation]] that\nit is in fact an invariant of $M$. (ii) The conventions in the degenerate\nranges are conventions, not theorems; they are the ones compatible with the\nStacks numbering and with independence of the presentation.",
