            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-coprime-torus-knot",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Identify the standard oriented three-sphere with $S^3=\\{(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2:|z_1|^2+|z_2|^2=1\\}$ and choose $0<r<1$. The standard unknotted Heegaard torus is $T_r=\\{|z_1|=r,\\ |z_2|=\\sqrt{1-r^2}\\}$; its two sides are solid tori. In the angular coordinates $(u,v)\\in(\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z)^2$ given by $(u,v)\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i u},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i v})$, let $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ be the first and second coordinate classes in $H_1(T_r;\\mathbb Z)$. For coprime nonzero integers $p,q$, the **oriented torus knot** $T(p,q)$ is the image of $s\\mapsto(r e^{2\\pi i p s},\\sqrt{1-r^2}e^{2\\pi i q s})$, for $s\\in\\mathbb R/\\mathbb Z$, oriented by increasing $s$; it represents the primitive slope $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The mirror convention is the reflection $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(z_1,\\overline{z_2})$, which sends $T(p,q)$ to $T(p,-q)$ with image orientation, and reversing the knot orientation gives $T(-p,-q)$. For nonzero $p,q$ with $d=\\gcd(|p|,|q|)>1$, the corresponding torus link is the union of $d$ disjoint parallel copies of the primitive slope $(p/d,q/d)$; it is not a single embedded curve of class $p\\alpha+q\\beta$. The exterior is $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ for a chosen closed tubular neighborhood. A positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, and a preferred longitude is a degree-one class on $\\partial E_K$ whose image in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$ is zero; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-torus-knot-group-presentation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $p,q$ be coprime nonzero integers, let $K=T(p,q)$ be the oriented primitive slope curve in the standard Heegaard torus, and let $\\rho_t(z_1,z_2)=(e^{2\\pi i p t}z_1,e^{2\\pi i q t}z_2)$ be the weighted circle action on the unit $S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2$. Choose $N(K)$ to be a sufficiently small closed metric tube around the orbit $K$ for the round metric; this tube is invariant because $\\rho_t$ is isometric and preserves $K$. Set $E_K=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$. Let $x$ and $y$ be based generators of the fundamental groups of the two solid-torus pieces obtained by cutting $E_K$ along the annulus left on the Heegaard torus, oriented so that the annulus core maps to $x^p$ and $y^q$. Then\n\n$$\\pi_1(E_K)=\\langle x,y\\mid x^p=y^q\\rangle.$$\n\nThe common element $h=x^p=y^q$ is central and represents a regular fiber of a Seifert fibration of $E_K$ over a disk. The fibration has two distinguished core fibers of multiplicities $|p|$ and $|q|$; a fiber of multiplicity one is regular, and a fiber of multiplicity greater than one is exceptional. Thus there are exactly two exceptional fibers when $|p|,|q|>1$, and one is regular when one absolute parameter is $1$.\n\nFor any integers $\\alpha,\\beta$ with $p\\alpha+q\\beta=1$, compatible based paths identify the positive normal-disk meridian and preferred longitude in the peripheral system as\n\n$$\\mu=y^\\alpha x^\\beta,\\qquad \\lambda=h\\mu^{-pq}.$$\n\nHere the positive meridian is the oriented boundary of a normal disk, and the preferred longitude has degree one around $K$ and maps to zero in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$. Their homology classes form a basis of $H_1(\\partial E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, so both classes are primitive.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-torus-knot-alexander-polynomial-from-presentation",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $p,q$ be coprime nonzero integers and let $K=T(p,q)$. Choose generators $x,y$ for the two-generator presentation $\\pi_1(E_K)=\\langle x,y\\mid x^p=y^q\\rangle$. Let $P_{p,q}$ be the two-dimensional presentation complex with one vertex, oriented edges $x,y$, and one two-cell attached along $r=x^py^{-q}$. The assignment $\\phi(x)=q$, $\\phi(y)=p$ defines an epimorphism $\\phi:\\pi_1(P_{p,q})\\to\\mathbb Z$; let $\\widetilde P_\\phi$ be its connected cyclic cover classified by $\\ker\\phi$. Let $A_K=H_1(\\widetilde{S^3\\setminus K};\\mathbb Z)$ be the one-variable Alexander module for the infinite cyclic cover classified by total linking, with $t$ acting by the deck transformation of the positive meridian. Put $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$ and define\n$$\\delta_{p,q}(t)=\\frac{(t^{pq}-1)(t-1)}{(t^p-1)(t^q-1)}.$$\nThen $\\delta_{p,q}(t)$ is a Laurent polynomial, $A_K\\cong\\Lambda/(\\delta_{p,q})$, and the Alexander polynomial $\\Delta_K$, defined as a generator of the zeroth elementary ideal $E_0(A_K)$, satisfies $\\Delta_K(t)\\doteq\\delta_{p,q}(t)$, where $f\\doteq g$ means $f=\\pm t^k g$ for some integer $k$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-torus-knot-unit-slope-is-unknot",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. If $p,q$ are coprime nonzero integers and $|p|=1$ or $|q|=1$, then the oriented torus knot $T(p,q)$ is ambient isotopic in $S^3$ to the standard unknot with its standard orientation.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-torus-knot-sign-changes-give-mirror-or-reversal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. For coprime nonzero integers $p,q$, the fixed orientation-reversing reflection used for the mirror convention sends the oriented curve $T(p,q)$ to the image-oriented curve $T(p,-q)$, and reversing the orientation of $T(p,q)$ gives $T(-p,-q)$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-knot-genus-invariant-under-mirror-and-reversal",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in $S^3$, and let $r:S^3\\to S^3$ be an orientation-reversing reflection. Give $r(K)$ the image orientation, and let $-K$ denote reversal of the knot orientation. Then $g(K)=g(r(K))=g(-K)=g(-r(K))$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-positive-torus-knot-hopf-plumbing-surface",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $p,q\\ge2$ be coprime integers. There is a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface $F_{p,q}\\subset S^3$ whose oriented boundary is $T(p,q)$ and whose genus is $(p-1)(q-1)/2$. It can be constructed from a disk by plumbing $(p-1)(q-1)$ positive Hopf bands: a positive Hopf band is the oriented annulus spanning the positively linked Hopf link, and plumbing along a square glues square neighborhoods of properly embedded arcs in two surfaces on opposite sides of a 3-ball, with the four boundary edges alternating between the surfaces and with orientations agreeing on the square. For a connected compact oriented surface with one boundary component, genus is defined by $\\chi(F)=1-2g(F)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-oriented-seifert-surface-and-knot-genus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For an oriented smooth knot $K\\subset S^3$, define\n\n$$\\mathcal G(K):=\\{n\\in\\mathbb N_0: \\text{there is a Seifert surface }F\\text{ for }K\\text{ with }g(F)=n\\}.$$\n\nEvery element of $\\mathcal G(K)$ is the genus of a connected compact oriented surface. The existence theorem makes $\\mathcal G(K)$ nonempty, and the well-ordering of $\\mathbb N_0$ gives its least element. The **smooth knot genus** is $g(K):=\\min\\mathcal G(K)$. This genus is the minimum over embedded spanning surfaces; it is not the number of Seifert circles or the genus of every surface produced from a diagram.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "thm-seifert-determinant-is-the-knot-alexander-polynomial",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot, and let $F\\subset S^3$ be a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface whose oriented boundary is $K$, with $\\chi(F)=1-2g$. Choose an ordered integral basis $a_1,\\ldots,a_{2g}$ of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$ and use the positive normal convention $V_{ij}=\\operatorname{lk}(a_i^+,a_j)$. Let $A_K$ be the first homology of the infinite cyclic cover of $S^3\\setminus K$ classified by total linking, with $t$ acting by the positive-meridian deck transformation. Let $\\Delta_K(t)$ be the generator of the zeroth elementary ideal $E_0(A_K)$ in $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$, and write $p\\doteq q$ when $p$ and $q$ differ by a unit $\\pm t^k$ of $\\Lambda$. Then for every such $F$ and basis,\n\n$$\\det(V-tV^T)\\doteq\\Delta_K(t).$$\n\nThe determinant is $1$ for the empty $0\\times0$ matrix when $g=0$. This formula concerns the polynomial $\\Delta_K$; the existing knot-normalized fraction $D_K=\\Delta_K/(1-t)$ is a separate invariant.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "cor-knot-alexander-normalization-symmetry-and-genus-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the full Axiom of Choice and let $K\\subset S^3$ be a smooth oriented knot. For a nonzero Laurent polynomial $f(t)=\\sum_j c_jt^j$, define $\\deg f=\\max\\{j:c_j\\ne0\\}-\\min\\{j:c_j\\ne0\\}$; this is its exponent width and is unchanged by multiplication by a unit $\\pm t^k$. Let $g(K)$ be the minimum genus of a connected compact smooth oriented embedded surface in $S^3$ with oriented boundary $K$, and let $\\Delta_K$ be any generator of the zeroth elementary ideal of its one-variable Alexander module. Then every such generator satisfies\n\n$$\\Delta_K(1)=\\pm1,\\qquad \\Delta_K(t^{-1})\\doteq\\Delta_K(t),\\qquad \\deg\\Delta_K\\le 2g(K),$$\n\nwhere $\\doteq$ means equality up to a unit $\\pm t^k$. Consequently, there is a unique associate $\\Delta_K^\\#(t)$ with $\\Delta_K^\\#(1)=1$ and $\\Delta_K^\\#(t^{-1})=\\Delta_K^\\#(t)$; its exponent support is centered in $[-g(K),g(K)]$. Under the existing convention the knot-normalized fraction $D_K(t)=\\Delta_K(t)/(1-t)$ is distinct from the polynomial $\\Delta_K$; because $\\Delta_K(1)=\\pm1$, it has a simple pole at $t=1$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Apply [F2] to identify the exterior group presentation and its positive-meridian homomorphism. The Fox-module calculation [F3] applied to that presentation gives $\\Delta_K\\doteq\\delta_{p,q}$ and proves $\\delta_{p,q}\\in\\Lambda$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Suppose $a,b\\ge2$. For the four possible sign patterns, $T(p,q)$ is obtained from $T(a,b)$ by no operation, the mirror, orientation reversal, or both, according to [F5]. The genus invariance in [F5] therefore gives $g(K)=g(T(a,b))$.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F1",
            "F5",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For every positive integer $n$, $t^{-n}-1=-t^{-n}(t^n-1)$, so each signed factor $t^{p}-1$, $t^q-1$, and $t^{pq}-1$ differs from its absolute-exponent factor by a unit of $\\Lambda$. Therefore $\\delta_{p,q}$ is a unit multiple of $\\delta_{a,b}$ as a rational function. By [F3] this places $\\delta_{a,b}$ in $\\Lambda$. Its defining rational function is finite at $t=0$, because numerator and denominator both have constant term $1$; a Laurent polynomial finite at $0$ has no negative powers. Thus $\\delta_{a,b}\\in\\mathbb Z[t]$, and $\\Delta_K\\doteq\\delta_{a,b}$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "By [F6], $T(a,b)$ bounds a connected oriented surface of genus $g_0=(a-1)(b-1)/2$, so the minimum in [F7] satisfies $g(K)=g(T(a,b))\\le g_0$.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F6",
            "F7",
            "step 1.2",
            "choose"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "If $a=1$ or $b=1$, the quotient defining $\\delta_{a,b}$ cancels to $1$. By [F4], $K$ is ambient isotopic to the standard unknot, which bounds a disk; an ambient isotopy carries spanning surfaces to spanning surfaces of the same genus in both directions. Since genus is nonnegative by [F7], the minimum is $g(K)=0=(a-1)(b-1)/2$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "L1",
            "F4",
            "F7",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-2",
          "claim": "Since $\\delta_{a,b}\\in\\mathbb Z[t]$, its degree is $ab+1-a-b=(a-1)(b-1)$: the numerator and denominator have leading coefficient $1$, and the quotient is polynomial by step 2.1. Its constant coefficient is $1$, since the numerator and denominator both have constant term $1$. Hence its exponent width is $(a-1)(b-1)=2g_0$, and multiplication by a unit does not change width.",
          "step": "3.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "step 2.2",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "By the Seifert-determinant theorem and the genus-bound corollary [F8], $\\operatorname{width}(\\Delta_K)\\le2g(K)$. Step 2.1 gives $\\operatorname{width}(\\Delta_K)=2g_0$, so $2g_0\\le2g(K)$. Together with step 2.2 this yields $g(K)=g_0$. The formulas hold in the nonunit case, and step 3.1 handles the unit case.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
