        {
          "id": "derive-4.1",
          "claim": "Since $A$ is invertible over $\\mathbb Q$, for every $x\\in\\mathbb Z^n$ some positive integer $m$ clears the denominators of $A^{-1}x$, so $mx\\in A\\mathbb Z^n$ and each class is torsion. If $F,F'$ bound $mC,m'C$, pass to a common multiple $M$; the difference $(M/m)F-(M/m')F'$ is a 2-cycle $Z$. If $r[D]=0$, write $rD=\\partial G$; the chain boundary identity gives $r(Z\\mathbin{\\cdot}D)=Z\\mathbin{\\cdot}\\partial G=\\partial Z\\mathbin{\\cdot}G=0$, hence $Z\\mathbin{\\cdot}D=0$. Thus the quotient is independent of the chosen bounding chain and multiple modulo integers. Replacing either cycle representative by a homologous one adds an integer by the same boundary identity.",
