          "claim": "After tensoring with $\\mathbb C$, multiplication by $t$ on $R_q\\otimes\\mathbb C$ is diagonalizable: its eigenvalues are the $q$ distinct roots $\\zeta_q^j$ for $0\\le j<q$, each with one-dimensional eigenspace. On the corresponding $2g$-dimensional summand, $Q_q$ acts by $V-\\zeta_q^jV^T$. Therefore $\\det Q_q=\\prod_{j=0}^{q-1}\\det(V-\\zeta_q^jV^T)$. The $j=0$ factor is $\\det(V-V^T)=\\pm1$ by [F3]. Thus $\\det Q_q\\ne0$ exactly when every factor with $1\\le j<q$ is nonzero, and its absolute value is the product of the absolute values of those factors.",
