          "source": "def-axiom-of-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.\n\n> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function\n> (Choice function).\n\nWritten out: for every set $\\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,\nthere exists a function $g$ with domain $\\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \\in S$ for\nall $S \\in \\mathcal{F}$.\n\nAn equivalent formulation is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if\n$X_i \\ne \\emptyset$ for every $i \\in I$, then $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i \\ne \\emptyset$.\nHere $\\prod_{i \\in I} X_i$ is the set of functions $f$ with domain $I$ such that\n$f(i) \\in X_i$ for every $i \\in I$; when a family of nonempty sets is indexed by\nitself, such an $f$ is precisely a choice function for it.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-double-branched-cover-homology-from-seifert-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot, let $F$ be a connected compact oriented Seifert surface of genus $g$, and let $V$ be its positive-push Seifert matrix in any ordered integral basis of $H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$. Then\n$$\nH_1(\\Sigma_2(K);\\mathbb Z)\\cong\\operatorname{coker}(V+V^T).\n$$\nFor $g=0$, the matrix is empty and the cokernel is trivial.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-square-integer-cokernel-finiteness-and-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $n$ be a nonnegative integer and let $A$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix with integer entries. Define $\\operatorname{coker}(A)=\\mathbb Z^n/A\\mathbb Z^n$. Then $\\operatorname{coker}(A)$ is finite if and only if $\\det A\\ne0$, and in that case its order is $|\\det A|$. For the empty $0\\times0$ matrix, take $\\det A=1$ and $\\operatorname{coker}(A)=0$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
