          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice, so that the Alexander module $A_L$ of an oriented\nnonempty link $L$ is defined and finitely presented over\n$\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$\n([[def-one-variable-alexander-module-of-an-oriented-link]],\n[[lem-the-alexander-module-of-a-link-complement-is-finitely-presented]]). Let\n$r$ be the number of components of $L$. Define the **one-variable Alexander\npolynomial** $\\Delta_L(t)\\in\\Lambda$ to be a greatest common divisor of the zeroth\nelementary ideal $E_0(A_L)$\n([[def-elementary-ideals-of-a-finitely-presented-module]]), i.e. a generator of\nthe smallest principal ideal of $\\Lambda$ containing $E_0(A_L)$\n([[lem-the-laurent-polynomial-ring-is-noetherian-and-a-unique-factorisation-domain]],\n[[def-unique-factorisation-domain]]); it is well defined up to multiplication\nby a unit $\\pm t^k$ of $\\Lambda$\n([[lem-units-and-powers-of-the-laurent-polynomial-ring]]). The **Alexander\ninvariant** is the normalisation\n$$D_L(t):=\\begin{cases}\\Delta_L(t),&r>1,\\\\[2pt] \\dfrac{\\Delta_L(t)}{1-t},&r=1,\\end{cases}$$\nunderstood for a knot as an element of the fraction field of $\\Lambda$ when\n$(1-t)\\nmid\\Delta_L(t)$; for the integer-valued link formulas of this page the\ndistinction matters and is stated with each use.\n\n**Caveats.** The ideal $E_0(A_L)$ is a well-defined invariant of $L$ because\n$A_L$ is finitely presented and elementary ideals are independent of the\npresentation\n([[lem-elementary-ideals-are-independent-of-the-presentation]]); a greatest\ncommon divisor in a unique factorisation domain is well defined up to units, and\nthe units of $\\Lambda$ are $\\pm t^k$. For every link the absolute one-variable Alexander module admits a square\npresentation, so $E_0(A_L)$ is principal, generated by its determinant (the\nstandard link fact recorded in [F3]). For a knot ($r=1$), every gcd\nrepresentative satisfies $\\Delta_L(1)=\\pm1$. These standard presentation and\nnormalisation facts are recorded here; the gcd definition itself uses only\nfinite presentability and unique factorisation.",
