    "lem-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-parameter-relations": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-and-cusp-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the upper half-space model\n$$\\mathbb H^3=\\{(x+iy,t):x,y\\in\\mathbb R,\\ t>0\\},\\qquad ds^2=t^{-2}(dx^2+dy^2+dt^2),$$\nwhose ideal boundary is $\\mathbb C\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. An oriented ideal tetrahedron in normalized position has ideal vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$, where $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. Its three edge-shape parameters are\n$$z,\\qquad z'=\\frac1{1-z},\\qquad z''=\\frac{z-1}{z},$$\neach assigned to one pair of opposite edges; the parameter at a corner of a truncated ideal vertex triangle is the parameter of the incident edge. For an oriented path $\\gamma$ in a triangulated torus link of ideal vertices, its cusp multiplier $H(\\gamma)$ is the product of the corner parameters encountered along $\\gamma$, using the parameter when the path cuts off that corner on its left and its reciprocal when it cuts off the corner on its right.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Since $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, the imaginary parts of $z$, $z'=1/(1-z)$ and $z''=1-1/z$ are all positive: the second is $\\operatorname{Im}z/|1-z|^2$, and the third is $\\operatorname{Im}z/|z|^2$. Thus their principal arguments lie in $(0,\\pi)$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Multiplication gives $zz'z''=z\\cdot(1-z)^{-1}\\cdot(z-1)z^{-1}=-1$. The sum of the three arguments is therefore an odd multiple of $\\pi$; because it lies strictly between $0$ and $3\\pi$, it must equal $\\pi$. The arguments are the dihedral angles by the edge cross-ratio convention in [F1].",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "For $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}$, $1-\\omega=\\omega^{-1}$ and $\\omega-1=\\omega^2$. Hence $1/(1-\\omega)=\\omega$ and $(\\omega-1)/\\omega=\\omega$; each parameter has argument $\\pi/3$. Each of the three parameter arguments occurs on a pair of opposite edges, proving the regular-angle assertion.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
