          "quote": "For an oriented ideal tetrahedron with normalized vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$ and $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, its three edge-shape parameters are $z$, $z'=1/(1-z)$, and $z''=(z-1)/z$. They satisfy $zz'z''=-1$, and their arguments in $(0,\\pi)$ are the three dihedral angles, whose sum is $\\pi$. For $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}$, the choice $z=\\omega$ gives $z=z'=z''=\\omega$ and all six dihedral angles equal to $\\pi/3$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The positive shapes determine nondegenerate ideal tetrahedra. The face maps are hyperbolic isometries. Around each finite edge, the edge product $1$ makes the accumulated complex similarity the identity, and the $2\\pi$ angle sum removes a cone angle. Thus the tetrahedra glue to a smooth hyperbolic metric away from the ideal vertex. Horospherical truncations give Euclidean triangles on the torus link; their face identifications are Euclidean similarities, maps of the form $x\\mapsto aRx+b$ with $a>0$, $R$ a rotation, and have no cone singularities. They define a Euclidean similarity structure: an atlas with these transition maps. Its linear holonomy, the product $aR$ accumulated around a loop, need not yet be trivial.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "For a loop $\\gamma$ on the link, the linear part of its similarity holonomy is $H(\\gamma)$: transporting a tangent vector across each corner multiplies it by the corner parameter or its reciprocal according to the side used. The basis loops have multiplier $1$, so the linear holonomy is trivial on $\\pi_1(T)$, since every loop is a word in that basis.",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Trivial linear holonomy lets a Euclidean metric in one similarity chart be transported consistently to every chart: two transport paths differ by a loop, whose linear holonomy is the identity by step 1.2. The local Euclidean metrics therefore agree on overlaps and give a global flat metric on the compact torus link. A compact Riemannian manifold is complete.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Lift the flat metric of step 2.1 to the simply connected universal cover and integrate its Euclidean charts along paths to obtain a local isometry into $\\mathbb R^2$; simple connectivity makes this construction path-independent up to one rigid motion. Completeness lets the local isometry extend along every straight segment, so it is a covering onto $\\mathbb R^2$ and hence an isometry. Its deck group acts by Euclidean isometries. Every deck transformation has identity linear part by step 1.2, so acts by translation; compactness of the quotient makes this group a rank-two lattice. The cusp is the corresponding horoball quotient.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 1.2",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "In horospherical coordinates the quotient end has metric $ds^2=dr^2+e^{-2r}g_T$ for $r\\ge0$, where $g_T$ is the complete flat torus metric from step 2.1. A path escaping toward the ideal vertex has $r\\to\\infty$ and length at least the change in $r$, hence infinite length. Thus the glued metric is complete at its only end, the torus cusp.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
