    "lem-ideal-orthoscheme-volume": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-and-cusp-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the upper half-space model\n$$\\mathbb H^3=\\{(x+iy,t):x,y\\in\\mathbb R,\\ t>0\\},\\qquad ds^2=t^{-2}(dx^2+dy^2+dt^2),$$\nwhose ideal boundary is $\\mathbb C\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. An oriented ideal tetrahedron in normalized position has ideal vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$, where $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. Its three edge-shape parameters are\n$$z,\\qquad z'=\\frac1{1-z},\\qquad z''=\\frac{z-1}{z},$$\neach assigned to one pair of opposite edges; the parameter at a corner of a truncated ideal vertex triangle is the parameter of the incident edge. For an oriented path $\\gamma$ in a triangulated torus link of ideal vertices, its cusp multiplier $H(\\gamma)$ is the product of the corner parameters encountered along $\\gamma$, using the parameter when the path cuts off that corner on its left and its reciprocal when it cuts off the corner on its right.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-lobachevsky-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real $\\theta$, define the Lobachevsky function\n$$\n\\Lambda(\\theta)=-\\int_0^\\theta \\log|2\\sin u|\\,du,\n$$\nwhere the integral is interpreted improperly at zeros of $\\sin u$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-lobachevsky-fourier-expansion-and-pi-third-bound",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $\\theta$, $\\Lambda$ is continuous, odd, and $\\pi$-periodic, with $\\Lambda(\\pi/2)=0$ and\n$$\\Lambda(2\\theta)=2\\Lambda(\\theta)+2\\Lambda(\\theta+\\pi/2).$$\nFor $0\\le\\theta\\le\\pi$,\n$$\\Lambda(\\theta)=\\frac12\\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty}\\frac{\\sin(2n\\theta)}{n^2}.$$\nIn particular,\n$$\\Lambda(\\pi/3)=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty} \\left(\\frac1{(3k+1)^2}-\\frac1{(3k+2)^2}\\right).$$\nFor every integer $N\\ge1$, the positive remainder after the terms $k=0,\\ldots,N$ is at most $\\sqrt3/(72N^2)$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Integrating the volume density in $t$ gives $\\operatorname{Vol}(\\mathcal O_\\zeta)=\\int_0^{\\cos\\zeta}\\int_0^{x\\tan\\zeta}\\frac{dy\\,dx}{2(1-x^2-y^2)}$. For fixed $x$, put $A=\\sqrt{1-x^2}$; since $x\\tan\\zeta<A$ for $0\\le x<\\cos\\zeta$, the inner integral is $\\frac1{4A}\\log\\frac{A+x\\tan\\zeta}{A-x\\tan\\zeta}$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Set $x=\\cos\\theta$, so $\\zeta\\le\\theta\\le\\pi/2$. Sine addition identities change the logarithm ratio to $\\sin(\\theta+\\zeta)/\\sin(\\theta-\\zeta)$, and therefore $\\operatorname{Vol}(\\mathcal O_\\zeta)=\\frac14\\int_\\zeta^{\\pi/2}\\log\\frac{\\sin(\\theta+\\zeta)}{\\sin(\\theta-\\zeta)}\\,d\\theta=\\frac14(\\Lambda(2\\zeta)-\\Lambda(\\pi/2+\\zeta)+\\Lambda(\\pi/2-\\zeta))$. The logarithmic singularity at $\\theta=\\zeta$ is integrable, so the substitution and improper endpoint evaluation are valid.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "By oddness and period $\\pi$ from [F3], $\\Lambda(\\pi/2-\\zeta)=-\\Lambda(\\pi/2+\\zeta)$. Also [F3] gives $\\Lambda(2\\zeta)=2\\Lambda(\\zeta)+2\\Lambda(\\zeta+\\pi/2)$. Substitution in step 2.1 cancels the two $\\Lambda(\\zeta+\\pi/2)$ terms and leaves $\\operatorname{Vol}(\\mathcal O_\\zeta)=\\frac12\\Lambda(\\zeta)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
