        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "On the unit-hemisphere face, draw the perpendicular arcs from $(0,0,1)$ to its three edges and the arcs from $(0,0,1)$ to $p,q,r$. Cone the six resulting sectors to $\\infty$. Because the vertical ray lies in the tetrahedron, the six simplices have disjoint interiors and cover the tetrahedron; their common faces have three-dimensional volume zero. Each simplex has two finite vertices and two ideal vertices. Its three right dihedral angles come from the perpendicular cuts; among its other angles, two equal the corresponding original tetrahedron edge angle $\\zeta$ and the third is $\\pi/2-\\zeta$. The tetrahedron is regular, so [F2] gives $\\zeta=\\pi/3$ for each of the six simplices.",
