          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "We first give the model geometry used below. Translations, complex rotations and positive dilations act by $(z,s)\\mapsto(az+b,|a|s)$ and preserve the metric by substitution. Euclidean inversion in the unit sphere, followed by reflection in a vertical plane, is $(z,s)\\mapsto(-\\overline z/(|z|^2+s^2),s/(|z|^2+s^2))$ and extends $z\\mapsto-1/z$. Its Euclidean differential scales lengths by $(|z|^2+s^2)^{-1}$, while its height scales by the same factor, so it also preserves the hyperbolic metric. These maps generate all Möbius transformations: a matrix with nonzero lower-left entry gives a translation, an inversion, a complex dilation and a translation by elementary fraction division; a zero lower-left entry gives an affine map. Thus every boundary Möbius transformation extends to an isometry. The Cayley map $T(z,s)=(2\\operatorname{Re}z,2\\operatorname{Im}z,|z|^2+s^2-1)/(|z|^2+(s+1)^2)$ is a bijection onto the unit ball, with inverse $z=2(u_1+iu_2)/Q$, $s=(1-|u|^2)/Q$, $Q=u_1^2+u_2^2+(1-u_3)^2$. It carries $(0,1)$ to the origin and gives metric $4|du|^2/(1-|u|^2)^2$: its Euclidean length factor is $2/(|z|^2+(s+1)^2)$ and $1-|T|^2=4s/(|z|^2+(s+1)^2)$. Radial integration in the ball, together with $|(|u|)'|\\le|u'|$ on every competing path, gives distance $2\\operatorname{artanh}|u|$ from the origin. Applying the affine normalization of a general basepoint yields $\\cosh d((z,s),(w,t))=\\frac{|z-w|^2+s^2+t^2}{2st}.$ Equality in that radial length argument characterizes the radial segment. Its inverse Cayley image shows that geodesics are the vertical lines and the Euclidean semicircles orthogonal to the boundary. In particular geodesics are unique, closed bounded hyperbolic balls are compact by the displayed formula, and the ideal endpoints of a complete geodesic are its distinct Euclidean boundary endpoints. A complete geodesic is uniquely determined by those endpoints. Consequently an isometry with identity boundary action preserves every complete geodesic and fixes the intersection of two distinct complete geodesics through each point; it is the identity. Thus the Möbius extension constructed above is unique.",
