          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Every geodesic triangle lies in a totally geodesic hyperbolic plane. To see this, send one vertex to the center of the ball by the isometries in step 1.1; the two other vertices and that center lie in a Euclidean plane through the center. Reflection in that plane is a ball isometry, and uniqueness of geodesics makes its fixed plane totally geodesic. In its upper-half-plane coordinates $(x,y)$, the Christoffel formula [F3] gives $\\Gamma^x_{xy}=\\Gamma^x_{yx}=-1/y$, $\\Gamma^y_{xx}=1/y$, $\\Gamma^y_{yy}=-1/y$, and all other symbols zero. Thus for the global positive orthonormal frame $e_1=y\\partial_x$, $e_2=y\\partial_y$, one has $\\nabla_{e_1}e_1=e_2$, $\\nabla_{e_1}e_2=-e_1$, $\\nabla_{e_2}e_1=\\nabla_{e_2}e_2=0$ and $[e_1,e_2]=-e_1$. Consequently $R(e_1,e_2)e_2=-e_1$ and $K=-1$. A noncollinear finite triangle bounds a compact regular disk with three embedded geodesic edges and ordinary corners: its sides are the unique segments in the three geodesic half-planes, and distinct sides meet transversely at their vertices. The global frame supplies the neighbourhood required by [F3]. Gauss–Bonnet therefore gives its area $A=\\pi-(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma)<\\pi$, since all three interior angles are positive. The area density of the displayed metric is $y^{-2}dx\\,dy$. On this plane a radius-$r$ hyperbolic disk has area $2\\pi(\\cosh r-1)$: in the disk metric the Euclidean radius is $R=\\tanh(r/2)$, and integrating $4\\rho(1-\\rho^2)^{-2}\\,d\\rho\\,d\\theta$ gives $4\\pi R^2/(1-R^2)=2\\pi(\\cosh r-1)$. Fix a point $p$ on one side of a nondegenerate finite triangle, and let $d_0$ be its distance to the union of the other sides. If $r<d_0$, the half-disk of radius $r$ on the interior side of the first edge is contained in the triangle. Indeed, a radial geodesic from $p$ to a point of that half-disk stays in the interior edge half-plane and would otherwise first exit across one of the other two actual edges at distance less than $d_0$. Its area is $\\pi(\