          "claim": "The quotient $X$ is a second-countable smooth manifold: left translation by a discrete subgroup is free, and each point of $G$ has a small open neighborhood disjoint from its nontrivial left translates. The projection restricts to a diffeomorphism there, giving the quotient charts. The subgroup is countable, since a discrete subgroup of the second-countable manifold $G$ is discrete and closed and its distinct points can be assigned distinct elements of a countable neighborhood basis. A Borel set in $X$ is $\\nu$-null exactly when its full inverse image is Haar null, by the local quotient definition of $\\nu$, countable quotient charts and countability of $\\Gamma$. Both measures are sigma-finite: $G$ and $X$ have countable relatively compact chart covers, and their Radon measures are finite on compact sets.",
