          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For a covering $p:E\\to B$, a **deck transformation** is an isomorphism $h:E\\to E$ over $B$, so $p\\circ h=p$ ([[def-map-and-isomorphism-of-covering-spaces]]). Deck transformations form the **deck group** $\\operatorname{Deck}(p)$ under composition, and this group acts on $E$ by evaluation ([[def-group]], [[def-group-action]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-deck-group-of-a-universal-cover-is-the-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a path-connected, locally path-connected, semilocally simply connected base $B$, the deck group of a universal cover is isomorphic to $\\pi_1(B,b_0)$. With the library's traversal-order path product the monodromy action is a right action, and the assignment carrying a loop class to the deck transformation that moves the chosen point of the fibre to the corresponding lifted endpoint is itself an isomorphism; no path reversal is inserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-riemannian-isometry-and-local-isometry",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "An **isometry** $F:(M,g)\\to(N,h)$ is a diffeomorphism with $F^*h=g$. A **local isometry** is a smooth local diffeomorphism with $F^*h=g$. An **isometric immersion** is a smooth immersion satisfying that same pullback identity.\n\nUse [[def-pullback-riemannian-metric]] and [[def-diffeomorphism-and-local-diffeomorphism-of-manifolds]]. Positivity forces injective differential by [[prop-pullback-of-a-riemannian-metric-is-riemannian-exactly-for-immersions]]. In equal dimensions on boundaryless manifolds, the inverse function theorem as applied in the next proposition makes a metric-preserving smooth map a local isometry. At a boundary the definition retains the local-diffeomorphism requirement. An isometric immersion need not have equal source and target dimensions.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-local-isometries-send-geodesics-to-geodesics",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $F:(M,g)\\to(N,h)$ be a local Riemannian isometry between smooth Riemannian manifolds without boundary. For every $p\\in M$, there are open neighborhoods $U\\ni p$ and $V\\ni F(p)$ such that $f=F|_U:U\\to V$ is a diffeomorphism and\n$$df\\bigl(\\nabla^M_XY\\bigr)=\\nabla^N_{f_*X}(f_*Y)\\circ f$$\nfor all smooth vector fields $X,Y$ on $U$, where $\\nabla^M$ and $\\nabla^N$ are the Levi-Civita connections.\n\nConsequently, if $I\\subseteq\\mathbb R$ is an interval with nonempty interior, $\\gamma:I\\to M$ is smooth, and $W$ is a smooth vector field along $\\gamma$, then\n$$D_t^N\\bigl(dF_{\\gamma(t)}W(t)\\bigr)=dF_{\\gamma(t)}\\bigl(D_t^M W(t)\\bigr).$$\nIn particular, $F\\circ\\gamma$ is an affinely parametrized geodesic whenever $\\gamma$ is. No completeness, connectedness, surjectivity, or length-minimizing hypothesis is required.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-path-lifting-for-covering-maps",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $p:E\\to B$ be a covering, let $\\alpha:I\\to B$ be a path, and let $e_0\\in E$ satisfy $p(e_0)=\\alpha(0)$. There is a unique path $\\widetilde\\alpha:I\\to E$ with $\\widetilde\\alpha(0)=e_0$ and $p\\circ\\widetilde\\alpha=\\alpha$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The lift of the complete metric to $\\widetilde M$ is complete: every geodesic upstairs projects by [F4] to a geodesic downstairs, which extends for all time; path lifting extends the original geodesic upstairs, and a local isometry preserves its geodesic equation.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F4",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "By [F2], the complete simply connected universal cover $\\widetilde M$ is diffeomorphic to $\\mathbb R^3$. Along each radial unit-speed geodesic from a fixed point, [F3] says the differential of the exponential map multiplies angular vectors by $\\sinh r$, while its radial differential preserves length. Thus in polar coordinates the pulled-back metric is $dr^2+\\sinh^2(r)g_{S^2}$, the standard hyperbolic metric; the exponential map identifies $\\widetilde M$ isometrically with $\\mathbb H^3$. By [F4], $\\Gamma=\\pi_1(M)$ acts freely and properly discontinuously by deck isometries; compactness of $M$ makes the action cocompact.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "A1",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Every nonidentity element of $\\Gamma$ is loxodromic. A Möbius map $z\\mapsto(az+b)/(cz+d)$ has fixed-point equation $cz^2+(d-a)z-b=0$ on the boundary sphere. If there are two distinct fixed points, conjugate them to $0,\\infty$; the map then has form $z\\mapsto \\lambda z$. If $|\\lambda|=1$ and $\\lambda\\ne1$, its extension fixes the axis joining $0$ to $\\infty$, contrary to the free deck action [F4]. If $|\\lambda|\\ne1$, it preserves that axis and translates it nontrivially. If the fixed-point equation has a double root, conjugate it to $\\infty$; the map has form $z\\mapsto az+b$, and its only fixed point is $\\infty$ precisely when $a=1$ and $b\\ne0$. This is parabolic: the upper-half-space distance formula gives $\\cosh d((0,t),(b,t))=1+|b|^2/(2t^2)$, so its displacement tends to zero as $t\\to\\infty$. To exclude such a parabolic in a cocompact deck group, choose compact $K$ with $\\Gamma K=\\mathbb H^3$ and points $y_n$ whose displacement tends to zero. Choose $h_n\\in\\Gamma$ so $x_n=h_ny_n\\in K$ and put $\\gamma_n=h_n\\gamma h_n^{-1}$. The points $x_n$ and $\\gamma_nx_n$ lie in a fixed compact neighborhood of $K$, so proper discontinuity permits only finitely many $\\gamma_n$. Along a subsequence this element is constant and $x_n$ converges in $K$; the displacement limit makes it fix the limit point, contradicting freeness. Hence every nonidentity element has two fixed endpoints and preserves a unique axis with nonzero translation.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "step 2.1",
            "given",
            "algebra",
            "contradiction"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Fix $\\gamma\\ne1$ and let $C_\\Gamma(\\gamma)=\\{\\delta\\in\\Gamma:\\delta\\gamma=\\gamma\\delta\\}$. Commutation preserves the attracting and repelling endpoints of $\\gamma$ individually, hence preserves its axis and acts there by a signed translation. The signed translation map $\\tau:C_\\Gamma(\\gamma)\\to\\mathbb R$ is a homomorphism. It is injective: an element in its kernel fixes every point on the axis, and [F4] makes it the identity. Its image is discrete: if infinitely many elements had bounded signed translations, their images of a fixed point on the axis would lie in a compact segment, contradicting proper discontinuity in [F4]. Every discrete subgroup of $(\\mathbb R,+)$ is cyclic, so $C_\\Gamma(\\gamma)$ is cyclic.",
