    "lem-ideal-triangulation-gluing-equations-and-peripheral-holonomy": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-and-cusp-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the upper half-space model\n$$\\mathbb H^3=\\{(x+iy,t):x,y\\in\\mathbb R,\\ t>0\\},\\qquad ds^2=t^{-2}(dx^2+dy^2+dt^2),$$\nwhose ideal boundary is $\\mathbb C\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. An oriented ideal tetrahedron in normalized position has ideal vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$, where $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. Its three edge-shape parameters are\n$$z,\\qquad z'=\\frac1{1-z},\\qquad z''=\\frac{z-1}{z},$$\neach assigned to one pair of opposite edges; the parameter at a corner of a truncated ideal vertex triangle is the parameter of the incident edge. For an oriented path $\\gamma$ in a triangulated torus link of ideal vertices, its cusp multiplier $H(\\gamma)$ is the product of the corner parameters encountered along $\\gamma$, using the parameter when the path cuts off that corner on its left and its reciprocal when it cuts off the corner on its right.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-parameter-relations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an oriented ideal tetrahedron with normalized vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$ and $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, its three edge-shape parameters are $z$, $z'=1/(1-z)$, and $z''=(z-1)/z$. They satisfy $zz'z''=-1$, and their arguments in $(0,\\pi)$ are the three dihedral angles, whose sum is $\\pi$. For $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}$, the choice $z=\\omega$ gives $z=z'=z''=\\omega$ and all six dihedral angles equal to $\\pi/3$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ideal-torus-cusp-holonomy-criterion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let an orientable finite ideal triangulation have exactly one ideal vertex, whose link is a torus. Suppose its tetrahedra carry positive shapes whose edge products equal $1$ and whose dihedral angles sum to $2\\pi$ around every edge. If the cusp multipliers $H(\\alpha)$ and $H(\\beta)$ are $1$ for a basis $\\alpha,\\beta$ of the torus fundamental group, then the glued hyperbolic metric is complete at that cusp.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-horocusp-truncation-has-finite-volume",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb C$ be a rank-two lattice acting on $\\mathbb H^3$ by horizontal translations, and let\n$$\nC=\\{(x+iy,t):t\\ge1\\}/\\Gamma\n$$\nbe the quotient of the horoball $t\\ge1$. If $A$ is the Euclidean area of a fundamental parallelogram for $\\Gamma$, then the end is at infinite hyperbolic distance and $\\operatorname{Vol}(C)=A/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-pared-hyperbolic-structures-and-volume",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A compact orientable three-manifold $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. A properly embedded surface is **incompressible** if it admits no embedded disk whose interior misses the surface and whose boundary is an essential simple closed curve in it. A boundary subsurface is **$\\pi_1$-injective** if the inclusion of each component induces an injection on fundamental groups. A **pared pair** is a pair $(M,P)$ in which $M$ is compact, orientable and irreducible, and $P$ is a possibly empty compact subsurface of $\\partial M$ whose pairwise disjoint components are $\\pi_1$-injective annuli or tori, every noncyclic abelian subgroup of $\\pi_1(M)$ is conjugate into $\\pi_1$ of a component of $P$ up to base-path conjugacy, and every map of pairs\n$$\n(S^1\\times I,S^1\\times\\partial I)\\longrightarrow(M,P)\n$$\nthat induces injections on fundamental groups is homotopic through maps of pairs into $P$, so its boundary remains in $P$ during the homotopy. Write $\\partial_0M=\\partial M\\setminus\\operatorname{int}P$. A **horoball** is a region bounded by a horosphere, a Euclidean plane or sphere tangent to the ideal boundary at one point. The **peripheral subgroup** for a component of $P$ is the image of its fundamental group in $\\pi_1(M)$, recorded up to conjugacy. A **hyperbolic structure** on $(M,P)$ is a curvature $-1$ metric on $\\operatorname{int}M$ described by an atlas of charts into $\\mathbb H^3$ whose transition maps are in $\\operatorname{Isom}^+(\\mathbb H^3)$. Its developing map $\\operatorname{dev}$ from the universal cover and holonomy homomorphism $\\rho:\\pi_1(M)\\to\\operatorname{Isom}^+(\\mathbb H^3)$ satisfy $\\operatorname{dev}(gx)=\\rho(g)\\operatorname{dev}(x)$. The metric is **complete** when it is geodesically complete. A **cusp** corresponding to a component of $P$ is a quotient of a horoball by the associated peripheral subgroup. The hyperbolic volume is the integral of the Riemannian volume density over $\\operatorname{int}M$; the structure has **finite volume** when this integral is finite.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For each tetrahedron, [F1] supplies a nondegenerate ideal simplex, and the assigned face maps are hyperbolic isometries because their three corresponding ideal vertices have the same cross-ratio shape. Around a finite edge, the product equation makes the accumulated complex similarity the identity, while the angle sum $2\\pi$ makes the total rotation one full turn. Thus the simplices glue to a smooth curvature $-1$ metric away from the ideal vertices.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Fix an ideal vertex $v$ and let $T_v$ be its torus link. Truncate the corners at $v$ by small horospheres. The resulting finite collection of Euclidean corner triangles triangulates $T_v$, and the face identifications restrict to Euclidean similarities. At a vertex of this link triangulation, corresponding to an incident edge of the ideal triangulation, the edge-shape product equation makes the accumulated complex similarity equal to $1$, while the $2\\pi$ angle sum makes the total cone angle exactly $2\\pi$. Thus the link similarity structure has no cone singularities. For a loop $\\gamma$ on $T_v$, the linear part of its similarity holonomy is the multiplier $H_v(\\gamma)$: crossing a corner contributes its shape factor or reciprocal according to the side traversed. The two assumed basis multipliers are $1$, so this linear part is the identity for every loop in $\\pi_1(T_v)$.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Fix a Euclidean metric in one link chart and transport it through the similarity charts. The metric is independent of the transport path, because every loop has identity linear holonomy and translations preserve the Euclidean metric. This gives a global flat metric $g_v$ on the compact torus $T_v$, hence a complete one. Its lift to the universal cover is complete as well: any geodesic projects to a geodesic on the complete torus, extends there, and the extension lifts through the covering. The lifted Euclidean charts develop to a local isometry into $\\mathbb R^2$. Every finite straight segment starting at a point in the image lifts to its endpoint: a partial lift has finite length, is therefore Cauchy, and extends from its limit by the local isometry. Hence the development is onto. For a small Euclidean ball about any point, lifting its radial segments from each preimage gives disjoint isometric copies of that ball; lifting the reverse radial segments shows these copies exhaust its preimage. Thus the development is a covering, and since $\\mathbb R^2$ is simply connected it is an isometry. Deck transformations have identity linear part by the trivial link holonomy, so act by translations. Compactness of $T_v$ makes their translation vectors a rank-two lattice $\\Lambda_v$. The assembled end is therefore the quotient of a horoball by $\\Lambda_v$, with metric $dr^2+e^{-2r}g_v$ in horospherical depth $r$. A path escaping toward the ideal vertex has length at least the change in $r$, so the end is complete. Repeating this argument at each of the finitely many vertices proves completeness at every end. This is the local cusp argument underlying [F3], carried out separately on every link; the one-vertex Statement of [F3] is not applied to the full triangulation.",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "step 2.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "A horospherical truncation of each ideal tetrahedron is compact and has finite volume because its smooth hyperbolic volume density is bounded on that compact region. Each removed vertex tail is contained in a rank-two horocusp and has finite volume by [F4]. There are finitely many tetrahedra and vertices, so the glued manifold has finite total volume.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The part remaining after all cusp neighborhoods are removed is a compact union of truncated tetrahedra. Let $(x_n)$ be a Cauchy sequence. If infinitely many terms lie in that compact part, a subsequence converges there and the Cauchy property forces the whole sequence to the same limit. Otherwise the sequence eventually lies in the union of the finitely many disjoint cusp ends; sufficiently deep points in distinct ends have positive separation through the compact core, so the Cauchy property forces it eventually into one end, where it converges by step 2.1. Thus the full metric is complete. Together with step 3.1 and the curvature $-1$ charts from step 1.1, this is a complete finite-volume hyperbolic structure as defined in [F5].",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "F5",
            "step 1.1",
            "step 2.1",
            "step 3.1",
            "construct"
          ]
        }
