          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "A compact orientable three-manifold $M$ is **irreducible** if every embedded $2$-sphere in its interior bounds an embedded $3$-ball in $M$. A properly embedded surface is **incompressible** if it admits no embedded disk whose interior misses the surface and whose boundary is an essential simple closed curve in it. A boundary subsurface is **$\\pi_1$-injective** if the inclusion of each component induces an injection on fundamental groups. A **pared pair** is a pair $(M,P)$ in which $M$ is compact, orientable and irreducible, and $P$ is a possibly empty compact subsurface of $\\partial M$ whose pairwise disjoint components are $\\pi_1$-injective annuli or tori, every noncyclic abelian subgroup of $\\pi_1(M)$ is conjugate into $\\pi_1$ of a component of $P$ up to base-path conjugacy, and every map of pairs\n$$\n(S^1\\times I,S^1\\times\\partial I)\\longrightarrow(M,P)\n$$\nthat induces injections on fundamental groups is homotopic through maps of pairs into $P$, so its boundary remains in $P$ during the homotopy. Write $\\partial_0M=\\partial M\\setminus\\operatorname{int}P$. A **horoball** is a region bounded by a horosphere, a Euclidean plane or sphere tangent to the ideal boundary at one point. The **peripheral subgroup** for a component of $P$ is the image of its fundamental group in $\\pi_1(M)$, recorded up to conjugacy. A **hyperbolic structure** on $(M,P)$ is a curvature $-1$ metric on $\\operatorname{int}M$ described by an atlas of charts into $\\mathbb H^3$ whose transition maps are in $\\operatorname{Isom}^+(\\mathbb H^3)$. Its developing map $\\operatorname{dev}$ from the universal cover and holonomy homomorphism $\\rho:\\pi_1(M)\\to\\operatorname{Isom}^+(\\mathbb H^3)$ satisfy $\\operatorname{dev}(gx)=\\rho(g)\\operatorname{dev}(x)$. The metric is **complete** when it is geodesically complete. A **cusp** corresponding to a component of $P$ is a quotient of a horoball by the associated peripheral subgroup. The hyperbolic volume is the in