          "claim": "If $k=0$, the collection of germs and every indexed assertion are empty. Both vectors are the unique vector of $\\mathbb C^0$, with norm zero; there is no parameter with $u\\ne0$, so the final limiting assertion is vacuous. This proves the Statement in that case. Henceforth suppose $k\\ge1$. Composition of the two affine maps shows that commutativity is exactly $a_i(e^{v_i}-1)=b_i(e^{u_i}-1)$. Let $E(t)=(e^t-1)/t$ for $t\\ne0$ and $E(0)=1$; its convergent series is $\\sum_{n\\ge0}t^n/(n+1)!$, so it is holomorphic and nonzero near zero. Since $a_i(o)\\ne0$, the holomorphic function $\\tau_i=(b_i/a_i)E(u_i)/E(v_i)$ is defined near $o$. The commutativity equation becomes exactly $v_i=\\tau_i u_i$, and $\\tau_i(o)=b_i(o)/a_i(o)$. The finitely many nonreal values permit one common neighborhood on which all $\\operatorname{Im}\\tau_i$ are nonzero. Thus all $\\tau_i$ are nonzero, proving the equivalence of their zero logarithms and the asserted quotient limit. The argument is an identity of holomorphic functions on $Z$, so singular points cause no difficulty.",
