          "claim": "Here are the local smoothing details needed to finish that graph-density argument. In a finite collection of trivializing charts around the support of $\\chi_Rv$, every differential or codifferential is a first-order matrix operator $P=\\sum_jA_j(x)\\partial_j+B(x)$ with smooth coefficients. Multiplication by a subordinate smooth partition reduces to forms supported inside individual charts. For convolution with a smooth unit-mass kernel $\\rho_\\epsilon$, the difference $P(v*\\rho_\\epsilon)-(Pv)*\\rho_\\epsilon$ is a sum of integral operators with kernels $(A_j(x)-A_j(y))\\partial_j\\rho_\\epsilon(x-y)+(\\partial_jA_j)(y)\\rho_\\epsilon(x-y)$ and a zero-order coefficient commutator. The $L^1$ kernel bounds are uniform on the compact chart, by the mean-value bound for $A_j$ and scaling of $\\rho_\\epsilon$; thus these commutators are uniformly bounded on $L^2$. They tend to zero on smooth functions by differentiation of convolutions, hence on all $L^2$ functions by smooth approximation and the uniform bound. Therefore local mollification converges in each asserted graph norm, also for the direct sum operator $(d_E,\\delta_E)$. The partition contributes only fixed zero-order commutators, so a finite sum completes the approximation by compactly supported smooth forms. Together with step 5.1 this proves the coercive inequality on the maximal joint graph domain. It also proves that maximal differentials are the adjoints of maximal formal codifferentials, since their compact-support integration-by-parts identities extend by this graph density.",
