      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The complete holonomy is irreducible and its adjoint invariants vanish. Indeed one cusp subgroup is a rank-two translation lattice fixing $\\infty$. If all of $\\Gamma$ fixed $\\infty$, its elements would be similarities $z\\mapsto az+b$. An element with $|a|\\ne1$ would conjugate a fixed nonzero lattice translation to translations tending to the identity, contradicting discreteness. An element with $|a|=1$ and $a\\ne1$ is elliptic about a vertical line, impossible in a torsion-free discrete group. Hence all elements would be translations, and the quotient would have infinite volume as height tends to zero. This contradicts the finite-volume hypothesis. Thus some element does not fix $\\infty$. A traceless matrix invariant under the cusp group is a multiple of $E=\\left(\\begin{smallmatrix}0&1\\\\0&0\\end{smallmatrix}\\right)$, by direct commutation with a nontrivial unipotent. A nonzero such matrix invariant under all of $\\Gamma$ would force preservation of its kernel line, hence of $\\infty$, a contradiction. Thus $H^0(M;E_{\\operatorname{Ad}\\rho_0})=0$. The same argument excludes a common invariant line for the holonomy matrices. The group centralizer in $G$ is trivial as well: a Möbius map commuting with the cusp translations fixes $\\infty$ and is itself a translation, by its affine formula; a nonidentity such translation has unique fixed point $\\infty$, so commuting with the entire group would force the whole group to fix $\\infty$.",
