          "claim": "The representation scheme of a torus is smooth near its noncentral complete peripheral pair. Use local $SL_2$ lifts $A,B$ of the two generators, both near nontrivial commuting unipotents; their commutator is $I$ because its sign is locally constant and is positive at the basepoint. Choose a fixed vector $v$ with $v,Av$ independent at that point and hence nearby. For a commuting matrix $B$, write $Bv=av+bAv$; commutation then forces $B=aI+bA$ by applying both sides to the basis $v,Av$. This argument works over truncated power-series rings too, because the basis determinant is a unit. Put $C=A-(\\operatorname{tr}A)I/2$ and write $B=\\alpha I+\\beta C$. Its determinant condition is $\\alpha^2-\\beta^2((\\operatorname{tr}A)^2/4-1)=1$. At the complete pair $\\alpha=1$, so [F3] solves uniquely for $\\alpha$ in terms of $A$ and $\\beta$. Thus the commuting-pair scheme is locally a smooth four-dimensional space, parametrized by the three coordinates of $A$ and one of $\\beta$. In particular every representation over $\\mathbb C[t]/(t^{n+1})$ near this pair extends one order further.",
