      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The topology assertion has a direct finite construction. Put the planar $k$-component unlink, $k\\ge1$, in its standard $k$-bridge position: the upper and lower balls each contain $k$ boundary-parallel arcs with the same noncrossing matching of their $2k$ endpoints. Choose their disjoint bridge disks. Cutting either ball along those disks trivializes the branch construction. Its double cover is two balls joined by $k$ one-handles; the first joins the two zero-handles, and each remaining handle adds one genus. Thus it is a genus-$k-1$ handlebody. The two bridge matchings and disk systems agree in the projection sphere, so the cover of the unlink is the double of this handlebody with matching meridian disks. Doubling each of its $k-1$ co-core disks gives disjoint nonseparating two-spheres. Cutting them removes those one-handles and leaves a sphere with paired ball holes; regluing the pairs gives $\\#^{k-1}(S^2\\times S^1)$. For $k=1$ it is the double of a ball, hence $S^3$. This finite cut-and-glue construction uses the actual local branched-cover convention of [F1], and no Floer map.",
