        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-double-branched-cover-of-an-oriented-link",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r$ be a nonempty finite oriented smooth link in the standard oriented three-sphere, and put $X_L=S^3\\setminus L$. A small meridian about $K_i$ is the boundary of a normal disk fiber; its orientation is immaterial modulo two. The mod-two meridian character is the homomorphism $\\epsilon_L:\\pi_1(X_L)\\to\\mathbb Z/2$ that sends every component meridian to $1$. For each $K_i$, choose an oriented tubular chart, meaning a diffeomorphism from a normal disk bundle onto a neighborhood, written $\\varphi_i:(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\times D^2\\to N_i$ and respecting the core orientation. Let $\\lambda_i$ be the parallel longitude given by $\\varphi_i((\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)\\times\\{z_0\\})$ for a fixed $z_0\\in\\partial D^2$, and set $\\eta_i=\\epsilon_L([\\lambda_i])\\in\\{0,1\\}$. The double branched cover $\\Sigma(L)$ is the closed three-manifold obtained by taking the connected two-sheeted cover of $X_L$ classified by $\\ker\\epsilon_L$ and extending it over each $K_i$ with local normal-disk model $(s,z)\\mapsto(s,e^{\\mathrm i\\eta_i s}z^2)$ in these coordinates. Give its total space the orientation for which the covering map is orientation-preserving away from the branch locus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-double-branched-cover-of-an-oriented-link-well-defined",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. For every nonempty finite oriented smooth link $L=K_1\\cup\\cdots\\cup K_r\\subset S^3$, the homomorphism $\\epsilon_L:\\pi_1(S^3\\setminus L)\\to\\mathbb Z/2$ sending every component meridian to $1$ is well-defined and surjective. Its connected cover extends over the link with a simple order-two branch model, independently of the tubular charts used to describe that model. If $L'$ is ambient isotopic to $L$, the resulting oriented branched covers $\\Sigma(L')$ and $\\Sigma(L)$ are orientation-preservingly diffeomorphic.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-double-cover-of-two-arc-rational-tangle-is-solid-torus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $B=D^2\\times[0,1]$ be a three-ball and let $A=\\{a_1,a_2\\}\\times[0,1]$ be the trivial pair of vertical properly embedded arcs, with $a_1\\ne a_2$ in the interior of $D^2$. The connected two-fold cover of $B\\setminus A$ whose meridians about both arcs have monodromy $1\\in\\mathbb Z/2$ extends across $A$ as a simple branched cover, and its total space is a solid torus.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-lift-of-two-strand-half-twist-is-annular-dehn-twist",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\tau$ be the positive half-twist mapping class of a disk interchanging two marked branch points and fixed near the disk boundary. Choose its standard supported half-turn representative in coordinates compatible with the local branched square charts, and take its unique smooth lift to the connected two-fold branched cover which fixes the annular boundary pointwise. Its smooth isotopy class relative to that boundary is the positive Dehn twist about the core when the half-twist orientation agrees with the annular orientation, and is the inverse twist when the orientation is reversed. A positive half-twist here is the usual supported rotation through $+\\pi$ in an oriented disk coordinate containing the two points, interpolated to the identity outside that coordinate disk; the assertion is independent of its cutoff representative.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-lens-space-surgery-orientation-convention",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$. An oriented lens space is an oriented closed $3$-manifold obtained by gluing two solid tori by an orientation-reversing boundary homeomorphism. For coprime integers $p>0$ and $q$, let $L(p,q)$ denote the oriented union of the exterior of the unknot in the oriented $3$-sphere and a solid torus whose meridian is identified with the slope $p[\\mu_U]+q[\\lambda_U]$ on the unknot exterior. Thus this library uses the convention $L(p,q)=E_U(p/q)$; no symbol $L(0,q)$ is assigned.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-seifert-van-kampen",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $X=U\\cup V$, where $U$ and $V$ are open path-connected subsets of $X$, let $U\\cap V$ be path-connected, and fix $x_0\\in U\\cap V$. For the inclusion-induced maps in the diagram\n\n$$\\pi_1(U,x_0)\\longleftarrow\\pi_1(U\\cap V,x_0)\\longrightarrow\\pi_1(V,x_0),$$\n\nthe group $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$, together with the two inclusion-induced homomorphisms, is a pushout ([[def-pushout-of-group-homomorphisms]]). Equivalently, the canonical homomorphism from any pushout $P$ of the displayed diagram to $\\pi_1(X,x_0)$ is an isomorphism. No injectivity of either map from the overlap group is assumed.",
          "uses": [
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**The covered disk pages and their binding fillings.** Use the standard disk open book of $S^3$ about the braid axis $U$: its complement of a small axis neighborhood is $D^2\\times S^1$, and the closed two-strand braid meets each disk in two points. This description is explicit in $S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2$: the axis is $\\{z_1=0\\}$ and the page parameter is $u=\\arg z_1/(2\\pi)$; the disk page is parametrized by $z_2$, with $z_1$ having the remaining modulus and chosen argument. Cut the page parameter at zero. The two moving points over the resulting interval are a braid isotopy of the twice-marked disk. Its double branched cover is the corresponding annular product by the same square-coordinate and sheet-gluing construction as [F2]. On gluing the ends, the annular monodromy is the boundary-fixed lift of the braid mapping class, hence $T^k$ by [F3]. There is no extra deck map: the page-parameter loop near the axis bounds a small meridian disk in its neighborhood disjoint from the branch link, and has trivial meridian character. Moreover the axis itself links the two-strand braid twice, so its mod-two character is zero as well. Thus the cover of its solid-torus neighborhood consists of two ordinary copies, one for each annular boundary. In each copy the meridian is the loop $u$ at a fixed boundary-page point. Consequently the whole branched cover is exactly the annular mapping torus with both boundary tori filled along these page-parameter meridians. The local branched models retain the smooth structures.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "given",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "**Untwist the mapping torus, retaining both meridians.** Write $A=(\\mathbb R/2\\pi\\mathbb Z)_\\theta\\times[-1,1]_t$, with a positive twist $T(\\theta,t)=(\\theta+2\\pi\\rho(t),t)$, where $\\rho=0$ near $-1$ and $\\rho=1$ near $1$. The mapping torus has the equivalence $(\\theta,t,1)\\sim(\\theta+2\\pi k\\rho(t),t,0)$. The function $$\\theta'=\\theta+2\\pi k\\rho(t)u\\pmod {2\\pi}$$ is well-defined across that equivalence and gives an actual diffeomorphism of the mapping torus with $S^1_{\\theta'}\\times[-1,1]_t\\times S^1_u$. Its inverse on the cut parameter interval is $\\theta=\\theta'-2\\pi k\\rho(t)u$, with the same endpoint compatibility. At the lower boundary the filling meridian $u\\mapsto(\\theta_0,-1,u)$ has class $(0,1)$ in the $(\\theta',u)$ basis. At the upper boundary the corresponding meridian has class $(k,1)$. This computes their actual slopes, not only their intersection number.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F3",
            "step 1.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "**The two solid tori.** Split the product at any fixed interior $t$. Fill its lower end along $(0,1)$. This caps the $u$ circle in the annulus $[-1,t]\\times S^1_u$ by a disk, so that piece is the solid torus $V_-=S^1_{\\theta'}\\times D^2$. Its meridian on the common torus is $m=(0,1)$ and its core longitude is $l=(1,0)$. The upper filled piece is also a solid torus: the integral shear $(\\theta',u)\\mapsto(\\theta'-2\\pi ku,u)$ extends over its whole product collar, is single-valued because $k$ is an integer, and takes its filling slope $(k,1)$ to $(0,1)$. After that shear it is the same disk-capped product as the lower piece. Before undoing the shear, its meridian on the common torus is precisely $kl+m$. Hence this is a genus-one gluing with that meridian slope.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "**Identify the fixed unknot peripheral basis.** Identify $V_-$ with the standard unknot exterior, using the same $S^3\\subset\\mathbb C^2$ decomposition: $E_U=\\{|z_1|\\ge\\varepsilon\\}$ for a small standard axis neighborhood $\\{|z_1|\\le\\varepsilon\\}$. On its boundary the unknot meridian $\\mu_U$ is the $\\arg z_1$ circle, which generates the core circle of $E_U$, and the preferred longitude $\\lambda_U$ is the $\\arg z_2$ circle, which bounds its meridian disk. Thus take $l=\\mu_U$, $m=\\lambda_U$ by an actual product-coordinate identification. Up to the global orientation choice allowed in the statement, the other solid torus is attached with meridian $k\\mu_U+\\lambda_U$. For $k=3$ this is exactly [F4]. For $k=-3$, the reflection $(z_1,z_2)\\mapsto(\\overline z_1,z_2)$ reverses the orientation of $S^3$, preserves the unknot, and sends $\\mu_U$ to $-\\mu_U$ and $\\lambda_U$ to $\\lambda_U$. It carries the slope $-3\\mu_U+\\lambda_U$ to $3\\mu_U+\\lambda_U$ and extends over the filled solid torus by the same meridian identification. Hence the other handedness has the same $L(3,1)$ description up to orientation reversal. Reversing the orientation of the branch knot does not change its mod-two meridian character.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "step 3.1",
            "construct",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In $E_U$ the group is generated by $\\mu_U$ and $\\lambda_U$ is trivial, by its product $S^1\\times D^2$ coordinates. Gluing the second solid torus kills the slope $k\\mu_U+\\lambda_U$. By [F5] there are no further relations: a primitive complementary boundary longitude identifies the core generator of the attached solid torus with the corresponding word in the exterior generator, so that generator is eliminated from the pushout presentation. The group is therefore $\\langle\\mu_U\\mid\\mu_U^k=1\\rangle$, cyclic of order $|k|=3$. All new coordinate and slope calculations were finite; the only inherited choice use is the already declared cover and peripheral-convention branch. This proves both assertions for either oriented trefoil without replacing a meridian-slope calculation by the determinant alone.",
