          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-os-link-surgery-filtered-complex-and-triangle",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the countable-choice Heegaard Floer analytic package. Let $Y$ be any closed connected oriented smooth three-manifold and let $\\mathbb L$ be an ordered framed $n$-component link in $Y$. On its $i$th exterior boundary torus let $\\mu_i$ be the meridian and $\\lambda_i$ the chosen framing longitude. Use the three filling slopes $\\lambda_i$, $\\mu_i+\\lambda_i$ and $\\mu_i$, labelled $0,1,\\infty$, respectively; $\\infty$ restores the original unfilled component. For $I\\in\\{0,1,\\infty\\}^n$ let $Y(I)$ be the simultaneous filling specified by $I$. Fix an admissible pointed Heegaard multi-diagram subordinate to these surgeries and the compatible perturbation and top-generator choices used by the polygon construction. The mod-two polygon maps on\n$$C(\\mathbb L)=\\bigoplus_{I\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}\\widehat{CF}(Y(I);\\mathbb F_2)$$\nform a differential with components only from $I$ to $J\\ge I$ coordinatewise. It preserves the increasing filtration\n$$F_pC(\\mathbb L)=\\bigoplus_{|I|\\ge n-p}\\widehat{CF}(Y(I);\\mathbb F_2),$$\nextended by zero below zero and by the whole module at and above $n$. Its total homology is isomorphic to $\\widehat{HF}(Y(\\infty,\\ldots,\\infty);\\mathbb F_2)=\\widehat{HF}(Y;\\mathbb F_2)$. For $n=1$, the total complex is the mapping cone of the $0$-to-$1$ surgery map and is quasi-isomorphic to the hat complex of the third, $\\infty$ filling; this yields the long exact surgery sequence for every surgery triad, with its cyclic ordering. For $n=0$ the cube is the single hat complex of $Y$. No filtered quasi-isomorphism between distinct surgery presentations is included.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-os-unlink-resolution-covers-and-floer-tqft",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every integer $k\\ge1$, let $U_k\\subset S^3$ be a $k$-component planar unlink with an arbitrary orientation on each component, and choose a basepoint $p$ on any one component. Its double branched cover is $\\#^{\\,k-1}(S^2\\times S^1)$, with $\\Sigma(U_1)=S^3$. Over $\\mathbb F_2$, the hat Floer group of this cover is identified with the exterior algebra on a $(k-1)$-dimensional vector space, hence has rank $2^{k-1}$. Under these identifications, the cobordism maps for saddle cobordisms between neighboring unlink resolutions, with $p$ carried outside the saddle disk, are the multiplication and comultiplication maps of the two-dimensional Frobenius algebra $\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)$ after reduction at the marked component. The identification includes the marked-circle cases and fixes the basis conventions needed to compare the first surgery differential with the reduced Khovanov cube.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-reduced-khovanov-cohomology-of-a-based-link-over-f2",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(D,p)$ be a finite regular oriented link diagram with a marked point $p$ away from crossings, and let $C(D;\\mathbb F_2)$ be its normalized Khovanov cochain complex over $\\mathbb F_2$ obtained from the algebra $\\mathcal A=\\mathbb F_2[X]/(X^2)$ with $\\deg_q(1)=1$ and $\\deg_q(X)=-1$. On each resolution state, let $X_p$ multiply by $X$ on the circle containing $p$ and act as the identity on the other circle factors. Define the reduced complex by $\\widetilde C(D,p)=C(D;\\mathbb F_2)/\\operatorname{im}X_p$, with the surviving based-circle label $1$ assigned reduced quantum degree $0$ by the shift $\\widetilde j=j-1$. Its cohomology is $\\widetilde{Kh}^{i,\\widetilde j}(D,p;\\mathbb F_2)$. The basepoint may lie on any component; for a link, independence of the chosen component is a separate theorem.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "1.3",
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "On the associated graded cube the diagonal differential is the ordinary vertex differential. Therefore its homology is the direct sum of the resolution Floer groups. The next differential is induced by the components changing one coordinate: higher polygon components lower the increasing filtration by at least two and cannot contribute to this first differential. Each surviving component is the actual surgery triangle map of that edge.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "given",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Use the SAME vertex state identification simultaneously for all incident edges. By the exact Floer supplier [F2], an unmarked merge has table $1\\otimes1\\mapsto1$, $1\\otimes X,X\\otimes1\\mapsto X$, $X\\otimes X\\mapsto0$; an unmarked split has $1\\mapsto1\\otimes X+X\\otimes1$ and $X\\mapsto X\\otimes X$. Marked merge kills the input with the other circle labelled $X$ and sends its $1$ input to $1_{\\rm marked}$. Marked split sends $1_{\\rm marked}$ to $1_{\\rm marked}\\otimes X_{\\rm new}$, with the tensor order determined by which child contains the point. These last two formulas are obtained by quotienting the marked factor, not by keeping an extra unreduced factor. Tensor the identity on every untouched circle. These are exactly the reduced Frobenius saddle maps of [F3].",
          "step": "1.2",
