          "claim": "Put $t=\\tau(C_K)$ and let $[x]$ be its nonzero total homology class in degree $n$. There is a cycle $x\\in F_tC_{K,n}$ representing $[x]$. Since $H(F_{t-1}C_K)\\to H(C_K)$ has zero image, every cycle in $F_{t-1}C_{K,n}$ is a boundary. Thus $x\\notin B_n+F_{t-1}C_{K,n}$, where $B_n$ is the subspace of boundaries. Extend a linear functional $\\lambda$ that vanishes on $B_n+F_{t-1}C_{K,n}$ and satisfies $\\lambda(x)=1$. It is a cocycle. For a finite-dimensional chain complex, $\\ker(\\partial^\\vee)=B^\\perp$ and $\\operatorname{im}(\\partial^\\vee)=Z^\\perp$, so evaluation identifies $H(C_K^\\vee)=B^\\perp/Z^\\perp$ with $H(C_K)^\\vee$; hence $\\lambda$ represents the nonzero dual class. It belongs to $F_{-t}C_K^\\vee$. If $s<-t$, every element of $F_sC_K^\\vee$ vanishes on $F_tC_K$, so its induced class pairs trivially with $[x]$ and maps to zero in total dual homology. Therefore $\\tau(C_K^\\vee)=-t$.",
