          "claim": "Write $t=\\tau(C)$ and $u=\\tau(D)$. For each degree $n$, let $B_n=\\operatorname{im}(\\partial_{n+1})$ and $Z_n=\\ker(\\partial_n)$. Finite-dimensional linear algebra gives complements $H_n,L_n$ with $Z_n=B_n\\oplus H_n$ and $C_n=Z_n\\oplus L_n$; the differential maps $L_n$ isomorphically onto $B_{n-1}$. Thus each complex is the direct sum of homology representatives with contractible two-term complexes. If $h$ contracts one such summand $A$, then $h\\otimes 1$ contracts $A\\otimes E$: the two cross terms cancel by the graded tensor signs, while $(\\partial h+h\\partial)\\otimes1=1$. Hence $H(C\\otimes D)\\cong H(C)\\otimes H(D)\\cong\\mathbb F_2$. By [F1], choose cycles $x\\in F_tC$ and $y\\in F_uD$ representing the nonzero homology classes. Their tensor is a nonzero class in $H(C\\otimes D)$ and lies in $F_{t+u}(C\\otimes D)$, so $\\tau(C\\otimes D)\\le t+u$.",
