          "claim": "Choose a smooth embedded arc on $S$ joining its two boundary components. Cut $S$ along a thin strip about that arc, and remove from $S^3\\times I$ the interior of a compatible tubular neighborhood of the arc. A properly embedded arc joining the two boundary spheres in this simply connected four-dimensional product is isotopic to a product arc relative its ends: a generic homotopy of such arcs is a path of embeddings, since the coincidence condition for two distinct arc points at the same time has dimension $1+1+1-4<0$, and the derivative-zero condition is also avoidable. These are finitely many compact smooth general-position choices. Removing the product arc neighborhood turns the ambient shell into $B^4$, after rounding its product corners. The cut surface has one boundary component $K_1\\#(-K_0)$ and Euler characteristic $\\chi(S)+1=1-2g$, hence genus $g$. Its product collars supply the ordinary connected-sum boundary convention. Thus it is an actual smooth genus-$g$ spanning surface for $K_1\\#(-K_0)$.",
