          "claim": "The finite grid in [F3] is an actual negative trefoil diagram. Subtract $1/2$ from the marking coordinates for this projection description. Its row segments are $[0,2]$ in row $0$, $[1,3]$ in row $1$, $[2,4]$ in row $2$, $[0,3]$ in row $3$, and $[1,4]$ in row $4$, with the orientations $O\\to X$. Vertical segments run $X\\to O$ and pass over. The only interior crossings are $(2,1),(3,2),(1,3)$. Following the single component $O_0\\to O_2\\to O_4\\to O_1\\to O_3\\to O_0$ encounters them as $A_{\\rm over},B_{\\rm under},C_{\\rm over},A_{\\rm under},B_{\\rm over},C_{\\rm under}$. The first two crossings have upward over-strand and rightward under-strand; the third has downward over-strand and leftward under-strand. Thus all have the same negative sign. Smoothing the degree-two grid corners gives the standard alternating three-crossing negative trefoil projection: each successive over-under pair forms one of the three braid crossings, and its two exterior connecting arcs give the closure. This is the closure of $\\sigma_1^{-3}$, with the displayed cyclic traversal fixing its orientation. Reflection gives the positive trefoil convention of the Example. This finite knot admission uses the actual segments, not a deduction of knot type from a Floer polynomial.",
