          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "thm-deck-group-of-a-universal-cover-is-the-fundamental-group",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For a path-connected, locally path-connected, semilocally simply connected base $B$, the deck group of a universal cover is isomorphic to $\\pi_1(B,b_0)$. With the library's traversal-order path product the monodromy action is a right action, and the assignment carrying a loop class to the deck transformation that moves the chosen point of the fibre to the corresponding lifted endpoint is itself an isomorphism; no path reversal is inserted.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "prop-the-first-hurewicz-map-in-degree-one-is-abelianization",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every path-connected topological space $X$ and $x_0\\in X$, the Hurewicz map\n$$h:\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\longrightarrow H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$$\nis surjective and has kernel $[\\pi_1(X,x_0),\\pi_1(X,x_0)]$. Hence it induces a natural isomorphism\n$$\\pi_1(X,x_0)^{\\mathrm{ab}}\\cong H_1(X;\\mathbb Z).$$\nThe proof is choice-free and requires no CW, separation, local path-connectivity or local simple-connectivity assumption.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-poincare-duality-for-oriented-topological-manifolds",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume AC. Let $M$ be an $R$-oriented boundaryless $n$-manifold, with $R$ commutative unital; manifolds are Hausdorff and second countable here. Then cap with the compatible compact orientation classes gives isomorphisms\n$$D_M:H_c^p(M;R)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_{n-p}(M;R)$$\nfor every integer $p$, including disconnected, noncompact and empty $M$.\n\nNaturality means that for an open inclusion $i:U\\hookrightarrow M$ with restricted orientation, $D_M e=i_*D_U$, where $e$ is extension of compact supports. For compact $M$, $H_c^p(M;R)=H^p(M;R)$ canonically and $D_M(a)=a\\cap[M]$. For arbitrary $M$ the isomorphism is the direct sum of those on its components. AC is used in the countable coordinate-neighborhood selection for exhaustion and in the local universal-coefficient proof.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-universal-coefficient-theorem-for-cohomology-over-a-pid",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $R$ be a PID, $C$ a chain complex of free\n$R$-modules, and $G$ an $R$-module. Then naturally\n$$0\\to\\operatorname{Ext}^1_R(H_{n-1}C,G)\\to H^n\\operatorname{Hom}_R(C,G)\\xrightarrow{\\operatorname{ev}_n}\\operatorname{Hom}_R(H_nC,G)\\to0$$\nis exact.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "universal-cover",
          "claim": "The free action makes $S^3\\to P$ a covering; since $S^3$ is simply connected by [F1], it is universal by [F2]. Its deck group is $I^*$, so [F3] gives $\\pi_1(P)\\cong I^*$. Since $|I^*|=120$, this fundamental group is nontrivial.",
