    "thm-knot-floer-detects-seifert-genus": {
      "authoring_status": "complete",
      "risk": "high",
      "kind": "theorem",
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "thm-smooth-schoenflies-for-embedded-two-sphere-in-s-three",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. Let $S\\subset S^3$ be the image of a smooth embedding of the standard $S^2$. There is a smooth ambient isotopy $H_t:S^3\\to S^3$, with $H_0=\\operatorname{id}$, such that $H_1(S)$ is the standard equatorial sphere $E$. In particular $S^3\\setminus S$ has exactly two components. Their closures $B_+$ and $B_-$ are smoothly embedded standard closed $3$-balls with common boundary $S$. If $D_+$ and $D_-$ denote the standard closed hemispheres bounded by $E$, the labels can be chosen so that the restrictions $H_1:B_\\pm\\to D_\\pm$ are diffeomorphisms with the same boundary restriction $H_1|_S:S\\to E$. Thus the ball identifications are compatible on their boundary and on a common two-sided collar of that boundary.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-integral-sutured-floer-taut-hierarchy-nonvanishing",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let a balanced sutured manifold be taut. For a specified coherent orientation class, its integral sutured Floer homology has a direct summand isomorphic to the integers. More precisely, a finite good taut hierarchy terminating in product balls induces a split inclusion of the terminal product homology into the original homology, with orientation classes induced along the cuts.",
          "uses": [
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ni-holomorphic-disk-and-jsj-analytic-inputs",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $K$ be a null-homologous knot in a closed connected oriented $3$-manifold $Y$, let $F$ be a genus-$g$ Seifert surface, and let $(M,\\gamma)$ be the balanced sutured manifold obtained by cutting the compact exterior of $K$ along $F$. For a surface-adapted admissible sutured Heegaard diagram, the generator correspondence between the top Alexander summand of the knot Floer complex and the sutured Floer disk-count module identifies their disk-count differentials. Under the hypotheses of a horizontal decomposition, the induced chain map realizes Ni's horizontal decomposition formula. Under the hypotheses of a separating product annulus with connected $R_+$ and $R_-$, the induced chain map realizes Ni's product-annulus tensor formula. These assertions use the specified basepoint domains, periodic-domain admissibility, and exact characteristic-product-region hypotheses; they make no claim that generic Heegaard disk existence alone supplies the decomposition maps.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-fiber-outer-grading-and-integral-cut-euler-matrix",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For the actual marked Seifert surface of genus g, oriented as minus the fiber for the cut, an adapted generator x has Alexander degree g minus the number of its interior polygon coordinates. Thus the top degree-g generators are exactly the outer generators and correspond to the cut diagram generators with their ordered intersection signs. If the integral top homology is one copy of the integers, the actual cut incidence matrix is unimodular, the cut is an integral homology product, and its periodic-domain group vanishes.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "def-oriented-seifert-surface-and-knot-genus",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. For an oriented smooth knot $K\\subset S^3$, define\n\n$$\\mathcal G(K):=\\{n\\in\\mathbb N_0: \\text{there is a Seifert surface }F\\text{ for }K\\text{ with }g(F)=n\\}.$$\n\nEvery element of $\\mathcal G(K)$ is the genus of a connected compact oriented surface. The existence theorem makes $\\mathcal G(K)$ nonempty, and the well-ordering of $\\mathbb N_0$ gives its least element. The **smooth knot genus** is $g(K):=\\min\\mathcal G(K)$. This genus is the minimum over embedded spanning surfaces; it is not the number of Seifert circles or the genus of every surface produced from a diagram.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "1.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-integral-sutured-floer-compactness-and-orientations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an actual weakly admissible balanced sutured diagram on a surface with boundary, the integral disk complex exists for each specified coherent orientation class. Its disk, triangle and quadrilateral moduli spaces admit finite signed counts and the corresponding oriented boundary identities on actual weakly admissible multidiagrams. Sphere and one-torus boundary bubbles are excluded by the zero boundary-region multiplicities. Orientation classes are transported rather than declared equivalent.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-countable-analytic-choice-for-fixed-sutured-floer-data",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Fix a finite collection of admitted compact marked sutured diagrams, a finite sequence of diagram moves or surface cuts, and finite-dimensional parameter families of dimension at most two. Suppose the local Fredholm, finite-dimensional Sard, compactness and gluing constructions are supplied for their actual Cauchy–Riemann operators, including the prescribed endpoint and protected-neighborhood conditions. All selections needed to apply those constructions, choose coherent determinant orientations, and obtain simultaneous regular data can then be made under Countable Choice. In particular no uncountable choice of analytic charts or regular data is needed for these fixed finite integral Floer constructions.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
