          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-skein-module-of-solid-torus-has-core-multicurve-basis",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and let $R=\\mathbb Z[A^{\\pm1}]$. Identify\nthe solid torus with $\\mathcal A\\times[-1,1]$, where $\\mathcal A$ is a planar\nannulus. A simple closed curve is called essential if it does not bound a disk\nin $\\mathcal A$. Every framed link in this product admits a regular annular\ndiagram and an integer framing correction from its blackboard framing. Let\n$z$ be one blackboard-framed annular core curve, and for $n\\geq1$ let $z^n$\ndenote $n$ parallel copies of that curve, with $z^0$ the empty link. In the polynomial\nring $R[z]$, $z$ is also the formal variable sent to the core class $[z]$.\nThen the classes $[z^n]$, $n\\geq0$, form an $R$-basis of\n$\\mathcal K_A(\\mathcal A\\times[-1,1])$. Equivalently, the map\n$J:R[z]\\to\\mathcal K_A(\\mathcal A\\times[-1,1])$ sending $z^n$ to $[z^n]$ is\nan $R$-module isomorphism.",
