          "source_section": "Remark",
          "quote": "Zheng et al.'s InverseBench reports 14 plug-and-play diffusion-prior algorithms and domain-specific baselines on five selected cases: first-Born optical scattering, raw multi-coil knee MRI, black-hole very-long-baseline interferometry, full waveform inversion, and two-dimensional vorticity-form Navier–Stokes inversion. For these experiments, the authors report that diffusion-prior methods often outperform conventional baselines when the training data are suitable, while a classical full-waveform-inversion optimizer initialized from a smoothed ground truth outperforms the tested diffusion-prior methods. In cases with closed-form forward models, methods using more forward-gradient queries can be more accurate and more expensive; the paper reports that this trend fails for its numerical PDE cases. Its Navier–Stokes split has one validation case and ten test cases. The paper reports step-size/Courant–Friedrichs–Lewy (CFL) failures in its PDE experiments. Its Figure 5 out-of-distribution diagnostics feed digit images to a black-hole prior trained on simulated black-hole images, and nine-cell scattering sources to a prior trained on one-to-six-cell sources; for the black-hole case, the authors report outputs with the prior's characteristic ring feature. These are empirical observations for the named data, solvers, methods, tuning protocol, and hardware; they establish no universal ranking, stability bound, posterior exactness, risk-transfer bound, or robustness theorem.\n\nThere is a first-Born notation discrepancy between the paper and its released implementation. Appendix B.1 defines a contrast-dependent total field and then displays a sensor equation with that field multiplied by the contrast, which does not itself exhibit a fixed linear map. The pinned implementation instead passes the fixed incident field into the sensor-propagation routine and constructs its cached linear matrix from that fixed field. This page therefore identifies the implemente