          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ip-operator-and-prior-mismatch",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(X,d_X)$ and $(\\mathsf Y,d_Y)$ be metric spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty class of admissible truths, and let $q:U\\to Q$ assign each truth its recovery target in a metric space $(Q,d_Q)$. Let $\\mathfrak A$ index forward maps $F_A:U\\to\\mathsf Y$; in this definition an **operator** means such a forward map, and linearity is not assumed. A specified set $\\mathcal C\\subseteq U\\times\\mathfrak A$ records which truth/operator pairs are admissible; no full-product assumption is made. Equip $\\mathsf Y$ with a sigma-algebra $\\mathcal Y$ and an addition operation such that every translation $\\tau_z(e)=z+e$ is measurable. A **noise law** is a probability measure $\\nu$ on $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$. For each admissible pair $(x,A)\\in\\mathcal C$, the observation random element on the probability space $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y,\\nu)$ is $e\\mapsto F_A(x)+e$, and its sampling law is\n$$P_{x,A,\\nu}(B)=\\nu\\bigl(\\{e\\in\\mathsf Y:F_A(x)+e\\in B\\}\\bigr),\\qquad B\\in\\mathcal Y.$$\nThe translation measurability assumption makes this law a probability measure.\n\nFor a true specification $(x_\\star,A_\\star,\\nu_\\star)$ with $(x_\\star,A_\\star)\\in\\mathcal C$ and a specified working operator $\\widetilde A\\in\\mathfrak A$ and noise law $\\widetilde\\nu$, there is **operator mismatch at the truth** when $F_{A_\\star}(x_\\star)\\ne F_{\\widetilde A}(x_\\star)$, and there is **noise-law mismatch** when $\\nu_\\star\\ne\\widetilde\\nu$. A **working statistical model** is a metric index space $(\\Xi,d_\\Xi)$, a family $(Q_\\xi)_{\\xi\\in\\Xi}$ of sampling laws on the same data space, and a prior probability measure $\\Pi$ on a sigma-algebra containing every open metric ball. The statistical model is **misspecified** for true observation law $P_\\star=P_{x_\\star,A_\\star,\\nu_\\star}$ when $P_\\star\\notin\\{Q_\\xi:\\xi\\in\\Xi\\}$. If a true model index $\\xi_\\star\\in\\Xi$ is specified, there is a **prior-support mismatch** at $\\xi_\\star$ when some open ball $B_\\Xi(\\xi_\\star,r)$, $r>0$, has $\\Pi(B_\\Xi(\\xi_\\star,r))=0$. These are distinct properties; an operator or noise-law mismatch need not by itself make the statistical model misspecified or exclude the true index from the prior's support. This definition makes no assertion about posterior existence or convergence.",
