          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring ([[def-commutative-ring]]). A function $a\\colon\\mathbb N\\to R$ has **finite support** when there is $N\\in\\mathbb N$ such that $a_i=0$ for every $i>N$. The **polynomial ring** $R[x]$ is the set of all finitely supported functions $a\\colon\\mathbb N\\to R$.\n\nFor $a,b\\in R[x]$ and $n\\in\\mathbb N$, define\n\n$$ (a+b)_n:=a_n+b_n,\\qquad (ab)_n:=\\sum_{i+j=n}a_i b_j. $$\n\nThe convolution sum is a finite sum in the additive commutative monoid of $R$ ([[def-finite-sum-in-a-commutative-monoid]]). Write $0$ for the zero sequence, $1$ for the sequence with coefficient $1_R$ at index $0$ and zero elsewhere, and $x$ for the sequence with coefficient $1_R$ at index $1$ and zero elsewhere. The coefficient sequence supported at $0$ with value $r$ is denoted again by $r$, and a polynomial $a$ is written formally as $\\sum_i a_i x^i$.\n\nThe closure of these operations and the commutative-ring axioms are established by [[lem-polynomial-operations-preserve-finite-support]] and [[thm-polynomial-ring-is-a-commutative-ring]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-polynomial-ring-is-a-commutative-ring",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every commutative ring $R$, the coefficientwise addition and convolution multiplication of [[def-polynomial-ring-over-a-commutative-ring]] make $R[x]$ a commutative ring. The constant-polynomial map $c\\colon R\\to R[x]$ is an injective unital ring homomorphism ([[def-ring-homomorphism]]).",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-generated-and-principal-ideals",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The ideal generated by a subset and principal ideals.\n\nFor $S\\subseteq R$, define $(S)$ to be the intersection of all two-sided ideals\nof $R$ containing $S$.  This family is nonempty because it contains $R$, and\n[[lem-ideal-criterion-and-intersections]] shows that $(S)$ is an ideal.  For\n$a\\in R$, the ideal $(\\{a\\})$ is written $(a)$ and is called **principal**.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-quotient-ring",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The quotient ring $R/I$ with $(r+I)(s+I)=rs+I$.\n\nLet $I\\mathrel{\\trianglelefteq}R$.  Since $I$ is an additive subgroup of the\nabelian group $(R,+)$, its additive cosets form the quotient group $R/I$\n([[def-quotient-group]], [[cor-subgroups-of-abelian-groups-are-normal]]).  Define\n\n$$(r+I)(s+I):=rs+I.$$\n\nThe well-definedness and ring laws are established by\n[[thm-quotient-ring-multiplication-well-defined-iff-ideal]] and\n[[thm-quotient-ring-laws]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-monic-polynomial-division",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $g\\in R[x]$ be monic. For every $f\\in R[x]$ there are unique $q,r\\in R[x]$ such that\n\n$$ f=qg+r,\\qquad r=0\\ \\text{or}\\ \\deg r<\\deg g. $$\n\nThis includes the zero dividend and a constant monic divisor.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "Let $Q=R[X]/(G)$. By [F1] and [F2], $R[X]$ is a commutative polynomial ring, and [F3] makes $(G)$ a two-sided ideal and $Q$ a quotient ring. Since $G$ is monic of degree two, [F4] gives every class in $Q$ a representative $a(p)+b(p)X$ of degree at most one in $X$. If two such representatives define the same class, their difference is a multiple of $G$ and has degree less than two; uniqueness in [F4] forces the difference to be zero. Thus this representative is unique.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "given",
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F3",
            "F4",
            "construct"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "In $Q$, the relation $X^2-pX-1=0$ gives $X(X-p)=1$, so $X$ is invertible with inverse $X-p$. The homomorphism $Q\\to\\mathbb Z[x,x^{-1}]$ sending $p\\mapsto x-x^{-1}$ and $X\\mapsto x$ respects $G=0$. Conversely, sending $x\\mapsto X$ defines a homomorphism $\\mathbb Z[x,x^{-1}]\\to Q$ because $X^{-1}=X-p$. The composites fix the generators $p,X$ and $x$, respectively, so these homomorphisms are inverse. The unique representatives from step 1.1 therefore give the asserted unique Laurent decomposition.",
