        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ip-mean-zero-ntd-and-smoothed-kernel-observation",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\ge2$, let $\\Omega\\subset(0,1)^d$ be nonempty, bounded, connected and $C^\\infty$, and let $\\gamma\\in X_d(R)$ be a real conductivity. Write $M=\\partial\\Omega$, let $dS$ be surface measure, and set\n$$L^2_0(M)=\\left\\{f\\in L^2(M):\\int_M f\\,dS=0\\right\\},\\qquad P_0f=f-|M|^{-1}\\int_Mf\\,dS.$$\nThe mean-zero Neumann-to-Dirichlet operator $\\mathscr R_\\gamma:L^2_0(M)\\to L^2_0(M)$ sends $f$ to the mean-zero boundary trace of the weak solution of $\\nabla\\cdot(\\gamma\\nabla u)=0$ with outward conormal flux $f$, as defined by the weak Neumann problem. Let $-\\Delta_M$ be the nonnegative Laplace–Beltrami operator on the compact boundary manifold, with eigenpairs $(e_j,\\lambda_j)$ on $L^2_0(M)$ from its spectral resolution. For $\\tau=1/2$ when $d=2$ and any fixed $\\tau>0$ when $d\\ge3$, put\n$$r_d:=\\frac{d-1}{2}-1+\\tau,\\qquad C_M^{r_d/2}e_j=(1+\\lambda_j)^{-r_d/2}e_j.$$\nThe smoothed observation is the bounded operator $A_\\gamma:=\\mathscr R_\\gamma C_M^{r_d/2}:L^2_0(M)\\to L^2_0(M)$. Its extension to all of $L^2(M)$ is $\\widetilde A_\\gamma f=\\iota_0A_\\gamma(P_0f)$, where $\\iota_0:L^2_0(M)\\hookrightarrow L^2(M)$ is inclusion. Thus its integral kernel, when it exists in $L^2(M\\times M)$, is fixed to have zero mean in each variable. No assertion of a kernel's existence or Hilbert–Schmidt regularity is part of this definition.",
