          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $d\\ge2$, let $\\Omega\\subset(0,1)^d$ be nonempty, bounded, connected and $C^1$, and let $\\gamma\\in L^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ satisfy $0<m\\le\\gamma\\le M<\\infty$ almost everywhere. Let $\\mathcal D(\\Omega)=H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)/H_0^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)$ be the complex trace quotient and write $\\dot{\\mathcal D}=\\mathcal D(\\Omega)/\\mathbb C\\mathbf1$. Let $\\dot{\\mathcal D}^{*}$ denote the continuous conjugate-linear dual of $\\dot{\\mathcal D}$. For each $F\\in\\dot{\\mathcal D}^{*}$ there is a unique weak solution class $[u]\\in H^1(\\Omega;\\mathbb C)/\\mathbb C\\mathbf1$ satisfying\n$$\\int_\\Omega\\gamma\\nabla u\\cdot\\overline{\\nabla v}\\,dx=F([\\operatorname{Tr}v])\\qquad(v\\in H^1(\\Omega)).$$\nThe resulting solution operator $\\mathscr R_\\gamma:\\dot{\\mathcal D}^{*}\\to\\dot{\\mathcal D}$ is bounded. Its unique representative whose boundary trace has surface mean zero solves the same weak equation. In particular, for $f\\in L^2(\\partial\\Omega)$ with $\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}f\\,dS=0$, the functional $F_f([g])=\\int_{\\partial\\Omega}f\\overline g\\,dS$ is admissible and defines the mean-zero Neumann-to-Dirichlet map on mean-zero $L^2$ fluxes.",
