          "claim": "On vector fields on $\\mathbb R^d$ put $P=-\\bigl(R_iR_j\\bigr)_{i,j}$, with multiplier $\\xi\\xi^T/|\\xi|^2$ away from zero. This is the orthogonal gradient projection, so $\\|P\\|_{L^2(\\ell^2)\\to L^2(\\ell^2)}=1$ by [F2]. The finitely many compositions in [F1] give a finite $L^4(\\ell^4)$ bound $C_4\\ge1$. Apply scalar [F2] on the product of Lebesgue measure and counting measure on $\\{1,\\ldots,d\\}$ to this matrix operator, whose $L^2$ norm there is exactly one. If $1/p=(1-\\theta)/2+\\theta/4$, then $\\|P\\|_{L^p(\\ell^p)\\to L^p(\\ell^p)}\\le C_4^\\theta$. Since $\\|z\\|_{\\ell^p}\\le\\|z\\|_{\\ell^2}\\le d^{1/2-1/p}\\|z\\|_{\\ell^p}$ for $p>2$, its Euclidean-vector $L^p$ norm is at most $D_p=d^{1/2-1/p}C_4^\\theta$, which tends to one as $p\\downarrow2$. These extensions agree with the $L^2$ projection on common inputs by density.",
