          "claim": "We record the local homogeneous-Neumann estimate used below. In boundary normal coordinates $(y,t)$ on a fixed collar, the Laplace equation is $\\partial_i(a^{ij}\\partial_jv)=0$ with smooth positive coefficients, $a^{t\\ell}=0$ for every tangential index $\\ell$, and homogeneous conormal condition $\\partial_tv=0$ on $t=0$. For a compactly supported collar cutoff $\\eta$, tangential testing with $-\\delta_{-h}(\\eta^2\\delta_hv)$ gives $c\\|\\eta\\nabla\\delta_hv\\|_2^2\\le C\\|v\\|_{H^1}^2+(c/2)\\|\\eta\\nabla\\delta_hv\\|_2^2$: the coefficient difference is bounded by $C|h|$ before division by $h$, the cutoff terms by $C\\|v\\|_{H^1}$, and $2ab\\le\\epsilon a^2+\\epsilon^{-1}b^2$ absorbs them. There is no boundary term because these are admissible weak Neumann tests. Applying [F4] gives all second derivatives with at least one tangential derivative. The equation then solves for $\\partial_{tt}v$, since $a^{tt}\\ge c>0$. Iterating with nested cutoffs proves $\\|v\\|_{H^k(V)}\\le C_k\\|v\\|_{H^1(V_0)}$ for every integer $k$, with $V$ a smaller collar. In detail, tangentially differentiating $r$ times introduces only terms $\\partial_i((D_y^\\beta a^{ij})\\partial_jD_y^{r-\\beta}v)$ with $|\\beta|\\ge1$; their energy pairings are bounded by the already known derivatives of total order at most $r$, leaving $\\nabla D_y^rv$ on the left. Derivatives with two or more normal indices are recovered successively by differentiating the equation and isolating $a^{tt}D^\\alpha\\partial_{tt}v$; every other derivative of the same total order has fewer normal indices, while differentiated coefficient terms have lower total order. This induction proves the asserted estimate, rather than importing a Neumann boundary theorem. For a smooth forcing the same induction includes its finitely many derivatives on the right; a smooth nonzero normal datum can first be subtracted using a smooth collar function $t f(y)$ with cutoff and the appropriate normal sign.",
