          "source": "def-ip-admissible-constraint-class",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$ and $Y$ be real vector spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, and let $A:X\\to Y$ be linear. Supply metrics $d_X$ on $X$ and $d_Y$ on $Y$; for a purely algebraic instance with no specified metrics, use the discrete metrics $d_X(x,x')=0$ if $x=x'$ and $1$ otherwise, and likewise on $Y$. These metrics require no compatibility with the vector operations, and no continuity of $A$ is assumed. The discrete triangle inequality holds because, if two endpoints differ, at least one of the two successive pairs differs. Give $U$ and its subsets their restricted metrics. Thus every pair of real vector spaces still admits this convention. The corresponding metric-space inverse-problem model has admissible truths $U$ and forward map $A|_U$. An **admissible constraint class** is a nonempty subset $C\\subseteq U$; its constrained forward map is the restriction $A|_C:C\\to Y$. The class $C$ is **identifiable for $A$** if $A|_C$ is injective, meaning $Ax=Ax'$ for $x,x'\\in C$ implies $x=x'$. Define the difference set and kernel by\n\n$$C-C:=\\{x-x':x,x'\\in C\\},\\qquad \\ker A:=\\{x\\in X:Ax=0_Y\\}.$$",
