          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice convention of the twisted-chain definition. Let $K\\subset S^3$ be an oriented knot, let $E_K$ be its compact exterior, let $X$ be a finite based deficiency-one two-dimensional spine with one vertex, $n\\ge2$ oriented $1$-cells and $n-1$ oriented $2$-cells, certified by a based cellular simple-homotopy equivalence with $E_K$, and let $\\psi:\\pi_1(E_K)\\twoheadrightarrow H\\cong\\mathbb Z^m$ have $m\\ge1$. Let $\\rho:\\pi_1(E_K)\\to\\mathrm{GL}_d(\\mathbb F)$ be a representation over a field, and assume the degree-one twisted homology over $\\Lambda=\\mathbb F[H]$ is a torsion module. For any admissible generator column of the Fox block matrix of the certified spine, its deleted-minor Wada quotient represents the based twisted Reidemeister torsion of $E_K$ in $Q^\\times/\\mathcal U_{\\rho,\\psi}$, where $Q=\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda)$ and $\\mathcal U_{\\rho,\\psi}=\\{\\pm\\det(\\rho(g))\\psi(g)^d:g\\in\\pi_1(E_K)\\}$. All admissible columns give the same class. If two such deficiency-one spines are related by a supplied finite based simple cellular equivalence carrying the same representation and deck map, their Wada quotient classes also agree. A group-presentation isomorphism alone is not such an equivalence.",
