          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "**Inputs.** An integer $n\\ge2$; a finite Artin word $\\beta\\in B_n$ in the positive-half-twist convention where $\\sigma_i$ sends the $i$th meridian $x_i$ to $x_ix_{i+1}x_i^{-1}$ and $x_{i+1}$ to $x_i$; a surjective color map $c$ from the cycles of the endpoint permutation to $\\{1,\\ldots,\\mu\\}$, written on strands as $c_1,\\ldots,c_n$ and constant around each cycle; a field $\\mathbb F$ with encoded exact arithmetic and equality; a positive integer $d$ and invertible matrices $\\rho(x_i)\\in\\mathrm{GL}_d(\\mathbb F)$; and compatible finite certificates of based deficiency-one $2$-spines for the closure exterior $X$ and the closure-with-axis exterior $Y$. The $X$ spine has generators $g_i=x_1\\cdots x_i$ and relators $r_i=g_i^{-1}\\beta_*(g_i)$ for $1\\le i<n$. The $Y$ spine has generators $g_1,\\ldots,g_n,x$ and relators $s_i=\\beta_*(g_i)(x^{-1}g_ix)^{-1}$ for $1\\le i\\le n$; $x$ is the meridian of the axis. The certificates include a based cell structure on $T$ with one vertex, $1$-cells $x,g_n$, and the commutator $2$-cell; the one-vertex, one-core-edge structure on $V=D^2\\times S^1$; and compatible cell bases for $X=Y\\cup_TV$. These spine and gluing certificates are geometric input and are not recognized by the procedure. Put $H=\\langle t_1,\\ldots,t_\\mu\\rangle\\cong\\mathbb Z^\\mu$, $\\psi(x_i)=t_{c_i}$, $g_n=x_1\\cdots x_n$, $\\Lambda=\\mathbb F[H]$, and $Q=\\operatorname{Frac}(\\Lambda)$.\n\n**Instructions.**\n\n1. Read the supplied $X$ and $Y$ spine/presentation correspondences and compatible gluing as certified geometric input; check their finite cell counts and relator lists, but do not attempt to recognize whether the data describe exterior spines. Return `not-applicable` if either certificate is absent. Compute the induced free-group automorphism $\\beta_*$ by substituting the displayed Artin rules in the declared word order. Return `not-applicable` if the coloring is not preserved on endpoints, if the representation fails any closure relator, or if, after setting the axis meridian to the identity, it fails any mapping-torus relator $s_i=\\beta_*(g_i)(x^{-1}g_ix)^{-1}$. Equivalently the braid invariance checks $\\rho(\\beta_*(x_i))=\\rho(x_i)$ must hold for all meridians.\n\n2. Set $\\psi(x)=1$ on the axis meridian. Verify the deck map on the relators $r_i$ and the axis relators $s_i$, and verify the colors are surjective, so $\\psi$ descends to both spine groups and is an epimorphism. Compute the positive-meridian full abelianization basis first, then use the explicit color map $H_L\\to H$; no unannounced equal-variable or total-linking specialization is performed.\n\n3. Rewrite $\\beta_*(g_i)$ in the free basis $g_1,\\ldots,g_n$, using $x_i=g_{i-1}^{-1}g_i$ and $g_0=1$. Compute the block Fox matrix $B_\\beta$ with $(i,j)$ block $\\Phi(\\partial\\beta_*(g_i)/\\partial g_j)$, using the product and inverse recursions of the Wada definition and $\\Phi(g)=\\rho(g)\\psi(g)$. The braid fixes the boundary word $g_n$, so its last block row is $(0,\\ldots,0,I_d)$. Let $\\overline B_\\rho(\\beta)$ be the upper-left $(n-1)d$-square block.\n\n4. Compute $D=\\det(\\rho(g_n)\\psi(g_n)-I_d)$. If $D=0$, return `not-applicable`: this designated deleted column is inadmissible, even if another column of the Fox matrix could be used.\n\n5. Form the twisted cellular boundary matrices $d_1,d_2$ of the supplied spine from the Fox lemma. Use exact Gaussian elimination over $Q$ to check the chain identity $d_1\\circ d_2=0$ (the row-matrix product $J A_{\\rm stack}=0$). If this identity fails, return `not-applicable` because the encoded data do not define a chain complex. If it holds but the ranks are not $\\operatorname{rank}(d_1)=d$ and $\\operatorname{rank}(d_2)=(n-1)d$, return $0$ under the declared torsion convention. Otherwise compute $N=\\det(\\overline B_\\rho(\\beta)-I_{(n-1)d})$ and return the rational function $N/D$ as the twisted Burau determinant value, modulo the determinant/deck units of the based torsion convention.\n\nThe braid convention is shown for two adjacent downward-oriented strands. The left strand crosses over the right strand for $\\sigma_i$; the gap records the underpass. The word, color map, and algebraic action above, rather than the diagram, determine the computation.\n\n```tikz\n\\begin{tikzpicture}[x=1.2cm,y=1.0cm,>=stealth,line width=1pt]\n  \\node at (0,2.2) {$x_i$};\n  \\node at (1,2.2) {$x_{i+1}$};\n  \\draw (0,2) -- (1,0);\n  \\draw (1,2) -- (0.53,1.06);\n  \\draw[white,line width=4pt] (0.47,0.94) -- (0,0);\n  \\draw (0.47,0.94) -- (0,0);\n  \\node at (0, -0.25) {$x_{i+1}$};\n  \\node at (1, -0.25) {$x_i$};\n\\end{tikzpicture}\n```\n\nThe displayed generator interchanges endpoint positions, with the original left strand passing over; the colors must follow the same permutation along each link component.",
