          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ip-differential-range-invariance",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X$, $Y$, and $Z$ be real Banach spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be nonempty, let $x_0\\in U$, and let $F:U\\to Y$ be a map. A **differential range-invariance factorization at $x_0$ on $U$** consists of a bounded linear map $K:Z\\to Y$ and a map $r:U\\to Z$ such that\n\n$$F(x)-F(x_0)=Kr(x)\\qquad(x\\in U).$$\n\nThus every data increment lies in the range of the fixed operator $K$. The factorization alone does not assert that $K$ is injective, that $r$ is unique, or that a tangential-cone inequality holds. If $U$ is open and $F$ and $r$ are Fréchet differentiable, differentiating this identity gives $F'(x)=Kr'(x)$ at every $x\\in U$; no converse is asserted without further hypotheses.",
