          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot in the oriented standard three-sphere and let $F$ be a connected compact smooth oriented surface with boundary $K$, oriented so that its induced boundary orientation agrees with the given orientation of $K$. Choose a sufficiently small oriented product tubular neighborhood $N(K)$ compatible with the inward conormal of $F$, set $E=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K)$ and $T=\\partial E$, and put $S=F\\cap E$. Thus $S$ is a connected properly embedded surface in $E$ and its boundary on $T$ projects to $K$ with degree $+1$. Orient the normal line of $F$ by requiring $(n,u,v)$ to be positively oriented in $S^3$ whenever $(u,v)$ is a positive tangent basis of $F$. Choose a product bicollar of $S$ in $E$, with $S_+$ on the $+n$ side and $S_-$ on the $-n$ side, and let $Y$ be the compact cut-open complement with these boundary copies. The inclusion $j:S\\hookrightarrow F$ obtained by removing the boundary collar induces an isomorphism $j_*:H_1(S;\\mathbb Z)\\xrightarrow{\\sim}H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)$. Let $\\iota_\\pm:S\\to Y$ be the two collar-face inclusions and define\n$$i_\\pm:=(\\iota_\\pm)_*\\circ j_*^{-1}:H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\to H_1(Y;\\mathbb Z).$$\nForm a space $\\widetilde E_F$ from copies $Y_n$ ($n\\in\\mathbb Z$) by gluing $S_-(Y_n)$ to $S_+(Y_{n+1})$ for every $n$, with the remaining boundary faces glued by the inherited product collar. Let $t$ be the deck shift $Y_n\\to Y_{n+1}$, which is the shift of the positive meridian, and let $\\Lambda=\\mathbb Z[t^{\\pm1}]$. Then $Y$ is connected, $\\partial S$ is the preferred longitude, and the linking-evaluation map\n$$H_1(Y;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow\\operatorname{Hom}(H_1(F;\\mathbb Z),\\mathbb Z),\\qquad [\\gamma]\\longmapsto\\bigl([a]\\mapsto\\operatorname{lk}(\\gamma,a)\\bigr)$$\nis an isomorphism. There is an exact sequence of $\\Lambda$-modules\n$$H_1(F;\\mathbb Z)\\otimes\\Lambda\\xrightarrow{i_- -t i_+}H_1(Y;\\mathbb Z)\\otimes\\Lambda\\longrightarrow H_1(\\widetilde E_F;\\mathbb Z)\\longrightarrow0,$$\nThus $H_1(\\widetilde E_F;\\mathbb Z)$ is the cokernel of the displayed map.",
