          "quote": "Let $f\\in C_c^\\infty(\\mathbb R^2;\\mathbb C)$ and, for $z=(z_1,z_2)\\in\\mathbb C^2$, define $F(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}f(x)e^{-2\\pi i(x_1z_1+x_2z_2)}\\,dx$ using the compact-support Riemann integral on any rectangle containing $\\operatorname{supp}f$. This value is independent of the containing rectangle. Each coordinate slice of $F$ is entire, with $\\frac{\\partial F}{\\partial z_j}(z)=\\int_{\\mathbb R^2}(-2\\pi ix_j)f(x)e^{-2\\pi i x\\cdot z}\\,dx$ for $j=1,2$, and the restriction of $F$ to $\\mathbb R^2$ is the compact-support Fourier transform in the stated line-normal convention. The proof uses no Countable Choice.",
