        {
          "id": "cor-ip-limited-angle-compact-support-uniqueness-1.1",
          "claim": "The hypothesis and the compact-support Fourier-slice identity [F2] give $\\widehat f(\\sigma\\theta)=0$ for every $\\theta\\in\\Theta$ and $\\sigma\\in\\mathbb R$. By [F1], this includes the nonempty open positive-radius sector generated by $J$; choose a product of open real intervals $I_1\\times I_2$ inside that sector. For each real $\\xi_2\\in I_2$, the entire function $z_1\\mapsto\\widehat f(z_1,\\xi_2)$ vanishes on $I_1$, so [F5] makes it zero for every complex $z_1$. Then for every real $\\xi_1$ the entire second-coordinate slice vanishes on $I_2$, and [F5] makes it zero everywhere. Thus $\\widehat f$ vanishes on all of $\\mathbb R^2$.",
