        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Let $C_0,C_1$ be essential simple closed curves whose primitive classes agree up to sign. Reverse one parameter if needed, so the classes agree. Their algebraic intersection is zero. Put them in transverse position; if they intersect, their lifts to $\\mathbb R^2$ are proper lines with the same primitive deck translation. An innermost pair of intersections of such lifts bounds a disk with interior disjoint from the lifts; projecting that disk gives a bigon. Isotoping one arc across the bigon removes two intersections and preserves the isotopy class. Repeating this finite reduction makes $C_0,C_1$ disjoint. Cutting the torus along $C_0$ gives an annulus, and $C_1$ is an essential curve in that annulus, hence is parallel to its core and cobounds a subannulus with $C_0$. An isotopy across that subannulus carries $C_0$ to $C_1$. Thus the unoriented slope depends only on the primitive class modulo simultaneous sign.",
          "step": "2.1",
