          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Choice. It supplies countable choice for the chosen tubular data: for any sequence of nonempty sets, apply full choice to its range and compose with the index map. Let $L=\\bigcup_{i\\in I}K_i$ be a finite oriented link in the standard oriented $3$-sphere, where $I$ is a finite index set. For each component $K_i$, include a chosen closed tubular neighbourhood $N(K_i)$, its exterior $E_{K_i}=S^3\\setminus\\operatorname{int}N(K_i)$, the positive meridian $\\mu_i$, and the preferred longitude $\\lambda_i$; the longitude is the degree-one parallel class in $H_1(\\partial E_{K_i};\\mathbb Z)$ that maps to zero in $H_1(E_{K_i};\\mathbb Z)$. For each $i\\in I$, choose an integer $n_i$. The resulting **integral framed-link surgery diagram** is this link and integer family, with the surgery result defined by removing the interiors of all $N(K_i)$ and gluing an oriented solid torus to each boundary torus so that its meridian maps to $n_i[\\mu_i]+[\\lambda_i]$. If $I=\\varnothing$, the surgery result is $S^3$. A diagram is considered up to orientation-preserving ambient isotopy carrying each component framing; a planar projection is only a way of drawing the link.",
