          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "**Sobolev matrix algebra and boundary approximation.** By [F3]–[F4] and the weak product rule obtained by smooth approximation, $\\|vw\\|_{W^{2,3}}\\le K_A\\|v\\|_{W^{2,3}}\\|w\\|_{W^{2,3}}$: the second-derivative cross terms obey $\\|DvDw\\|_3\\le\\|Dv\\|_6\\|Dw\\|_6$, and [F3] controls both $L^6$ norms. Similarly $\\|cv\\|_{W^{1,3}}+\\|vc\\|_{W^{1,3}}\\le K_A\\|c\\|_{W^{1,3}}\\|v\\|_{W^{2,3}}$. Passing the derivative test identities to $L^3$ limits proves completeness of the ambient Sobolev spaces from [F4]. The power series for $e^u$ therefore converges in $W^{2,3}$, locally uniformly in that norm, and defines a continuously differentiable composition there; termwise differentiation is justified by the convergent exponential majorants. For $u\\in\\mathfrak{su}(2)$ pointwise, $u^2=-s^2I$ for a real $s$, so $e^u=\\cos s\\,I+(\\sin s/s)u$ (with the continuous value at zero) is unitary with determinant one. Polynomial Neumann approximants to $u\\in X$ exponentiate to smooth $SU(2)$ maps with zero normal derivative; continuity gives $e^u\\in\\mathcal G_N$. Products of maps in $\\mathcal G_N$ remain in it, and inverse is matrix adjoint and hence continuous. Gauge transformation by such maps sends $Y$ to $Y$, by approximating both inputs with smooth zero-normal inputs and using the above product estimates. Also $du\\in Y$ for $u\\in X$.",
