          "claim": "**The critical Coulomb coefficient and the perturbation.** In $d^*(e^{-u}ae^u-a)$ the term $e^{-u}(d^*a)e^u-d^*a$ vanishes. Only derivatives falling on the exponentials remain, so their pointwise norm is at most $C|a||Du|$ for $|u|\\le1$. Subtracting at $u,v$ gives at most $C|a|(|D(u-v)|+|u-v|(|Du|+|Dv|))$. By $L^4\\cdot L^{12}\\subset L^3$, this gives a difference bound $C\\|a\\|_4\\|u-v\\|_{W^{2,3}}$ when $\\eta\\le1$. The contracted derivative of $e^{-u}\\lambda e^u$ consists of $e^{-u}(d^*\\lambda)e^u$ and terms bounded by $C|\\lambda||Du|$. The same subtraction, [F3] and Holder give a difference bound $C\\|\\lambda\\|_{W^{1,3}}\\|u-v\\|_{W^{2,3}}$. Thus, after choosing one universal $C_0$, the preceding estimates prove $\\|d^*(R(u)-R(v))\\|_3\\le C_0(\\eta+\\|a\\|_4+\\|\\lambda\\|_{W^{1,3}})\\|u-v\\|_{W^{2,3}}$. The cancellation of $d^*a$ is why no higher norm of $a$ occurs in this bound.",
