          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "In the chosen closure, the standard marked circle meets $\\bar R$ once, so $c_1(w)$ evaluates oddly on $\\bar R$. The ordinary admissible theory in [F3] therefore applies. The torus has genus one, so the extreme-surface identity in [F3] gives $\\ker(\\mu(y)-2)\\cap\\ker\\mu(\\bar R)^N=\\ker(\\mu(y)-2)\\cap\\ker\\mu(\\bar R)$ for every $N\\ge1$. Taking the union over $N$ therefore identifies the Definition's generalized surface-$0$/ordinary point-$+2$ subspace with the source's ordinary simultaneous $(0,2)$ eigenspace $I_*(\\bar Y\\mid\\bar R)_w$. This is the exact torus convention in [F4], rather than an assumption of semisimplicity. It is finite-dimensional, and [F4] identifies the allowed closures up to isomorphism. This proves well-definedness of the rational group used here, without choosing a canonical basis or confusing it with the doubled full ordinary group.",
