      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "step": "1.1",
          "claim": "Put $X=S^3\\setminus K$ and $\\Sigma^\\circ=\\Sigma\\setminus K$. Since $\\Sigma$ is compact in $S^3$, it is closed; hence $\\Sigma^\\circ$ is a closed embedded oriented surface in $X$. Since $J$ is transverse to $\\Sigma$, its inverse image of $\\Sigma$ is a compact $0$-manifold and hence finite; choose a basepoint $x_0\\in J\\setminus\\Sigma$. Then $x_0\\in X\\setminus\\Sigma$. Every based loop class has a smooth representative: on a lift $t\\in[0,1]$ with basepoint $0=1$, fix $0<a<1/2$ and reparametrize by $r(t)=0$ on $[0,a]$, $r(t)=(t-a)/(1-2a)$ on $[a,1-a]$, and $r(t)=1$ on $[1-a,1]$. The interpolation $(1-u)t+ur(t)$ is a based homotopy, and the reparametrized loop is constant on a closed arc about $x_0$. Apply [F5] relative to that arc, then [F4] to make the smooth representative transverse to $\\Sigma^\\circ$ while keeping it fixed near the arc, which maps into $X\\setminus\\Sigma^\\circ$. For such a loop $C$, let $I_\\Sigma(C)$ be its oriented intersection number with $\\Sigma^\\circ$, using the local sign that is $+1$ when $C$ crosses from the negative to the positive side under the ambient-induced coorientation. This is the local sign of [F3]: the positive normal followed by a positive basis of $\\Sigma$ gives the ambient orientation. For any based homotopy between transverse loops, reparametrize its interval variable by $\\chi_a$ from [F7]; it then agrees with the smooth endpoint loops on endpoint collars. Extend it constantly at both ends to a continuous map $\\widehat H:\\mathbb R\\times S^1\\to X$. Put $A=((-\\infty,a/2]\\cup[1-a/2,\\infty))\\times S^1$, a closed subset of the boundaryless smooth source $\\mathbb R\\times S^1$. The extension is smooth on an open neighbourhood of $A$, since there it is independent of the first variable and equals a smooth endpoint loop. Apply [F5] on this boundaryless source relative to $A$, then [F4] there relative to $A$ to make the resulting smooth map transverse to the closed submanifold $\\Sigma^\\circ$. The second application is valid because the map is unchanged near the tails and its endpoint loops are transverse. Both operations leave a neighbourhood of $A$ fixed. Restrict the final map to $[0,1]\\times S^1$; it and its boundary restrictions are transverse, so the boundary-preimage assertion in [F4] applies to this compact cylinder. In particular its intersection trace is a compact oriented one-manifold whose boundary consists exactly of the endpoint intersections. Equivalently [F3] gives equality of their signed counts, so $I_\\Sigma$ is homotopy invariant. Concatenation can be smoothed in a ball about $x_0$ disjoint from $\\Sigma$, so intersection counts add. Thus $I_\\Sigma$ is a group homomorphism $\\pi_1(X,x_0)\\to\\mathbb Z$ and factors through $H_1(X;\\mathbb Z)$ by [F2].",
