        "evidence": "Read the complete Definition and local trace-quotient supplier: real matrix energy form, uniform ellipticity, quotient trace data, its fixed-atlas H1/2 identification, and the subsequent local well-posedness lemma. Scalar and matrix boundary interfaces now match through the proved quotient isomorphism.",
        "source_section": "Definition",
        "source_exact": "Assume the Axiom of Choice and Countable Choice. Let $n\\ge2$ and let $\\Omega\\subset\\mathbb R^n$ be a bounded connected open set with smooth boundary. Write $H^1(\\Omega)=W^{1,2}(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$ and let $H_0^1(\\Omega)$ be the $H^1$-closure of $C_c^\\infty(\\Omega;\\mathbb R)$. Define the boundary trace-data space $\\mathcal B_\\Omega:=H^1(\\Omega)/H_0^1(\\Omega)$ with quotient norm; the trace-quotient lemma identifies this quotient with the fixed-atlas $H^{1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ boundary data convention of the scalar conductivity map. Let $\\gamma\\in C^\\infty(\\overline\\Omega;\\operatorname{Sym}_n(\\mathbb R))$ be uniformly positive definite, so some $0<c\\le C<\\infty$ satisfy $c|\\xi|^2\\le \\xi^T\\gamma(x)\\xi\\le C|\\xi|^2$ for all $x\\in\\overline\\Omega$ and $\\xi\\in\\mathbb R^n$. For $f=[U]\\in\\mathcal B_\\Omega$, a weak solution with boundary datum $f$ is a class $u_{\\gamma,f}\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ with $[u_{\\gamma,f}]=f$ and\n\n$$\\int_\\Omega \\gamma\\nabla u_{\\gamma,f}\\cdot\\nabla\\varphi\\,dx=0\\qquad(\\varphi\\in H_0^1(\\Omega)).$$\n\nThe weak anisotropic Dirichlet-to-Neumann map is the bilinear-form candidate $\\Lambda_\\gamma:\\mathcal B_\\Omega\\times\\mathcal B_\\Omega\\to\\mathbb R$ given, whenever the weak solution exists, by\n\n$$\\langle\\Lambda_\\gamma f,h\\rangle:=\\int_\\Omega\\gamma\\nabla u_{\\gamma,f}\\cdot\\nabla V_h\\,dx,$$\n\nwhere $V_h\\in H^1(\\Omega)$ is any representative of $h$. The local anisotropic conductivity well-posedness lemma proves existence and uniqueness for every boundary class, independence of the representative $V_h$, and boundedness on $\\mathcal B_\\Omega\\times\\mathcal B_\\Omega$; it therefore makes this candidate a bounded bilinear map. The map is the boundary energy form for the equation $\\operatorname{div}(\\gamma\\nabla u)=0$. Under the trace-quotient isomorphism and the local boundedness result, this form gives the usual weak map into the dual $H^{-1/2}(\\partial\\Omega)$ with the scalar convention.\n\n"
      }
    },
