        {
          "fact": "A1",
          "source": "def-countable-choice",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$, is the following\nstatement.\n\n> For every family $(X_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by\n> $\\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\\mathbb{N}$ such that\n> $f(n) \\in X_n$ for every $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.\n\nEquivalently, in the vocabulary of Choice function: every at most\ncountable family of nonempty sets has a choice function.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ip-composite-double-regularization",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y,Z$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces over one scalar field, let $F:X\\to Y$ and $G:Y\\to Z$ be compact linear maps, and set $A:=G\\circ F:X\\to Z$. For spectral regularization families $R^G_\\alpha:Z\\to Y$ for $G^\\dagger$ and $R^F_\\gamma:Y\\to X$ for $F^\\dagger$, and a parameter rule $(\\alpha,\\gamma):(0,\\infty)\\times Z\\to(0,\\infty)^2$, define $T_\\delta(z^\\delta):=R^F_{\\gamma(\\delta,z^\\delta)}(R^G_{\\alpha(\\delta,z^\\delta)}z^\\delta)$ for $z^\\delta\\in Z$. The order is fixed by the factorization: first regularize $G$, then $F$; no general identity $A^\\dagger=F^\\dagger G^\\dagger$ is part of this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.2",
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-ip-compact-linear-inverse-model",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $X,Y$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces over the same scalar field, and let\n$K:X\\to Y$ be a compact linear operator. The associated compact linear inverse\nmodel has parameter space $X$, data space $Y$, forward map $K$, and admissible\ntruth class $X$: for a true parameter $x^\\dagger\\in X$, the exact datum is\n$y=Kx^\\dagger\\in Y$. Given an exact datum $y\\in Y$, solving this inverse\nproblem means finding a parameter $x\\in X$ such that $Kx=y$. No injectivity,\nsurjectivity, or closed-range assumption is included in the model.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ip-power-source-set",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $K:X\\to Y$ be compact between real or complex Hilbert spaces over the same scalar field and let $\\nu,\\rho>0$. The power source ball is $X_{\\nu,\\rho}(K):=\\{|K|^\\nu w:w\\in X,\\ \\|w\\|_X\\le\\rho\\}$, where $|K|^\\nu=(K^*K)^{\\nu/2}$ is defined by compact spectral calculus. It lies in $N(K)^\\perp$ and consists of minimum-norm representatives of the exact data $Kx$. In the convention $S=K^*K$ this is the source condition $x=S^{\\nu/2}w$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "def-ip-unbounded-hilbert-moore-penrose-inverse",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $X$ and $Y$ be real or complex Hilbert\nspaces over the same scalar field, and let $K:X\\to Y$ be a bounded linear\noperator. Write $R(K):=\\{Kx:x\\in X\\}$ for its range,\n$N(K):=\\{x\\in X:Kx=0\\}$ for its kernel, and\n$M^\\perp:=\\{z:\\langle z,m\\rangle=0\\text{ for every }m\\in M\\}$ for the\northogonal complement of a subspace $M$. The kernel $N(K)$ is closed, so the\nrestriction\n$$\\widetilde K:=K|_{N(K)^\\perp}:N(K)^\\perp\\to R(K)$$\nis a linear bijection. Define the Hilbert-space Moore–Penrose inverse $K^\\dagger$\nto have domain\n$$D(K^\\dagger):=R(K)\\oplus R(K)^\\perp$$\nand, for the unique decomposition $y=r+z$ with $r\\in R(K)$ and\n$z\\in R(K)^\\perp$, set\n$$K^\\dagger y:=\\widetilde K^{-1}r.$$\nThus $K^\\dagger y$ is the unique minimum-norm solution of $Kx=r$, and the\northogonal-range component is mapped to zero. Boundedness of $K^\\dagger$ on\nthis domain is not part of the definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-singular-value-decomposition-for-compact-operators",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]). Let $H$ and\n$K$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces ([[def-hilbert-space]]), let\n$T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a compact operator\n([[def-compact-linear-operator]]), let $|T|$ and the singular values $s_n(T)$ be\nas in the absolute-value definition\n([[def-absolute-value-and-singular-values-of-a-compact-operator]]). Let\n$J=\\{1,2,3,\\ldots\\}$ when $\\operatorname{ran}T$ is infinite-dimensional. When\n$\\operatorname{ran}T$ is finite-dimensional, put\n$r:=\\dim\\operatorname{ran}T\\in\\mathbb N$ ([[def-dimension]]) and let\n$J=\\{1,\\dots,r\\}$, interpreted as $\\varnothing$ when $r=0$. Thus $J$ indexes\nexactly the positive singular values counted with multiplicity, which we write\nas $(s_j)_{j\\in J}$ in nonincreasing order. Then:\n\n1. there are orthonormal families $(e_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $(\\ker T)^\\perp$ and\n   $(f_j)_{j\\in J}$ in $\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}T}$, indexed by exactly $J$,\n   with $|T|e_j=s_je_j$ and $f_j=s_j^{-1}Te_j$ for every $j\\in J$;\n2. for every $x\\in H$ the series converges in norm and\n   $$Tx=\\sum_{j\\in J}s_j\\langle x,e_j\\rangle f_j,$$\n   and its finite partial sums\n   $T_n:=\\sum_{j\\le n}s_j\\langle\\cdot,e_j\\rangle f_j$ satisfy\n   $\\|T-T_n\\|\\le s_{n+1}$ for every $n$ with $n+1\\in J$ (and $T_n=T$ for\n   $n\\ge r$ when $r<+\\infty$);\n3. the linear map $U$ defined on the span of $\\{e_j:j\\in J\\}$ by $Ue_j:=f_j$,\n   extended by continuity to $(\\ker T)^\\perp$ and by zero on $\\ker T$, is a\n   **partial isometry** with\n   $$T=U|T|,\\qquad U^*U=P_{(\\ker T)^\\perp},\\qquad U^*U\\text{ is the orthogonal projection onto }(\\ker T)^\\perp,$$\n   and $UU^*$ the orthogonal projection onto $\\overline{\\operatorname{ran}T}$;\n4. the zero-padded sequence $(s_n(T))_{n\\ge1}$ is *not* used to index the\n   orthonormal systems: the systems carry exactly the index set $J$ of the\n   positive singular values, and the terms $s_n(T)=0$ beyond the rank in the\n   finite-rank case are numerical padding only.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-hilbert-space-adjoint",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume the Axiom of Countable Choice. Let $H$ and $K$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces and let $T\\in\\mathcal B(H,K)$ be a bounded linear operator ([[def-bounded-linear-operator]], [[def-space-of-bounded-linear-operators]]). For fixed $y\\in K$ the map\n\n$$x\\longmapsto\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K$$\n\nis a bounded linear functional on $H$: it is linear in $x$ because $T$ is linear and the pairing is linear in its first argument, and $|\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K|\\le\\|Tx\\|\\,\\|y\\|\\le\\|T\\|\\,\\|x\\|\\,\\|y\\|$ by Cauchy–Schwarz and the operator-norm inequality ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-in-an-inner-product-space]], [[def-operator-norm]]). By Riesz representation ([[thm-riesz-representation-for-hilbert-space]]) there is therefore a unique vector $T^*y\\in H$ with\n\n$$\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K=\\langle x,T^*y\\rangle_H\\qquad\\text{for every }x\\in H .$$\n\nThe **Hilbert adjoint** of $T$ is the map\n\n$$T^*:K\\longrightarrow H,\\qquad y\\longmapsto T^*y .$$\n\nIt is the unique map $K\\to H$ satisfying the displayed identity, since two such maps have $\\langle x,(T^*_1-T^*_2)y\\rangle=0$ for all $x$ and hence $(T^*_1-T^*_2)y=0$ by positive definiteness.\n\n**The dictionary with the Banach transpose.** Write $R_H:H\\to H^*$ and $R_K:K\\to K^*$ for the Riesz maps $R_H(x)=\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,x\\rangle_H$ and $R_K(y)=\\langle\\,\\cdot\\,,y\\rangle_K$, and let $T'\\in\\mathcal B(K^*,H^*)$ be the transpose of $T$, $(T'g)(x)=g(Tx)$ ([[def-transpose-of-a-bounded-operator]]). Then for all $y\\in K$ and $x\\in H$,\n\n$$\\bigl(R_H(T^*y)\\bigr)(x)=\\langle x,T^*y\\rangle_H=\\langle Tx,y\\rangle_K=(R_Ky)(Tx)=\\bigl(T'(R_Ky)\\bigr)(x),$$\n\nso $R_H\\,T^*=T'\\,R_K$. The Hilbert adjoint is thus the Banach transpose conjugated by the Riesz identifications of $H$ and $K$ with their duals; it is a different operator from $T'$ whenever the Riesz maps are conjugate-linear.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-compact-spectral-filter-and-qualification",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $X,Y$ be real or complex Hilbert spaces and let\n$A:X\\to Y$ be compact and bounded. Put $S=A^*A$. By\n[[lem-compact-gram-operator-is-positive-and-has-kernel]], $S$ is compact,\npositive, and self-adjoint, with $\\ker S=\\ker A$. Let $\\Sigma$ be the set of\nnonzero eigenvalues of $S$ and $P_t$ the orthogonal projection onto the\neigenspace of $t\\in\\Sigma$; let $P_0$ be the orthogonal projection onto\n$\\ker S$. The compact spectral multiplier of a bounded scalar function\n$q:\\{0\\}\\cup\\Sigma\\to\\mathbb F$ is\n$$q(S)x:=q(0)P_0x+\\sum_{t\\in\\Sigma}q(t)P_tx,$$\nwhere the finite-subset net converges in norm by\n[[lem-compact-spectral-multiplier-is-well-defined]]. For $\\nu>0$, define\n$S^\\nu$ by the multiplier $q_\\nu(0)=0$ and $q_\\nu(t)=t^\\nu$ for $t>0$.\nThis multiplier is bounded: if $t\\in\\Sigma$ and $u\\in E_t$ is a unit vector,\nthen $0<t=\\|Su\\|\\le\\|S\\|$, so $|q_\\nu(t)|\\le\\|S\\|^\\nu$.\n\nFor $\\lambda>0$, the Tikhonov filter and residual filter are\n$$g_\\lambda(t)=\\frac1{t+\\lambda},\\qquad r_\\lambda(t)=1-tg_\\lambda(t)=\\frac{\\lambda}{t+\\lambda}.$$\nThe Tikhonov solution for data $y_\\delta\\in Y$ is\n$$x_\\lambda^\\delta=g_\\lambda(S)A^*y_\\delta =(A^*A+\\lambda I_X)^{-1}A^*y_\\delta.$$\nIndeed, the spectral decomposition gives $S+\\lambda I_X$ the multiplier\n$t+\\lambda$ on each $E_t$ and $\\lambda$ on $\\ker S$, so $g_\\lambda(S)$ is\nits inverse, whose existence is also supplied by\n[[lem-hilbert-tikhonov-normal-operator-invertible]].\nThe source condition of order $\\nu>0$ and radius $R\\ge0$ means that\n$x^\\dagger=S^\\nu w$ for some $w\\in X$ with $\\|w\\|\\le R$. A family of\nfilters $g_\\alpha$ with residual filters $r_\\alpha(t)=1-tg_\\alpha(t)$ has\nqualification at least $p>0$ when, for each $0<\\nu\\le p$, some finite\nconstant $C_\\nu\\ge0$ satisfies\n$$t^\\nu|r_\\alpha(t)|\\le C_\\nu\\alpha^\\nu \\quad\\text{for every }\\alpha>0\\text{ and }t\\in\\Sigma.$$\nWhen $\\Sigma=\\varnothing$, this condition is vacuous.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.2",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F8",
          "source": "lem-ip-spectral-interpolation-for-power-source-balls",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume Countable Choice. Let $K:X\\to Y$ be compact between real or complex Hilbert spaces over the same scalar field, let $\\nu>0$ and $w\\in X$, and put $x=|K|^\\nu w$. Then $\\|x\\|_X\\le\\|Kx\\|_Y^{\\nu/(\\nu+1)}\\|w\\|_X^{1/(\\nu+1)}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
