          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Put $e_A:=x^\\dagger-R^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha z$. On the common singular system, $R^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha A$ has multiplier $(1-p^F_\\gamma(\\lambda_j^2))(1-p^G_\\alpha(\\mu_j^2))$. The bounds on $FR^F_\\gamma$ and $GR^G_\\alpha$ bound the two factors by $C_F,C_G$; on $N(A)$ the product with $A$ is zero. Therefore $e_A=|A|^\\nu u$ with $u=(I-R^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha A)w$ and $\\|u\\|\\le(1+C_FC_G)\\rho$. The same coefficient formula, now on $Z$, gives $\\|AR^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha\\|\\le C_FC_G$. The exact composite residual decomposes as $(z-z^\\delta)+(z^\\delta-GR^G_\\alpha z^\\delta)+(GR^G_\\alpha z^\\delta-AR^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha z^\\delta)+AR^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha(z^\\delta-z)$. These four terms have norms at most $\\delta,\\tau_G\\delta,\\tau_F\\delta,C_FC_G\\delta$, respectively, by the noise bound, the two stopping rules, and the composite operator bound. Since $Ae_A=z-AR^F_\\gamma R^G_\\alpha z$, it follows that $\\|Ae_A\\|\\le(1+\\tau_G+\\tau_F+C_FC_G)\\delta$. Applying [F8] to $e_A=|A|^\\nu u$ proves $\\|e_A\\|\\le C_1\\rho^{1/(\\nu+1)}\\delta^{\\nu/(\\nu+1)}$.",
