          "source": "def-markov-conjugation-and-stabilization-moves",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(B_n)_{n\\ge0}$ be the braid groups of the Artin presentation\n([[def-braid-group-by-the-artin-presentation]]), with generators\n$\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_{n-1}$ and the two Artin relations, and let\n\n$$\\iota_n\\colon B_n\\longrightarrow B_{n+1},\\qquad \\iota_n(\\sigma_i):=\\sigma_i\\quad(1\\le i\\le n-1),$$\n\nbe the **standard inclusion homomorphism**. It is well defined: the defining\nrelators of $B_n$ are among the defining relators of $B_{n+1}$ under the\nassignment $\\sigma_i\\mapsto\\sigma_i$, so [[thm-von-dyck]] extends the\nassignment to a homomorphism. Its image is generated by\n$\\sigma_1,\\dots,\\sigma_{n-1}$, and we write $\\iota_n(\\beta)$ for it; whether\nthis homomorphism is injective is not needed anywhere on this page, and no\nsubgroup identification is made. For the geometric description, use the base configuration $Q^{(n+1)}$ of\n[[def-elementary-geometric-half-twist]]. Express $\\beta$ as an Artin word and\nreplace its letters by the half twists with the same indices at\n$Q^{(n+1)}$. The letters with $i<n$ fix the rightmost point, since their support\ndiscs exclude it. This gives a representative of $\\iota_n(\\beta)$ under\n[[prop-the-artin-presentation-surjects-onto-geometric-braids]]; it is independent\nof the word as a geometric braid class. Thus adding the strand on the right\nincludes expressing the old braid at the first $n$ target basepoints. Those\npoints differ from $Q^{(n)}$, so the original parametrized strands are not\nliterally left untouched. The final half twist is the target $\\sigma_n$.\n\nOn the disjoint union $\\bigsqcup_{n\\ge0}B_n$ define two kinds of elementary\n**Markov moves**.\n\n**Conjugation.** For $\\beta,\\gamma\\in B_n$, replace $\\beta$ by\n$\\gamma\\beta\\gamma^{-1}$, an element of the same group $B_n$. The number of\nstrands does not change. When $\\beta$ is a geometric braid and $\\gamma$ the\nclass of a braid in $G_n$, this is realized on closures by reading $\\gamma$\naround the axis, as the closure-preservation lemma below shows.\n\n**Stabilization and destabilization.** For $n\\ge1$ and $\\beta\\in B_n$ the **positive\nstabilization** is $\\beta\\sigma_n:=\\iota_n(\\beta)\\cdot\\sigma_n\\in B_{n+1}$ and\nthe **negative stabilization** is\n$\\beta\\sigma_n^{-1}:=\\iota_n(\\beta)\\cdot\\sigma_n^{-1}\\in B_{n+1}$; the inverse\noperations, from $B_{n+1}$ to $B_n$, are called **destabilizations**. Both\nsigns occur, and the sign is the sign of the exponent of the new generator. By\n[[prop-the-artin-presentation-surjects-onto-geometric-braids]] and\n[[def-elementary-geometric-half-twist]] the product $\\beta\\sigma_n^{\\pm1}$ is\nthe class of the target-basepoint geometric word just described, composed\nwith the target half twist $\\sigma_n^{\\pm1}$ in its fixed product position;\nthat half twist fixes the first $n-1$ target points. Inserting the generator inside a factorization need not produce a conjugate of that fixed stabilization. It nevertheless gives a Markov sequence: if $\\beta=uv$, old conjugation by $v$ gives $vu$, its right stabilization is $(vu)\\sigma_n^{\\pm1}$, and conjugation by $v^{-1}$ gives $u\\sigma_n^{\\pm1}v$. Only moving a factor past the whole word is cyclic conjugacy. At $n=0$ no $\\sigma_0$ exists, so the empty braid has only conjugation moves and is an isolated Markov class.\n\n**Markov equivalence.** Two braids $\\beta,\\beta'$ (possibly with different\nnumbers of strands) are **Markov equivalent** when they can be connected by a\nfinite sequence of moves each of which is a conjugation, a positive or negative\nstabilization, or a destabilization. Markov equivalence is an equivalence\nrelation on $\\bigsqcup_n B_n$: it is reflexive, symmetric by allowing inverses\nof the listed moves, and transitive by concatenating sequences.\n\n**Convention on orientation and side.** All braids are read with the product\nand side conventions fixed on the pages `geometric-braids-and-artin-generators`\nand `artin-presentation-completeness-and-braid-combing`: the rightmost factor is\ntraversed first in geometric time, and stabilization always adds the new\nstrand on the right. The definition is choice-free: no choice principle is used\nin the Artin presentation, in the inclusion, or in the identification of\n$\\sigma_n$ with the elementary geometric half twist.",
