            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "def-ip-noise-level-free-parameter-rule",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Fix a compact Hilbert inverse model $K:X\\to Y$ and a family of reconstruction\nmaps $(R_\\alpha)_{\\alpha>0}$, with each $R_\\alpha:Y\\to X$, both held fixed. A\ndeterministic parameter-choice rule is a function\n$\\alpha:Y\\to(0,\\infty)$. It is **noise-level-free** (also called **heuristic**)\nwhen the value $\\alpha(z)$ for an observed datum $z\\in Y$ uses only $K$, the\nfixed family, and $z$; it does not take the exact datum or a noise bound\n$\\delta$ as input. Here $\\delta\\ge0$ is a valid noise bound for $z$ and an\nexact datum $y$ when $\\|z-y\\|_Y\\le\\delta$. The resulting reconstruction is\n$R_{\\alpha(z)}z$.",
